{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API.md vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API.md vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md'","aggregated_output":"# my-3D2dge API card (v0.89.1)\n\nmy-3D2dge (\"My 3D 2D Game Engine\") is a general-purpose retro-modern 2D game engine built for AI models. Use it to port, remaster, reimagine and remix games from the 8-bit to 64-bit eras (NES, SNES, Genesis, N64, PS1), or to make spiritual successors to them. Everything is drawn by code: no image or sound files. Characters are 3D skeletons drawn as pixel art, and the camera can be any classic 2D view.\n\n**Start from a kit:** each genre has one, a complete small game written to be copied (the Genre playbook below names it). In a single file it sits between `GAME START` and `GAME END`: the starter's shell (the game, its title menu, the proof), the slice and the start; a genre kit holds one slice, `dist/my-3d2dge.html` every one and the ANIMATION LAB (how each animation is driven). In the handoff package each kit's game is `kits/<id>.js`, readable. **To make a game, change the slice into yours, with its own title, play and game-over scenes, and delete the starter's menu and the slices you do not use. The engine is yours to change too:** fix, extend or rewrite any of it as you see fit.\n\n**Looking a name up:** grep `API-INDEX.md` (every public name, one line; members are filed under instance names: `rig.`, `talk.`, `mover.`, `flow.`, `map.`), not the engine's source. It, the guide (`AI_GUIDE.md`) and the tools are in the handoff package (my-3d2dge.vercel.app/handoff/) and the repository.\n\n## Quick start (a complete game)\n```js\n(() => {\nconst E = My3D2dge, { px, clamp } = E;            // always start like this\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'snes', view: 'threequarter' });\nconst map = new E.TileMap({\n  rows: ['##########', '#@...s...#', '#..##....#', '#........#', '##########'],\n  legend: { '#': 1, '@': 'hero', 's': 'slime' },  // number = wall type, string = spawn tag\n  types: { 1: { h: 30, top: '#57506a', side: '#3d3750' } },\n  floorTex: (x, y) => E.tex.flagstone(x, y, { stones: ['#4b4559', '#554f64'].map(E.hex), mortar: E.hex('#221e2b'), hi: E.hex('#6f6782'), lo: E.hex('#322d3e'), speck: E.hex('#2a2634') })\n});\ngame.cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v);\nconst s = map.find('hero'), hero = new E.Body({ x: s.x, y: s.y, r: 5 });\nconst rig = new E.Humanoid({ hat: 'pointed' });\ngame.start({\n  update(dt) {\n    const m = game.input.move(), d = game.view.screenDirToGround(m[0], m[1]);\n    hero.vx = d[0] * 80; hero.vy = d[1] * 80; hero.update(dt, map);\n    if (Math.hypot(d[0], d[1]) > .1) hero.facing = Math.atan2(d[1], d[0]);\n    rig.update(dt, { x: hero.x, y: hero.y, z: hero.z, vx: hero.vx, vy: hero.vy, facing: hero.facing || 0 });\n    game.focus(hero.x, hero.y, 10);                // the camera looks here: call every update\n  },\n  draw(r) {\n    map.drawFloor(r); r.shadow(hero.x, hero.y, 5); map.queueWalls(r);\n    r.actor(hero.x, hero.y, hero.z, (g, ox, oy) => rig.draw(g, ox, oy, r.view));\n    r.text('HELLO', 4, 4, '#ffffff');              // HUD text in screen pixels\n  }\n});\n})();\n```\n\n## Look target (read this before drawing anything)\nAim above SNES, level with the best PS1 / N64 2D games (Symphony of the Night, Metal Slug, Legend of Mana, Klonoa). Games that skip these steps look like Atari games. **Every game should use all of them:**\n1. **Resolution** `res: 'ps1'` (320x240) or `'snes'`, with `expand: true` so it fills any screen (anchor the HUD to `r.W` / `r.H`), and characters 40-60 px tall: `new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3 })` (HD style is the default: shaded volumetric limbs, outfits, hair).\n2. **Dress the character**: an `outfit`, `hair`, a `hat`, `armor`, a `cape`, `sleeves` (Characters), and a real palette in `colors` (never leave the defaults on everyone).\n3. **Backdrop behind every side-view level**: `const bg = new E.Backdrop('castle-night')` then `bg.draw(r)` first in `draw` (a ground view's floor runs on to the screen's edges: `map.outside`). Presets: `day dusk night castle-night city-night desert forest ocean cave space`, or your own `{ sky, sun | moon, stars, starsY: .7, layers: [{ kind: 'mountains' | 'hills' | 'forest' | 'city' | 'castle' | 'clouds' | 'sea' | 'fog' | 'stalactites', color, y, height, parallax, fill }] }` (`starsY` = how far down the stars reach; a layer but clouds, fog and stalactites fills the screen below its skyline unless `fill: false`).\n4. **Textured tiles**: PlatformMap styles `stone` (with `moss`), `brick`, `wood`, `metal`, `ground` (with `flower`), `block`, `pipe`, `bonus`; TileMap floors from `E.tex.*`, TileMap walls with `face: 'brick' | 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge'` and tops with `roof: 'tiles' | 'slab' | 'leaves' | 'grass'` (a village = timber and plaster houses with tile roofs, hedges, rock cliffs).\n5. **Props in every room** (3-8 per screen): `r.prop(name, x, y, z, { size, color, light: true })` with `torch candle candelabra chandelier brazier window banner pillar statue crate barrel chest pot sign fence lamp door tree pine bush flowers grass rock crystal bones skull vines cobweb lantern streetlamp awning hydrant trashcan firedrum sandbags cactus palm mushroom`. Flames animate, glow and can light the scene. `chest` / `door` take `{ open: true }`, `pillar` takes `{ broken: true }`, `window` is a gothic rose window with lead cames (`colors: [...]` for the glass, `size: 2-3` for a cathedral); hang `vines`, `cobweb`, `lantern` with `{ anchor: 'top' }`. With heroes at size 1.2+, give the scene `propSize: 1.4` (or set `E.style.propSize`) so props match them.\n6. **Effects on every hit**: `particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)`, `smoke`, `fire`, `glints`, `sparks`, `game.flash(color, s)`, `game.freeze`, `game.shake`.\n7. **Color**: `E.tones(hex)` gives `{ deep, sh, base, lt, hi }` hue-shifted shades and `E.ramp(hex, n)` a palette ramp. Shade everything you draw yourself with them (shadow side `sh`, lit edge `lt`, a `hi` sparkle).\n8. **Typography**: the default font is a 5x7 proportional font with lower case. Titles: `E.font.title(g, 'NAME', x, y, { scale: 3, colors: [...], align: 'center' })`. HUD text: `{ shadow: '#000', outline: false }` or `gradient: [top, bottom]`. `font: 'tiny'` is the old 3x5 font.\n9. **Light every scene**: `game.lights.enabled = true; game.lights.ambient = .5` in a scene's `enter`, then each draw a few lights where light comes from (`r.prop('lamp', x, y, 0, { light: true })`, `game.lights.add(x, y, z, 60, .9, { color })`): dusk, torchlight and glows give depth. It looks the same in every browser and check; `game.enableGPU({ map })` adds shadows in ground views, and falls back to it.\n10. **Faces in dialog**: `talk.say(lines, { name: 'MIRA', portrait: miraRig })` puts a live close-up of that Humanoid (blinking, hair, hat) beside every line. `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 40)` draws one anywhere (HUD, RPG party windows).\n\n## Coordinates: what x, y, z mean in each view\nWorld units: one tile = 16. **z is always up.** Game logic never stores screen pixels.\n\n| View | Looks like | Ground plane | Use for levels | Move with |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `'iso'` (yaw 45, pitch 30) | Diablo, Landstalker | x/y ground, z up | `E.TileMap` | `E.Body` |\n| `'threequarter'` (pitch 55) | Zelda LttP, Stardew | x right, y down the screen | `E.TileMap` | `E.Body` |\n| `'topdown'` (pitch 80) | GTA 1, Hotline Miami | x right, y down | `E.TileMap` | `E.Body` |\n| `'brawler'` (pitch 25) | Final Fight, 2.5D | x right, y into the screen | `E.TileMap` or `E.PlatformMap` | `E.Body` or `E.Platformer` |\n| `'side'` (pitch 0) | Mario, Mega Man | **x right, z up, y = 0 (depth)** | `E.PlatformMap` | `E.Platformer` |\n| `'overhead'` (pitch 90, scale 1) | 1942, Galaga, Pac-Man | x = screen x px, y = screen y px | none, or a TileMap | your own x/y, or `E.Body` |\n\n- A unit is `r.ppu` pixels: at zoom 1 iso 1.41, overhead 1, the others 1.5 (a tile about 24). A sprite's pixel is a screen pixel (`{ world: true }`: it grows with the view, as rigs and maps do).\n- In `'side'` the screen shows x (right) and z (up). **y is depth and is normally 0.** Focus the camera with `game.focus(hero.x, 0, hero.z + 24)`.\n- In ground views, the screen direction the player pushes becomes a world direction through `game.view.screenDirToGround(mx, my)`.\n- `r.actor(x, y, z, (g, ox, oy) => ...)` calls your function with `(ox, oy)` = the buffer pixel where world point (x, y, z) lands. Draw the character around that point.\n\n## Rules\n1. Keep game logic in world units, and use `dt` everywhere. `update(dt)` runs in exact fixed steps: every 1/120 s of real time is one step of `dt = 1/120 * game.timeScale`, the same at any refresh rate (`game.alpha` = the part of a step left over).\n2. Draw only with `E.px.*`, `E.font`, `E.ui`, sprites, props and the `r.*` helpers. Never use `ctx.arc`, `ctx.stroke`, `ctx.fillText` or canvas gradients on the game buffer (they blur pixels). For translucency and glow use `px.blend(g, alpha, 'add' | 'multiply' | 'screen' | 'normal', () => {...})`, `px.polyDither`, `px.ddisc`, `r.glowDisc` and `r.shadow`: they use stepped alpha and blend modes like PS1 / SNES hardware (or dithering with `E.style.trans = 'dither'` for an NES / Genesis look).\n3. Anything that can overlap anything else goes through `r.actor`, `r.sprite` or `r.queue` (depth sorted). Backgrounds (`r.sky`, `map.drawFloor`, `level.draw`) come first in `draw`.\n4. Read input only in `update`. Use `pressed(a)` for one-shot actions (menus, jumps, shots), `down(a)` for held actions, and `buffered(a)` + `consume(a)` for forgiving combat input.\n5. Animate characters from continuous values (velocity, timers, easing): the rigs, not frame lists.\n6. Colors are `'#rrggbb'` (or `'#rgb'`) strings.\n7. Put the whole game in one IIFE `(() => { ... })();` between the markers, and start it with `game.start(...)`.\n\n## Common mistakes (check these first)\n- Nothing on screen: the camera looks at the wrong place. Use `game.follow(hero)` once (in the scene's `enter`), or call `game.focus(...)` every update. In side view the hero has y = 0.\n- Walls or level missing: you forgot `map.drawFloor(r)` + `map.queueWalls(r)` (TileMap) or `level.draw(r)` (PlatformMap).\n- Using a TileMap for a side-scroller. Side-scrollers use `E.PlatformMap` + `E.Platformer`.\n- HUD drawn in world coordinates. Use `r.text(...)` or `r.overlay(g => ...)` with screen pixels (`r.W`, `r.H`).\n- An old key layout: platformers want `input: 'PLATFORMER'` (Space jumps), shooters `input: 'SHMUP'` (Space fires).\n- Errors show in a red box on screen and in the console. Fix the first one first.\n- Colours are `'#rrggbb'`, `rgb()`, `hsl()` or a basic name ('orange'); any other draws magenta.\n- `mouseGround()` is `[x, y]`; `settings.reset(group)` resets a group ('Sound'; none: all), not a key; `particles.explosion()` adds a hit-stop and a shake (`{ freeze: false, shake: 0 }`); `E.font.text` outlines unless `outline: false`.\n- A frame past 50 ms gives up its time (no catch-up): a test in a slow browser waits on the game's state, not the clock.\n\n## Game\n`new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', view, views, res, expand, input, bg })`\n- `res`: `'nes'` 256x240, `'snes'` 256x224, `'genesis'` 320x224, `'gb'` 160x144, `'gba'` 240x160, `'ps1'` / `'n64'` 320x240, `'wide'` 400x225, or `[w, h]`. Fixed pixels: whole-number scaling on a desktop screen, the largest fit on a phone (Settings, Pixel scaling). Leave it out for an adaptive size.\n- `expand: true`: full screen at any shape, no bars. `res` becomes the least the screen shows and `r.W` x `r.H` grows to fill the window (a 16:9 monitor shows about 427x240 of `'ps1'`, a phone held upright about 320x690). Anchor the HUD to `r.W` and `r.H`, and give the level something to show in the extra space (more of it, a backdrop, a HUD deck under it on a tall screen). `expand: { maxW, maxH }` caps the growth. A scene may set it too.\n- Adaptive size: `minW` / `minH` set the scale (the screen shows at least that many pixels), `maxW` / `maxH` cap the view (the rest is letterboxed). `portrait: { maxH: 1000 }` overrides them while the screen is taller than wide, so a phone held upright (9:16) is filled instead of showing a band across its middle. `game.screen.portrait` says which way it is.\n- `input`: `'DEFAULT'` (top-down action), `'PLATFORMER'`, `'SHMUP'`, `'THIRDPERSON'`, or your own `{ action: ['KeyQ', 'Pad0'] }`.\n- `views`: the views your game supports; `game.nextView()` cycles them (bind it to a key).\n- Start: `game.start({ update(dt), draw(r) })`, or with scenes (below).\n- Methods: `follow(obj, { z: 16, lead: 20 })` (camera tracks it automatically), `focus(x, y, z)` (or aim it yourself every update), `setView(id)`, `nextView()`, `freeze(s)` (hit-stop: `update` waits; `game.hitstop` is the time left), `shake(n)`, `after(s, fn)`, `every(s, fn)` (timers in game time, return `{ cancel() }`), `hook('input' | 'step' | 'enter', fn)` (your own each step, paused too for input; returns its removal), `mouseGround()`, `go(scene, data)`, `enableGPU({ map })`.\n- Fields: `W`, `H` (screen pixels), `time`, `real`, `timeScale` (0.25 = slow motion), `paused`, `input`, `audio`, `particles`, `lights`, `view`, `errors`, `cam`. `game.fail('my power', err)` reports an error in your code (logged, in `errors`, on the error panel) and plays on.\n- Camera zoom and turn: `game.setZoom(1.5)` (.5 .. 3; everything drawn by the engine re-rasterizes crisply, the HUD stays), `game.rotateView(45)` (ground views only), `game.resetCamera()` (to the scene's zoom), `game.zoom`, `game.yaw`, `game.note('ZOOM 2x')` (a short notice at the top of the screen; `game.shownNote` is the one showing). Custom painters can read `r.view.zoom` (undefined at 1x) to scale their own art.\n- The player's zoom: a scene's `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5 }` lets the player zoom within `zoom` from `start` (wheel, `-` `=`, pinch, Settings; `false`: none; `AI_GUIDE.md`, Camera).\n- Tests and tools: `new E.Game({ headless: true })` (the default in Node: no screen, sound or DOM; `draw` is skipped, or with `draw: true` run on a stand-in, its errors in `game.errors`) or `{ loop: false }` runs only by `game.advance(seconds, { draw })` (exact fixed steps at once); `input.tap(action)` presses for one step, `input.hold(action, on)`; `game.debug = { snapshot() {...} }` is your game's handle (`tools/check.mjs` prints it); `game.stop()`, `start()`, `dispose()`.\n- Sessions: `?record=1`, then the pause menu's Save session; `node tools/replay.mjs session.json` replays it exactly, headless (`AI_GUIDE.md`, Sessions).\n- Camera: `cam.offset = [dx, dy]` sits the focus that many screen pixels from the centre (above a phone's touch buttons: `[0, -60]`); `cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v)` keeps it inside the level (in side views a level shorter than the screen sits on the bottom edge; `cam.align = 'center'` to change); `cam.room = [160, 128]` moves screen by screen (Zelda, Metroid) and `cam.moving` is true while it slides; `cam.smooth` = lag in seconds (0: locked to the target); `cam.window = [48, 24]` a dead zone the focus moves in before the camera follows; `cam.snapY = true` follows a followed body's height only when it lands (side-scrollers); `cam.frame([p1, p2], { margin, zoom: [.5, 1] })` keeps them all in view, zooming out as they part (`frame(null)` stops); `cam.free = { keys, edge: 10, drag, speed: 300, bounds: [x0, y0, x1, y1] }` a free camera (strategy, sims): keys, a stick, the screen's edge or a drag pan it, a zoom goes toward the pointer or the pinch.\n\n## Scenes (title, play, game over)\n```js\ngame.start({ scene: 'title', scenes: {\n  title: { update(dt) { if (game.input.pressed('start')) game.go('play', { level: 1 }); },\n           draw(r) { r.sky(['#101848', '#402a70']); r.text('PRESS START', r.W / 2, 100, '#fff', { align: 'center', scale: 2 }); } },\n  play: { pausable: true, view: 'side', input: 'PLATFORMER', res: 'snes', views: ['side', 'brawler'], touch: ['jump', 'attack'],\n          enter(data) { /* build the level */ }, exit() {}, update(dt) {}, draw(r) {} }\n} });\n```\n`game.go(name, data)` switches after a short dithered fade (`game.fadeTime`: a test advances past it; `{ fade: 0 }` cuts; `{ fade: 'wipe' | 'iris' | 'mosaic' | 'swirl' | 'dissolve' | 'white', seconds: .4, at: hero }` a transition, a plain fade under reduce motion); timers (but `after(s, fn, { keep: true })`) and particles are cleared, a hit-stop ends (`timeScale` stays). `pausable: true`: Escape / P / Start pauses it, with the pause menu (below). `view`, `views`, `input`, `res`, `expand`, `touch`, `camera`, `propSize` are options applied on entry (a method so named warns). A scene is one object every visit: reset its state in `enter`. `back()` takes a phone's back gesture first (true if it went back: a sub-menu closed).\n\n## Motion and timing: tweens, scripts, paths, weather\n- Tweens (Phaser's): `game.tween(door, { y: 40 }, .5, { ease: 'outBack', delay, repeat, yoyo, onDone, keep })` eases numbers on game time: paused with the game, slowed by `timeScale`, ended by a scene change unless `keep`. Returns `{ done, cancel() }`. `E.ease` has Penner's set: `linear`, `inSine` `outSine` `inOutSine`, `inQuad` `outQuad` `inOutQuad`, the same for `Cubic`, `Back`, `Elastic`, `Bounce`.\n- Scripts (Unity's coroutines) for cutscenes, boss patterns, wave timelines, tutorials:\n```js full-script\ngame.script(function* () {\n  yield 1;                                   // seconds of game time\n  yield E.until(() => door.open);            // a condition\n  yield game.tween(boss, { y: 80 }, 1.5);    // a tween (or another script, or a generator) finishing\n  for (let w = 0; w < 3; w++) { spawnWave(w); yield E.any([8, () => enemies.length === 0]); }\n});\n```\n  `E.all([...])` waits for all; a bare `yield` waits one step. It runs at once to its first `yield`, the same at any refresh rate; `{ keep: true }` outlives the scene; `.cancel()`, `.done`, `.value`.\n- Paths: `const p = new E.Path([[x, y, z], ...], { closed, smooth: 'catmull' })`: `p.length`, `p.at(d)` (`{ x, y, z, angle }` by distance along: moving platforms, dives, rails), `p.nearest(x, y)` (a racer's progress), `p.draw(r)` to see it.\n- Weather: `game.weather = { kind: 'rain' | 'snow' | 'ash' | 'leaves' | 'sand', amount: .8, wind: .3, lightning: .2 }` (null: clear) falls through the world in every view, settles or splashes, flashes and thunders; cosmetic only (its own random stream), and it ends with the scene: set it in `enter`.\n- Wrap-around (Asteroids): `x = E.wrap(x, 0, W)`, `E.wrapDiff(a, b, W)` (the short way across the seam), and `wrap: [W, H]` on `r.sprite` or `r.actor` draws a copy across each edge.\n\n## Standard features (every game has them: don't build your own)\nWith no code: a **pause menu** on pausable scenes (Resume, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Quit to title; keys, mouse, gamepad and touch), **settings** saved per game (`game.settings`: volume, music, effects, mute, reduce motion, shake and flashes, pixel scaling, a screen filter, the frame rate and its readout), **controls** the player rebinds (conflicts said first; dead zone, invert aim and move, button symbols, vibration; touch button side, size, opacity), **full screen** (a phone's first tap, a top-right button, the menu; turning it off is remembered), a **menu button** at the top right (on a touch screen it stays in play: it is the touch START), a **way out** of every menu screen, the **back gesture** stepping back in the game (`game.back()`) before it leaves the page, **pausing** when the page is hidden or the controller unplugs, and a page set up for phones (safe areas, no scrolling, zooming or pull to refresh on the canvas).\n- Adjust: `new E.Game({ menu: { items: [['Restart', fn]], settings: [{ key: 'hints', label: 'Hints', value: true }, { key: 'difficulty', options: ['Easy', 'Normal', 'Hard'], value: 'Normal' }], controls: { attack: 'Sword', skill: false }, quit: 'title' } })`, then read `game.settings.hints`. Leave parts out: `menu: false` (your own pause), `fullscreen: 'button' | false`, `corner: false`, `backGesture: false`, `autoPause: false`, `touch: 'never'` (a phone is told a controller or keyboard is needed). `game.cornerArea()`: where the corner's buttons cover the screen (or null): keep a HUD clear of it.\n- Save slots (`game.saves`): three slots and an autosave, each with its place, level and time played. `game.saves.write = () => ({ data, place, level })` and `read = save => {...}`; then `save(slot)` (0, 1, 2 or `'auto'`), `load(slot)`, `copy(a, b)`, `erase(slot)`, `autosave()`, `list()`, `latest()` (a title's Continue). `menu: { saves: true }` (or `{ slots, version, migrate(data, from) }`) puts Save (while `saves.canSave`: a save point) and Load in the pause menu, and `saves.show('save' | 'load')` opens the screen from your own menu. Blocked storage keeps them for the visit.\n- Prompts: `game.input.label('jump')` is that action's key or button on the player's device, after rebinding ('Space', 'A', '✕'). PlayStation symbols by default: `settings: [{ key: 'glyphs', value: 'playstation' }]` (a standard key without a label sets its default). A canvas title's OPTIONS: `game.menu('settings')`. From code: `game.settings.set(key, v)`, `game.fullscreen.toggle()` (from a key, click or tap), `game.pause(reason)`, `game.resume()`. `live: true` (or `game.menu(name, { pause: false })`): menus over a world that keeps stepping (online play).\n- **Boot**: heavy setup in steps behind a boot screen, and a report for any error before play: `game.start({ scenes, scene, boot: { title, steps: [['Building the city', () => { map = build(); }]] } })` (`AI_GUIDE.md`, Boot).\n\n## Input: `game.input`\n- Actions in every preset: `up down left right` (WASD, arrows, d-pad), `start` (Enter, Start), `pause` (Escape, P, Start), `confirm` (Enter, Space, J, Z, pad A), `cancel` (Escape, Backspace, X, K, pad B).\n- DEFAULT adds `attack` (J, X, click), `dash` (Space, Shift, K), `skill` (L, E, right click), `jump` (Z). PLATFORMER: `jump` (Space, Z, K, pad A), `attack` (X, J, click), `dash` (Shift, C, L), `skill`. SHMUP: `fire` (Space, Z, J, click), `bomb` (X, K). THIRDPERSON: `sprint` (Shift), `jump` (Space), `crouch` (C, Ctrl), `interact` (E, F), `attack` (J), `block` (K), `dodge` (L), `use` (Q), `map` (M, Tab).\n- `down(a)`, `pressed(a)`, `released(a)`, `repeat(a, delay, rate)` (menus), `buffered(a, win)` + `consume(a)`, `consumeAll()`, `anyPressed()`, `move()` (screen direction `[x, y]`, length up to 1), `use(preset)`, `touchButtons(['jump', 'attack'])`, `touchSet('confirm', 'TALK', near)` (a context button: relabel it, or hide it while it has nothing to do).\n- Moves: `input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing })` is true on the step a quarter circle forward and punch ends (numpad notation, mirrored when `facing` is -1: `'623'`, `'41236'`); `input.sequence(['up', 'up', 'down', 'down+right', 'attack'], { window: .5 })` any run of inputs (a cheat code); `input.history` the last second of them (`{ t, dir, pressed }`, for an input display).\n- Players (two to four on one machine): `game.players` is `[game.input, ...]`, each an input with this API, its `player` number, `name` (`'P2'`), `color` (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`) and device. `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin: (input, n) => {} })` lets them in: Start on a free gamepad, or 2 on the keyboard (player 2 takes its right half: arrows, `.` `/` `,` and the numpad; player 1 keeps WASD with F G H; a preset's `halves: { left: { pass: ['KeyR'] }, right: {...} }` its own); `game.addPlayer({ pad: 0 })` or `({ keys: 'right' })`, `game.removePlayer(p)`. Prompts, the pause (P2 PAUSED), a lost controller and the Controls screen are per player. One screen: `cam.frame(heroes)`. Split: `game.splitScreen(2, { follow: [h1, h2], layout: 'auto' })` (`'columns'`, `'rows'`, `'grid'`; up to 4) draws the scene once per viewport (`r.viewport` is `{ i, x, y, w, h }`), then `scene.hud(r)` once over the whole screen; `game.viewports[i].follow(t)` or `.focus(x, y)`.\n- `clear()` releases held inputs, pending presses, virtual sticks and touch buttons; focus loss does this automatically. `suppress(a)` blocks an action for the current tick; `suppressCode('Mouse0')` lets a HUD widget consume that mouse button while other devices bound to the action still work.\n- Controller settings: `deadzone` (default `.18`, radial and rescaled), `invertAimY`, `lastDevice` (the index has the rest). The left stick and the touch stick, pushed past halfway, press `up` / `down` / `left` / `right` as the d-pad does (menus, dialog choices, ladders); bind `PadAxisUp/Down/Left/Right` to your own menu actions for the same; the right stick is `PadRAxisUp/Down/Left/Right`, the wheel `WheelUp`/`WheelDown`; `value(a)`: 0 to 1 (a trigger's travel, a stick's push). Options while Create is held is no press (the AI Hub's chord).\n- Touch settings: `touchEnabled`, `stickSide` (`'left'` or `'right'`), `stickRadius` (CSS pixels), `touchFilter(event)` (return false to reserve a canvas tap for UI). `touchArea()`: where the touch buttons cover the screen (`{ x0, y0, x1, y1 }`, or null): keep a prompt clear of it. `clicked()`: where a click or tap on the canvas landed (`[x, y]` in screen pixels) the step after it, else null; `pointer()`: the mouse in screen pixels; `drag()`: how far the stick's finger moved this step (`[dx, dy]`, or null), to steer one to one; `steer = 'drag'`: a finger anywhere steers, no stick drawn (`stickOn`: a finger is on it). `zones = [{ x0, y0, x1, y1, act }]` (fractions of the canvas): a finger in one holds its act until it lifts (a pinball's halves). Gestures (a mouse or one finger, screen pixels): `box()` → `{ x0, y0, x1, y1, done }` (a left drag; on touch a long press, then a drag), `longPress()`, `doubleTap()`, `pan()` → `[dx, dy]` (a finger's drag on open ground, a middle or right drag), `zoomPoint()`; `feed('down' | 'move' | 'up', x, y, { touch, button, id })` plays a pointer in a test. `bindButtons(root)` wires `[data-act]` elements.\n\n## Drawing: `r` in `draw(r)`\n- Background: `r.sky(['#top', '#mid', '#bottom'])`, `r.starfield({ count, speed, dir: 'down' | 'left' })`.\n- Depth sorted: `r.actor(x, y, z, (g, ox, oy) => rig.draw(g, ox, oy, r.view), { flash: '#ffffff', flashMix: .75, alpha, xray, outline, ghost, emissive, every, key })` (the flash tints over the sprite so shading shows: `flashMix` .5 by default, 1 solid; `every: 2, key: npc` redraws a background character every other frame; actors up to about 250 px tall), `r.sprite(x, y, z, sprite, { flip, anchor: 'bottom' | 'center', glow, flash })`, `r.queue(x, y, z, g => {...}, { bias })` (sorted by that one point: a long wall by `r.queueSpan(a, b, z, h, fn)`), `r.actorNow(...)` (as `r.actor`, drawn at once over what is drawn so far), `r.box(g, x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1, top, side)` (inside a queue function). `{ pick: unit }` on any of them (and `r.shape`), then `r.pick(sx, sy)` → what the last frame drew under a screen point, front to back.\n- Floor marks: `r.shadow(x, y, radius, alpha, color, surfaceZ)`, `r.decal(g => r.groundArc(...))`, `r.groundDisc`, `r.groundRing`, `r.groundPts(x, y, rad, n, z)`.\n- On top, unlit, screen pixels: `r.text(str, x, y, color, { align, scale, outline })`, `r.textAt(x, y, z, str, color)`, `r.overlay(g => {...})`.\n- `r.w(x, y, z)` = buffer pixel of a world point. `r.W`, `r.H`, `r.ix`, `r.iy` (pass to dither functions), `r.view`, `r.visible(x, y, z)`.\n- X-ray: `r.actor(..., { xray: true })` shows the actor's hidden part, a translucent tint with a 1-px outline (a checker in `E.style.trans = 'dither'`), wherever a wall, a prop or shape at least chest high (22; `hides: true | false` says otherwise) or anything queued with `{ hides: true }` is drawn in front of it; not by one rising less than 8 above its feet (the roof it stands on, a cut-down wall). `xray: { color, outline (false: none), actors: true }`: with `actors` other bodies in front hide it too (a hero stays findable in a crowd). Afterimages: `ghost: { color, life, alpha, owner }`; `owner` (the actor's `{x, y, z}`) keeps a trail left nearer the camera sorted behind the live body.\n- Glowing things you draw yourself: `r.queue(x, y, z, fn, { emissive: true })` is drawn again after lighting so darkness never dims it (flames, lasers, magic, neon). It still hides behind whatever stands in front of it: walls and `r.actor` bodies do this on their own, and any other queued draw can join them with `{ solid: true }`. (Canvas lighting builds a depth-ordered mask for this; `game.r.glowMask = false` skips it on slow machines.)\n\n## Pixels, sprites, text, UI\n- `px.rect(g, x, y, w, h, c)`, `dot`, `line(g, x0, y0, x1, y1, c, width)`, `disc(g, x, y, r, c)`, `ell`, `poly(g, pts, c)`, `polyDither(g, pts, c, 0..1, r.ix, r.iy)`, `ddisc`, `sprite(g, spr, x, y, flip)` (or `{ flip, rot, scale }`), `glow(g, 0..1)` (GPU glow for what follows).\n- Sprites from strings (for coins, bullets, ships, icons, NES-style enemies): `const COIN = E.sprite(['.yy.', 'yYYy', '.yy.'], { y: '#f0b020', Y: '#fff0a0' }, scale)`. `.` and space are transparent. Frames: `const walk = E.anim([f1, f2, f3], 8, { pingpong })` (or one sheet: `E.anim(sheet, 8, { frames: 4 })`), drawn with `r.sprite(x, y, z, walk, { t })`. `r.sprite(..., { rot: angle, scale })` turns and scales in whole pixels (cached in 16 steps a turn; `steps: 32`); `E.recolor(spr, { '#3a8a4a': '#8a3a4a' })` swaps a palette.\n- Font (5x7 proportional with lower case: ASCII, the face buttons, arrows, ★ ♥ ♪ ✓ … ° € £, the accented Latin letters; any other is `?`; `font: 'tiny'` for 3x5): `E.font.text(g, str, x, y, color, { outline, shadow, gradient, scale, align, wrap, font })`, `E.font.title(g, str, x, y, { scale, colors, outline, depth, depthColor, align })`, `E.font.width(str, opts)`, `E.font.lineHeight(opts)`, `E.font.wrap(str, maxW, opts)`. Glyphs: ASCII plus `♥ ★ ← → ↑ ↓ • × ♪ ▸ ©`.\n- UI (inside `r.overlay(g => ...)`): `E.ui.box(g, x, y, w, h, { bg, border, gradient, alpha })` (a gradient window like Final Fantasy; `bg` can be `[top, bottom]`, `gradient: false` for flat, `alpha: .6` for a see-through HUD panel), `E.ui.bar(g, x, y, w, h, 0..1, color)`, `E.ui.hearts(g, x, y, hp, max)` (half hearts allowed), `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, size, { zoom: 3, turn: .6, bg, frame, cape: false, weapon: false, tint: '#808080' })` (character close-up; it stays upright while the rig kneels or lies down; `tint` greys out a KO'd hero).\n- Dialog: `const talk = new E.Dialog(game)`; `talk.say(['Line one.', 'Page two.'], { name: 'ELDER', portrait: elderRig, onDone, choices: ['YES', 'NO'], onChoice(i) })` (choices show on the last page: the keys, or a tap or click on one, pick); in update `if (talk.update(dt)) return;` (true while a modal box is open: play waits); in draw `talk.draw(r)`. `portrait` takes a Humanoid (live close-up; `portraitSize: 40`, `portraitOpts: { zoom, turn }`) or a function `(g, x, y, size) => {...}`. Radio chatter during play: `talk.say('Enemy fighters ahead!', { name, portrait, auto: 2.5, modal: false })` turns its pages and closes by itself while the game keeps running. Per line: `place: 'top' | 'middle' | 'bottom'`, `y` (pixels) and `alpha` (see-through box). `new E.Dialog(game, { speed, lines, color, nameColor, voice, width, bg, border, vars })` (`voice`: a sound, or `(ch, i, talk) => {}` per letter); `talk.close()`, `talk.open`. Markup in the text: `{c=#ffd36a}gold{/c}` a color, `{w=.5}` a pause, `{s=12}slowly{/s}` letters a second, `{shake}..{/shake}`, `{wave}..{/wave}`, `{$name}` a value from `say(text, { vars })` or `talk.vars`.\n- Menu: `const menu = new E.Menu(game, ['START', { label: 'LOAD', disabled: true }], { x: 'center', y: 100, title, onPick(i, item), onCancel, scale: 2, rows: 6 })` (rows: it scrolls); `menu.update(dt)`; `menu.draw(r)`. Keys, d-pad and sticks move it; a click or tap on an entry picks it (so a phone can leave a title), and the mouse over one moves the cursor. A tap on a dialog box turns its page as confirm does.\n- HTML screens and HUD: `const ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme: { scale: 2 } (a TV; or `tv: true`), settings, screens: { pause, help, dev: { rows: ['glyphs'] } } })`; `ui.show('title' | 'results' | 'map' | ..., spec)`; each step `ui.hud({ objective, status, bars, prompt, minimap })` (`minimap: { map, fog, camera: true, markers, onClick(x, y) }`); `ui.toast(title, text)`.\n\n## Sound: `game.audio` (chip synth, no files, starts after the first key press)\n- `sfx(name, { vol, pitch, pan, loop })` → `{ stop(), set({ pan, vol }) }` (a loop sounds on: an engine, rain). Presets: `jump jump2 land step coin pickup key powerup oneup heal hit hurt stomp bump swing whoosh punch kick shoot laser charge explode boom door secret warp die select confirm cancel pause text blip`.\n- Custom: `sfx({ wave: 'square' | 'pulse' | 'pulse12' | 'triangle' | 'saw' | 'sine' | 'noise', freq: 440 or 'A4', to: 880, dur: .2, vol: .3, arp: [0, 4, 7], step: .05, vib: [8, .03] })`, or `audio.define('zap', voice)`; `audio.instrument('pluck', (ctx, bus, time, freq, dur, vol) => ...)` is a `wave` too.\n- Music: `music('title' | 'adventure' | 'dungeon' | 'boss' | 'victory')` (built in; any other name warns and plays nothing), or your own `{ bpm: 120, steps: 4, tracks: [{ wave: 'square', vol: .15, notes: 'C5 - E5 - G5 . . . | ...' }, { wave: 'triangle', notes: '...' }, { wave: 'drums', notes: 'k . h . s . h .' }] }`. Tokens: a note (`C4`, `F#3`, `Bb2`), `-` holds, `.` rests, `|` is ignored; drums `k s h o c t`. `music(null)` stops. `mute()`, `setVolume(0..1)` (or set `audio.musicVolume`, `sfxVolume`, then `audio.applyVolume()`), `duck(on)` (quieter music under your own pause or map screen). Your own Web Audio connects to `audio.bus('sfx' | 'music' | 'voice')`, under the volume and mute.\n- The music clock (the full engine): a song's place is game time, headless too, and the sound follows it. `music(song, { sync: true })` pauses with the game (else it plays on); `audio.pos` → `{ t, step, beat, bar, at, loop, bpm, layers, playing }`; `at(step, fn)`, `onBeat(fn)` (on the update step); `tempo(bpm)`; `layer(name or index, vol, fade)` (a track's `name`, `on: false` starts it silent: the chase's drums in by layers); `music(song, { fade: 1 })` crossfades; `sting(song, { at: 'bar' })` over the music; `sfx(voice, { step })` on a step of the music. `settings.audioOffset` (ms, Settings, Sound: Calibrate) sets `audio.offset`; `audio.calibrate()` → `{ tap(), result, done, stop() }`.\n\n## Levels\n**TileMap** (top-down, isometric, brawler): walls stand on the ground plane.\n- `new E.TileMap({ rows, legend, types, floorTex, block: ['water', 'lava'], ao: 5, outside })` (`ao` = width of the contact shadow along wall bases, 0 = none; `outside`: the ground past the map, a color, or `false` for none). `types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut: true, cutH, face, roof, beam }` (`cut` lowers walls that block the camera, those `map.isFront(cx, cy, view)` says; a map may replace it; `face`: `'brick'` (default) `'stone' 'rock' 'plank' 'timber' 'plaster' 'hedge' 'none'`; `roof`: `'speckle'` (default) `'plain' 'tiles' 'slab' 'leaves' 'grass'`; `beam` = timber color). `floorTex(x, y, floorTag, view)` returns `[r, g, b]` (or `[r, g, b, glow]`); ready-made: `E.tex.flagstone`, `grass`, `dirt`, `water`, `planks`, `checker`, `plain`. A map of more than 128 x 128 cells bakes its floor in squares as the camera comes to them and lets the far ones go (`chunks: true | false` to choose; `map.floorBytes()` what it holds), so a 256 x 256 world map costs what the screen shows.\n- Legend values: a number = wall type, a string = spawn tag, `{ tile, floor, spawn, block }` for both. `block: true` (or the map's `block` list of floor tags) makes deep water, lava or pits stop walkers (jumping bodies pass over); `walkable(cx, cy)`, `block(cx, cy, on)` (a bridge appears). FlowField paths go around blocked cells and follow `block` and `set` at their next `update`.\n- `find(tag)` / `findAll(tag)` give `{ x, y, z, cx, cy }`. Also `cell(cx, cy)`, `set(cx, cy, id)` (open doors), `toCell(x, y)`, `center(cx, cy)`, `heightAt(x, y)`, `floorAt(x, y)`, `groundAt(x, y, r, z)`, `collide(body)`, `los(x0, y0, x1, y1)` (ground only), `bounds(view)` (`{ x0, y0, x1, y1 }`, buffer pixels), `drawFloor(r)`, `queueWalls(r)`.\n\n**Heights** (collision, standing, sight, reach and navigation share one rule): a body's `z` is its feet; a cell's top is `map.height(cx, cy)` (0 = floor); a body stands on the highest top within `z + 2`. Eyes at `z + E.HEIGHTS.eye` (30) see a target at `z + E.HEIGHTS.chest` (22); a body's own `eye` / `chest` override them (crouching). Same level: `|dz| <= 8`. On a TileMap (`dir` = an angle or `[dx, dy]`): `ray(a, b, { eye, target })` → `{ clear, t, x, y, z }`, `sight(a, b)`, `standable(x, y, z, r)`, `ledge(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, rise, facing, edge }` (climb), `landing(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, d }` (a jump across a gap), `drop(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, fall }`, `arcClear(a, b, apex, r)`, `pick(sx, sy)` → `{ cx, cy, z, face }` (the cell under a screen point: `input.clicked()`, any view and turn). Helpers: `E.sameLevel(a, b)`, `E.targets(from, list, { range, arc, dz, sight: map })` → `[{ target, d, dz, angle }]` nearest first, `E.inArc(..., dz)`. `new E.FlowField(map, { climb, drop, jump })` paths over roofs, between levels and across gaps.\n\n**PlatformMap** (side-scrollers; 2.5D in tilted views): tiles on the x/z plane, top row first.\n- `new E.PlatformMap({ rows, legend, types })`. `types[id] = { kind, style, side, top, line, mat }` (`mat`: any material for a solid tile's face). Example: `rows: ['      ?    ', '   ===     ', '@  g   ^^  ', '###########']`, `legend: { '#': 1, '=': 2, '?': 3, '^': 4, '@': 'hero', 'g': 'walker' }`, `types: { 1: { style: 'ground', side: '#8a5a32' }, 2: { kind: 'oneway' }, 3: { style: 'bonus', side: '#e8a830' }, 4: { kind: 'hazard' } }`. Kinds: `'solid'` (default), `'oneway'` (jump up through), `'ladder'`, `'slope'` (`dir: 1` rises to the right, `-1` to the left; `from` / `to` heights 0..1 for gentle slopes), `'hazard'` (spikes, lava), `'deco'`, `'back'` (background wall). Styles: `'stone'` (`moss`) `'brick' 'wood' 'metal' 'ground'` (`flower`) `'block' 'pipe' 'bonus' 'plank' 'spikes' 'ladder' 'liquid' 'plain'`.\n- Spawns: `find(tag)` gives `{ x, z }` with z = the bottom of that cell (feet). Standing on the ground means z = the top of the floor tiles (`level.groundBelow(x, z)`, which returns `null` over a pit), not z = 0. `cell(cx, cz)` / `set(cx, cz, id)` count cz from the bottom row. `slopeZ(cx, cz, x)`, `touching(body, 'hazard')`, `cellsTouching(body, kind)`, `groundBelow(x, z)`, `solidAt(x, z)`, `move(body, dt, solids)`, `bounds(view)`, `draw(r)` (call first, after `r.sky`), `width`, `height`.\n\n## Movement and physics\n**Platformer** (side-scroller character): `const hero = new E.Platformer({ x, z, w: 8, h: 22, run: 95, jump: 285, gravity: 800, airJumps: 0, wallJump: false, dash: 0 })`.\n- Each update: `hero.update(dt, level, game.input, movingPlatforms)`. For enemies pass `{ x: -1..1, jump, jumpPressed, up, down, dash }` instead of the input.\n- Read: `onGround`, `jumped`, `landed`, `facing` (1 or -1), `air`, `climbing`, `hitWall`, `hitCeiling`, `bumped` (`{ cx, cz, id }` of a block hit from below). Methods: `knock(dir)`, `rigState(extra)` (feed it to `rig.update`).\n- Built in: coyote time, jump buffering, variable jump height (let go early for a short hop), slopes and small steps (sticks to the ground going down), ladders (up / down), drop through one-way tiles (down + jump), optional double jump, wall jump and dash.\n- Further moves, each on request: `crouch` (down: half as tall, it crawls), `ledge` (catches a ledge falling past it; up climbs), `wallCling`, `momentum` (slopes, a roll, a charged dash); tile kinds `'water'` (it swims), `'rope'` (up grabs it), `'bars'` (it hangs and travels), grip `friction` (ice .15), `carry` (a conveyor), `bounce` (a spring), `crumble` and `respawn` (`level.update(dt)` each step); `hero.swingFrom(x, z)` (a grapple: a pendulum, jump lets go); draw `level.drawWater(r)` after the actors. State: `crouching`, `hanging`, `clinging`, `swimming`, `onRope`, `onBars`, `swinging`, `rolling`; `rigState()` poses them.\n- Moving platforms: `{ x, z, w, h, vx, vz }` boxes you move yourself and pass as `solids`; riders are carried.\n\n**Body** (top-down / isometric / brawler): `new E.Body({ x, y, r: 5, friction: 0, bounce: 0, gravity: 700 })`. Set `vx`, `vy`, then `body.update(dt, map)`. Also `push(ix, iy, iz)` (knockback), `jump(v)`, and `onGround`, `z`, `groundZ`, `hitWall`, `landed`, `bounced` (hit the ground and bounced: dust, a thud). Walls lower than your feet are walkable, so bodies can jump onto blocks.\n\n**Mover** (third person): `const mover = new E.Mover({ x, y })`; `mover.update(dt, map, game.input)` (input `'THIRDPERSON'`) or an AI's `{ move: [dx, dy], sprint, crouch, jumpPressed }`: it sprints, sneaks, climbs, vaults, leaps gaps, hops down and rolls; `climbAny: true` climbs any wall (jump at it; up the screen is up the wall; jump leaps up, crouch lets go; `mover.wall`, `map.wall(from, dir)`), `glide: true` glides (jump again in the air; `lift`: an updraft), `staminaMax: 100` (climbing, gliding and sprinting spend `mover.stamina`); `rig.update(dt, mover.rigState())`; `mover.play('takedown', { other })`, your own `E.ACTIONS`. Stealth: `E.sees(guard, hero, { map })` → `{ seen, exposure }`, `E.hears`, `new E.Suspicion()` (`update(dt, seen, hero)` → `'calm' | 'notice' | 'search' | 'alert'`), `r.sightCone(guard, { map })`, `r.marker(x, y, z, { label })`.\n\n**Attack** (melee timing for swords, punches, kicks): `const slash = new E.Attack('slash')` (a move from `E.MOVES`, optionally with overrides: `new E.Attack('thrust', { reach: 10 })`) or a spec `{ a0, a1, z0, z1, reach, wind, active, recover }`. `if (input.buffered('attack') && slash.start()) input.consume('attack')`; `slash.update(dt)` (returns the phase that just began); `slash.hits(enemies, e => E.inArc(hero, facing, e, 22, 1.4), e => {...})` hits each target once per swing during `'active'` (moves wait until `hitAt: .35` of the swing, so hit-stop freezes the strike, not the wind-up); `rig.update(dt, { ..., attack: slash.state })`. Also `busy`, `active`, `cancel()`. **Combo**: `const combo = new E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook', 'uppercut'], { window: .3 })` (names, specs or Attacks); `combo.press()` starts or chains the next hit; `update`, `hits`, `state`, `step` work the same. **Knockback**: `E.knockback(attacker, target, 160, up)` pushes a Body (or anything with vx / vy) away.\n\n**Bullets**: `const shots = new E.Bullets(game, { plane: 'ground' | 'side' })`; `shots.fire({ x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, r: 2, team: 'player', color, life, dmg, pierce, sprite })`; `shots.burst(base, E.pattern.spread(angle, n, arc, speed))`; `shots.update(dt, map)`; `shots.hit(targets, (b, t) => {...}, 'player')`; `shots.draw(r)`; `shots.clear(team)`. Patterns: `E.pattern.dir(angle, speed)`, `aim(a, b, speed)`, `aimSide(a, b, speed)`, `spread(angle, n, arc, speed)`, `ring(n, speed, offset)`. In side view the second number of a pattern velocity is vz.\n\n**Helpers**: `E.dist(a, b)`, `E.angleTo(a, b)`, `E.arc(a, b, gravity, { apex | time | speed })` → `{ vx, vy, vz, t }` (a throw that lands at b: a lob, a grenade), `E.overlap(a, b)` (circles x, y, r), `E.overlapBox(a, b)` (side boxes x center, z feet, w, h), `E.inArc(a, facing, b, range, halfAngle, dz)` (melee cone; `dz` limits the height difference), `E.rand(a, b)`, `E.randInt(a, b)`, `E.pick(list)`, `E.chance(p)`, `E.prune(list, fn)`, `E.store.get(key, fallback)` / `set(key, value)` (save data; slots: `game.saves`), `new E.SpatialHash(32)` (`build(list)`, `near(x, y, r, fn)`), `new E.FlowField(map)` (`update(tx, ty)` or `update([[x, y], ...])`: the nearest; `dir(x, y, tx, ty)` walks around walls and `map.solid` shapes), math `clamp lerp approach ease.* angDiff approachAng smoothDamp rng(seed) noise2 hex shade mix`; `E.version` (the release) and `E.versionLabel()` ('v0.7.0 · a1b2c3d 2026-10-07': show it on a title or a debug screen).\n\n## Characters\n**Humanoid** (knights, heroes, soldiers, zombies): `new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build: 'chibi' (default) | 'heroic' (tall) | 'bulky' (brawlers, bosses) | 'skeleton' (tint with colors.bone), weapon: 'sword' (the default) | 'gun' | 'staff' | null (fists) | a game's own (`E.Humanoid.weapon(id, { length, rest, draw(w) })`; `.hat(id, draw)`, `.pose(id, (J, w) => ...)`; `draw: { under, over }`; state `tilt`, `roll`), outfit: 'shirt' | 'tunic' | 'robe' | 'coat', hair: 'short' | 'spiky' | 'long' | 'ponytail' | 'bald', hat: 'cap' | 'pointed' | 'helmet' | 'band' | 'crown' (or { style, color }), sleeves: 'short' | 'long' | 'none', armor, hood (in colors.hair), cape: { len: 6, width: 5, seg: 2.5 }, eyeGlow, hunch, lean, style: 'hd' (default) | 'classic' (stick figures for huge crowds), colors: { skin, hair, cloth, coat, pants, boot, belt, trim, glove, cape, capeIn, metal, plate, hilt, eye, iris } })` (`metal` colors the weapon, `plate` the armor, defaulting to `metal`).\n- `rig.update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, dash, hurt, air, point, aim, attack })`. `facing` is an angle (side view: 0 = right, `Math.PI` = left). `air: true` tucks the legs for jumps; `point: true` holds a gun forward; `aim` tilts it (radians, + = up: `aim: .8` shoots diagonally up, `Math.PI / 2` straight up).\n- **Moves** (`E.MOVES`, use them instead of hand-made specs; every one has anticipation, a committed strike with lunge / step / lean / twist, follow-through and a clean return to the stance, on every build and in every view):\n  - blades and clubs: `slash backslash overhead rising thrust spin plunge twohand`;\n  - fists: `jab` (left hand) `cross hook uppercut haymaker elbow`;\n  - kicks: `kick roundhouse sweep flyingkick knee axekick`;\n  - other: `cast` (both hands) `throw bash claw`.\n  Give each character its own set (a knight: `overhead`, `thrust`, `spin`; a mage: `cast`; a monk: `jab`, `cross`, `roundhouse`; a boss: `haymaker`, `sweep`). `E.move('uppercut', .5)` is the attack state of any move at any moment (previews, cutscenes).\n- `attack: { spec, phase: 'wind' | 'active' | 'recover', u: 0..1 }`, spec `{ a0, a1, z0, z1, reach, r0, r1, hand, rel, blade, spin, kick, plane, lunge, hop, crouch, lean, twist, hold }`. Angles are relative to facing (+ = right hand side). `rel: true`: heights relative to the shoulder and reach scaled by arm length (kick heights as a share of hip height). `r0`/`r1`: reach that extends (thrusts, jabs). `hand: 'L' | 'both'`. In side views horizontal swings turn into the screen plane (a Castlevania chop) by themselves; `plane: 'side' | 'ground'` (or the rig option `swingPlane`) chooses. `lunge` steps into the strike (body motion in local units, clamped to -4..5; to move the character itself, add speed to your body), `hop` lifts the body, `crouch` dips in the wind-up, `hold` is how long the end pose holds (share of recover). You time the phases.\n- In steep ground views (threequarter) rigs and Blobs are drawn from a lower, sprite-like angle so faces show, like SNES RPG sprites (`charView: false` turns it off for one rig, `E.style.charPitch = false` for all).\n- Faces (eyes with catch-lights, brows, mouth), a cheated 3/4 turn toward the camera in side views (`cheat: .5`, 0 turns it off) and hair layers are automatic. `face: { eyes: 'big' (anime / Mana style), bangs: 0..1 }`.\n- Poses: `rig.update(dt, { ..., pose: 'cheer' | 'cast' | 'guard' | 'kneel' | 'crouch' | 'wave' | 'hips' | 'down' })` (they blend in and out), `'block'` (weapon upright across the body), `'die'` (a stagger, then the body topples), `expr: 'smile' | 'shout' | 'angry' | 'wince'` (faces and portraits), `run: 0..1` (run in place), or `down: 0..1` to lie a defeated body flat. `stance: 'guard'` keeps fists up while walking (brawlers; the free hand guards the chin during punches), `stance: 'ready'` holds the weapon forward. `climb: true` with `vz` climbs a ladder hand over hand (`Platformer.rigState()` passes both). Traversal: `crouch` (a sneaking gait), `sprint` (a lean), `hang` with `hangZ` (hands on an edge that high above the feet), `flip: 0..1` (a roll), `land: k` (a heavy landing); `mover.rigState()` sets them. Every wind-up starts from the rig's current pose, so chained hits and hand changes never snap. Idle rigs breathe and shift their weight by themselves.\n- `rig.draw(g, ox, oy, view)` inside `r.actor`, `rig.drawSmear(r)` (a glowing trail along the real blade, fist or foot path, in every view), `rig.debug(r)` (skeleton), `rig.kick(v)` (squash and stretch), `rig.hand()` and `rig.tip()` (world points to spawn bullets from), `rig.worldOffset(p)` (where a point in the rig's own frame, such as a joint in `rig.J`, sits from its feet: to draw or spawn at any joint), `rig.lastView` (the view it last drew with), `rig.solvePose()` (sets `rig.J` from its state each step: wrap it on one rig to move joints).\n**Blob** (slimes, rabbites, bats, imps, round monsters): `new E.Blob({ R: 6, colors: { dk, base, lt, spec, eye, pupil }, ears: 'rabbit' | 'cat', horns: true, wings: true, feet: true, tail: true, mouth: true | 'fangs', face: 'front' })`, `update(dt, { squash: -0.4..0.4, look: [dx, dy], squint, walk: 0..1, flap: 0..1 (wing speed; 0 folds them), hang: true (roost upside down) })`, `kick(v)`, `draw(g, ox, oy, view)`. Mix the parts for a bestiary: `wings` + `mouth: 'fangs'` is a bat, `ears: 'rabbit'` + `feet` a Mana rabbite, `horns` + `tail` an imp.\n\n## Effects and lighting\n- Particles: `game.particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)` (fireball, smoke, sparks, debris, ring, shake, sound), `fire(x, y, z, n, { size })`, `smoke(x, y, z, n, { size, color })`, `glints(x, y, z, n, color)`, `dust(x, y, z, n)`, `sparks(x, y, z, n, angle)`, `bits(x, y, z, n, colors)`, `ring(x, y, r0, r1, color, dur)`, `text(x, y, z, '12', color, { scale: 2, bounce: true })` (bouncing damage numbers), `add({ kind: 'dust' | 'spark' | 'bit' | 'ember' | 'fire' | 'smoke' | 'glint', ... })`. `explosion(x, y, z, size, { debris, freeze: false, shake: 0 })` for chains of small blasts. Side-scrollers: `particles.ground = (x, y, z) => level.groundBelow(x, z)` so sparks and debris bounce on platforms. Screen flash: `game.flash('#ffffff', .12, strength 0..1)`.\n- Lighting (step 9; every view, side-scrollers included, no WebGPU needed): `game.lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity, { color: '#ffa050' })` every draw. Dark areas get a smooth cool tint, lights add their own color, and emissive items (flames, sparks, bullets, `emissive: true`) stay bright. `lights.darkness` (0..1) and `lights.tint` tune it; after setting `lights.dark = [r, g, b]`, `levels` or `glow`, call `lights.refresh()`. Props with flames can light the scene: `r.prop('torch', x, y, z, { light: true })`. Lamps, street lamps, lanterns and crystals glow on their own (`haloR`, `haloColor`, `halo: false`); `cone: true` adds a light cone under a street lamp.\n- Hits: `game.hitFx(x, y, z, { power: 1, angle, damage: 12, textScale: 2, textZ: 22 })` does hit-stop, shake, an impact star, sparks, a bouncing damage number (`bounce: false` floats it) and a sound in one call; `particles.impact(x, y, z, size, color)` for the star alone.\n- GPU lighting (optional, ground views with a TileMap): `const gpu = game.enableGPU({ map })`; mark glowing things with `px.glow(g, 1)`; add `lights.caster(x, y, r, top)` and `lights.heat(x, y, z, w)`. Where it can't draw (no WebGPU, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with `game.lights.ambient` (`gpu.fallback = false`: unlit).\n\n## Genre playbook (NES, SNES, Genesis, N64, PS1)\n| Genre (examples) | View | Build it with | Copy slice |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Platformer (Mario, Mega Man, Sonic, Castlevania) | `side` (+ `brawler` for 2.5D) | PlatformMap, Platformer, Humanoid, sprites, Bullets `'side'` | PLATFORMER |\n| Metroidvania (Metroid, Symphony of the Night) | `side` | an `E.World` of PlatformMaps (the rooms module: doors at open edges, a slide, the explored map, gates opened by abilities, saves), a Platformer given `airJumps` and `dash` as they are found, its further moves, an arm cannon aimed eight ways (`point`, `aim`, `rig.tip()`, Bullets) | METROIDVANIA |\n| Action adventure (Zelda, Secret of Mana) | `threequarter` or `topdown` | TileMap rows, `cam.room`, Body, Humanoid + `E.Attack` sword, Dialog, `E.ui.hearts` | ADVENTURE |\n| Point-and-click adventure (Monkey Island, Day of the Tentacle, Broken Sword) | none: rooms painted in screen pixels (`r.overlay`) | `E.Click` (the click module): rooms with walk areas and holes (`E.Walkbox`), depth scale, hotspots, the nine verbs, Sierra's icons or a verb coin, an inventory, puzzles as rules; talk through `E.Story` | POINT-AND-CLICK |\n| Visual novel (Phoenix Wright, Snatcher, Clannad) | none | `E.Story` nodes and choices over a painted backdrop, `rig.drawPortrait` at size, `story.log` (the backlog), `story.auto`, `story.skip`; `game.saves` | POINT-AND-CLICK |\n| Action RPG / dungeon (Diablo, Landstalker) | `iso` | TileMap, FlowField, Body (jump onto blocks), GPU lighting | ADVENTURE |\n| Beat-'em-up (Final Fight, Streets of Rage, TMNT) | `brawler` | TileMap street strip, Body, Humanoid (`weapon: null`) + `E.Combo` punches and `kick: true` kicks, `E.knockback`, waves that lock the screen; a second player drops in (`game.allowJoin`, `cam.frame` on both) | BRAWLER |\n| Fighting (Street Fighter II, Darkstalkers) | `side` | two `E.Fighter`s in an `E.Match` (the fight module): moves with frame data, guards, throws, specials by motion (`'236'`) or charge, a meter, projectiles; `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`, training | FIGHTING |\n| Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius, R-Type) | `overhead` (vertical, and horizontal: world x scrolls) | sprites, Bullets, `E.pattern`, a timeline of waves; horizontal: rock edges as functions of x, a power bar, options on the ship's past path, `E.Path` loops; `E.Arcade` for the continue and the table | SHOOTER (both stages) |\n| Pinball (Pinball Fantasies, Space Cadet, Devil's Crush) | a steep table view of its own (`new E.View('pinball', 'Table', 0, 62, 1, 1)`) | `E.Physics`: chains for the walls, `flipper`, `bumper`, `slingshot`, `kicker`, a ramp on a layer of its own, the ball a `bullet`; `input.zones` flip on a phone; `E.Arcade` for the score and table. Breakout and Asteroids with real collisions are its recipes | PINBALL |\n| Artillery (Worms, Scorched Earth) | `side` at a whole scale (`new E.View(id, label, 0, 0, 1, 1)`) | `E.Terrain` (`carve` the blasts, `walk` the soldiers, `ray` the shots), grenades as `E.Physics` bodies on `land.collider()`, `E.Turns`; a drag aims (`steer = 'drag'`, `drag()`, `stickOn`); a CPU tries its shots in simulation | ARTILLERY |\n| Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids) | `overhead` (one fixed screen, cabinet art on the sides) | `E.Arcade` (score, lives, extra lives, turns, initials, tables, attract mode); a maze of `E.GridMover`s; moths on `E.Path`s turned by `rot`; `E.wrap` and `wrap: [w, h]` | ARCADE |\n| Twin-stick / run-and-gun (Smash TV, Contra) | `topdown` / `side` | Body or Platformer, Bullets, `weapon: 'gun'`, `point: true` | SHOOTER + ADVENTURE |\n| JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy, EarthBound) | `threequarter` town, `brawler` battles | TileMap towns and a big world map, the story and rpg modules, Dialog with markup, `game.saves` slots | RPG |\n| Puzzle (Tetris, Dr. Mario, Puyo, Columns) | `side` (a field in screen pixels) | a cell array, a bag from `E.rng`, `input.repeat` (auto-shift), `grid.groups` (chains), `input.drag()` and `clicked()` for touch; versus: two fields, rows sent across (`game.players`) | PUZZLE |\n| Roguelike (Rogue, Mystery Dungeon, Shiren) | `threequarter` / `iso` | `E.gen` floors, a TileMap of its rows, `E.Grid.from(map)` paths and `fov`, `E.Fog`, `E.Turns` by speed, `game.tween` steps | ROGUELIKE |\n| Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Tactics Ogre, Fire Emblem) | `iso`, turned a quarter | TileMap of block heights, `map.pick` under the cursor, `E.Grid.from(map, { tops: true })` reach over heights, `grid.shape`, `grid.side`, `grid.threat`, `E.Turns` charge time, `E.Story` | TACTICS |\n| Real-time strategy (Warcraft II, StarCraft, Dune II) | `iso` | `E.Strategy` (the strategy module): kinds as data, selection by click, box and group, orders, gold, building, training, fog, an AI's build order and waves; `cam.free`, `{ pick: unit }` and `r.pick`, buildings as `E.SHAPES`, E.Shell's minimap; bodies drawn `every: 3` | RTS |\n| Theme park or city sim (Theme Park, RollerCoaster Tycoon, SimCity) | `iso` | paths as floor tags walked on an `E.Grid`, agents with `E.needs` picking by `E.choose`, `E.Clock` days and light, `game.timeScale` 0 to 4, `E.overlay`, `E.tooltip`; rides as shapes stepped through angles (`params`, `facing`) | SIM |\n| 3D platformer or adventure remake (Mario 64, Ocarina, Crash) | `iso` / `threequarter` / `brawler` | TileMap blocks of different heights + Body `jump()` (z is real height); in real 3D: `E.World3D` (the 3D engine's manual), `camera.lock` | ADVENTURE; 3D ACTION, 3D PLATFORMER |\n| Survival horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark) | the 3D engine's `'zones'` camera | `camera.zones` (fixed cameras by room), `camera.steer` (tank controls), `camera.lock` to aim, `world.through` doors, `world.flashlight`, fog; `E.RPG` inventory, `game.saves` | 3D HORROR |\n| First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder) | the 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera at the party's eyes | cells of 2 x 2 tiles stepped and turned a quarter at a time (tweened), `E.Grid` paths for the monsters, `E.Turns` `'atb'` (real time) or `'speed'` (turns), `E.Fog` for the automap, `world.thing('door')` gates and `world.unlock`, `rig.drawPortrait` for the party | 3D CRAWLER |\n| Rhythm (Dance Dance Revolution, PaRappa, Beatmania, Space Channel 5) | any (a highway on the overlay) | `E.Rhythm` (the rhythm module): a chart of lane strings in parts on a song's steps, judged in ms of the music clock, combo, life, grade, holds, calls a teacher plays and you answer, `rh.draw` (`arrows: true`); the songbook's songs (`E.Songbook`); lanes as `input.zones` on a phone; `audio.onBeat` moves the dancer | RHYTHM |\n| Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA Jam) | `threequarter` / `topdown` | the ball an `E.Body` (`bounce`; curl: turn its velocity across its path), passes led to the teammate, `E.arc` for a lob, a shot's meter, formations by where the ball is, `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })` | SPORTS |\n| Third-person stealth (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear, Tenchu) | `iso` / `threequarter` | TileMap roofs, Mover, `E.sees`, `E.Suspicion`, FlowField `jump` | STEALTH |\n| Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero) | Mode 7 (the mode7 module, over any view) | `E.Mode7` over a TileMap track (floor tags: grass, sand, boost), `E.Car` karts (`kart: true`: hop, drift, boost) in an `E.Race` round an `E.Path` (laps, checkpoints, drivers, items), rigs in karts as billboards, `game.splitScreen(2)` for two | KART |\n| 3D kart racing (Mario Kart 64, Crash Team Racing) | the 3D engine's `'behind'` camera | `world.track` (banked, walls, pads, `gaps` for a shortcut), `world.vehicle` karts raced by `E.Race`, split screen through `world.cameras` | 3D RACING |\n| Rail shooters (Star Fox 64, Panzer Dragoon) | the 3D engine's `'behind'` camera | `world.flyer` on rails or free in bounds, rolls (`f.rolling`) turn shots aside, `f.seek` for a charged lock, `Dialog`'s radio | 3D FLIGHT |\n| Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Road Rash) | the pseudo-3D road (the road module) | `E.Road` segments (curves, hills, tunnels), scenery and traffic placed on it, `curveAt` throwing the car outward, a clock and checkpoints | ROAD |\n| Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA) | `topdown` / `iso` | `E.Car` on a TileMap (its floor tags slow it, its walls bounce it), `E.Race` for laps and drivers, cars drawn with `px.poly` on `r.w()` corner points | KART |\n| First-person shooter (Doom, GoldenEye 007, Apex Legends) | the 3D engine's first person (or third) | a level of text rows or a terrain, `world.ray` hitscan, recoil and reloads, doors (`world.thing('door')`), raiders on `world.flow()` | 3D SHOOTER |\n| Bomberman, Frogger, Sokoban, Gauntlet, Robotron; Sonic, Prince of Persia; light guns | as the recipe says | `AI_GUIDE.md`'s Genre recipes: each on the kit closest to it | (a recipe) |\n\n**Sizes:** heroes 40-60 px tall at 320x240 (`size: 1.2-1.4`), bosses 1.8-2.4. Small things (coins, bullets, ships, pickups) are string sprites designed at 8-16 px, drawn at scale 2 if they are the player's ship. Keep hitboxes smaller than the art.\n\n**Feel numbers** (world units per second, tile = 16): walk 70-90, run 95-140, jump 250-320 with gravity 800, fall cap 330, dash 200-260, bullets 250-320 (player) and 70-120 (enemy), enemy walk 25-45. Hit feedback: `game.hitFx` (Effects).\n\n## More: animation and examples outside this card\nThe repo (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge; any file at `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/main/<path>`) holds more to take from. Its files are large: search them and take only what the game needs. Real 3D (`E.World3D`: first person, racing, flight) is there: `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`.\n- **Motion clips**: 325 curated and 2,548 motion-capture takes that play on any Humanoid: find one in `src/mocap/catalogs/`, cut a small set with `tools/anim-set.mjs`, then `Mocap.drive(rig, lib)` (`AI_GUIDE.md`, Where to find more; `docs/MOCAP.md`).\n- **Objects and materials** (full engine): `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws a still object from any view and facing, `map.solid(...)` makes it solid; `E.MATERIALS` dresses floors (`floorMat`), walls (`face`, `roof`) and platform tiles (`mat`). `docs/ASSETS.md` is the card: the format, the library, writing one and checking it with `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs`.\n- **Worked examples**: Emberdeep (`src/emberdeep/`), a whole game on this engine: 29 monster, boss and dummy bodies, 3 heroes and 30 skills, registered with `def(kind, id, spec)` (one search lists them); adapt them, they lean on the game's own helpers. `docs/CHARACTERS.md` is the recipe for a new body.\n- **Modules** (`engine/modules/<name>.js`; `modules/` in the handoff package): general systems in files of their own, each a `<script>` after the engine, its header its manual (read it there, or its section of the index). A kit carries the modules its slice names (`@modules` in its header); the full engine (`dist/my-3d2dge.js`; `my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package) carries them all.\n  - `grid`: square and hex grids: A* paths, move ranges over heights and past allies, fields of view, `E.Fog`, groups, shapes of effect, threat maps, `E.GridMover`, `E.Turns` (speed, charge time, phases, ATB). ROGUELIKE, PUZZLE, TACTICS, ARCADE, ARTILLERY, RTS, SIM, 3D CRAWLER.\n  - `gen`: generated maps from a seed: rooms and corridors, BSP, caves, mazes, Poisson scatter, as ASCII rows a TileMap reads. ROGUELIKE, ARCADE.\n  - `fight` (the full engine only): one-on-one fighting: `E.Fighter` (frame data, hurt boxes from the rig's joints, guards, throws, cancels, juggles, specials by motion or charge, a meter, projectiles), `E.Match` (rounds, KO, the HUD, training), `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`. FIGHTING.\n  - `rooms`: a world of rooms on a grid of screens (`E.World`): walking between them at open edges, the explored map (`drawMap`, or `shellMap()` for `E.Shell`), gates that abilities or keys open, saves in `E.store`. METROIDVANIA.\n  - `mode7`: the SNES's Mode 7 (`E.Mode7`): a camera over a flat floor (a TileMap's, a function's or a canvas), a turning sky, billboards far to near (rigs, sprites, shapes, your drawing), fog, `project` and `toFloor`, split screens. KART.\n  - `road`: the pseudo-3D road (`E.Road`): segments with curves and hills, lanes, rumble strips, tunnels, scenery and cars scaled by distance and hidden past a crest, the sky's parallax on curves. ROAD.\n  - `drive`: cars on the ground plane (`E.Car`: grip and slide, a kart's hop and drift boost, floor tags, walls and cars it bounces off) and races (`E.Race`: checkpoints along an `E.Path`, laps, places, drivers that pass and rubber-band, items, the HUD; the 3D engine's `world.vehicle` too). KART.\n  - `story`: dialogue as data (`E.Story`): lines with speakers and portraits, choices with conditions, flags and values, gotos, the game's events, a quest log, balloons over heads, a backlog, auto and skip; its state for a save. RPG, POINT-AND-CLICK, TACTICS.\n  - `rpg`: a role-playing game's rules (`E.RPG`): items, bags (slots or a grid of sizes), members with stats, levels and gear, status effects, a party (items used, rewards shared), shops, damage, random encounters by zone, list and card windows. RPG, 3D HORROR.\n  - `strategy` (the full engine only; needs `grid`): `E.Strategy` for real-time strategy (selection by click, box and group; queued orders; formations; production, resources, placement; fog, the minimap; an AI's build order and waves) and for sims `E.Clock` (days, the light), `E.needs` and `E.choose`, `E.overlay`, `E.tooltip`. RTS, SIM.\n  - `click` (the full engine only): point-and-click adventures (`E.Click`): painted rooms, walk areas with holes (`E.Walkbox`), depth scale, hotspots with verbs, SCUMM's nine verbs, Sierra's icons, a verb coin or two buttons, an inventory, rules as data, exits, `story`'s talk; taps on a phone. POINT-AND-CLICK.\n  - `physics`: 2D rigid bodies (`E.Physics`): circles, boxes, polygons, segments, chains and arcs; bounce, friction, sensors, layers, sleep; fast balls swept, never passing through; the same world at any refresh rate; flippers, bumpers, slingshots, kickers. PINBALL, ARTILLERY.\n  - `terrain`: destructible ground in the side plane (`E.Terrain`): carved and added by circles and polygons, the ground below a point and its normal, rays for shots, walkers on slopes, a collider for `physics`. ARTILLERY.\n  - `songs`: the songbook (`E.Songbook`): songs by mood (field, town, battle, race, horror, chase, dance ...) and jingles (stage clear, game over, level up); `get(id)`, `find(mood)`. RHYTHM.\n  - `rhythm` (the full engine only): `E.Rhythm`: charts on a song's steps, judgments in ms of the music clock, combo, score, a life gauge, grades, holds, call and response, a note highway in any direction. RHYTHM.\n  - `arcade`: the coin-op's frame: scores that float up, extra lives, two players taking turns, credits and a continue, high-score tables with initials on a letter wheel, stage cards, the attract mode (a demo, the table, the title). ARCADE, SHOOTER, PINBALL.\n- **Everything running**: https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/labs\n\n## Before you hand the game back\n- One IIFE starting with `const E = My3D2dge` (between the markers in a single file; `game/game.js` in the handoff package). Only your game: no leftover starter menu or unused slices. No network, no libraries.\n- Title screen and game-over screen as scenes; `pausable: true` on gameplay scenes. No pause, settings, rebinding or full-screen code of your own (they're standard); prompts from `game.input.label(action)`.\n- The camera follows the player (`game.focus` every update) and shows the level; the HUD uses screen pixels, or `E.Shell`'s HTML for a modern game (the STEALTH kit; the handoff package's template).\n- Sound effects on jump, hit, pickup and death; music per scene.\n- The look target list above: backdrop, textured tiles, props, sized and dressed HD characters, effects, title font, lights. Compare your screenshots with Symphony of the Night or Metal Slug, not with NES games.\n- If you can run commands: `node tools/check.mjs game/index.html` (your page; add `#scene` to jump to a scene). It presses start, plays, cycles the views, prints how much of the screen is filled and how many colors it uses, adds look notes (flat areas, checkerboard dithering, low contrast, a thin palette), and fails on any error. Act on every look note. `node tools/filmstrip.mjs game/index.html#scene --steps \"wait:1500 press:Enter wait:800 rec:16:2 press:KeyJ\" --crop x,y,w,h` records an animation frame by frame into one contact sheet: check every attack with it (a swing must wind up, strike, and settle back without jerks or backward sweeps).\n- Animation variety: every character has its own moves from `E.MOVES` (not one shared swing), enemies telegraph with a visible wind-up, and reactions play out (hurt, knockback, `down`, victory `cheer`).\n# my-3D2dge API index (v0.89.1)\n\nEvery public name in `engine/my-3d2dge.js` and its modules (`engine/modules/`: a section each, after the core's), one line each: its signature (and default, for a field) and what its doc comment says (a long one's options on lines of their own: `new E.Game({ expand })`). Written by `node tools/api-index.mjs` from the engine itself; `doctrine-check` fails when a public name has no description here. Search it for a name; `API.md` and the agent edition's header are the overviews. † marks a name the agent edition (`my-3d2dge-agent.js`) doesn't have.\n\n## Units and the height rules\n\n| Quantity | Unit |\n| --- | --- |\n| Distances, positions, sizes in the world | world units: one floor tile is 16; x east, y south, z up |\n| Speeds, gravity | world units per second, per second squared |\n| Time, `dt` | seconds (`dt` is one fixed step: 1/120 s of real time times `game.timeScale`) |\n| Angles, `facing`, `dir` | radians: 0 east, π/2 south (a direction may also be a ground vector `[dx, dy]`) |\n| Screen positions | pixels of the game's own low-resolution buffer (`game.screen.W` × `H`) |\n| Colors | `'#rrggbb'` strings |\n\n- A body's `z` is its feet. A cell's top is `map.height(cx, cy)`: 0 on the floor; a wall is solid from 0 up to its `h`.\n- A body stands on the highest top within `z + 2` under its footprint (`E.HEIGHTS.step`, `Body.step`).\n- Eyes are at `z + 30` and a target is seen at `z + 22` (`E.HEIGHTS.eye`, `chest`; a body's own `eye` and `chest` override them).\n- Two things are on the same level when `|dz| <= 8` (`E.HEIGHTS.level`), unless a call says otherwise.\n- Collision, standing, sight (`map.sight`, `map.ray`), reach (`E.targets`, `E.inArc` with `dz`) and navigation (`E.FlowField`) all follow these rules. `E.dist` is 3D; `E.overlap` is flat.\n\n## Examples\n\nEach runs in `tools/contract-test.mjs`.\n\n```js headless\n// a game with no display, in Node or a test: fixed steps, scripted input, its state\nconst E = My3D2dge, game = new E.Game({ headless: true, res: 'snes' });\nconst hero = new E.Body({ x: 40, y: 40, r: 5 });\ngame.start({ update(dt) { hero.vx = game.input.move()[0] * 80; if (game.input.pressed('jump')) hero.jump(210); hero.update(dt); }, draw() {} });\ngame.input.hold('right'); game.input.tap('jump');\ngame.advance(0.25);   // 30 fixed steps of 1/120 s, at once\nif (!(hero.x > 55 && hero.z > 20)) throw new Error('the hero should run right and be in the air');\ngame.dispose();\n```\n\n```js heights\n// a guard on a roof, a hero in the street: sight, reach and a climb read heights the same way\nconst E = My3D2dge, map = new E.TileMap({ rows: ['#######', '#.....#', '#..RR.#', '#######'], legend: { '#': 1, R: 2 }, types: { 1: { h: 80 }, 2: { h: 40 } } });\nconst guard = { x: 56, y: 40, z: 40, facing: Math.PI }, hero = { x: 36, y: 40, z: 0, r: 5 };\nif (!map.sight(guard, hero)) throw new Error('eyes on a roof see down into the street');\nif (E.targets(guard, [hero], { range: 60 }).length) throw new Error('not on the same level: no melee target');\nconst up = map.ledge(hero, 0);   // east, toward the roof\nif (!up || up.z !== 40) throw new Error('a ledge to climb onto the roof');\n```\n\n## Contents\n\nThe line each section starts on, and the name its classes' instances go by: `E.Attack: slash` lists its members as `slash.start`.\n\n- 108 The engine object (E = My3D2dge)\n- 119 Math\n- 195 Pixel primitives\n- 214 Sprite frames, turns and swaps\n- 220 Views (projections); E.View: view\n- 247 Screen; game.screen\n- 269 Input; E.Input: input\n- 357 Pixel fonts\n- 368 Lighting; game.lights\n- 387 Particles; E.Particles: game.particles\n- 407 Renderer; r\n- 457 Game; E.Game: game\n- 572 Standard features; settings, fullscreen\n- 589 10c. Boot\n- 594 10d. Tweens and scripts; E.Script: script, E.Tween: tween\n- 611 10e. Paths; E.Path: path\n- 621 10g. Weather\n- 625 10k. Save slots; E.Saves: saves\n- 648 Humanoid rig; E.Attack: slash, E.Combo: combo, E.Humanoid: rig\n- 727 Blob rig; E.Blob: blob\n- 747 Tile map; E.TileMap: map\n- 811 Materials\n- 832 Flow field; E.FlowField: flow\n- 850 Texture helpers for floortex functions\n- 861 Platform map; E.PlatformMap: level\n- 895 Platformer; E.Platformer: hero\n- 936 Body; E.Body: body\n- 956 Actions; E.Action: action\n- 978 17c. Mover; E.Mover: mover\n- 1017 17d. Perception; E.Suspicion: suspicion\n- 1034 Bullets; E.Bullets: shots\n- 1054 Spatial hash; E.SpatialHash: grid\n- 1064 Audio; game.audio\n- 1098 Backdrops; E.Backdrop: backdrop\n- 1106 21c. Props\n- 1111 21e. Shapes\n- 1123 Ui; E.Dialog: talk, E.Menu: menu\n- 1184 21d. Shell; E.Shell: shell\n- 1213 Gpu lighting (webgpu); game.gpu\n- 1234 Sessions\n- 1243 Module arcade: the coin-op's frame: scores, high-score tables and initials, lives, credits, turns, cards, the attract mode (engine/modules/arcade.js); E.Arcade: arcade\n- 1278 Module click: point-and-click adventures: walk areas with holes, depth, hotspots, verbs, an inventory (engine/modules/click.js); E.Click: click, E.Walkbox: walkbox\n- 1323 Module drive: cars on the ground plane, a race round a track, the drivers who race it (engine/modules/drive.js); E.Car: car, E.Race: race\n- 1361 Module fight: one-on-one fighting: fighters with frame data, guards, throws, specials, a meter; rounds; a CPU; training (engine/modules/fight.js); E.FightAI: fightai, E.FightSelect: fightselect, E.FightStage: fightstage, E.Fighter: fighter, E.Match: match\n- 1442 Module gen: generated maps: rooms and corridors, BSP, caves, mazes and scatter, from a seed (engine/modules/gen.js)\n- 1453 Module grid: square and hex grids: paths, ranges, lines, fields of view, fog, groups, movers and turns (engine/modules/grid.js); E.Fog: fog, E.Grid: grid, E.GridMover: gridmover, E.Turns: turns\n- 1512 Module mode7: a perspective floor drawn a row at a time, a sky that turns with it, everything on it scaled by its distance (engine/modules/mode7.js); E.Mode7: mode7\n- 1535 Module physics: 2D rigid bodies: circles, boxes, polygons, segments; fast balls that never pass through (engine/modules/physics.js); E.Physics: physics\n- 1564 Module rhythm: charts on a song's steps, judged on the music clock: combos, grades, holds, a highway (engine/modules/rhythm.js); E.Rhythm: rhythm\n- 1596 Module road: the pseudo-3D road: segments with curves and hills, lanes, rumble strips, roadside scenery, tunnels (engine/modules/road.js); E.Road: road\n- 1613 Module rooms: a world of rooms joined at their edges, the map of what was explored, gates and saves (engine/modules/rooms.js); E.World: world\n- 1642 Module rpg: items, inventories, equipment, shops, a party's stats and levels, status, damage, encounters (engine/modules/rpg.js); E.RPG: rpg\n- 1661 Module songs: the songbook: the kits' best songs and more, and jingles, tagged by mood (engine/modules/songs.js)\n- 1669 Module story: dialogue as data, flags and values, a quest log and emote balloons (engine/modules/story.js); E.Story: story\n- 1698 Module strategy: real-time strategy and simulation: selection, orders, armies, production, resources, placement, fog, AI; a day clock, needs, overlays (engine/modules/strategy.js); E.Clock: clock, E.Strategy: strategy\n- 1744 Module terrain: destructible ground in the side plane: carved and added, walked on, drawn in chunks (engine/modules/terrain.js); E.Terrain: terrain\n\n## The engine object (E = My3D2dge)\n\n- `E.DEG: 0.017453292519943295`: π/180, one degree in radians (angle * DEG)\n- `E.TAU: 6.283185307179586`: 2π, a full turn in radians\n- `E.build: \"dev-build\"`: the commit this copy was built from, a placeholder in the repo that tools/stamp.mjs replaces when the site deploys (e.g. 'a1b2c3d 2026-10-07')\n- `E.current: null`: the latest game made (My3D2dge.current, for tools and tests)\n- `E.name: \"my-3D2dge\"`: the engine's name, 'my-3D2dge'\n- `E.version: \"0.89.1\"`: the release (package.json, written everywhere by node tools/version.mjs)\n- `E.versionLabel()`: 'v0.7.0 · a1b2c3d 2026-10-07': the label to show on screens and in reports\n- `E.warn(key, msg)`: one console warning per distinct problem, prefixed so tools (and people) can spot engine advice: warn(key, message)\n\n## 1. Math\n\n- `E.HEIGHTS: eye, chest, level, step`: the numbers of THE HEIGHT RULES, which collision, standing, sight, reach, navigation and drawing all read (API.md, \"Heights\", and the API index's head give the rules), in world units above a body's feet. The defaults fit a size-1.3 Humanoid; a body's own eye and chest override these (crouching lowers them)\n- `E.V3`: small 3D vector helpers on [x, y, z] arrays\n- `E.V3.add(a, b)`: a + b\n- `E.V3.dot(a, b)`: dot product\n- `E.V3.len(a)`: length\n- `E.V3.lerp(a, b, t)`: the point t of the way from a to b\n- `E.V3.mul(a, s)`: a scaled by s\n- `E.V3.norm(a)`: unit vector the same way\n- `E.V3.rz(v, a)`: v turned by angle a (radians) about the z axis\n- `E.V3.sub(a, b)`: a - b\n- `E.angDiff(a, b)`: the shortest signed turn from angle a to angle b, -π..π (radians)\n- `E.angleTo(a, b)`: ground angle from a to b (radians, 0 = east, PI/2 = south)\n- `E.approach(a, b, s)`: a moved toward b by at most s, never past it: approach(speed, top, accel * dt)\n- `E.approachAng(a, b, s)`: angle a turned toward b by at most s radians: facing = approachAng(facing, target, turnSpeed * dt)\n- `E.arc(a, b, g, o = {})` †: the throw from a to b ({ x, y, z }) under gravity g (units a second squared, pulling z down): a lob to a teammate's feet, a grenade, a leap to a ledge. o: { apex: how far it rises over the higher end (24), or time: the seconds it flies, or speed: a launch speed (the lower arc that reaches; null when it can't) } -> { vx, vy, vz, t }\n- `E.bayer(x, y)` †: the 4 x 4 ordered-dither threshold 0..1 at pixel (x, y): pixel on when value > bayer(x, y)\n- `E.chance(p)`: true with probability p (0..1)\n- `E.clamp(v, a, b)`: v kept between a and b\n- `E.ctx2d(c)`: a canvas's 2D context with smoothing off (crisp pixels)\n- `E.dist(a, b)`: distance between two objects with x, y (and optional z)\n- `E.ease`: easing curves for u 0..1: linear, outQuad, inQuad, inOut (inOutQuad), outCubic, outBack (it overshoots a little more than Penner's: 1.9), and the rest of Penner's set (sine, quad, cubic, back, elastic and bounce, each in, out and inOut: inSine, outSine, inOutSine, ...), the names every tween library uses (section 1b)\n- `E.ease.inBack(u)` †: pulls back before it goes\n- `E.ease.inBounce(u)` †: bounces, then leaps away\n- `E.ease.inCubic(u)` †: slower start than inQuad\n- `E.ease.inElastic(u)` †: wobbles, then springs away\n- `E.ease.inOut(u)`: slow start and end\n- `E.ease.inOutBack(u)` †: pulls back, then overshoots\n- `E.ease.inOutBounce(u)` †: bounces at both ends\n- `E.ease.inOutCubic(u)` †: slow start and end, quicker between\n- `E.ease.inOutElastic(u)` †: wobbles at both ends\n- `E.ease.inOutQuad()` †: slow start and end: inOut's other name\n- `E.ease.inOutSine(u)` †: slow start and end (the gentlest)\n- `E.ease.inQuad(u)`: slow start, fast end\n- `E.ease.inSine(u)` †: slow start (the gentlest)\n- `E.ease.linear(u)` †: even speed\n- `E.ease.outBack(u)`: overshoots past 1, then settles: pop-ups, landings\n- `E.ease.outBounce(u)` †: drops and bounces to rest\n- `E.ease.outCubic(u)`: fast start, smooth stop\n- `E.ease.outElastic(u)` †: springs past and wobbles to rest\n- `E.ease.outQuad(u)`: fast start, smooth stop (gentler than outCubic)\n- `E.ease.outSine(u)` †: smooth stop (the gentlest)\n- `E.hash2(x, y)`: a fixed random number 0..1 for whole-number grid coordinates (texture specks, scatter that never flickers)\n- `E.hex(h)`: '#rrggbb' or '#rgb', rgb(), rgba(), hsl(), hsla(), a basic CSS name ('orange', 'navy') or an [r, g, b] array -> [r, g, b]\n- `E.hsl(hh, s, l)`: '#rrggbb' from hue (degrees), saturation and lightness (0..1)\n- `E.ik3(a, b, L1, L2, hint)`: two-bone IK in 3D: returns [joint, end]. hint points the way the joint should bend.\n- `E.inArc(a, facing, b, range, halfAngle, dz)`: melee cone on the ground: is b (with optional radius r) within range of a and within halfAngle of facing? dz (optional) also limits the height difference, so a hero on a roof can't hit a guard in the street (left out, height is ignored)\n- `E.lerp(a, b, t)`: a + (b - a) * t: the point t of the way from a to b (t 0..1)\n- `E.lerpAng(a, b, t)`: an angle t of the way from a to b by the shorter way round\n- `E.mix(h1, h2, t)`: the color t of the way from h1 to h2 ('#rrggbb')\n- `E.mkCanvas(w, h)`: a new canvas of w x h pixels (scratch images and sprite sheets). With no document (Node: a headless game) a stand-in whose drawing does nothing, so sprites and other images made while a game loads don't stop it\n- `E.noise2(x, y)`: smooth value noise 0..1 over the plane (one bump per whole unit): blotches, clouds, wobble\n- `E.overlap(a, b)`: circles on the ground plane: { x, y, r }\n- `E.overlapBox(a, b)`: side-view boxes: { x (center), z (feet), w, h } (the Platformer / PlatformMap convention)\n- `E.pick(list)`: a random item of list (Math.random)\n- `E.prune(list, fn)`: remove items in place: prune(bullets, b => b.dead)\n- `E.ramp(c, n = 5, strength = 1)`: a ramp of n colors from dark to light around a base color (hue shifted), for palettes and gradients\n- `E.rand(a = 1, b)`: rand() 0..1, rand(b) 0..b, rand(a, b) a..b\n- `E.randInt(a, b)`: whole number from a to b, both included\n- `E.rng(s)`: a seeded random stream: const R = E.rng(42); R() gives 0..1, the same numbers for the same seed (mulberry32)\n- `E.sameLevel(a, b, tol = HEIGHTS.level)`: within tol (default E.HEIGHTS.level, 8) of the same height: sameLevel(hero, guard)\n- `E.shade(h, a)`: shade('#336699', -0.3) darker, (+0.3) lighter. Hue-shifted like painted pixel art: darker drifts cool, lighter drifts warm\n- `E.smoothDamp(cur, target, vel, st, dt)`: critically damped follow: [next, nextVelocity] = smoothDamp(cur, target, vel, smoothTime, dt); settles in about smoothTime seconds without overshoot\n- `E.store`: saved data that survives reloads (localStorage, JSON). Never throws. Where there is no storage (Node, a sandboxed frame such as the AI Hub's) or it is blocked or full, a value is kept for the visit instead\n- `E.store.clear()`: forget every key (all of this engine's on the page's origin): tests that make several games in one process\n- `E.store.get(key, fallback = null)`: the value saved under key (any JSON), or fallback\n- `E.store.remove(key)`: forget key\n- `E.store.set(key, value)`: save value (any JSON) under key; false when it lasts for this visit only (no storage, blocked or full)\n- `E.targets(from, list, o = {})`: the things in list that `from` can reach, nearest first, each as { target, d (ground distance), dz, angle (from -> it) }: melee auto-aim, lock-on, takedowns, interaction prompts. o: { range, arc (half angle around facing, radians; default all round), facing (default from.facing), dz (most height difference, default E.HEIGHTS.level), sight: map (only those its eyes see), filter: t => bool }. A target's r adds to the range\n- `E.toHex(c)`: [r, g, b] -> '#rrggbb'\n- `E.toHsl(h)`: a color's hue (degrees), saturation and lightness (0..1): toHsl('#e05a3a') -> [h, s, l]\n- `E.tones(c, strength = 1)`: five shades of a color for painted-looking pixel art: { deep, sh, base, lt, hi }. Shadows drift toward blue-violet, highlights toward warm yellow (strength 0..2, default 1).\n- `E.wrap(v, lo, hi)` †: v wrapped into lo..hi (hi left out): wrap(-1, 0, 10) is 9, wrap(330, 0, 320) is 10. A world whose edges meet (Asteroids, a looping stage): x = E.wrap(x, 0, W)\n- `E.wrapDiff(a, b, size)` †: the shortest signed difference from a to b on a span of size that wraps round: wrapDiff(9, 1, 10) is 2 (across the seam, not -8). Aim and chase across the edge\n\n## 2. Pixel primitives\n\n- `E.px`: the pixel primitives: px.rect, px.line, px.dot, px.disc, px.poly, px.sprite, ... (each snaps to whole pixels)\n- `px.blend(g, a, mode, fn)`: draw fn() at alpha a (stepped, like PS1 hardware) with a blend mode: 'normal', 'add', 'multiply', 'screen' (E.style.trans = 'dither' dithers instead)\n- `px.col(g, c)`: set the fill color (px.* do it themselves)\n- `px.ddisc(g, cx, cy, r, c, a, wx = 0, wy = 0)`: soft disc that fades toward its edge (glows, smoke, dust): stepped alpha rings, or dithering in 'dither' style\n- `px.disc(g, cx, cy, r, c)`: a filled circle\n- `px.dot(g, x, y, c)`: one pixel\n- `px.ell(g, cx, cy, rx, ry, c)`: a filled ellipse\n- `px.glow(g, e = 1)`: GPU lighting: what follows glows (0..1). No effect in Canvas mode, so call it freely.\n- `px.line(g, x0, y0, x1, y1, c, w = 1)`: a line w pixels thick, no smoothing\n- `px.poly(g, pts, c)`: a filled polygon through [[x, y], ...]\n- `px.polyDither(g, pts, c, a, wx = 0, wy = 0)`: translucent polygon: a = 0..1 opacity (stepped alpha, or a dither pattern in 'dither' style). wx/wy = world pixel offset for the pattern\n- `px.rect(g, x, y, w, h, c)`: a filled rectangle, snapped to whole pixels\n- `px.reset(g)`: forget the color cache (after drawing on g with the canvas API directly)\n- `px.sprite(g, spr, x, y, flip = false)`: draw a sprite with its top-left corner at buffer pixel (x, y). flip mirrors it left-right; or o: { flip, rot (radians, turned about its center: E.rotSprite), steps, scale (nearest-neighbour) }\n- `E.sprite(rows, colors = {}, scale = 1)`: Pixel sprite written as strings. Each character is one pixel; colors maps a character to a hex color; '.' and ' ' are transparent. scale (whole number) enlarges each pixel. const COIN = E.sprite(['.yy.', 'yYYy', 'yYYy', '.yy.'], { y: '#f0b020', Y: '#fff0a0' }); Draw it with r.sprite(x, y, z, COIN) (depth sorted) or px.sprite(g, COIN, sx, sy).\n- `E.style: trans`: Translucency style. 'alpha' (default, PS1 / N64 / SNES color math): real transparency in 1/8 steps, plus blend modes. 'dither' (NES, Game Boy, Genesis look): ordered-dither patterns. Set E.style.trans = 'dither' for the older look.\n\n## 2b. Sprite frames, turns and swaps\n\n- `E.anim(frames, fps = 8, o = {})` †: frames to play: anim([spr1, spr2, spr3], fps = 8, { loop: true, pingpong: false }), or anim(sheet, 8, { frames: 4 }) for several frames side by side in one sprite's rows. Draw it with r.sprite(x, y, z, a, { t }) (t: its clock, seconds; the game's time by default); a.frame(t) is the sprite at time t, a.index(t) its number, a.duration one play (seconds)\n- `E.recolor(spr, map)` †: a copy of a sprite with its colors swapped (palette swaps: the second player, a hurt flash, a stronger enemy): recolor(spr, { '#3a8a4a': '#8a3a4a', h: '#e0b040' }), each key a color or one of the sprite's own characters\n- `E.rotSprite(spr, a, steps = 16)` †: the sprite turned by angle a (radians; the way the world's angles turn, so a ship drawn facing east turns to its facing): nearest-neighbour, whole pixels, about its center, in `steps` steps a turn (16; 32 for a smoother one) and cached, as games of the era pre-rotated theirs. r.sprite's and px.sprite's rot option use it\n\n## 3. Views (projections)\n\n- `E.VIEWS: iso, threequarter, topdown, brawler, side, overhead`: the ready-made views: iso (yaw 45, pitch 30), threequarter (pitch 55), topdown (80), brawler (25), side (0) and overhead (90, scale 1: world x/y = screen pixels); new E.Game({ view: 'iso' }) and game.setView('side') take their names\n- `E.VIEW_ORDER: iso, threequarter, topdown, brawler, side`: the order game.nextView() cycles through when a game doesn't set game.views ('overhead' is left out)\n- `new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale = 1, zBoost = 1) → view`: new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost): an orthographic camera (degrees; scale = screen pixels per world unit; zBoost stretches heights)\n- `view.depth(x, y, z = 0)`: true camera depth: use it to sort parts inside one object\n- `view.order(x, y, z = 0)`: painter's order for whole objects standing on the ground\n- `view.p(x, y, z = 0)`: world -> projected screen pixels (not yet camera-relative)\n- `view.screenDirToGround(dx, dy)`: a direction the player pushed on screen (x right, y down) -> normalized ground direction\n- `view.set(yaw, pitch, scale = 1, zBoost = 1)`: change the angles and scale in place (game.setView and rotateView use their own copies); returns the view\n- `view.toGround(sx, sy)`: projected screen point -> ground point (z = 0). null in side view\n- `view.ax`, `view.ay`: screen x = ax * x + ay * y (the projection's first row)\n- `view.bx`, `view.by`, `view.bz`: screen y = bx * x + by * y + bz * z (the second row; bz < 0 lifts things up the screen)\n- `view.dx`, `view.dy`, `view.dz`: the direction toward the camera, for true depth (view.depth)\n- `view.fx`, `view.fy`: the ground direction that points at the camera (the front of a wall faces it)\n- `view.id`: its name ('iso', or yours)\n- `view.inv`: the inverse of the ground projection (screen -> ground at z 0), null in side views\n- `view.isSide`: a side view (pitch 1 or less: x right, z up, y is depth)\n- `view.isTop`: looking straight down (pitch 89 or more)\n- `view.label`: its name for people ('Isometric')\n- `view.pitch`, `view.yaw`: the same angles in radians\n- `view.pitchDeg`: the tilt, 0 side view to 90 straight down\n- `view.scale`: screen pixels per world unit\n- `view.yawDeg`: the turn about the up axis, degrees (0: north is up the screen; iso is 45)\n- `view.zBoost`: how much heights are stretched on screen (1 = true)\n- `E.charView(view)`: the view a rig in `view` is drawn from (the sprite-like angle above; the view itself outside pitch 40-89)\n\n## 4. Screen\n\n- `E.RES: nes, snes, gb, gbc, gba, genesis, md, pce, n64, ps1, wide`: classic console resolutions for new Game({ res: 'snes' }) (width x height in pixels)\n- `game.screen.clear` †: true when the game's bg is null (or 'transparent'): the buffer and the canvas are cleared instead of filled, so a picture under the canvas shows through the game's (a 3D world drawn by engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js)\n- `game.screen.clientToScreen(cx, cy)`: browser client coords -> projected screen pixels (same space as view.p)\n- `game.screen.resize()`: fit the buffer to the canvas again (on window resize; after changing sizing options, setOptions does it)\n- `game.screen.setOptions(o)`: change sizing at runtime, e.g. { minH: 400, maxW: 1080, maxH: 660 } to pull the camera back\n- `game.screen.setScale(v)`: how a fixed resolution fills the screen: 'sharp' | 'fill' | 'auto' (see scale)\n- `game.screen.H = 200`, `game.screen.W = 320`: the buffer's size in game pixels\n- `game.screen.OX = 0`, `game.screen.OY = 0`: the letterbox margins on the canvas, in canvas pixels\n- `game.screen.S = 3`: the whole-number scale up to the canvas\n- `game.screen.buf`: the low-resolution buffer the game draws into (W + 2 by H + 2 pixels; null when headless, a stand-in when a headless game draws: o.draw)\n- `game.screen.canvas`: the page's canvas (null in a headless game); o: the sizing options (the game's o)\n- `game.screen.ctx`: its 2D context (r.ctx is the same)\n- `game.screen.dpr = 1`: the device pixel ratio used (at most 3)\n- `game.screen.filter = 'clean'` †: 'clean' | 'scanlines' | 'crt', drawn over the picture as it is presented, never on the buffer, and not under a test harness (navigator.webdriver). game.settings.filter sets it\n- `game.screen.fx = 0`, `game.screen.fy = 0`: its sub-pixel part (0..1, applied when presenting)\n- `game.screen.ix = 0`, `game.screen.iy = 0`: the camera's whole-pixel offset this frame\n- `game.screen.listeners = []` †: functions called after every resize\n- `game.screen.portrait`: true while the screen is taller than wide (a phone held upright)\n- `game.screen.scale = 'auto'`: how a fixed resolution fills the screen: 'sharp' (whole-number scale, every pixel the same size, black bars unless o.expand grows the picture into them), 'fill' (the largest fit, pixels a device pixel apart in size) or 'auto' (fill on high-density screens, phones and tablets, where that difference can't be seen; sharp elsewhere). game.settings.scale sets it\n\n## 5. Input\n\n- `new E.Input(screen, map, signal) → input`: signal: an AbortSignal that removes every listener this adds (game.dispose()). A screen without a canvas (headless) gets no DOM listeners: input comes from press(code), release(code), tap(action) and hold(action, on)\n- `E.Input.DEFAULT { attack, dash, skill, jump }`: top-down / isometric action (Zelda, Diablo): Space dashes, J or click attacks\n- `E.Input.PLATFORMER { jump, attack, dash, skill }`: side-scrollers (Mario, Mega Man): Space / Z / K jump, X / J attack or shoot, Shift / C dash\n- `E.Input.SHMUP { fire, bomb }`: shoot-'em-ups (1942, Gradius): Space / Z / J fire (hold for autofire), X / K bomb\n- `E.Input.THIRDPERSON { sprint, jump, crouch, interact, attack, block, dodge, use, map }` †: third-person action and stealth (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear): WASD / stick move, Shift sprint, Space jump and parkour, C crouch (a toggle), E interact, J attack, K block, L dodge, Q use, M map. Pad: A jump, B crouch, X attack, Y interact, LB block, RB dodge, LT use, RT or L3 sprint, Back map\n- `E.Input.halfKeys(preset, side)` †: a preset for half a keyboard ('left' or 'right'): its moves and menu keys, the preset's other actions on its buttons in order, and the preset's gamepad codes (Input.halfKeys(Input.SHMUP, 'right') -> fire on . and Numpad 1, bomb on /). A preset's own halves come first: preset.halves = { left: { pass: ['KeyR'] }, right: { pass: ['KeyL'] } } (an action a menu reads beside confirm would take confirm's F otherwise)\n- `input.anyPressed()`: any action pressed this step (title screens: \"press any button\")\n- `input.bindButtons(root)` †: wire every element with data-act=\"<action>\" inside root to the action\n- `input.bindings` †: the player's bindings over the preset: { action: { keys: [codes], pad: [codes] } } (game.settings.bindings keeps them)\n- `input.box()` †: the pointer's gestures, read each step in screen pixels (the HUD's space), from a mouse or one finger: box() -> { x0, y0, x1, y1, from, to, done } while a box is drawn (a mouse's left button dragged; on a touch screen a long press, then a drag), done the step it is let go; else null\n- `input.buffered(a, win = .15)`: was it pressed within the last `win` seconds and not yet consumed? (input buffering)\n- `input.clear()`: Release all devices and discard buffered input on focus loss or a modal transition.\n- `input.clicked()` †: where a click or tap on the canvas landed, in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space: r.W, r.H), during the step after it lifted (a press that drags away, like the touch stick's, is none); else null. Menus and dialogs answer to it; a HUD button of your own can: if (hit(input.clicked())) ...\n- `input.codeLabel(code)`: a code as a person reads it: 'W' (or the keyboard's own letter there), 'Space', '↑', 'Left click', and a gamepad button by its symbol on the controller in use ('A', '✕', 'B'...; glyphs picks the family)\n- `input.consume(a)`: mark a buffered press of action a as used, so buffered(a) is false until the next press\n- `input.consumeAll()`: forget every pending press (after closing a menu or dialog, so the same press does not also attack)\n- `input.doubleTap()` †: [x, y] the step a second tap or click lands within .35 s and 8 pixels of the first (a double tap), else null\n- `input.down(a)`: is the action held right now?\n- `input.drag()` †: how far the touch stick's finger moved since the last step, in screen pixels [dx, dy] (the HUD's space), or null while no finger holds it: to steer one to one (a shooter's ship follows the thumb) instead of by the stick's direction\n- `input.feed(type, x, y, o = {})` †: a pointer event in screen pixels, as the browser's would give: feed('down' | 'move' | 'up', x, y, { touch: false, button: 0, id: 0 (which finger, for zones) }) (a test's or a bot's mouse and fingers: clicks, boxes, long presses, double taps and pans follow; a finger in one of the zones holds its act), or feed('zoom', x, y, { k: 1.1 }): a wheel's notch or a pinch about that point\n- `input.history` †: the last second of input, oldest first: [{ t (input.clock), dir (numpad 1-9 for a fighter facing right: 5 neutral), pressed: the actions pressed then, directions aside }], one entry each time the direction changes or a button is pressed: an input display draws it; sequence() and motion() read it (section 10h: a kit without it has none)\n- `input.hold(a, on = true)`: hold an action down (on) or let it go (false), as its key would\n- `input.label(a, device = this.lastDevice)`: the key or button to show in a prompt for action a, on the device the player used last (or the one named: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch'), with their bindings: input.label('jump') -> 'Space', 'A' or '✕'\n- `input.longPress()` †: [x, y] the step a press held still has been held .45 s (a long press), else null\n- `input.motion(digits, button, o = {})` †: a fighter's move in numpad notation, mirrored by facing: input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing: hero.facing, window }) is a quarter circle forward and punch (down, down-forward, forward, attack: a fireball); '623' a dragon punch, '41236' a half circle. facing: 1 (right, the default) or -1. button: an action, 'attack+kick', or none (the motion alone)\n- `input.mouseScreen()`: mouse position in projected screen pixels, or null\n- `input.move()`: movement intent in screen directions: x right, y down, length <= 1\n- `input.padStyle()`: the controller family whose symbols label() shows: glyphs, or with glyphs 'auto' the one its name says\n- `input.pan()` †: [dx, dy] how far a drag moved this step: one finger on open ground (not a box), or a mouse's middle or right button; else null. A free camera pans by it (game.cam.free)\n- `input.pointer()` †: the mouse pointer in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space), or null before it has moved over the canvas\n- `input.press(code)`: press a key or button code ('KeyJ', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0', 'Act:attack') as if the person did: scripted input, tests, bots\n- `input.pressed(a)`: true only during the first update step after the press (menus, jumps, one-shot actions)\n- `input.rebind(a, device, code)` †: bind action a to one code for a device ('keys' = keyboard and mouse, 'pad' = gamepad), in place of that device's codes: input.rebind('attack', 'keys', 'KeyF'). Returns null when done, the action that already uses the code (nothing changes: free it first), or 'reserved' for the codes the menus keep (Escape, Enter, F keys, Start, the d-pad). The arrow keys and the d-pad always move as well\n- `input.rebindable(a, map = this.preset)` †: can the player rebind action a? Every action but the menu's (start, pause, confirm, cancel: they are how a player gets back to the menu), and those the game hides with new E.Game({ menu: { controls: { a: false } } })\n- `input.release(code)`: let go of a code pressed with press()\n- `input.released(a)`: true only during the first update step after the action was let go\n- `input.repeat(a, delay = .3, rate = .08)`: pressed, then repeats while held (after `delay` s, every `rate` s): menu cursors, puzzle pieces\n- `input.resetBindings()` †: forget the player's bindings: every action back to its preset's codes\n- `input.rumble(strength = .5, seconds = .15)` †: shake the gamepad: strength 0..1 for seconds (scaled by rumbleScale; nothing where the controller can't). Returns true when it rumbled\n- `input.screenPoint(cx, cy)` †: browser client coords -> screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space, with no camera), or null without a canvas\n- `input.sequence(steps, o = {})` †: true on the step a sequence of inputs ends, each one after the last within window seconds (.5) of the first: input.sequence(['down', 'down+right', 'right', 'attack']). A step names actions joined by +: its directions are held exactly as it says (up, down, left, right; 'neutral' for none) as they change to that, its buttons pressed. A cheat code: input.sequence(['up', 'up', 'down', 'down', 'left', 'right', 'left', 'right', 'kick', 'attack'], { window: 3 })\n- `input.stopRumble()` †: stop a rumble now (pause, focus lost, unplugged)\n- `input.suppress(a)` †: Suppress a held action until the next tick (a HUD widget owns the pointer this step).\n- `input.suppressCode(code)` †: A widget owns this button; other devices bound to the same action still work.\n- `input.tap(a)`: press an action for one update step, as a quick tap of its key (scripted input, tests, an AI): pressed() and down() are true in the next step, released() in the one after\n- `input.tick(dt)`: advance input by one step: read the gamepad and turn presses since the last step into this step's pressed() and released() (the engine calls it every step)\n- `input.touchArea()` †: where the touch buttons cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (the same space as r.W and r.H), or null while none show: keep a prompt or a box clear of it (a bottom E.Dialog does by itself)\n- `input.touchButtons(actions, o = {})` †: On-screen buttons for phones and tablets (shown on touch screens; the player's settings can show them always or hide them, put them on the left, and size them): input.touchButtons(['jump', 'attack'], { labels: { jump: 'A', attack: 'B' }, force }). The other half of the screen is a move stick (past halfway it presses up, down, left and right too, as a d-pad does: menus and ladders). START is the corner's menu button, top right: it opens the menu, or pauses a pausable scene into it (without the corner a START button there starts a title and pauses, as a gamepad's Start)\n- `input.touchSet(a, label = null, show = true)` †: one touch button as the moment changes: its label (TALK beside someone, NEXT in a dialog; null: its own) and whether it shows (false: nothing for it to do here). Cheap to call every step: input.touchSet('confirm', near ? 'TALK' : null, near || talking)\n- `input.unbind(a, device)` †: take away action a's codes for one device ('keys' or 'pad'); the arrow keys and d-pad stay on the moves\n- `input.use(map)`: switch key layout at runtime: input.use(E.Input.PLATFORMER) or input.use('SHMUP'). The player's own bindings (bindings, set by rebind() and the Controls screen) are laid over it. Every preset (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, THIRDPERSON, SHMUP) has the moves (up, down, left, right) and the menu's actions (start, pause, confirm, cancel). The keys held stay held (down() in the new layout, not pressed() again), and a press no step has seen counts in it\n- `input.value(a)` †: how far action a is pressed, 0 to 1: a gamepad button by its travel (the triggers, even short of a press), a stick's direction (PadAxisUp ..., or up / down / left / right on the touch stick) by its push, any other code held 1\n- `input.zoom()` †: the camera zoom the player asked for since the last step, as a factor (1.1: 10% closer, .9: farther; 1: none): the wheel, - and = and a pinch, where the scene lets them zoom. The game applies it (game.zoomBy)\n- `input.zoomPoint()` †: [x, y] where the last zoom was asked for: the mouse pointer for the wheel, between the fingers for a pinch; null when the keys zoomed (about the screen's middle)\n- `input.aimSource = 'move'` †: what aims right now: 'move' | 'mouse' | 'pad'\n- `input.clock = 0`: seconds of input time (the steps' dt added up)\n- `input.deadzone = .18`: how far a gamepad stick moves before it counts (0..0.8)\n- `input.glyphs = 'auto'`: the gamepad's button symbols for label(): 'auto' (from the controller's name) | 'xbox' | 'playstation' | 'nintendo' | 'generic' (positions)\n- `input.held`: the key and button codes down right now ('KeyD', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0', 'Act:jump')\n- `input.invertMoveX`, `input.invertMoveY = false` †: the move stick turned round (the Controller settings)\n- `input.keysTo = null` †: the other players' inputs the keyboard's codes go to as well (players sharing it, each with half its keys: game.players)\n- `input.lastDevice = 'keyboard'`: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch', the last one used (show the right button prompts)\n- `input.map`: each action's codes ({ jump: ['Space', 'Pad0', 'Act:jump'] }); every action also answers to 'Act:<name>' (DOM buttons with data-act, tap, hold)\n- `input.mouse`: { cx, cy } its page position, active once it has moved over the canvas, t the input clock when it last moved\n- `input.names = null` †: the actions' names on the Controls screen ({ attack: 'Sword' }; false hides one from rebinding: the game's menu.controls); onBindings(b): called with the player's new bindings (the game saves and applies them)\n- `input.pad = 'any'` †: the gamepad this input reads: 'any' (the first connected one another player hasn't: player 1's), an index (a joined player's own), or null (none)\n- `input.padAim = null` †: its right stick, or null when centered\n- `input.padAxes = []` †: the gamepad's raw axes\n- `input.padId = ''`, `input.padIndex = null`: the gamepad in use (null and '' without one)\n- `input.padLost = false`: true for the step after the gamepad in use was unplugged (a pausable scene pauses)\n- `input.padMove = [0, 0]`: the gamepad's left stick after the dead zone\n- `input.padPrev = []`: which gamepad buttons were down at the last step\n- `input.padValues = []` †: each gamepad button's travel, 0 to 1 (the triggers; input.value reads them)\n- `input.preset` †: the layout last given to use() (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, SHMUP or your own)\n- `input.rumbleScale = .6` †: vibration strength 0..1 (0 = off; game.settings.rumble sets it)\n- `input.screen`: the game's screen (mouse positions are read through it)\n- `input.steer = 'stick'` †: 'stick' (a stick drawn under the thumb) or 'drag' (a finger anywhere on the screen but the buttons steers by drag(), with no stick drawn: a shooter's ship that follows the thumb)\n- `input.stick = [0, 0]` †: the touch stick's direction [x, y], length <= 1\n- `input.stickDeadzone = .12` †: the part of that radius a thumb moves before the stick counts (0..0.5)\n- `input.stickId` †: the pointer (finger) holding the touch stick\n- `input.stickOn = false` †: a finger is on it\n- `input.stickRadius = 44` †: how far (CSS pixels) a finger drags for full tilt\n- `input.stickSide = 'left'` †: 'left' or 'right', the half of the screen the stick lives on\n- `input.touchEnabled = true` †: false turns the touch stick off\n- `input.zones = null` †: areas of the screen a finger holds an action down in, [{ x0, y0, x1, y1, act }] (fractions of the canvas, 0..1; the first that holds the finger wins), or null: a pinball's two halves, a rhythm game's lanes. A finger landing in one presses act until it lifts (several fingers, several zones), and starts no stick\n\n## 6. Pixel fonts\n\n- `E.font`: Pixel fonts. 'normal' (default) is a 5x7 proportional font with lower case; 'tiny' is the 3x5 upper-case font. text(g, str, x, y, color, opts) where opts is an outline color, false, or { font: 'normal' | 'tiny', outline: '#0b0814' | false, shadow: '#000' (1 px drop shadow), gradient: ['#fff', '#8ad'] (top to bottom), scale: 1, align: 'left' | 'center' | 'right', wrap: maxWidthPx, lineGap } '\\n' starts a new line. Returns { w, h } of the drawn block. title(...) draws big logo text.\n- `E.font.default` †: the font used when none is named: 'normal' (5 x 7) or 'tiny' (3 x 5)\n- `E.font.height(lines = 1, o, lineGap)`: the height in pixels of that many lines (with o's scale and font, and lineGap between them)\n- `E.font.lineHeight(o)`: pixels from one line to the next\n- `E.font.text(g, s, x, y, color = '#ffffff', o = '#0b0814')`: draw text at (x, y) (its top-left; align: 'center' or 'right' moves it) in color. o: an outline color, or { outline, shadow, gradient, scale, align, wrap, font }; returns { w, h }\n- `E.font.title(g, s, x, y, o = {})`: Big logo text for title screens: scaled glyphs with a vertical color gradient, an outline and an extruded 3D edge. title(g, 'CASTLE OF NIGHT', x, y, { scale: 3, colors: ['#fff6c8', '#ffd36a', '#e07a2a'], outline: '#1a0c10', depth: 3, depthColor: '#6a2a1a', align: 'center' })\n- `E.font.width(s, o)`: width in pixels of the widest line. opts: a scale number or { font, scale }\n- `E.font.wrap(s, maxW, o)`: split text into lines no wider than maxW pixels\n\n## 7. Lighting\n\n- `game.lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity = 1, o)` †: radius in world units; intensity ~0.5..1.5. opts: { color: '#ffaa55' (both lightings), shadow: true (GPU lighting: walls and casters block it) }\n- `game.lights.caster(x, y, r, top)` †: something that blocks light (GPU shadows): a cylinder of radius r from the floor up to height top\n- `game.lights.heat(x, y, z, width, strength = 1)` †: heat shimmer above a point (GPU only). width in world units, strength ~0.5..1.5\n- `game.lights.on` †: true when the canvas lighting draws this frame: enabled, or standing in for GPU lighting the game turned on that can't draw (no WebGPU, still loading, a side view); gpu.fallback = false keeps such a frame unlit\n- `game.lights.refresh()` †: rebuild the darkness bands after changing dark, levels or glow (lights.dark = [r, g, b]; lights.refresh())\n- `game.lights.render(r)` †: darken the frame by the lights (the renderer calls it after the queue, when enabled)\n- `game.lights.ambient = .15` †: how lit unlit places are, 0 (black) .. 1\n- `game.lights.dark = [10, 6, 24]` †: the color of darkness, [r, g, b]\n- `game.lights.darkness = .9` †: how dark the darkest places get, 0..1 (the smooth style, E.style.trans 'alpha')\n- `game.lights.enabled = false` †: false (the default) turns the canvas lighting off\n- `game.lights.glow = [255, 200, 140, 0]` †: the color, [r, g, b, a], where light is brightest\n- `game.lights.glowAt = 1.7` †: the light level (1 = fully lit) above which it glows\n- `game.lights.levels = [.9, .7, .48, .26, .08, 0]` †: the darkness of each lighting band, darkest first (dithered between them)\n- `game.lights.list = []` †: this frame's lights from add()\n- `game.lights.screen = false` †: true measures lights on the screen, not the ground (side views and PlatformMaps do it by themselves)\n- `game.lights.tint = .32` †: how strongly lights add their own color, 0..1\n\n## 8. Particles\n\n- `new E.Particles(game) → game.particles` †: game.particles; game: its game\n- `game.particles.add(p)`: add one particle { x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, life, max (seconds), g (gravity, > 0 pulls down), drag, bounce (0..1), kind: 'dust' | 'spark' | 'bit' | 'ember' | 'ring' | 'text', color, size }; returns it, or null when full\n- `game.particles.bits(x, y, z, n, colors)`: n pieces of debris that fly, fall and bounce (colors: a list to pick from)\n- `game.particles.draw(r)`: queue them for drawing (the renderer calls it)\n- `game.particles.dust(x, y, z, n, o = {})`: n puffs of dust from (x, y, z) that drift and fade. o: { speed: 30, color, size }\n- `game.particles.explosion(x, y, z, size = 1, o = {})`: a full explosion in one call: fireball, smoke, sparks, debris, shockwave ring, screen shake and flash, sound. explosion(x, y, z, size = 1, { debris: ['#6a5a4a'], sound: true, flash: true })\n- `game.particles.fire(x, y, z, n = 6, o = {})`: fireball puffs for explosions and fire breath\n- `game.particles.glints(x, y, z, n = 5, color = '#fff6c0', spread = 10)`: twinkling star glints for magic, treasure and pickups\n- `game.particles.impact(x, y, z, size = 6, color = '#ffe070')`: a hit star at the point of impact: impact(x, y, z, size = 6, color)\n- `game.particles.ring(x, y, r0, r1, color, dur = .35, z = 0)`: a ring on the ground growing from radius r0 to r1 over dur seconds (shockwaves, landings)\n- `game.particles.smoke(x, y, z, n = 4, o = {})`: rising smoke puffs: smoke(x, y, z, n, { size, color, dark, light })\n- `game.particles.sparks(x, y, z, n, dir = null, o = {})`: n bright sparks thrown from (x, y, z), every way or around angle dir (radians). o: { color, speed }\n- `game.particles.text(x, y, z, text, color = '#fff2c4', o = {})`: floating number or word: text(x, y, z, '12', color, { scale: 2, bounce: true }) (bounce: lands and holds, Final Fantasy style)\n- `game.particles.update(dt)`: move and age them by dt (the engine calls it every step)\n- `game.particles.ground = null` †: null (the floor is z 0), or (x, y, z) -> the floor's height under a particle (a side-scroller's platforms, a 3D level: engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js sets it), asked again when a particle enters a new 8-unit cell\n- `game.particles.list = []`: the live particles\n- `game.particles.max = 900`: at most this many at once (add() skips the rest)\n\n## 9. Renderer\n\n- `r.H`: the screen's height in game pixels\n- `r.W`: the screen's width in game pixels\n- `r.actor(x, y, z, drawFn, o = {})`: Queue a character. drawFn(g, ox, oy) must draw with (ox, oy) as the projected position of world point (x, y, z). The engine adds outline, rim light, hit flash, afterimages and x-ray silhouettes. opts: {outline=true, rim=true, flash=false, alpha=1, xray=false, ghost:{color, life, alpha, owner}, hides, bias, every, key, emissive (it stays bright under lighting, as r.queue's)}\n- `r.actor({ xray })`: true shows the actor's hidden part (a translucent tint with a 1-px outline, r.xrayColor and r.xrayOutline; a checker in E.style.trans 'dither') wherever a wall, a prop or shape at least chest high, or anything queued with hides is drawn in front of it (not one rising less than E.HEIGHTS.level, 8, above its feet: the roof it stands on, a cut-down wall); xray: { color, outline (false: none), actors } picks the colors, and with actors: true other bodies drawn in front hide it too (a hero lost in a crowd keeps his shape)\n- `r.actor({ hides })`: true: this actor counts as an x-ray occluder even when no x-ray actor asked for actors\n- `r.actor({ ghost })`: ghost.owner (any {x, y, z}, usually the actor itself) keeps its afterimages sorted behind the live body\n- `r.actor({ every, key })`: every: n, key (any object, the character's own): its finished image is drawn again for n - 1 frames before drawFn runs again, at the place it is now (a background character animating at a lower rate, as classic games' townsfolk do: a phone's frame keeps the time for the ones that matter); not while it flashes, with a ghost, x-ray or GPU lighting\n- `r.actor({ wrap })`: [w, h], as r.sprite's: a world that wraps round shows the actor across each edge it crosses\n- `r.actorNow(x, y, z, drawFn, o = {})`: draw an actor at once, over what is drawn so far, not sorted by depth (a pass of the game's own: a body falling into a void painted over the level), with r.actor's options (outline, flash, alpha, ...)\n- `r.bh`: the buffer's height (H + 2)\n- `r.box(g, x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1, top, side)`: extruded box prop (crate, pedestal, table). Call inside a queue fn with its g.\n- `r.bw`: the buffer's width (W + 2: a pixel of margin each side for the sub-pixel camera)\n- `r.ctx`: the low-resolution buffer's 2D context: draw on it with E.px\n- `r.decal(fn, o)`: draw on the floor before the queue. opts.emissive (0..1) makes it glow under GPU lighting\n- `r.glowDisc(g, cx, cy, rad, color, a)`: glow halo from a cache: additive light rings (alpha style) or a dithered disc (dither style). a = 0..1 strength\n- `r.groundArc(x, y, r0, r1, a0, a1, color, alpha = 1, z = 0, g = this.tgt)`: a band of a ring on the ground from radius r0 to r1 and angle a0 to a1 (radians): melee telegraphs, sight cones\n- `r.groundDisc(x, y, rad, color, alpha = 1, z = 0)`: a dithered disc lying on the ground (radius in world units, z its height): shadows, auras, area attacks\n- `r.groundPts(x, y, rad, n = 18, z = 0)`: n screen points round a circle of radius rad on the ground at (x, y), height z: the outline of a ring or a fan\n- `r.groundRing(x, y, rad, color, alpha = 1, z = 0)`: a ring lying on the ground (radius in world units)\n- `r.marker(x, y, z = 0, o = {})`: an objective marker over a world point: a bobbing diamond (and its label) while it is on screen, an arrow at the screen's edge pointing to it while it is off (offscreen: false hides it then). o: { label, color: '#ffd040', offscreen: 'arrow' }\n- `r.overlay(fn)`: draw fn(g) on top of everything, in screen pixels (HUD, dialog, menus)\n- `r.pick(sx, sy)` †: what was drawn under a screen point (the HUD's pixels: input.clicked(), input.pointer()) in the last frame, front to back: the pick values of the actors, sprites, props and queued draws given one (r.actor(x, y, z, fn, { pick: unit })), each where its pixels went (an actor's drawn bounds; others' box, or a body's size round its point). [] for none\n- `r.ppu` †: pixels per world unit on the ground here: the view's scale times the zoom (iso 1.41, the others 1.5, overhead 1, at zoom 1). Rigs, maps and props grow with it; a sprite's pixel is a screen pixel at zoom 1 unless drawn with world: true\n- `r.prop(name, x, y, z, o = {})` †: set dressing from E.prop: r.prop('torch', x, y, z, { size, color, flip, light: true, halo: true, bias, hides, footprint }) feet on the world point; flames animate and glow. light: true also adds a warm light to game.lights. A prop at least chest high (E.HEIGHTS.chest, 22: a pillar, a statue, a lamp) drawn in front of an x-ray actor shows the actor's silhouette through it; a lower one (grass, a crate, a barrel) doesn't; hides: true or false says so.\n- `r.queue(x, y, z, fn, o)`: queue a depth-sorted draw. fn(g) draws into g. opts: {bias, occluder, hides, xray, emissive, solid (hides a glow behind it), footprint, box, top} occluder: a wall (x-ray silhouettes show through it, it sorts as a block). hides: only lets x-ray silhouettes show through it (props, sprites, bodies), nothing else changes. box: [x0, y0, x1, y1], what it covers in buffer pixels: an occluder joins the x-ray only where its box meets a silhouette (without one, a 48 x 140 box round its foot: give a long wall its box, or queue it with queueSpan); top: its top's height (one under the knees hides nothing). It sorts by its one point (x, y, z): a long thing that other things stand along sorts wrong somewhere: queueSpan\n- `r.queueSpan(a, b, z, h, fn, o = {})` †: queue a long piece of geometry (a wall, a fence, a roof's edge, drawn whole by fn(g)) a cell at a time from a to b ([x, y] world units) at height z, h tall: each cell-long piece sorts at its own middle, clipped to its part, with its box (as the engine's own walls are). o: queue's options, depth (its thickness across, 0), step (16 units)\n- `r.shadow(x, y, rad, alpha = .5, color = '#0c0818', z = 0)`: soft contact shadow under a character: a cached dithered ellipse, correct for the current view. z = height of the surface it falls on (0 = floor; the top of a block or platform otherwise)\n- `r.shape(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})` †: Queue a shape standing at (x, y, z), feet on the point, depth sorted like an actor: r.shape(id | entry, x, y, z, o). o: { facing (radians, where its front points; default PI/2, south, as written), scale, mats: { slot: material }, flip (mirror it x to -x before facing), params (a generator's: { h: 30 }), bias, hides (x-ray silhouettes show through it: by default when it stands at least E.HEIGHTS.chest, 22, high), footprint, light (add its lights to game.lights), shadow (default true), charView (default true: steep views draw it from the characters' lower angle), flash, alpha, xray, pick (r.pick finds it) }\n- `r.sightCone(obs, o = {})` †: an observer's view on the ground: a dithered fan fov wide (radians) about its facing, out to range, cut short where the map hides a body's chest (map.ray, the height rules), draped over the ground below. o: { range: 120, fov: 1.6, facing, map, eye, color: '#ffe080', alpha: .3, rays }. Call it in draw (it is a decal: under walls and bodies)\n- `r.sky(colors, o = {})`: background of dithered color bands, top to bottom: r.sky(['#0b1030', '#3b2d6b', '#e0806a']). Call first in draw.\n- `r.sprite(x, y, z, spr, o = {})`: Queue a pixel sprite (from E.sprite) standing at world point (x, y, z). Depth sorted like an actor. opts: { flip, anchor: 'bottom' (feet on the point, default; E.shapeSprite's: its own feet) | 'center' | 'top', glow: 0..1, bias, flash, outline, alpha, xray, ghost (the last five use the actor path), t (an E.anim's clock: spr may be one), rot (radians, about its center: anchor 'center' unless given), steps (16), scale, world: true (it grows with the view's scale, as rigs and maps: r.ppu pixels a unit), wrap: [w, h] (a world that wraps round: a copy across each edge it crosses; w alone wraps x) }\n- `r.starfield(o = {})`: scrolling star dots for space and night skies. opts: { count: 70, speed: 30 px/s, dir: 'down' | 'up' | 'left' | 'right', colors, layers: 3, seed }\n- `r.text(str, x, y, color = '#ffffff', o)`: HUD text in screen pixels, drawn on top and never lit: r.text('SCORE 120', 4, 4, '#fff', { align, scale, outline })\n- `r.textAt(x, y, z, str, color = '#ffffff', o = {})`: text floating at a world point (names, prompts): r.textAt(x, y, z, 'OPEN', '#ffe08a')\n- `r.tgt` †: current draw target for ground helpers (switches during GPU info and overlay passes)\n- `r.view`: the view this frame is drawn in (the game's, with zoom and turn)\n- `r.visible(x, y, z = 0, mx = 60, up = 30, down = 90)`: is a world point near the visible area? margins in pixels (characters extend upward from their feet)\n- `r.w(x, y, z = 0)`: world -> buffer pixel coords\n- `r.game`: its game; items: this frame's queue (sorted by depth when the frame ends)\n- `r.glowMask = true` †: false lets emissive glows show over everything under canvas lighting (cheaper: the default keeps walls and bodies in front of a glow dark)\n- `r.gpu = false` †: true during a frame drawn with GPU lighting (draw code can check r.gpu)\n- `r.ix`, `r.iy`: the camera's whole-pixel offset this frame (a world point is at r.w(x, y, z) on the buffer)\n- `r.outline = '#140c1c'`: the color of the 1-pixel outline round every actor\n- `r.rim = '#ffe2b8'` †: the rim light's color on actors' lit edges\n- `r.rimAlpha = .28` †: its strength 0..1\n- `r.xrayColor = '#8fe3ff'` †: the default color of x-ray silhouettes\n- `r.xrayOutline = '#e8fdff'` †: the default 1-px outline round a silhouette's hidden part (null for none)\n\n## 10. Game\n\n- `new E.Game(o = {}) → game`: new E.Game({ canvas, view, views, res, expand, input, bg, step: 1 / 120, loop: true, headless: false, menu, fullscreen: 'tap', corner: true, backGesture: true, autoPause: true, live: false, id, pace, camera }). The standard features (section 10b) every game gets: menu, fullscreen, corner, backGesture, autoPause.\n- `new E.Game({ res })`: a fixed resolution ('ps1' or [w, h]), whole pixels\n- `new E.Game({ expand })`: true makes res the least the screen shows and grows the picture to fill the window, any shape, no bars (expand: { maxW, maxH } stops it growing there)\n- `new E.Game({ bg })`: the color behind everything, or null: the canvas is clear, and the game draws its HUD and menus over a picture under it (a 3D world, engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js)\n- `new E.Game({ headless })`: no screen, renderer, sound or DOM input (tests, tools, a simulation in Node, where it is the default): scenes update and draw is skipped; drive it with advance() and input.press / release / tap / hold\n- `new E.Game({ draw })`: true (headless): draw runs each advance on a stand-in renderer, its errors in game.errors\n- `new E.Game({ loop })`: false makes a game in a page that runs only by advance()\n- `new E.Game({ menu })`: the pause, settings and controls screens ({ items, settings, controls, quit } adjusts them, false leaves them out)\n- `new E.Game({ fullscreen })`: 'tap' (a phone goes full screen at its first tap and when a game starts, unless the player turned it off), 'button' (only when asked) or false\n- `new E.Game({ corner, backGesture })`: corner: the menu and full-screen buttons (top right); backGesture: a phone's back steps back in the game first (false: it leaves the page)\n- `new E.Game({ autoPause })`: a pausable scene pauses when the page is hidden or loses focus\n- `new E.Game({ live })`: true: a world that never stops for one player (online, a shared screen): a pause opens the menus over it, the scene's update goes on (game.paused says a menu is up) and the menu holds that player's input back\n- `new E.Game({ id })`: the name the player's settings are saved under (the page's path)\n- `new E.Game({ pace })`: false draws every refresh of a fast display even when the frames can't keep up (by default it draws every other one, evenly), unless the player picks Frame rate 60\n- `new E.Game({ camera })`: the camera of every scene without its own (a scene's camera option: the player's zoom, start)\n- `game.H`: the screen's height in game pixels (W is its width)\n- `game.W`: internal buffer size in pixels (HUD layout)\n- `game.addPlayer(o = {})` †: a player in now: { pad: index } (a gamepad) or { keys: 'right' } (the keyboard's right half; player 1 takes the left). Returns its input (null when four play already)\n- `game.advance(seconds = this.o.step, o = {})`: run the game for `seconds` of real time in exact fixed steps, at once, without requestAnimationFrame: tests, tools and headless runs (advance(1) is 120 updates). o.draw: then draw one frame (not in a headless game). A headless game made with draw: true draws one frame each advance, on a stand-in renderer, so a draw error lands in game.errors (a test that catches what a player would see break). Returns the updates run\n- `game.after(seconds, fn, o = {})`: run fn once after `seconds` of game time. Cleared on scene change, unless { keep: true } (a site's clock, a watcher of the whole game: it outlives scenes, as a kept tween does). Returns { cancel() }\n- `game.allowJoin(o = {})` †: let players join, up to max (2..4): Start on a gamepad no one holds, or 2 on the keyboard (o.keys: false not); onJoin (input, number) as each does. allowJoin(false) closes it (the names split and join are String's and Array's)\n- `game.back()`: a step back, as a phone's back gesture asks for (the engine calls it for one; a back button of the game's own can): the open menu screen goes back one; else the scene's own back() takes it (it returns true when it did); else a scene in play pauses into the menu, and a paused one goes on. Returns false where there is nowhere to go (a title)\n- `game.cornerArea()` †: where the corner's buttons (menu and full screen, top right; the touch START) cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (r.W and r.H's space), or null while none show: keep a HUD clear of it\n- `game.dispose()`: end the game for good: stop the loop and remove everything the engine added to the page (its listeners on the window, the document, the canvas and the buttons, the touch controls, the GPU lighting's canvas) and close the sound. Tests and tools that make many games call it; the canvas stays\n- `game.enableGPU(o = {})` †: turn on WebGPU lighting: where it can't draw (no WebGPU, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with its own ambient (game.lights.ambient) and the same lights. opts: { map, bands, ambient, fallback, ... }\n- `game.every(seconds, fn, o = {})`: run fn every `seconds` of game time. Cleared on scene change, unless { keep: true }. Returns { cancel() }\n- `game.fail(where, e)`: report an error in game code without stopping the game: logged once per place and message, kept in game.errors and shown on the error panel (the engine reports its own callbacks' errors this way): game.fail('power ' + id, err)\n- `game.flash(color = '#ffffff', seconds = .12, strength = 1)`: flash the whole screen with a color for `seconds` (dithered fade out): hits, explosions, lightning. Shorter when reduceMotion, dimmer by the player's settings.flashes\n- `game.focus(x, y, z = 0)`: camera follows this world point. Side-scrollers: game.focus(x, 0, z)\n- `game.follow(target, o = {})`: keep the camera on an object automatically (instead of calling focus every update). opts: { z: extra height to look at (default 16), lead: look ahead in the facing / moving direction }. follow(null) stops.\n- `game.freeze(s)`: hit-stop: the game stands still for s seconds (.04-.08 for a hit); input still ticks\n- `game.go(name, data, o = {})`: switch scene: game.go('play', { level: 2 }). It fades through black (dithered) unless opts.fade is 0 (a cut) or another number of seconds; opts.fade can name a transition instead: 'black', 'white', 'wipe', 'iris', 'mosaic', 'swirl' or 'dissolve', each way taking opts.seconds (.4), round opts.at (iris and swirl: a world point { x, y, z }, which each scene's camera places, or [x, y] on the screen; its centre by default). With reduce motion each is a plain fade\n- `game.hitFx(x, y, z, o = {})`: everything a good hit needs in one call: hit-stop, shake, an impact star, sparks along the hit direction, a sound, a damage number and a rumble on a gamepad. hitFx(x, y, z, { power: 1, angle, damage, color, sound: 'hit', rumble: true })\n- `game.hook(kind, fn)` †: a function of the game's own on the engine's clock: kind 'input' fn(dt) every step, paused or not, after the input is read (a host's guard, an idle timer); 'step' fn(dt) every update step in play, before the scene's update (an attract mode's watcher); 'enter' fn(name, data) as a scene starts. Returns a function that removes it\n- `game.menu(name, o)`: open the menu (the corner's menu button and the touch START do it): a pausable scene (or a game started without scenes) pauses on its pause screen; any other scene, a title, waits under the settings. menu('settings') or menu('controls') opens that screen. Without a menu (menu: false, or the agent edition) it pauses a scene in play. o: { pause: false } opens it over a world that keeps stepping, as game.live does for every pause. Returns true when a menu opened\n- `game.mouseGround()`: the mouse's point on the ground: [x, y] in world units (an array, not { x, y }), or null with no mouse over the canvas\n- `game.nextView(dir = 1)`: cycle through game.views (or every view): bind it to a key for players who like to switch\n- `game.note(text, seconds = 1.2)`, `game.shownNote`: a short notice at the top of the screen (zoom level, saved, ...): game.note('ZOOM 1.5x', 1). game.shownNote is the one showing, { text, end } (end in game.real seconds), or null: a game that draws it its own way takes it and sets shownNote to null\n- `game.onView(f)` †: call f(view) whenever the view changes (re-bake caches, move HUD)\n- `game.pause(reason = 'player')`: pause the game, saying why ('player' | 'focus' | 'controller' | 'menu': pauseReason); returns true if it paused. The player resumes (Escape, Start, Resume); resume() does it from code\n- `game.players` †: the players: [game.input (player 1), ...the ones who joined (game.allowJoin, game.addPlayer: section 10j)]; each an input with player (1..4), name ('P1'), color (E.PLAYER_COLORS) and device. A game of one: [game.input]\n- `game.removePlayer(p)` †: a player leaves (its input, or its number, 2 to 4); player 1 has the whole keyboard back when no one shares it\n- `game.resetCamera()` †: undo setZoom and rotateView: the scene's own zoom again (camera.start, or camera.tall on a screen held upright; 1 without), no turn. The player's zoom stays (settings.zoom)\n- `game.resume()`: take the game out of pause (and close the pause screen)\n- `game.rotateView(deg)` †: turn the camera around the vertical axis in ground views: game.rotateView(45) (degrees). Side views never turn\n- `game.saves()` †: game.saves: the save slots (section 10k), made when first read\n- `game.script(fn, o)` †: run a coroutine on game time, in the update step: game.script(function* (game) { yield .5; yield E.until(() => door.open); yield game.tween(cam, { y: 0 }, 1); yield E.all([a, b]); ... }, { keep }). Each yield waits: a number (seconds), nothing (one step), a condition (a function, or E.until(fn)), a tween or script (until it is done), a generator (run as a script of its own, and waited for), E.all([...]) or E.any([...]) (each of these). It runs at once to its first yield. Cutscenes, boss patterns, wave timelines, tutorials, attract modes. Returns the Script\n- `game.setView(v)`: switch the view: an id from E.VIEWS ('iso', 'side', ...) or a View; keeps zoom and turn\n- `game.setZoom(z)` †: camera zoom on top of the view: game.setZoom(1.5) (.5 .. 3), the game's own; where the scene lets the player zoom (camera.zoom), theirs multiplies it (game.zoom is the two together). Characters, maps, props, particles and lights are drawn by code, so they re-rasterize crisply at the new size; HUD text and overlays stay the same.\n- `game.shake(a)`: shake the screen by a (pixels, decaying; at most 10 in all); none when reduceMotion, scaled by the player's settings.shake\n- `game.shaking` †: how hard the screen shakes now (0 still, up to 10): shake() adds to it and it dies away. A camera of the game's own (a 3D world's) shakes by it\n- `game.splitScreen(n, o = {})` †: split the screen into n viewports (2 to 4; 0 or 1: one screen again), o: { layout: 'auto' (side by side on a wide screen, stacked on a tall one; a grid for 3 or 4) | 'columns' | 'rows' | 'grid', follow: [targets], z: 16, gap: 1 }\n- `game.start(hooks)`: Start the loop. Either one scene: game.start({ update(dt), draw(r) }) or several: game.start({ scenes: { title: {...}, play: {...} }, scene: 'title' }) A scene is { enter(data), exit(), update(dt), draw(r), back(), pausable, view, views, input, res, expand, touch, camera } (all optional). start() with nothing resumes a stopped loop. A headless game, or one made with loop: false, runs only by advance().\n- `game.start({ view, views, input, res, expand, touch })`: a scene's, applied when it starts: input ('PLATFORMER'), res ('snes'), expand (true), touch (['jump', 'attack']); res and expand stay until another scene sets them\n- `game.start({ back })`: a scene's back() takes a phone's back gesture (game.back()) first: return true when it went back somewhere (a sub-menu closed)\n- `game.start({ camera })`: a scene's { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5, wheel: true }: the player zooms the camera within zoom (the wheel, - and =, a pinch, Settings, Camera zoom: theirs, kept, on top of the game's; false or none: they can't; a function of the game: a range per screen shape, g => [g.upright ? 1 : .85, 2]), from start, the zoom it starts at (tall: on a screen held upright, game.upright; 1 without); wheel: false leaves the mouse wheel to the game\n- `game.start({ boot })`: { title, version, steps: [[label, fn]], messages, min, checks, accent } runs the boot first (section 10c): the steps one a frame behind the boot screen (all at once when headless or loop: false); boot: false hides a splash\n- `game.stop()`: stop the loop; the game keeps its state, and start() resumes it\n- `game.tween(target, to, seconds, o)` †: ease target's numbers to new values over seconds of game time: game.tween(door, { y: 40 }, .5, { ease: 'outBack', delay, repeat (more plays; -1 forever), yoyo (every other play runs back), onDone(target), keep (outlive the scene) }). ease: a name in E.ease ('outQuad' by default) or a function of u 0..1. It starts from the values the target has once its delay is over. Returns the Tween: tween.done, tween.cancel()\n- `game.upright`: true while the game's screen (r.W x r.H) is upright, taller than 1.3 times its width: a phone held upright, with expand: true (a fixed res keeps its own shape on any phone, so it is never upright there). A scene's camera.tall zoom applies then: it zooms a tall picture in to fill it\n- `game.viewports` †: a split screen's viewports ({ i, x, y, w, h, cam, focus(x, y, z), follow(target, { z }): game.splitScreen), or [] on one screen\n- `game.zoomBy(k)` †: the player zooms the camera by a factor (1.1: 10% closer, .9: farther), as the wheel, - and = and a pinch do (a zoom button of the game's own can): within the scene's camera.zoom range, kept as their setting (settings.zoom, on top of the game's zoom), and noted at the top of the screen ('ZOOM 1.65x': the number a scene's camera.start takes). Does nothing where the scene doesn't let the player zoom. Returns the zoom\n- `game.zoomRange()` †: the zooms the player may take the camera to here, [min, max] (the scene's camera.zoom, or the game's; a function of the game gives one per screen shape or level), or null where they can't (camera.zoom false or none)\n- `game.alpha = .5`: the fraction of a fixed step (0..1) of real time not yet run: interpolate drawing with it if motion must be smooth at any refresh rate. The accumulator starts half a step in, so frame times that wobble keep a steady step count\n- `game.audio`: chip-sound synthesizer: game.audio.sfx('jump'), game.audio.music(song)\n- `game.baseView` †: the chosen view without zoom and turn\n- `game.bootError = null`: the error a boot step failed with (the game did not start), or null\n- `game.booted`: a promise of the game once its boot (start's boot option, section 10c) has run and it has started: await it in a test whose boot steps return promises\n- `game.cam`: camera. smooth = seconds of lag; bounds(view) = clamp rect; room = [w, h] world units for screen-by-screen rooms; moving = sliding between rooms; offset = [dx, dy] screen pixels from the screen's centre to where the focus sits (above a phone's touch buttons: [0, -40]); x, y: the camera's position in projected pixels (its top-left corner); vx, vy: how fast it is moving there; tx, ty, tz: the world point it is following (focus sets it); snap: true makes it jump there at the next step instead of gliding (a new level, a teleport); window: [w, h] screen pixels, a dead zone round the focus it moves in before the camera follows (Super Mario World's; [48, 0] for sideways only); snapY: true follows a followed body's height only when it lands (onGround) or nears the screen's edge (side-scrollers); frame(targets, { margin: 24, zoom: [min, max] or false }) keeps every one of them (anything with x, y, z) in view, centered between them and zoomed out (no further than min) when they spread apart, until frame(null); free: a free camera (strategy, sims, board games): true or { keys: true, edge: 10, drag: true, speed: 300, bounds: [x0, y0, x1, y1] }: the keys or a pad's stick pan it (input.move(), speed screen pixels a second), the mouse at the screen's edge (within edge pixels), a drag (input.pan(): a finger on open ground, a mouse's middle or right button) one to one; a zoom (wheel, pinch) zooms toward the pointer or the pinch; its focus keeps to bounds (world x, y); focus() still moves it; in every view and turn; null (as it starts): off\n- `game.debug = null`: a game's own handle for tools and tests: game.debug = { snapshot() { return {...} }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }. tools/check.mjs prints snapshot() and tools/replay.mjs logs it at the end\n- `game.disposed = true`: true after dispose()\n- `game.drawEvery = 1`: 1, or 2 while a fast display (a 120 Hz phone) refreshes quicker than the game can draw (a frame's cost, or refreshes missed: its GPU behind): every other refresh is drawn, evenly, instead of random ones missed (the updates still run every refresh). The player's Frame rate setting (game.settings.frameRate: 'auto', '60', 'full') can fix it at 2 or 1\n- `game.errors = []`: the errors caught in update and draw, each { where, scene, message, stack }, once per kind\n- `game.fadeTime = .22`: seconds for the dithered fade between scenes (0 = instant cuts)\n- `game.fps = 60`: frames drawn per second over the last half second of real time\n- `game.fullscreen`: full screen: game.fullscreen.toggle(), .on, .supported\n- `game.gpu = null` †: the WebGPU lighting after enableGPU(), or null\n- `game.headless`: true for a game with no display (o.headless, or no DOM: Node)\n- `game.hitstop = 0`: seconds of hit-stop left (freeze: a scene's entry ends it)\n- `game.hz = 60`: the display's refresh rate, measured\n- `game.input`: keyboard, mouse, gamepad and touch, read as actions (pressed, down, move, ...)\n- `game.lights` †: canvas lighting (lights.enabled, lights.add in draw)\n- `game.live` †: true (o.live): a pause opens the menus over a world that keeps stepping (an online or shared world can't stop for one player): paused is true and the scene's update goes on, the menu holding the player's own input back\n- `game.o`: the options the game was made with, the defaults filled in (view, res, expand, minW, minH, maxW, maxH, bg, step, input, loop, headless)\n- `game.particles`: dust, sparks, debris and text in the world\n- `game.pauseOverlay = true`: false hides the engine's PAUSED overlay (a game drawing its own pause screen)\n- `game.pauseReason = null`: why the game paused: 'player' | 'focus' (the page was hidden) | 'controller' (unplugged) | 'menu', or null\n- `game.paused = false`: true while paused (a pausable scene toggles it on 'pause'; steps still run, scenes don't update)\n- `game.r`: the renderer handed to draw(r) (null when headless)\n- `game.real = 0`: seconds the game has stepped through, pauses and hit-stop included (blinking HUD, notes)\n- `game.reduceMotion`: the player asked for less motion (prefers-reduced-motion): shake and flashes are softened\n- `game.scene = null`: the scene running\n- `game.sceneName = null`: its name ('main' for a game started with one scene)\n- `game.scenes = null`: the scenes given to start(), by name\n- `game.screen`: the low-resolution screen (its W, H, scale and buffer)\n- `game.settings`: the player's choices (game.settings.volume, game.settings.set('volume', .5), your own from menu.settings), saved and applied (section 10b)\n- `game.shell = null` †: the E.Shell over this game (an HTML interface: screens and HUD), or null\n- `game.stats`: per-frame timings (ms) and counts, for profiling overlays\n- `game.stats.updateMs`, `game.stats.renderMs`: milliseconds the last frame spent updating and drawing\n- `game.stats.frameMs`: the real time since the last frame\n- `game.stats.steps`: the updates it ran\n- `game.stats.items`: what it queued for drawing\n- `game.stats.actors`: actors drawn\n- `game.stats.culled`: actors skipped off screen\n- `game.time = 0`: game seconds since start (it stops during pause and hit-stop, and slows with timeScale)\n- `game.timeScale = 1`: game speed, 1 normal, .25 slow motion, 0 stopped (it outlives a scene change: a game's speed setting; restore a slow motion with a kept timer)\n- `game.view`: the view the game draws in now (the chosen one, with zoom and turn: read it, change it with setView)\n- `game.views`: views this game is designed for (view cycling and the checker use it). null = every view\n- `game.yaw = 0` †: its turn in degrees on top of the view (rotateView)\n- `game.zoom = 1` †: the camera's zoom now (1 = as the view is): the game's (setZoom, a scene's camera.start) times the player's (settings.zoom: the wheel, a pinch), within the scene's camera.zoom range\n- `E.PLAYER_COLORS: #4a8aff, #ff4a4a, #4ad860, #ffd040` †: the players' colors: player 1 blue, 2 red, 3 green, 4 yellow (game.players)\n- `E.showError(title, e)`: show an error in the red box at the top of the page (the engine does it for errors in update and draw); a click hides it. Before play has started it shows on the boot screen instead, as a report a player can screenshot\n\n## 10b. Standard features\n\n- `settings.default(key)`: a setting's default value\n- `settings.define(sp)`: add a setting: { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', value, min, max, step, options: [[value, label]] or [value], help, group: 'Game', apply(game, value) }. Its saved value is loaded (when it is a valid one) and applied; it shows on the settings screen. Returns its value\n- `settings.keys(group)`: the keys of a group's settings, in order ('Display', 'Sound', 'Comfort', 'Controller', 'Touch', 'Game'), or every key\n- `settings.reset(group)`: put a group's settings (or all of them) back to their defaults; a game's saves are not touched\n- `settings.set(key, v)`: change a setting: checked (a slider kept in range, a choice among its options), saved, applied, onChange told. Returns the value kept (the old one when v is not a valid value)\n- `settings.spec(key)`: the spec a setting was defined with (its label, type, range, options), or undefined\n- `settings.saved`: false when the last save failed (storage blocked or full: the choices last for this visit)\n- `fullscreen.enter(quiet)`: go full screen. Browsers allow it only from a tap, click or key press: call it from one (a button, the menu, a key's update step). Returns a promise of whether it worked (a refusal is kept in failed and noted on screen, unless quiet)\n- `fullscreen.exit()`: leave full screen (Escape and the phone's back gesture do it too)\n- `fullscreen.on`: true while the page is full screen\n- `fullscreen.standalone`: true when the page was opened from a home screen as an app (no browser bars: nothing to go full screen from)\n- `fullscreen.supported`: true where this page can go full screen (not an iPhone, which has Add to Home Screen instead; not a frame without permission; not headless)\n- `fullscreen.toggle()`: full screen on or off, as the player asked (the corner's button, the menu): their choice is kept in settings.fullscreen, so a game starting on a phone goes full screen by itself only while they haven't turned it off\n- `fullscreen.failed = null`: game: the game it serves; failed: why the last request was refused (a message), or null\n\n## 10c. Boot\n\n- `E.bootSplash(title = '')`: the boot screen's markup, for the very start of a page's <body>: it shows from the first paint, its bar moving while the page's scripts load (a pure CSS animation), and says so if the game's script never runs. The engine takes it over\n- `E.diagnose(game = E.current)`: the report a bug needs, at any time (and on the boot screen when a game can't start): { engine, game, time, page, browser, device, screen, canvas, gpu, sound, storage, input, scene, errors, text (all of it, one line each, for a screenshot or a bug report), short (one line) }. game: the game to report on (E.current)\n\n## 10d. Tweens and scripts\n\n- `new E.Script(game, fn, o = {}) → script` †: a script, made by game.script: done (it returned, or was cancelled), value (what it returned), cancel(); another script can yield it\n- `script.cancel()` †: stop it, and the scripts it is waiting on (its finally blocks run)\n- `script.done = false` †: it returned, or was cancelled\n- `script.keep` †: it outlives the scene\n- `script.value` †: what it returned\n- `new E.Tween(game, target, to, seconds, o = {}) → tween` †: a tween, made by game.tween: done (it ended, or was cancelled), cancel(); a script can yield it\n- `tween.cancel()` †: stop it where it is (no onDone)\n- `tween.done = false` †: it ended, or was cancelled\n- `tween.keep` †: it outlives the scene\n- `tween.target` †: what it moves\n- `tween.to` †: the values it moves them to\n- `E.all(list)` †: a script's wait for every one of its waits (seconds, conditions, tweens, scripts): yield E.all([a, b])\n- `E.any(list)` †: a script's wait for the first of its waits to be over: yield E.any([boss.script, 30])\n- `E.until(fn)` †: a script's wait until fn() is true: yield E.until(() => door.open)\n\n## 10e. Paths\n\n- `new E.Path(points, o = {}) → path` †: new E.Path(points, { closed: false, smooth: null }): points [x, y, z] (z optional) or { x, y, z } (a side view's height is z); closed runs back to the first; smooth: 'catmull' curves through every point (Catmull-Rom); at(d), nearest(x, y, z), draw(r) below\n- `path.at(d)` †: the point d along it: { x, y, z, angle (its heading on the ground, radians: 0 east), d } (closed: d wraps round; open: held at the ends)\n- `path.draw(r, color = '#ffe070')` †: draw it over the scene to see it (debugging): a line, and a dot at each of its points\n- `path.nearest(x, y, z)` †: the distance along it of its point nearest (x, y) on the ground (and z, when given): a racer's progress round a track, a rail's grab point\n- `path.closed` †: it runs back to the first\n- `path.length` †: world units from end to end (round, when closed)\n- `path.points` †: the points it was made from, [x, y, z] each\n\n## 10g. Weather\n\n- `E.WEATHER: rain, snow, ash, leaves, sand` †: the kinds of weather: n drops at amount 1, fall speed (world units a second), colors, streak (seconds of motion a drop's line shows), sway, rest (seconds it lies where it lands), splash, wind (how hard the wind pushes it), size, big (the share a pixel bigger). Add your own: E.WEATHER.embers = { ... }\n\n## 10k. Save slots\n\n- `new E.Saves(game, o) → saves` †: game.saves (made when first read): o is menu.saves ({ slots, version, migrate, write, read })\n- `saves.any` †: is there any save to load (a title's Continue)?\n- `saves.autosave(o)` †: save to the autosave (what write() gives)\n- `saves.copy(from, to)` †: copy a slot's save onto another (over what is there); false when the first is empty\n- `saves.erase(slot)` †: erase a slot\n- `saves.get(slot)` †: a slot's save as it is stored, checked: null when empty; { slot, damaged: true } when it can't be read; { newer: true } from a newer version of the game; else { slot, data, place, level, time, at (the date saved), version }\n- `saves.latest()` †: the most recent save that loads, or null (a title's Continue loads it)\n- `saves.list()` †: every slot, the autosave first: [{ slot, empty, place, level, time, at, damaged, newer }] (a menu's rows)\n- `saves.load(slot)` †: load a slot: its data brought up to this version (migrate), played set from it, read(save) called. Returns the save ({ data, place, level, time, slot }), or null when the slot is empty, damaged or from a newer version\n- `saves.save(slot, o)` †: save to a slot (0.., or 'auto'): o ({ data, place, level }) or what write() gives. Returns true when it is kept (in storage, or for the visit when storage is blocked: blocked tells), false when there was nothing to save\n- `saves.show(mode = 'load', done)` †: open the standard save screen over the game (E.Shell's): 'save' (to a slot) or 'load'; done(save) after a load or a save. False without a menu (a headless game, menu: false)\n- `saves.blocked = false` †: storage refused a save (they last for the visit)\n- `saves.canSave = true` †: the pause menu offers Save (a JRPG turns it off away from save points)\n- `saves.game` †: its game\n- `saves.migrate` †: an older version's data brought up to this one (or null: kept as it is)\n- `saves.played = 0` †: seconds played in the game's scenes (paused time and titles left out), saved with each save and set by a load\n- `saves.read` †: what a load does with { data, place, level, time, slot }\n- `saves.slots` †: how many slots besides the autosave (3)\n- `saves.version` †: the game's version of its data (1), saved with each save\n- `saves.write` †: what a save holds ({ data, place, level })\n\n## 11. Humanoid rig\n\n- `new E.Attack(spec = {}, over) → slash`: new E.Attack(spec) or new E.Attack('slash', { reach: 9 }): a name from E.MOVES plus overrides\n- `slash.active`: true while the strike can connect\n- `slash.busy`: true from the wind-up to the end of the recovery\n- `slash.cancel()`: stop the swing at once (hurt, dodged into)\n- `slash.hits(targets, test, fn)`: during 'active', call fn(target) once per swing for each target where test(target) is true. Returns how many were hit\n- `slash.start(canCancel = false)`: begin a swing if idle (or when canCancel and already recovering). Returns true if it started\n- `slash.state`: what Humanoid.update wants as `attack` (null when idle)\n- `slash.u`: 0..1 progress through the current phase\n- `slash.update(dt)`: advance the swing by dt seconds; returns the phase that began during it ('active' = play the swing sound), or null\n- `slash.hit`: the targets this swing has hit (each is hit once)\n- `slash.phase = null`: 'wind' | 'active' | 'recover', or null when idle\n- `slash.spec`: the move: seconds of wind, active and recover, and its shape for the rig (a0, a1, z0, z1, reach, hitAt)\n- `slash.t = 0`: seconds into the phase\n- `new E.Combo(specs, o = {}) → combo`: new E.Combo(items, { window: .3 }): items are move names, specs or Attacks\n- `combo.active`: true while the current hit can connect\n- `combo.busy`: true while a hit of the chain is swinging\n- `combo.cancel()`: stop the chain; the next press starts it over\n- `combo.current`: the Attack of the current hit\n- `combo.hits(targets, test, fn)`: the current hit's hits(targets, test, fn): fn(target) once per swing where test(target) is true\n- `combo.press()`: start or continue the chain. Returns true if a hit started\n- `combo.state`: what Humanoid.update wants as `attack` (null when idle)\n- `combo.update(dt)`: advance the current hit; returns the phase that began, as Attack.update\n- `combo.idle = 99`: seconds since the last hit ended\n- `combo.moves`: the Attacks\n- `combo.step = -1`: the index of the current hit (-1 before the first)\n- `combo.window`: seconds after a hit ends that a press still chains\n- `new E.Humanoid(o = {}) → rig`: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build, weapon, outfit, hair, hat, sleeves, armor, hood, cape, colors, ... }): a character rig posed from math every step (API.md, \"Characters\", lists the options); update(dt, state) each step, draw(g, ox, oy, view) inside r.actor. hat: 'cap' | 'band' | 'crown' | 'helmet' | 'pointed' (or { style, color }; E.Humanoid.hat adds one); weapon: 'sword' | 'gun' | 'staff' (E.Humanoid.weapon adds one); hood: true (drawn in colors.hair); draw: { under(g, Q, rig), over(g, Q, rig) } the game's own drawing before and after the body (Q: the joints on screen, [x, y, depth]: outfits, accessories); seed: its own random stream (blinks, its clock)\n- `E.Humanoid.hat(id, draw)` †: a hat of the game's own: draw(h) with h.at(forward, right, up) a point round the head on screen, h.r its radius there, h.c its color (o.hat.color), h.dk a darker one, h.g, h.rig\n- `E.Humanoid.hats` †: the hats a Humanoid may wear, by id: the built-ins (cap, band, crown, helmet, pointed) and a game's own\n- `E.Humanoid.pose(id, fn)` †: a held pose of the game's own: fn(J, w, rig) moves the joints (the rig's frame: forward, right, up) toward the pose by w, 0..1, which eases in and out as state.pose names it or not (blended over the rig's solve)\n- `E.Humanoid.poses` †: the held poses of a game's own, by id (the built-in poses are the rig's own solve: state.pose names either)\n- `E.Humanoid.weapon(id, spec)` †: a weapon of the game's own (or a built-in replaced): { length: the tip's distance from the hand (a number, or rig => one; tip() and the swing's trail), rest: [forward, right, up] the way it points held at rest, blade: true (a swing smears along it), draw(w): its drawing in both styles (w.style, w.at(t), w.push(depth, fn), w.u: rig._weaponDraw) }\n- `E.Humanoid.weapons` †: the weapons a Humanoid may hold, by id: the built-ins (sword, gun, staff) and a game's own\n- `rig.debug(r)` †: skeleton overlay for debugging, draws straight into the buffer\n- `rig.draw(g, ox, oy, view)`: draw into g with (ox, oy) = projected position of the rig's root\n- `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, size = 40, o = {})` †: a close-up bust for dialog boxes and menus, drawn live from the rig (blinks, hair and hats included): rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 40, { zoom: 3, turn: .6, bg: '#1a2050', frame: true }) // inside r.overlay(g => ...) zoom: pixels per world unit (3 = face fills the frame), turn: 0 = facing you, .6 = three-quarter. The bust stays upright while the rig kneels or lies down (upright: false shows the real pose); cape: false hides the cape; tint: '#808080' greys it out (a KO'd party member).\n- `rig.drawSmear(r, colors = ['#ffffff', '#dff8ff', '#8fe0f2', '#4bb1d4'])` †: motion smear for the current swing, queued in front of the rig\n- `rig.hand(which = 'R')`: world position of a hand ('R' holds the weapon): spawn bullets, sparks or thrown things here\n- `rig.head()`: world position of the head (for speech bubbles, portraits, halos)\n- `rig.kick(v)`: a squash-and-stretch kick: + stretches (a jump), - squashes (a landing, a hit)\n- `rig.land(strength = 1)` †: a heavy landing: knees bend deep and spring back over a moment; strength 1 or more drops a hand to the ground (a three-point landing), .3-.6 is a soft one. Call it on the step the body lands\n- `rig.solvePose()`: set the joints J from the rig's state this step (update and setPose call it); a clip player may wrap it on one rig: const base = rig.solvePose; rig.solvePose = function () { base.call(this); ...move J... }\n- `rig.tip()`: world position of the weapon tip (sword tip, gun muzzle, staff orb)\n- `rig.update(dt, s = {})`: s: { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, dash, hurt, attack, air, point } air: true while jumping or falling (legs tuck), point: true to hold the weapon hand forward (aiming a gun) attack: { spec, phase: 'wind' | 'active' | 'recover', u: 0..1 }. spec.kick = true swings the right foot instead of the hand also aim (radians up), crouch, sprint, climb, hang (hangZ: the top it hangs from, above the feet), flip: 0..1 (a roll), pose: 'cheer' | 'cast' | 'guard' | 'kneel' | 'crouch' | 'wave' | 'hips' | 'down' | 'die' | 'block' or one of E.Humanoid.pose's, tilt (radians: the whole body forward about the feet, a dive) and roll (sideways), stance: 'guard' | 'ready', down: 0..1, vz (with climb: the climbing speed), land: k (a heavy landing), expr: 'smile' | 'shout' | 'angry' | 'wince', run: 0..1 (run in place)\n- `rig.worldOffset(p)`: where a point in the rig's own frame ([forward, right, up]; a joint such as rig.J.head, or one offset from it) sits, in world units from the rig's feet: add rig.x, y, z for the world point, or project it to draw at a joint\n- `rig.C`: its colors (skin, hair, cloth, pants, boot, belt, cape, capeIn, metal, hilt, eye, ...), the defaults with o.colors over them; change one and it shows at the next draw\n- `rig.J`: its joints this step in its own frame ({ hipL, kneeL, footL, shC, head, handR, ... }; rig.worldOffset(J.handR) gives one in the world)\n- `rig.atkZ = 10`: the height of the weapon hand during an attack\n- `rig.blink = 2`: seconds to the next blink\n- `rig.capeL = null`: the cape's left edge points (null until it has a cape and has moved)\n- `rig.capeR = []`: the cape's right edge points\n- `rig.dieT`: pose 'die': a stagger, the knees give, then the body topples\n- `rig.downW`: how far down it is, 0..1 (state down, pose 'down' or 'die'): it falls faster and faster and gets up steadily\n- `rig.expr` †: the face shown ('smile', 'shout', 'angry', 'wince'), or null\n- `rig.facing = 0`: the angle it faces, radians\n- `rig.hop`: how high an attack's hop lifts it now\n- `rig.hurtW = 0`: 0..1, how far into the hurt flinch\n- `rig.lastView` †: drawSmear projects the trail with the same view\n- `rig.lunge`: how far the body leans into an attack now, world units\n- `rig.o`: its options, with the build's proportions and the defaults filled in (size, build, weapon, outfit, ...; legUpper, torso, headR and the other lengths are at size 1)\n- `rig.phase = 0`: the walk cycle's phase, radians\n- `rig.poseW`: held poses: 'cheer' (item get, victory), 'cast' (both hands forward), 'guard' (fists up), 'kneel', 'crouch', 'wave', 'hips' (hands on hips), and down: 0..1 (knocked flat). stance: 'guard' (fists up while moving) | 'ready' (weapon held forward)\n- `rig.reach = 7`: how far it reaches forward\n- `rig.spin = 0`: the body's extra turn during a spinning move, radians\n- `rig.sq = 0`: squash and stretch, -0.45..0.45 (kick adds to it)\n- `rig.sqV = 0`: its speed\n- `rig.t`: seconds of its own clock (idle breathing, blinks)\n- `rig.trail = []` †: recent points of the blade (or fist) tip, for the swing's smear\n- `rig.x = 0`, `rig.y = 0`, `rig.z = 0`: where its feet are (from the last update)\n- `E.MOVES: slash, backslash, overhead, rising, thrust, spin, plunge, twohand, jab, cross, hook, uppercut, haymaker, elbow, ... (24)`: a library of named attacks with timing and body motion (anticipation, lunge, step, twist, hop). new E.Attack('overhead'), new E.Attack('thrust', { reach: 10 }), new E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook', 'uppercut']) rig.update(dt, { ..., attack: E.move('roundhouse', .5) }) // any move at any moment, for previews and cutscenes The same move works on every build and in every view: heights are relative to the shoulders (rel: true), reaches scale with arm length, and horizontal swings turn into screen-plane swings in side views. A move's spec fields:\n- `E.MOVES { a0, a1, z0, z1 }`: a0 / a1 angles (+ = the right side, or up in the screen plane), z0 / z1 heights\n- `E.MOVES { reach, r0, r1, hand, plane, blade }`: reach, or r0 / r1 (an extending thrust); hand: 'R' | 'L' | 'both', plane: 'side' (always vertical), blade: 0 (no trail)\n- `E.MOVES { wind, active, recover }`: the phases' seconds\n- `E.MOVES { kick, spin, lunge, hop, crouch, lean, twist, hold }`: the body's motion (kick: true swings the right foot)\n- `E.knockback(from, target, speed = 160, up = 0)`: push a target away from a point (attacker): uses target.push (E.Body) or its vx / vy. up = upward speed (knock into the air)\n- `E.move(name, u = .5, phase = 'active')`: any move from E.MOVES at any moment, for previews and cutscenes: rig.update(dt, { ..., attack: E.move('roundhouse', .5) }) (u 0..1 through phase)\n\n## 12. Blob rig\n\n- `new E.Blob(o = {}) → blob`: new E.Blob({ R: 6.5, colors, ears, horns, wings, feet, tail, mouth, face }): a squash-and-stretch rig for slimes and jellies; blob.update(dt, state) each step, blob.draw(g, ox, oy, view) in an r.actor\n- `blob.draw(g, ox, oy, view)`: draw(g, ox, oy, view). Creature features from the constructor options: ears: 'rabbit' | 'cat', horns: true, wings: true (flapping bat wings), feet: true, tail: true, mouth: true | 'fangs', face: 'front' (eyes always on the camera side, for Mana-style monsters) update(dt, { flap: 0..1, hang: true }) slows or folds the wings and hangs a bat upside down from its anchor.\n- `blob.kick(v)`: a squash kick: + stretches (a hop), - squashes (a landing, a hit)\n- `blob.update(dt, s = {})`: s: {squash target -0.4..0.4, look:[dx,dy] ground dir, squint}\n- `blob.C`: its colors: dk, base, lt, spec, eye, pupil\n- `blob.flap`: the wings' speed now, 0 (folded) .. 1\n- `blob.flapP`: the wing beat's phase\n- `blob.hang`: roosting upside down\n- `blob.look = [1, 0]`: where its eyes look, a ground direction\n- `blob.o`: its options (R: the radius, 6.5; ears, horns, wings, feet, tail, mouth, face, colors)\n- `blob.scale = 1`: drawing scale (1)\n- `blob.sq = 0`: squash -0.45..0.45 (- flat, + tall)\n- `blob.sqV = 0`: its speed\n- `blob.squint = false`: eyes narrowed\n- `blob.t = 0`: its clock, seconds\n- `blob.target = 0`: the squash it springs toward\n- `blob.walk`: 0..1, how fast it is walking (its feet patter)\n\n## 13. Tile map\n\n- `new E.TileMap(o = {}) → map`: Walls standing on a floor (top-down, isometric, brawler). Either { rows: [...ASCII...], legend: { '#': 1, '@': 'hero' }, types, floorTex } (recommended) or { w, h, cells: [ids...], floor: [tags...], blocked: [0 | 1...], types, floorTex } (built in code: fill, setFloor; with no cells it starts as open floor).\n- `new E.TileMap({ types })`: types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut, cutH, face, roof }\n- `new E.TileMap({ types[id].face })`: the wall's material (E.MATERIALS): 'brick' (default) | 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge' | 'none' | any material id or '#hex pattern'\n- `new E.TileMap({ types[id].roof })`: the top's material: 'speckle' (default) | 'plain' | 'tiles' (roof shingles) | 'slab' (paving) | 'leaves' | 'grass' | any material id or '#hex pattern'\n- `new E.TileMap({ floorTex })`: floorTex(x, y, floorTag, view) -> [r, g, b] or [r, g, b, glow]\n- `new E.TileMap({ floorMat, floorMats })`: a material for the floor, floorMats: { tag: material } for tagged cells (in place of floorTex)\n- `new E.TileMap({ block })`: ['water', 'lava'] floor tags nobody can walk on (or put block: true in a legend entry)\n- `new E.TileMap({ chunks })`: bake the floor in squares near the camera (true, false; by default a map of more than 128 x 128 cells)\n- `map.arcClear(a, b, apex = 20, r = 0)`: does a jump arc from a to b (feet, { x, y, z }) clear every top on the way? The arc is a parabola peaking apex above the higher end; r > 0 also checks a track on each side of it. Jumps, throws, aerial takedowns\n- `map.block(cx, cy, on = true)`: mark a cell blocked or free at runtime (a bridge appears, lava cools)\n- `map.bounds(view)`: projected pixel bounds of the whole map for this view, { x0, y0, x1, y1 } with an 8-pixel margin (camera clamping: game.cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v))\n- `map.cell(cx, cy)`: the wall type id at a cell (0 = floor, -1 outside the map)\n- `map.center(cx, cy)`: the world point at a cell's center\n- `map.collide(b)`: push a circle { x, y, r } out of walls. If the body has a number z, walls lower than z + step (default 2) do not block it (it is standing or jumping above them). Returns true when it touched a wall.\n- `map.drawFloor(r)`: draw the baked floor (first thing in draw, after r.sky); walls come from queueWalls(r)\n- `map.drop(from, dir, o = {})`: what lies past the edge ahead: the first drop of more than step within reach (default 12) of `from` in direction dir, and the ground a body of its radius lands on just past it: { x, y, z, fall } or null (no edge, or a wall first). AI that won't walk off roofs, hop-downs, fall warnings\n- `map.fill(cx, cy, w, h, id)`: put wall type id in every cell of a w x h block of cells (0 clears them): build maps in code\n- `map.find(tag)`: first spawn with this tag ({ x, y, z, cx, cy }), or null\n- `map.findAll(tag)`: every spawn with this tag\n- `map.floorAt(x, y)`: floor tag from the legend ({ floor: 'water' }) at a world point, or null\n- `map.floorBytes()` †: the bytes the baked floors hold now (each view's, chunks and the lighting's canvases counted): what a big map's floor costs in memory\n- `map.groundAt(x, y, r = 0, z = 1e9, step = 2)`: highest surface under a circle footprint that a body at height z can stand on (floor = 0)\n- `map.height(cx, cy)`: a cell's top: its wall's h, 0 on the floor, 1e9 outside the map (the height rules: E.HEIGHTS)\n- `map.heightAt(x, y)`: wall height at a world point (0 on the floor)\n- `map.isFront(cx, cy, view)`: is wall cell (cx, cy) in front of what the camera looks at, so cutaway cuts it down to its type's cutH (6 by default) in this view? A game may replace it on its map (map.isFront = (cx, cy, view) => ...)\n- `map.landing(from, dir, o = {})`: where a jump from `from` in direction dir lands across a gap: past the first drop ahead (a top more than step below its feet), the nearest spot min..max away that rises at most rise and falls at most drop from its feet, stands above the gap's bottom, has room to stand, and that a jump arc peaking apex above the higher end clears (arcClear). { x, y, z, d } or null (a wall before any gap, or nowhere to land). o: { min: 35, max: 83, rise: 11, drop: 18, apex: 20 }\n- `map.ledge(from, dir, o = {})`: a ledge to climb: in direction dir from `from`, the nearest top that rises min..rise above its feet within reach, with room to stand past the edge, looking straight ahead and spread radians to each side (a gap first means a jump: landing). { x, y, z (the spot to stand on top), rise, facing (the angle climbed), edge: [x, y] (where the hands grip) } or null. o: { reach: 18, min: 4, rise: 82, spread: .28 }\n- `map.los(x0, y0, x1, y1)`: line of sight across the ground (true if nothing solid in between). It ignores heights: sight() is the one that sees over a low wall and from a roof\n- `map.pick(sx, sy, at = E.current)`: the cell under a screen point on a map with heights (tactics, strategy, board games): the first top or wall face the line of sight meets, front to back, in any view, turn and zoom. sx, sy: the HUD's pixels (input.clicked(), input.pointer()); at: the game whose view and camera drew it (E.current by default) or { view, ix, iy }. Returns { cx, cy, x, y, z, face: 'top' | 'side' } (the world point it meets) or null off the map. Cells only, as height() has them (not props, actors, or walls cut away)\n- `map.queueWalls(r)`: queue every visible wall box. Call once per frame after drawFloor.\n- `map.ray(a, b, o = {})`: the first cell top that rises above the straight segment from a to b ({ x, y, z } world points; o.eye and o.target lift each end): { clear, t (0..1 along it), x, y, z } where it stops (b's point when clear). It walks every cell the segment crosses, not samples; a top level with the segment doesn't block it, and outside the map is solid\n- `map.set(cx, cy, v)`: put wall type v at a cell (0 opens it: a door, a broken wall); bumps version, so caches and flow fields rebuild\n- `map.setFloor(cx, cy, tag, block)`: give a cell a floor tag ('water', 'grass', what floorTex and floorAt read), and block it (deep water, lava) or not\n- `map.sight(a, b, o = {})`: can a's eyes (z + eye) see b's chest (z + chest)? E.HEIGHTS gives both unless o ({ eye, target }) or the bodies' own eye and chest say otherwise. A guard sees over a planter lower than its eyes, and down from a roof\n- `map.solid(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})` †: make a shape solid in this map: map.solid(id | entry, x, y, z, o) with r.shape's placement (facing, scale, flip, params) -> the solid ({ x, y (the footprint's middle), z, top, hx, hy, c, s, shape, px, py (where it was placed) }; map.solids holds them; what stands on one sorts after it). A body overlapping its span in height is pushed out of its footprint; one coming down onto it lands on its top; one wholly below passes under. Register solids before the bodies' updates; drawing never sets them\n- `map.solidAt(x, y)`: solidCell at a world point\n- `map.solidCell(cx, cy)`: true where a cell has a wall (or is outside the map); walkable() also counts blocked floor\n- `map.standable(x, y, z, r = 5, step = HEIGHTS.step)`: can a body of radius r stand at (x, y) with its feet at z? Nothing under its footprint rises above z + step (it would collide), no blocked floor (water, lava) is under it at floor level, and a top within step below z holds it up\n- `map.toCell(x, y)`: world point -> [cx, cy] cell (center(cx, cy) goes back)\n- `map.walkable(cx, cy)`: true where a body can walk: no wall and not a blocked floor (water, lava). Pathfinding uses this.\n- `map.wall(from, dir, o = {})` †: the wall face a body touches or faces: from `from`, in direction dir (an angle or [dx, dy]), the first point within reach whose top rises more than a step above its feet. { x, y (on the face), nx, ny (the face's normal, out toward the body), top (how high it rises there), bottom (the floor at its foot), facing (the angle into it) } or null. Cells give axis faces; a map.solid box, the face back along dir. o: { reach: from.r + 4 }\n- `map.T`: the tile size in world units (16)\n- `map.ao`: contact shadow width (world units) along the foot of walls; 0 turns it off\n- `map.blocked`: cells nobody can walk on (deep water, lava, pits): legend { floor: 'water', block: true } or TileMap { block: ['water'] }\n- `map.cells`: the wall type id of every cell, row by row (index cy * w + cx; change one with set)\n- `map.chunks` †: the floor baked in squares of 128 screen pixels as the camera comes to them (its colors and the GPU lighting's canvas both), the far ones let go, so a 256 x 256-cell map costs what the screen shows; a change to cells re-bakes only the squares over them. True, false, or by default when the map has more than 128 x 128 cells\n- `map.cutaway`: walls between the camera and the floor drop low (types with cut: true; false turns it off)\n- `map.floorBudget` †: the bytes the floor's bakes may hold (64 MB): past it the least recently used go (floorBytes())\n- `map.floorMat` †: a material (E.MATERIALS id or '#hex pattern') for every floor cell\n- `map.floorMats` †: { tag: material } for the cells with that floor tag, over floorMat (untagged cells have the tag null). Either stands in for floorTex\n- `map.floorTags`: each cell's floor tag from the legend ('water', 'lava', ...), row by row, or null for a map built from cells\n- `map.floorTex`: floorTex(x, y, tag, view) -> [r, g, b], the floor's color per pixel\n- `map.floors`: the baked floor images by view (floors = {} re-bakes them after floorTex changes)\n- `map.h`, `map.w`: the map's size in cells\n- `map.light`: null shades wall faces with the shapes' light (from the viewer's upper left: a face toward it lit, the other face of a corner a shade darker, in every view), or a direction [x, y, 0] the light comes from\n- `map.outside`: the ground beyond the map (ground views): undefined continues the map's floor past its edge, darkening into one color that fills the rest of the screen, a color ('#2a2630') fades into that, false leaves it empty\n- `map.shimmer` †: animated sparkle on floor cells tagged 'water' or 'lava'\n- `map.spawns`: spawn points from the ASCII rows: { tag, ch, cx, cy, x, y, z } (x, y = cell center)\n- `map.types`: the wall types by id ({ h, top, side, face, roof, cut, ... })\n- `map.version = 0`: counts every change to cells and blocked cells (set, block), so caches and flow fields know to rebuild\n- `E.parseLevel(rows, legend = {})`: Parse an ASCII level (top row first; every character is one tile). legend maps a character to a number -> tile / wall type id (0 = empty) a string -> a spawn tag; the cell stays empty and spawns[] gets { tag, ch, cx, cy } an object -> any of { tile: id, floor: 'water', spawn: 'hero', block: true } (block: nobody can walk on this cell) '.' and ' ' are empty unless the legend says otherwise. Unknown characters warn once and become empty.\n\n## 13b. Materials\n\n- `E.MATERIALS: brick, stone, rock, plank, timber, plaster, hedge, speckle, plain, tiles, slab, leaves, grass, flagstone, ... (61)`: the materials by id: { color, pattern, ...its numbers, ramp, glow } or a painter of a surface (face, roof, floor, tile); add your own: E.MATERIALS.oak = { color: '#8a5a36', pattern: 'planks', bw: 3 }. Any material id takes '#hex pattern key=value' too\n- `E.PATTERNS`: the patterns, by name: (s, m) => a tone step (-1 .. +2) at a point s of a surface, for a material m's numbers. Add your own: E.PATTERNS.waves = (s, m) => Math.sin(s.u / (m.size || 3)) > .8 ? 1 : 0\n- `E.PATTERNS.bark(s)` †: fissures running up a trunk; a cut end shows its rings\n- `E.PATTERNS.bricks(s, m)` †: running bond, bw x bh (6 x 3), mortar a step darker (mortar: its step)\n- `E.PATTERNS.cloth(s, m)` †: a fine weave and soft folds (fold .9)\n- `E.PATTERNS.crate(s, m)` †: a framed and braced crate side (frame 2 wide, a diagonal brace, boards bw 3 inside); lids are planks\n- `E.PATTERNS.foliage(s, m)` †: leaf clumps (size 3.5), each lit at its top and dark at its foot, a few bright leaves\n- `E.PATTERNS.glaze(s)` †: a glossy shine where the light is strongest, a few specks\n- `E.PATTERNS.metal(s, m)` †: hard light (a shine where lit, darker in shade); rivet: a rivet every rivet units\n- `E.PATTERNS.noise(s, m)` †: soft patches (scale 4)\n- `E.PATTERNS.none()` †: the light's bands alone\n- `E.PATTERNS.planks(s, m)` †: boards bw wide (3) with joints and butt ends every len (14); along u on lids and flat sides, along v (staves) on turned surfaces; dir: 'v' turns them on flat faces\n- `E.PATTERNS.shingles(s, m)` †: staggered rows of roof tiles (course 2.5, bw 4)\n- `E.PATTERNS.speck(s, m)` †: dark and light specks (amount .08)\n- `E.PATTERNS.stones(s, m)` †: irregular stones (Voronoi cells about size across, 5) with dark joints and a lit rim\n- `E.PATTERNS.thatch(s, m)` †: straw in overlapping courses (course 3)\n- `E.PATTERNS.tiles(s, m)` †: square tiles size (4) with grout\n- `E.material(id, local, kind = 'material', depth = 0)`: a material ready to draw: { q (its entry), ramp (five [r, g, b], dark to light), hexes (the same as '#rrggbb'), pat (its pattern), glow }. id: an id (looked up in local first, a set's own, then E.MATERIALS), a shorthand string or an entry; kind names the surface, for the warning when the id is unknown (then null)\n\n## 14. Flow field\n\n- `new E.FlowField(map, o = {}) → flow`: new E.FlowField(map, { step: 2, climb: 0, drop: 0, jump: 0 }): distances over the tops a body can stand on (E.HEIGHTS). With no options it covers the floor (and walls no higher than step, which bodies walk over); a target on a roof gets a field across the roofs it connects to\n- `new E.FlowField({ step, climb, drop })`: next-door cells connect when their tops differ by at most step, or going up by up to climb and down by up to drop (agents that climb walls or hop down)\n- `new E.FlowField({ jump })`: (world units) links tops across a gap that a jump clears, straight along a row or column, by map.landing's rules (up to jumpRise higher, 11, and jumpDrop lower, 18): an agent with E.Mover's leap chases across roofs. With jump, distances are travel: a jump of k cells costs k, and a climb 1 plus its rise in cells (climbing is slow), so a chase leaps a gap rather than drop into the street and climb back up\n- `flow.dir(x, y, fx, fy)`: normalized ground direction to walk from (x, y) toward the target (straight at it where the field doesn't reach)\n- `flow.next(x, y)` †: the next cell to head for from (x, y): { x, y, z (its center and top), kind: 'walk' | 'climb' | 'drop' | 'jump', edge (how far (x, y) is from the side of its cell it leaves by) }, or null where the field doesn't reach (or at the target). An AI standing on the ground presses jump for a 'climb' or a 'jump' once edge is under its radius plus a little (E.Mover climbs or leaps; a press in the air would be kept for the landing)\n- `flow.update(x, y)`: aim the field at a world point, or at several (update([[x, y], ...]): each cell leads to the nearest, the heroes a horde chases); it rebuilds only when a target's cell or the map (map.version) changes. A map.solid shape standing on the floor raises the cells under it to its top (a crate is walked round, a low step climbed)\n- `flow.climb`: how much higher a next-door top may be (an agent that climbs walls)\n- `flow.d`: each cell's distance in steps to the target cell (1e9 where it can't get there), row by row\n- `flow.drop`: how much lower (an agent that hops down)\n- `flow.jump` †: the widest gap jumped (world units, 0 = none)\n- `flow.jumpDrop`, `flow.jumpRise` †: how much higher and lower a jump may land\n- `flow.map`: the map it paths over\n- `flow.step`: the height difference walked over freely (2)\n- `flow.tx = -1`, `flow.ty = -1`: the target's cell\n- `flow.version = -1`: the map's version it was built for\n\n## 15. Texture helpers for floortex functions\n\n- `E.tex`: floor textures for a TileMap's floorTex(x, y), each giving [r, g, b] at a world point; as materials too: floorMat: 'grass'\n- `E.tex.checker(x, y, o = {})`: checkerboard tiles (castle floors, bonus rooms). o: { a, b, size }\n- `E.tex.dirt(x, y, o = {})`: dirt, sand or snow: base color with soft noise and specks. o: { base, dark, light }\n- `E.tex.flagstone(x, y, pal, size = 16)`: stone flagstones with running-bond rows. pal: {stones:[hex..], mortar, hi, lo, speck}\n- `E.tex.grass(x, y, o = {})`: grass with darker tufts and a few flowers. o: { base, dark, light, flower } hex colors\n- `E.tex.plain(x, y, o = {})`: plain color with light noise, for roads, carpets and space floors. o: { base, amount }\n- `E.tex.planks(x, y, o = {})`: wooden floor boards running along x. o: { base, dark, line }\n- `E.tex.water(x, y, o = {})`: water with static ripple lines (baked, so it does not move). o: { base, dark, light }\n\n## 16. Platform map\n\n- `new E.PlatformMap(o = {}) → level`: { rows: [...ASCII, top row first...], legend: { '#': 1, '@': 'hero' }, types: { 1: {...} }, tile: 16, depth: 16 }. Tile kinds: 'solid' (default) | 'oneway' | 'ladder' | 'slope' | 'hazard' | 'deco' | 'back'\n- `level.bounds(view)`: projected pixel bounds of the level for this view (use for game.cam.bounds)\n- `level.cell(cx, cz)`: tile id at column cx, row cz (cz = 0 is the bottom). Left and right of the level are solid walls unless edges: 'open'\n- `level.cellsTouching(b, what)`: tiles overlapped by a body box { x, z, w, h }: [{ cx, cz, id, kind }] (optionally only one kind or id)\n- `level.center(cx, cz)`: a cell's center -> [x, z]\n- `level.crumble(cx, cz, holds = .5, respawn = 3)` †: start a crumbling block at a cell going, once (it falls away after holds seconds and comes back after respawn more; 0: never; update(dt) runs them, each step); returns its record\n- `level.draw(r)`: draw the level as the background layer: call right after r.sky() and before anything else\n- `level.drawWater(r, alpha = .45)` †: draw the water tiles see-through over what is in them (call it after the actors are queued: an overlay)\n- `level.find(tag)`: the first spawn with this tag ({ x, y: 0, z, cx, cz }), or null\n- `level.findAll(tag)`: every spawn with this tag\n- `level.groundBelow(x, z)`: top of the highest solid or one-way tile at or below height z in this column, or null over a pit\n- `level.height`: the level's height in world units (width is its width)\n- `level.kind(cx, cz)`: a cell's kind: 'solid', 'oneway', 'ladder', 'slope', 'hazard', 'deco' or 'back', null when empty ('solid' outside)\n- `level.kindAt(x, z)`: kind() at a world point\n- `level.move(b, dt, solids)`: Move a body { x, z, vx, vz, w, h } (x = center, z = feet, vz > 0 = up) by its velocity, colliding with the level. Sets b.onGround, b.onOneWay, b.hitWall (-1 left, 1 right, 0), b.hitCeiling, b.bumped ({ cx, cz, id } of a ceiling tile hit from below, for ? blocks and bricks) and b.ground (the moving solid it stands on). One-way tiles hold the body from above unless b.drop > 0. solids: optional array of extra boxes { x, z, w, h, vx, vz, oneway } (moving platforms, elevators, crates) that also carry what stands on them.\n- `level.set(cx, cz, id)`: put tile type id at a cell (0 clears it; cz counts from the bottom row); bumps version\n- `level.slopeZ(cx, cz, x)`: surface height of a slope tile at world x\n- `level.solidAt(x, z)`: true where a world point is inside a solid tile\n- `level.toCell(x, z)`: a world point -> [cx, cz]\n- `level.touching(b, what)`: does the body box overlap a tile of this kind ('hazard', 'ladder', ...) or id?\n- `level.update(dt)` †: run the crumbling blocks one step: a block falls away when its time is up, and comes back after its respawn\n- `level.width`: width and height of the level in world units\n- `level.T`: the tile size (16)\n- `level.cells`: stored bottom row first: cz = 0 is the lowest row and z grows upward\n- `level.crumbling` †: the blocks going ({ cx, cz, id, t: seconds before it falls, back })\n- `level.depth`: how deep tiles are drawn in tilted views, world units (the tile size)\n- `level.edges`: 'solid' (the default) makes the level's left and right edges walls, 'open' lets bodies walk off them\n- `level.h`, `level.w`: the level's size in cells\n- `level.spawns`: spawn points: { tag, ch, cx, cz, x, y: 0, z } with x = cell center and z = bottom of the cell (feet)\n- `level.types`: the tile types by id, with their defaults filled in ({ kind, style, side, top, ... })\n- `level.version = 0`: counts every change to the cells (set)\n\n## 16b. Platformer\n\n- `new E.Platformer(o = {}) → hero`: a side-scroller character body with modern, forgiving controls (coyote time, jump buffering, variable jump height, optional double jump, wall jump, dash, ladders, knockback). const hero = new E.Platformer({ x, z, w: 8, h: 22 }); update: hero.update(dt, level, game.input) (or an intent object for enemies: { x: -1..1, jump, jumpPressed, up, down, dash }) then read hero.onGround, hero.jumped, hero.landed, hero.facing (1 or -1), hero.climbing, hero.air draw a Humanoid with rig.update(dt, hero.rigState()) Units: world units per second. One tile = 16. A 285 jump with gravity 800 clears about 3 tiles.\n- `hero.knock(dir, speed = 130, up = 170, stun = .3)`: push the body (getting hit): dir = -1 / 1\n- `hero.release(up = 0)` †: let go of a swing, keeping its speed (up: an extra upward speed)\n- `hero.rigState(extra)`: state for Humanoid.update: rig.update(dt, hero.rigState({ attack }))\n- `hero.swingFrom(x, z, len)` †: hang from a point (a grapple's hook, a whip's ring): a pendulum from (x, z) to its hands, len long (the distance now by default); left and right pump it, jump lets go with its speed (release() does too)\n- `hero.update(dt, level, ctl = {}, solids)`: move it one step: ctl is game.input or an intent { x: -1..1, jump, jumpPressed, up, down, dash } (enemies, bots); solids: moving platforms [{ x, z, w, h, vx, vz }] to stand on\n- `hero.accel = 900`, `hero.airAccel = 650`, `hero.decel = 1100`: how fast it speeds up and slows down on the ground and in the air\n- `hero.air = false`: in the air, not climbing\n- `hero.airJumps = 0`: extra jumps in the air (1 = double jump)\n- `hero.buffer = .13`: seconds a jump pressed before landing is kept\n- `hero.climb = 70`: ladder speed\n- `hero.climbing = false`: on a ladder\n- `hero.coyote = .09`: seconds a jump still works after walking off a ledge\n- `hero.cut = .45`: the share of upward speed kept when jump is let go early (short hops)\n- `hero.dash = 0`: dash speed (0 = no dash)\n- `hero.dashTime = .22`: seconds a dash lasts\n- `hero.dashing = 0`: seconds of dash left\n- `hero.drop = 0`: seconds left of dropping through a one-way platform\n- `hero.facing = 1`: 1 right, -1 left\n- `hero.fallGravity = 1150`: while falling (snappier landings)\n- `hero.gravity = 800`: while rising\n- `hero.h = 22`, `hero.w = 8`: its box (x is its center, z its feet)\n- `hero.hanging` †: the ledge it hangs from ({ dir, top, cx }), or null\n- `hero.hitWall`: keep wall contact while holding toward it\n- `hero.jump = 285`: the jump's starting speed\n- `hero.jumped = false`, `hero.landed = false`: it jumped or landed this step\n- `hero.maxFall = 330`: the fastest fall\n- `hero.onBars` †: the bars it hangs from ({ z }), or null\n- `hero.onGround = false`: standing on something\n- `hero.onRope = null` †: true while on a rope (section 16c sets them)\n- `hero.run = 95`: top running speed\n- `hero.stun = 0`: seconds of knockback left (no control)\n- `hero.swinging` †: the point it swings from ({ x, z, L: the length, a: the angle, w: the angular speed }), or null\n- `hero.vx = 0`, `hero.vz = 0`: its speed\n- `hero.wallJump = false`: jumps off walls\n- `hero.wallSlide = 70`: the slowest fall against a wall\n- `hero.x = 0`, `hero.z = 0`: its feet's position (side view: x right, z up)\n- `hero.y = 0`: its depth (0)\n\n## 17. Body\n\n- `new E.Body(o = {}) → body`: new E.Body({ x, y, r: 5, ... })\n- `body.jump(v = 220)`: leave the ground with upward speed v (only when standing). Returns true if it jumped\n- `body.push(ix, iy, iz = 0)`: add an impulse (knockback, explosion): push(vx, vy, vz)\n- `body.update(dt, map)`: move it one step: speed, friction, walls (map.collide: lower walls are walked onto), gravity and the ground under it (map.groundAt)\n- `body.bounce = 0`: 0..1, how much speed it keeps off walls and the floor\n- `body.bounced = false`: it hit the ground and bounced up this step (dust, a thud)\n- `body.friction = 0`: slows vx and vy per second (0 = none)\n- `body.gravity = 700`: pulls z down (world units per second squared)\n- `body.groundZ = 0`: the height of what is under it\n- `body.h = 32` †: its height (32, a standing actor's: it spans z to z + h, so it passes under a solid shape above that and walks into one in that span)\n- `body.hitWall = false`: it touched a wall this step\n- `body.landed = false`: it landed this step\n- `body.onGround = true`: standing\n- `body.r = 5`: its radius\n- `body.step = 2`: the height it walks up without jumping (E.HEIGHTS.step)\n- `body.vx = 0`, `body.vy = 0`, `body.vz = 0`: its speed, world units per second\n- `body.x = 0`, `body.y = 0`, `body.z = 0`: its position (z = feet)\n\n## 17b. Actions\n\n- `E.ACTIONS: climbUp, climbJump, vault, hopDown, roll, getUp, takedown` †: the actions E.Action plays. Add your own: E.ACTIONS.name = { beats, setup, at }; setup(A) and at(A, beat, u) get the action: its options A.o, A.start (where the body began), A.body; setup may change A.beats. Play one: mover.play('takedown', { other })\n- `new E.Action(name, o = {}) → action`: new E.Action(name, { body, to, other, dir, dist, top, aerial, reverse, speed: 1 }): play E.ACTIONS[name] on a body (and on o.other for a paired action). It moves them itself: run update(dt) every step instead of their physics, and feed rigState() to the rig. Each body ends standing where the action put it\n- `action.canCancel` †: true during a beat marked as one the action may be cut short in (spec.cancel)\n- `action.cancel()` †: stop now: the bodies stay where they are\n- `action.invulnerable` †: true while hits should miss (spec.safe: [from, to] of the action's progress)\n- `action.otherState(extra)` †: the rig state for the other body of a paired action (the victim of a takedown)\n- `action.rigState(extra)` †: the rig state for the body (merge extra over it): x, y, z, its speed, facing and the beat's pose\n- `action.u` †: 0..1 through the whole action\n- `action.update(dt)` †: move the bodies one step; returns true while the action runs (false once it has ended)\n- `action.beat` †: the name of the beat playing now\n- `action.beats` †: [[name, seconds], ...]\n- `action.body`, `action.other` †: the bodies it moves\n- `action.done = false` †: it has ended (or was cancelled)\n- `action.duration` †: the action's length in seconds (o.speed shortens it)\n- `action.name` †: the action's name\n- `action.o` †: the options it was given\n- `action.spec` †: its E.ACTIONS entry\n- `action.start` †: where the body was when it began\n- `action.t = 0` †: seconds played\n\n## 17c. Mover\n\n- `new E.Mover(o = {}) → mover`: new E.Mover({ x, y, z, r: 5, ... }): any of its fields, below, as options. An E.Body: it has body.jump, push and the body's fields too\n- `mover.dodge(dir, dist = 34)` †: a dodge roll toward dir (an angle or [dx, dy]; default its facing) when it stands free. Returns the action, or null\n- `mover.play(name, o = {})` †: start an action now (E.ACTIONS name, its options): it moves the body until it ends\n- `mover.respawn()` †: back to the last safe spot (after water, a pit, a fall it shouldn't survive)\n- `mover.rigState(extra)` †: the rig state: rig.update(dt, hero.rigState({ attack })): its motion, crouch, sprint and landing, or the playing action's pose; extra is merged over it\n- `mover.update(dt, map, ctl = {}, view)` †: one step. ctl: game.input (needs the actions move, jump, and sprint / crouch if it has them; screen directions turn into ground ones through view, the game's by default) or an intent { move: [dx, dy], sprint, crouch, jumpPressed }\n- `mover.accel = 700`, `mover.airAccel = 260` †: how fast it gets there\n- `mover.action = null` †: the E.Action playing, or null\n- `mover.buffer = .12` †: seconds a jump press is kept\n- `mover.climb = true` †: climbs ledges (true)\n- `mover.climbAny = false` †: climbs any wall face (see the section's header)\n- `mover.climbMax = 82` †: the highest ledge it climbs\n- `mover.climbSpeed = 34` †: its climbing speed\n- `mover.coyote = .1` †: seconds a jump still works after leaving an edge\n- `mover.crouch = false` †: sneaking (a toggle)\n- `mover.exhausted = false` †: out of stamina until a third is back\n- `mover.facing = 0` †: the angle it faces\n- `mover.fell = 0`, `mover.hurt = false`, `mover.inHazard = false` †: what happened this step\n- `mover.gapAssist = true` †: a jump at a gap aims for the far side\n- `mover.glide = false` †: opens a glide in the air (see the header)\n- `mover.glideMin = 24`, `mover.glideSink = 26`, `mover.glideSpeed = 72` †: how high over the ground it opens, how fast it sinks and flies\n- `mover.gliding = false` †: gliding now\n- `mover.hopMax = 48` †: drops up to this deep are hopped down\n- `mover.hurtAt = 72`, `mover.rollAt = 26` †: fall heights that roll and that hurt\n- `mover.jumpSpeed = 220` †: a jump's upward speed\n- `mover.lastSafe = null` †: the last spot it stood safely, for respawn()\n- `mover.lift = 0` †: an updraft's rising speed (a number, or a function of the mover)\n- `mover.run = 85`, `mover.sneak = 32`, `mover.sprintSpeed = 125`, `mover.walk = 50` †: top speeds (world units a second)\n- `mover.sprinting = false` †: sprinting now\n- `mover.stamina` †: what is left (full to start)\n- `mover.staminaMax = 0` †: its stamina (0: none)\n- `mover.staminaRegen = 35` †: refilled a second standing\n- `mover.tire` †: { climb, climbJump, glide, sprint } it costs\n- `mover.turn = 14` †: how fast it turns, radians a second\n- `mover.vault = 14` †: walls up to this high are vaulted at a run\n- `mover.wall = null` †: the face it holds on to (map.wall's), or null\n\n## 17d. Perception\n\n- `new E.Suspicion(o = {}) → suspicion`: new E.Suspicion({ rise, decay, notice, search, alert, forget }): a guard's mind (its fields, below, are its options). Its level rises while it sees the target (faster the more exposed and nearer), decays while it doesn't, and names a state: 'calm', 'notice' (turn and look), 'search' (go to last), 'alert' (chase). An alert guard that loses the target searches for forget seconds. Each step: suspicion.update(dt, seen, target)\n- `suspicion.hear(strength, at)` †: a noise heard (strength 0..1, from E.hears) at a point: at least noticed, never more than searching\n- `suspicion.reset()` †: back to calm\n- `suspicion.update(dt, seen, target)` †: one step: seen is E.sees' result (or true / false), target the body seen. Returns the state\n- `suspicion.alert = 1`, `suspicion.notice = .3`, `suspicion.search = .65` †: the levels where each state starts\n- `suspicion.changed = false` †: the state changed this step\n- `suspicion.decay = .3`, `suspicion.rise = 1.5` †: per second\n- `suspicion.forget = 6` †: seconds an alert guard searches after losing the target\n- `suspicion.last = null` †: where it last saw or heard the target ({ x, y, z }), or null\n- `suspicion.level = 0` †: 0..1.2, how suspicious\n- `suspicion.since = 0` †: seconds since it last saw or heard anything\n- `suspicion.state = 'calm'` †: 'calm' | 'notice' | 'search' | 'alert'\n- `E.hears(lis, noise, map)`: how loud a noise ({ x, y, z, loud: its radius in world units, 80 }) is where listener stands: 1 at the noise, 0 at its radius and beyond, halved when a top of map stands between them (a wall muffles it)\n- `E.sees(obs, tgt, o = {})`: can observer see target? { seen, d (ground distance), angle (observer -> target), exposure: 0..1 }. The target must be within range and inside the cone fov wide (radians) about the observer's facing; exposure is the share of three points on it (its feet, its chest, its head) that the observer's eyes see past every top of o.map, times the target's own exposure (default 1) and .6 when it crouches (it also shows lower). o: { range: 120, fov: 1.6, facing, map, eye }\n\n## 18. Bullets\n\n- `new E.Bullets(game, o = {}) → shots`: new E.Bullets(game, { plane: 'ground' | 'side', max: 800, sparks: true }); game: its game\n- `shots.burst(base, vels)`: fire several at once from a pattern list of [vx, v2] (v2 = vy on the ground plane, vz in side view)\n- `shots.clear(team)`: remove every bullet, or only one team's\n- `shots.draw(r)`: queue them for drawing (glowing cores, or their sprites)\n- `shots.fire(b)`: fire one bullet. Fields: x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, r, dmg, life, team, color, core, sprite, pierce, grav, data\n- `shots.hit(targets, fn, team)`: test bullets of `team` against targets (objects with x, y, z and r, or w/h in side view). fn(bullet, target). Returns hits\n- `shots.update(dt, map)`: move them one step; with a map they stop at walls (a TileMap, or a PlatformMap in side views)\n- `shots.list = []`: the live bullets\n- `shots.max`: at most this many at once\n- `shots.plane`: 'ground' (they fly over x and y) or 'side' (x and z)\n- `shots.sparks`: a puff of sparks where one hits a wall\n- `E.pattern`: velocity helpers for bullet patterns. Angles in radians (0 = east / right). Each returns [vx, v2] or a list of them\n- `E.pattern.aim(a, b, speed)`: aim from a to b on the ground plane (x, y); aimSide uses x, z\n- `E.pattern.aimSide(a, b, speed)`: a velocity from a toward b in a side view (x, z)\n- `E.pattern.dir(angle, speed)`: one velocity at an angle\n- `E.pattern.ring(n, speed, offset = 0)`: n bullets in a full circle, rotated by offset\n- `E.pattern.spread(angle, n, arc, speed)`: n bullets fanned across `arc` radians around `angle`\n\n## 19. Spatial hash\n\n- `new E.SpatialHash(cell = 32) → grid`: new E.SpatialHash(cell = 32): buckets things by position, to find what is near cheaply; map: the buckets\n- `grid.add(o, x = o.x, y = o.y)`: put o in the bucket at (x, y)\n- `grid.build(list, xk = 'x', yk = 'y')`: clear, then add every live thing in list (skips o.dead); do it once a step\n- `grid.clear()`: empty it\n- `grid.near(x, y, r, fn)`: call fn(o) for everything in the cells touching the circle (check the exact distance yourself)\n- `grid.query(x, y, r)`: what lies within about r of (x, y), as a list (near() calls a function instead)\n- `grid.cell`: new E.SpatialHash(cell = 32): buckets things by position, to find what is near cheaply. cell: the bucket size in world units; map: the buckets\n\n## 20. Audio\n\n- `E.note(n)`: note name -> frequency: E.note('A4') = 440, E.note('C#5'), E.note('Bb2'). Numbers pass through\n- `E.songs: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory`: ready-made chiptune loops (original compositions). game.audio.music('adventure')\n- `game.audio.applyVolume()`: apply volume, musicVolume, sfxVolume and mute after setting them directly (setVolume and mute call it)\n- `game.audio.at(step, fn)` †: call fn(step) on the update step the music reaches step (counted from its start, on as it loops); the song's change cancels it. Returns { cancel() }\n- `game.audio.bus(name = 'sfx')`: a bus for a sound of the game's own (Web Audio nodes it makes on audio.ctx: a babble voice, a loop, an instrument) to connect to, so it follows the volume, the mute and the player's settings: 'sfx', 'music', 'voice' (its level is voiceVolume) or 'master'. Returns a GainNode, or null where there is no sound (headless, no Web Audio)\n- `game.audio.calibrate()` †: the audio offset's calibration: a click on each beat (100 bpm, the first of a bar higher) as the music; tap(time) on each one heard (an event's timeStamp, or nothing: this update step). After 8 taps, result is the offset to set (ms: settings.audioOffset) and done is true; taps go on refining it. stop() puts the music back\n- `game.audio.define(name, voice)`: add or replace a named sound: audio.define('zap', { wave: 'saw', freq: 1200, to: 200, dur: .2 })\n- `game.audio.duck(on = true)`: quiet the music (to 35%) while on: under a game's own pause, map or dialog screen. The engine also ducks it while game.paused\n- `game.audio.instrument(name, fn)` †: an instrument of the game's own: fn(ctx, bus, time, freq, dur, vol) makes a note's sound (Web Audio, into bus, from time); its name is a wave a music track (wave: 'pluck') or a voice takes\n- `game.audio.layer(track, vol = 1, fade = .5)` †: a track's volume as the song plays, 0..1, faded over fade seconds: layers of one song (the chase's drums in when the guards see you, out when they lose you). track: its index in song.tracks, or its name\n- `game.audio.music(song, o = {})`: Loop a song (or play once with { loop: false }). song = { bpm: 120, steps: 4, tracks: [ { wave: 'square', vol: .2, notes: 'C5 - E5 - G5 - . . | ...' }, one token per step { wave: 'triangle', notes: 'C3 . C3 . G2 . G2 .' }, { wave: 'drums', notes: 'k . h . s . h . k k h . s . h .' } ] } tokens: C4 F#3 Bb2 = note, '-' holds the previous note, '.' = rest, '|' is ignored (bar lines). steps = steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Each track loops on its own length within the song's (len: steps, else its longest track's); a track may have a name, and on: false starts it silent (a layer that audio.layer brings in). THE MUSIC CLOCK: a song starts at the game's time and where it is is game time (audio.pos, headless too: the same steps hear the same beats); the sound follows that clock. o: { loop, sync: true (it pauses with the game, on game.time; else it plays on through a pause, on game.real), fade: seconds (a crossfade from the song playing), restart: true (again from its start, though it is the one playing) }. E.songs has ready-made loops: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory.\n- `game.audio.mute(on = !this.muted)`: toggle (or set) mute; returns the new state\n- `game.audio.onBeat(fn)` †: call fn(beat, pos) on the update step of each beat of whatever music plays (a dancer's step, a light's pulse). Returns { cancel() }\n- `game.audio.pos` †: where the music is, on the game's clock (headless too): { t: seconds since it started, step (from 0, counting on as it loops), beat, bar (four beats), at (the step in its loop), loop (times round), bpm, layers (each track's volume), playing }\n- `game.audio.ready`: true once the browser lets the game make sound\n- `game.audio.setVolume(v)`: set the master volume, 0..1\n- `game.audio.sfx(what, o = {})`: Play a sound effect: a preset name ('jump', 'coin', 'hit', 'explode', ...), a voice object, or a list of voices. opts: { vol: 0..1, pitch: multiplier, vary: random pitch spread (default 0.03), step: a step of the music playing (it sounds on that step of the music clock, scheduled with the music: a rhythm game's calls and claps), pan: -1 (left) .. 1 (right), loop: true (it sounds on until stopped: an engine, rain; through the effects' bus, so it follows the volume and the mute) }. Returns its handle: { stop(fade .03), set({ pan, vol }), playing, pan }: a handle that does nothing when nothing plays (headless, before the first key, an unknown name)\n- `game.audio.sting(song, o = {})` †: play a short song once over the music (a goal's jingle): o.at 'now', 'beat' or 'bar' (on the music's next beat or bar), o.duck (the music's volume under it, .4)\n- `game.audio.stopMusic(fade)`: stop the music (it fades out over fade seconds, .06 by default)\n- `game.audio.tempo(bpm)` †: change the music's tempo as it plays (hurry up!): where it is stays, the steps after come at the new bpm\n- `game.audio.anchors = 0` †: how many times the sound was moved to meet the clock\n- `game.audio.ctx = null`: the Web Audio context, made at the first sound (null before, or where there is none)\n- `game.audio.drift = 0` †: what is heard against the game's clock at the last frame (seconds: the sound follows the clock, so it stays near 0)\n- `game.audio.ducked = false`: true while duck(true) holds the music down\n- `game.audio.failed = null`: why there is no sound (a message), or null\n- `game.audio.musicVolume = .5`, `game.audio.sfxVolume = 1`, `game.audio.voiceVolume = 1`: 0..1 for effects, music and the voice bus (bus('voice'))\n- `game.audio.muted = false`: true while mute() is on\n- `game.audio.offset = 0` †: seconds the music sounds early, for speakers and headphones that lag (settings.audioOffset)\n- `game.audio.presets`: the named sound effects, sfx(name) plays one and define(name, voice) adds one\n- `game.audio.song = null`: the music playing (null for none)\n- `game.audio.volume = .7`: master volume 0..1\n\n## 21b. Backdrops\n\n- `new E.Backdrop(o = {}) → backdrop`: new E.Backdrop('castle-night') (a preset: E.Backdrop.presets) or new E.Backdrop({ sky: [colors top to bottom], sun, moon, stars, layers: [{ kind: 'mountains' | 'hills' | 'forest' | 'city' | 'castle' | 'clouds' | 'sea' | 'fog' | 'stalactites', color, y, height, parallax, seed }] }): a parallax sky and scenery drawn behind a level. sun, moon: { x, y (0..1 of the screen), r, color }\n- `E.Backdrop.presets` †: the preset names, all ten: day, dusk, night, castle-night, city-night, desert, forest, ocean, cave, space\n- `backdrop.draw(r)` †: draw the backdrop first in draw(r), before the level; it scrolls with the camera by each layer's parallax\n- `backdrop.layers` †: its scenery layers, back to front, with their defaults filled in\n- `backdrop.sky` †: the sky's gradient colors, top to bottom\n\n## 21c. Props\n\n- `E.PROPS: torch, candle, candelabra, chandelier, brazier, window, banner, pillar, statue, crate, barrel, chest, pot, sign, fence, lamp, door, tree, pine, bush, flowers, grass, rock, crystal, bones, skull, vines, cobweb, lantern, streetlamp, awning, hydrant, trashcan, firedrum, sandbags, cactus, palm, mushroom` †: the names of the built-in props for r.prop(name, x, y, z, opts) and E.prop(name, opts)\n- `E.prop(name, o = {})`: build (and cache) a prop: { frames: [sprite], glow: [sprite] | null, w, h }\n\n## 21e. Shapes\n\n- `E.SHAPES: barrel, crate, chest, table, chair, stool, bench, bed, bookcase, shelf, pot, jar, sack, cart, ... (25)`: the shapes by id: { about, tags, mats: { slot: material }, parts | make(params), params, foot, lights, flames } (the core library's, the sets' as 'SET:id' from E.addAssets, and your own: E.SHAPES.stool = {...})\n- `E.addAssets(set)`: register a store set (src/assets/sets/*.js: { set, format: 1, credit, sources, materials, shapes, sprites }): its entries are named 'SET:id' (so one set never changes another's, or the core's) and find their own set's materials and shapes first. An id that is already there warns and keeps the first. Returns { shapes: [ids], materials: [ids], sprites: { id: sprite } } (sprites are E.sprite data; a color may be 'material:tone', tone deep | sh | base | lt | hi)\n- `E.materialTile(id, o = {})`: a material's pattern as a tile of pixels, for a 3D renderer's texture: E.materialTile(id, { size: 32 (world units square: it repeats every size; a face pattern's whole face), px: 2 (pixels a unit), lid: false (true: as on a face looking up), curved (as on a turned face), band: 2 (the light: 0 dark .. 4 bright) }) -> { w, h, rgba (Uint8ClampedArray, top row first, v up the tile) }; null for an unknown material\n- `E.shapeBox(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})`: where a placed shape stands, as both engines collide with it: E.shapeBox(id | entry, x, y, z, { facing, scale, flip, params }) -> { x, y (its footprint's middle), z, top, hx, hy (half its footprint, along its own x and y), c, s (the turn's cosine and sine) }; null for an unknown shape. map.solid makes one solid; world.shape gives it a collider\n- `E.shapeInfo(id, o = {})`: what a shape draws, for tools and tests: E.shapeInfo(id, { view: 'iso', facing, scale, mats, params }) -> { w, h, ox, oy, tris, prims (its primitives), parts (each primitive's pixels on screen: 0 is never seen there), colors (distinct), pixels, bounds [x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1], rgba }; null for an unknown shape\n- `E.shapeMesh(id, o = {})`: a shape's triangles, for a 3D renderer (the 3D engine's world.shape): E.shapeMesh(id | entry, { params, mats, seg }) -> { groups: [{ mat, key, lid, curved, face, pos, nrm, uv }], bounds [x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1] } in the entry's own units and frame (z up, its front +y, its own scale applied: place it with the placement's facing, scale and flip). The same parts as r.shape draws; null for an unknown shape\n- `E.shapeMesh({ seg })`: seg(r): the segments round a radius r (default 8 to 24)\n- `E.shapeMesh() → groups`: each one's mat (the material ready to draw: E.material's), key (its id), lid (true: the faces looking up or down, which some patterns draw their own way), curved (turned or round faces: planks run up them), face (a pattern drawn once per face, the crate's sides and bark's ends: uv runs 0 to 1 across each face), pos, nrm, uv (Float32Arrays: three numbers a corner, three corners a triangle; uv two a corner, world units across the surface)\n- `E.shapeSprite(id, o = {})`: a shape as a sprite (E.sprite's kind: r.sprite, px.sprite, inventories, HUDs, portraits): E.shapeSprite(id, { view: 'iso', facing, size (its scale, 1), mats, params, outline: true | a color | false }). The view is a name or a View, as given (r.shape draws steep views from the characters' angle: E.charView(view) gives that); a bag icon is the item's shape seen at an angle. r.sprite stands it on its feet (foot: [x, y] in the sprite) unless an anchor is given\n\n## 21. Ui\n\n- `new E.Dialog(game, o = {}) → talk`: new E.Dialog(game, { speed, lines, place, color, nameColor, voice, width, bg, border, vars }): a dialog box that types its text; say() opens it, update(dt) and draw(r) run it\n- `talk.choiceAt(i)` †: where choice i is on the screen while the choices show: [x, y] (its middle, for a test's tap), else null\n- `talk.close()`: close it now (and forget pending presses, so the same press doesn't also attack)\n- `talk.draw(r)`: draw it (anywhere in draw: it goes to the overlay)\n- `talk.pageLen`: the letters on the page shown\n- `talk.say(text, o = {})`: open with one string or a list of strings (each starts a new page). opts: { name, onDone, choices, onChoice, portrait: rig (a live close-up of that Humanoid beside the text) or a function (g, x, y, size), portraitSize: 40, portraitOpts: { zoom, turn, bg }, auto: seconds a page shows before it turns by itself, modal: false (play goes on while it shows: radio chatter), place: 'top' | 'middle' | 'bottom', y, alpha, vars: the values {$name} reads, voice: this call's (a sound effect, null, or a function per letter) } choices: ['YES', 'NO'], shown on the last page (picked by the keys, or a tap or click on one); onChoice(i) gets the one picked. update(dt) returns true while a modal box is open: return from your update then, and play waits\n- `talk.typing`: true while the page is still typing\n- `talk.update(dt)`: type and turn pages (confirm finishes the line, then turns the page); returns true while open and modal, so gameplay can wait: if (talk.update(dt)) return;\n- `talk.auto`: seconds each page stays after typing\n- `talk.bg = '#141a3a'`, `talk.border = '#e8e4ff'`: the box's colors\n- `talk.choice = 0`: the choice under the cursor\n- `talk.choices`: the answers offered on the last page, or null\n- `talk.color = '#ffffff'`: the text's color\n- `talk.game`: its game\n- `talk.i = 0`: the page shown\n- `talk.lines = 3`: text lines per page\n- `talk.modal`: false lets play go on (radio chatter)\n- `talk.name`: the speaker's name shown on the box\n- `talk.nameColor = '#ffd36a'`: the speaker's name's color\n- `talk.onChoice`: called with the index picked\n- `talk.onDone`: called when the last page closes\n- `talk.open = false`: true while it shows\n- `talk.pages = []`: the text wrapped into pages of lines\n- `talk.place = 'bottom'`: 'bottom' | 'top' | 'middle'\n- `talk.portrait`: the portrait beside the text (a Humanoid, or a function (g, x, y, size)), or null; pSize: its size in pixels; pOpts: its options ({ zoom, turn, bg })\n- `talk.shown = 0`: letters typed so far on it\n- `talk.speed = 50`: letters typed per second\n- `talk.vars = null`: the values {$name} in the text reads (say's vars first)\n- `talk.voice = 'text'`: the sound effect each letter blips ('text'; null for silence), or a function (letter, index, dialog) called once for each letter as it types (a babble voice: Animalese; dialog.name is the speaker)\n- `talk.width = 0`: the box's width in pixels (320 at most by default, the screen's less 12 at most)\n- `new E.Menu(game, items, o = {}) → menu`: Menu with a cursor (title screens, pause menus, shops, battle commands). const menu = new E.Menu(game, ['START', 'OPTIONS', { label: 'CONTINUE', disabled: !save }], { onPick(i, item) {...}, onCancel() {...} }); update: menu.update(dt) draw: menu.draw(r) opts: { x: 'center' | px, y: 'center' | px, title, box: true, color, pick: '#ffd36a', columns: 1 }\n- `menu.draw(r)`: draw it to the overlay\n- `menu.rowAt(i)` †: entry i's box on the screen, [x, y, w, h] in screen pixels: where a click or tap picks it (a test's feed('down') and feed('up') there); null while it is scrolled out of view\n- `menu.selected`: the entry under the cursor\n- `menu.update()`: move the cursor (up, down; held keys repeat; the mouse over an entry) and pick (confirm, or a click or tap on an entry) or cancel; returns true when it picked or cancelled\n- `menu.active = true`: false stops it reading input\n- `menu.bg = '#141a3a'`, `menu.border = '#e8e4ff'`: the window's colors\n- `menu.box = true`: draw a window round it\n- `menu.color = '#ffffff'`: the entries' color\n- `menu.dim = '#6a6488'`: disabled entries' color\n- `menu.game`: its game\n- `menu.i = 0`: the one under the cursor\n- `menu.input = null` †: the one input it reads (default: every player's, so any player moves and picks; the mouse and taps are player 1's)\n- `menu.items`: the entries (strings, or { label, disabled })\n- `menu.onCancel = null`: called on cancel\n- `menu.onMove = null`: called (i, item) when the cursor moves\n- `menu.onPick = null`: called (i, item) on confirm\n- `menu.pick = '#ffd36a'`: the color of the one under the cursor\n- `menu.rows = 0` †: at most that many entries shown, the list scrolling under its cursor (0: all)\n- `menu.scale = 1` †: its text and rows that many times (2, 3: across a room on a TV)\n- `menu.sound = true`: false silences it\n- `menu.title = null`: a heading, or null\n- `menu.x = 'center'`, `menu.y = 'center'`: where it sits, 'center' or pixels\n- `E.ui`: pixel-drawn interface pieces for r.overlay\n- `E.ui.bar(g, x, y, w, h, frac, color = '#e0463c', o = {})`: a bar (a meter: health, magic, stamina, a boss's): ui.bar(g, x, y, w, h, 0..1, '#e0463c')\n- `E.ui.box(g, x, y, w, h, o = {})`: window frame: ui.box(g, x, y, w, h, { bg, border, shadow, gradient }) bg: one color (drawn as a soft top-to-bottom gradient, like Final Fantasy windows) or [top, bottom] colors. gradient: false for a flat fill.\n- `E.ui.counter(g, x, y, label, value, color = '#ffffff', o = {})`: label + value, e.g. ui.counter(g, 4, 4, 'RUPEES', 12)\n- `E.ui.hearts(g, x, y, hp, max, o = {})`: row of hearts: hp and max in hearts (halves allowed, e.g. 2.5 of 3)\n\n## 21d. Shell\n\n- `new E.Shell(game, o = {}) → shell`: new E.Shell(game, { theme, screens: { name: spec }, settings: { items, onChange(settings, key) }, store: 'shell-settings', meters: .125, standard: true }): an HTML interface over the game (see the section's header)\n- `new E.Shell({ theme })`: CSS variables (ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font: a family, titleFont), and scale: the whole interface that many times (a number, or a function of the window) for a TV across a room; tv: true picks one by the window's width (14 px text at 875 px, up to 30 at 1,875)\n- `new E.Shell({ screens })`: specs ui.show(name) starts from (the pause screen follows game.paused; screens: { pause: false } leaves it out)\n- `new E.Shell({ settings })`: settings.items: the game's own settings, { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', min, max, step, options, value } (kept in game.settings, beside the standard ones)\n- `new E.Shell({ store, meters })`: store: where an older version saved them; meters: meters per world unit for distances\n- `new E.Shell({ standard })`: false leaves out the standard menu (the pause screen's Settings, Controls, Full screen and Quit, and the settings' groups)\n- `shell.back()` †: what cancel does: the screen's own back(), or close it (back to the screen under it; closing the pause screen resumes)\n- `shell.dispose()` †: take its elements away and let go of the game (game.dispose() does it)\n- `shell.hide()` †: close every screen (and resume, when the menu paused the game)\n- `shell.hud(st = {})` †: the HUD, from this step's state (call it every step; a piece left out is hidden, and only what changed is redrawn)\n- `shell.hud({ objective })` †: { step: '01 / 04', title, text, at: { x, y, z }, from: hero } (the distance in meters, and above or below)\n- `shell.hud({ status, place, tip })` †: status: 'UNSEEN' or { text, color }; place: 'The Old Harbour'; tip: 'text' or { title, text }\n- `shell.hud({ bars, counts })` †: bars: [{ label, value, max, kind: 'bar' | 'pips', color }] (health, stamina, a meter); counts: [[label, value]]\n- `shell.hud({ prompt })` †: { key: 'E' (or act: 'interact': its key on the player's device), label, hint, progress: 0..1 }\n- `shell.hud({ keys, buttons })` †: keys: [['WASD', 'Move'], ...] (hidden on touch screens); buttons: [[label, fn or action]] (top right: pause and map where there is no Escape key)\n- `shell.hud({ minimap })` †: { map, at (with facing), markers: [{ x, y, kind: 'dot' | 'diamond' | 'ring' | 'cross', color }], onClick(x, y) (the world point clicked), fog (an E.Fog: unseen cells black, seen ones dim), camera: true (the ground the screen shows) }\n- `shell.screen` †: the name of the open screen ('pause', 'settings', 'controls', ...), or null while the game plays\n- `shell.settings` †: the player's settings: game.settings (the standard ones and the game's own, by key)\n- `shell.show(name, spec = {})` †: open a screen: 'title' | 'pause' | 'settings' | 'help' | 'results' | 'map' or a name of your own, from spec over o.screens[name]. A spec: { kicker, title, text (strings or functions), actions: [[label, fn] or { label, fn, act, primary, disabled }], back (fn for cancel), rows: ['glyphs', 'invertY', '#A heading'] (settings' rows, changed as on the settings screen) }. From another screen it stacks (back() returns); shown again, it updates (the new spec over the old). 'settings' takes its items\n- `shell.show('title')` †: { name, subtitle, meta: [...], footer }\n- `shell.show('help')` †: { keys: [[key, what]], sections: [[heading, text]] }\n- `shell.show('results')` †: { emblem, stats: [[value, label]], medals: [[label, earned]] }\n- `shell.show('map')` †: { map, at, markers (a list or a function), labels: [[text, x, y]], onPick(x, y) }\n- `shell.toast(title, text = '', seconds = 4)` †: a message that slides in at the top for a few seconds\n- `shell.tutorial(title, text = '', seconds = 15)` †: a tutorial card (bottom left, dismissable) for some seconds; tutorial(null) takes it away\n- `shell.root = null` †: game: the game it is over; o: its options; root: its DOM element (null in a headless game)\n\n## 22. Gpu lighting (webgpu)\n\n- `game.gpu.active(view)` †: true when it lights frames in this view (ready, enabled and a ground view)\n- `game.gpu.snapshot()` †: offscreen mode only: read the last frame back as a PNG data URL\n- `game.gpu.status()` †: 'loading' | 'on' | 'off' | 'unavailable'\n- `game.gpu.adapterInfo` †: the GPU's description from the browser (vendor, architecture), or null\n- `game.gpu.ambient` †: the unlit color, [r, g, b] 0..1\n- `game.gpu.bands = 6` †: how many light bands (fewer is more retro)\n- `game.gpu.enabled = true` †: false switches it off for now (and the canvas lighting with it, unless game.lights.enabled)\n- `game.gpu.failed = null` †: why it couldn't start, or null\n- `game.gpu.fallback = true` †: false keeps a frame it can't draw unlit (by default the canvas lighting stands in, game.lights.on)\n- `game.gpu.glow = .22` †: the strength of px.glow surfaces\n- `game.gpu.map = null` †: the TileMap it measures walls on\n- `game.gpu.offscreen = false` †: render into a texture instead of the page (automated tests; read it with snapshot())\n- `game.gpu.onStatus = null` †: called (gpu) when its status changes\n- `game.gpu.ready = false` †: true once the GPU is set up\n- `game.gpu.shadows = true` †: casters block light\n- `game.gpu.skipped` †: never queue more than two frames: a slow GPU drops frames instead of building up lag\n- `game.gpu.spill = 1.1` †: how far light spills past its radius\n- `game.gpu.wrap = .45` †: how far light wraps round wall corners, 0..1\n\n## 23. Sessions\n\n- `E.session` †: records a play session on any device and replays it exactly (this section's header says how)\n- `E.session.frame` †: the frames run since the page loaded\n- `E.session.record(on)` †: turn recording on or off for the next page loads (each load is a session of its own)\n- `E.session.recording` †: true while this page load is recorded\n- `E.session.replaying` †: true while it is a replay\n- `E.session.wanted()` †: true when recording is on for page loads (record(true))\n\n## Module arcade: the coin-op's frame: scores, high-score tables and initials, lives, credits, turns, cards, the attract mode (engine/modules/arcade.js)\n\n- `new E.Arcade(game, o = {}) → arcade`: new E.Arcade(game, { id, lives: 3, extra: [] (the scores that give a life) or every (a life each that many points), credits: null (free play) or the coins a game takes, idle: 20, demo: 30, scores: 6 (the attract mode's seconds), hud: true, colors: [label, number, highlight] })\n- `arcade.add(points, at)`: add points to the player up (at: a world point { x, y, z } where they float up); an extra life at each threshold\n- `arcade.attract(dt)`: the attract mode, each step on the title: 'title', 'demo' (o.idle seconds without input; it lasts o.demo seconds or until endDemo()), 'scores' (o.scores seconds), then 'title' again; any input goes back to 'title'\n- `arcade.card(text, seconds = 2)`: a card in the middle of the screen for seconds ('STAGE 2', 'PLAYER 2, READY!')\n- `arcade.carding`: is a card up? (hold play while it is)\n- `arcade.clear()`: take every card and word down\n- `arcade.coin()`: add a coin (credits on); returns the credits\n- `arcade.continue(seconds = 10, done)`: a continue: CONTINUE? counts down seconds; start, confirm or a tap continues (with the credits it takes): every player's lives back, the scores kept, done(true); at zero, done(false)\n- `arcade.continuing`: is the continue counting down?\n- `arcade.die()`: a life lost: 'again' (lives left), 'next' (the other player's turn) or 'over' (none left for anyone)\n- `arcade.draw(r, box)`: the frame over the game, after its drawing: the HUD (o.hud: 1UP, HI, 2UP in a row) along the top of box [x, y, w, h] (the playfield; the screen when none), the lives along its foot, the cards, DEMO, the continue and the entry\n- `arcade.drawTable(r, mode = '', y = 40, title = 'HIGH SCORES')`: a table as a screen (the attract mode's, or after a game), its title at y: rank, name, score\n- `arcade.endDemo()`: the demo is over (a demo in a scene of its own: call it on the way back): the attract mode shows the scores\n- `arcade.enter(score, mode = '', done)`: open the initials entry for score; done(name) once it is taken and saved\n- `arcade.qualifies(score, mode = '')`: would score make the top ten (never in a demo)?\n- `arcade.say(text, seconds = 1.2)`: a short word under the cards for seconds ('READY!', 'EXTRA LIFE!')\n- `arcade.start(players = 1, o = {})`: begin a game for 1 or 2 players (taking turns), o: { demo: true } for the attract mode's; spends the credits a game takes unless it is free play or a demo. Returns false without them\n- `arcade.table(mode = '')`: the top ten of a mode: [{ name, score }]\n- `arcade.update(dt)`: a step: the cards count down, the entry and the continue read the keys\n- `arcade.wake()`: the attract mode back to the title, its idle clock at zero (as any input does)\n- `arcade.credits`: coins in (null: free play)\n- `arcade.demo = false`: a demo is playing (no table)\n- `arcade.entry = null`: the initials being entered ({ score, mode, name, i }), or null\n- `arcade.game`: the game it serves\n- `arcade.hi = 0`: the high score shown\n- `arcade.id`: the game's name (its tables')\n- `arcade.mode = 'title'`: 'title' | 'play' | 'over'\n- `arcade.o`: the options\n- `arcade.p = null`: the player up\n- `arcade.phase = 'title'`: the attract mode's, 'title' | 'demo' | 'scores'\n- `arcade.players = []`: [{ n, score, lives, save (the game's own: a stage kept while the other plays), next (the score of the next extra life) }]\n- `arcade.wheel = null`: where the letter wheel was last drawn (taps answer to it): { x, y, cw, up, down, ok } in screen pixels: three columns cw wide from x, the up and down bands [y0, y1], the OK button [x, y, w, h]\n\n## Module click: point-and-click adventures: walk areas with holes, depth, hotspots, verbs, an inventory (engine/modules/click.js)\n\n- `new E.Click(game, o = {}) → click` †: new E.Click(game, { rooms, items, rules, hero, ui, story, room, at, on }): see the manual\n- `click.act(verb, target, item = null)` †: do verb on target (a hotspot's id, or an item's: in the inventory), with item (held) or not: the hero walks there first (a hotspot), then the first rule that matches, else the hotspot's own, else a line. Tests and bots call it\n- `click.at(x, y)` †: what is under a room point: an inventory item never; a hotspot (the nearest-drawn), or null\n- `click.busy` †: true while play waits on it: a fade, a line, a talk, a walk\n- `click.draw(r)` †: the room over the whole screen: its backdrop, bodies and things by depth, the lines, the panel (call after the scene)\n- `click.end()` †: the step's pointer stops changing the canvas's cursor (call on leaving the scene)\n- `click.give(id)` †: add an item\n- `click.go(id, at, now)` †: to room id, the hero at at (or the room's start), with a fade (now: none)\n- `click.has(id)` †: is the item held?\n- `click.restore(s)` †: put back what state() gave\n- `click.say(lines, who)` †: the hero says lines (over his head; a line's time by its length); who: anything with x, y and k to speak instead\n- `click.scaleAt(y)` †: a body's scale where its feet are (the room's depth: [yFar, kFar, yNear, kNear])\n- `click.screen(x, y)` †: a room point on the screen: [x, y] in screen pixels (the room scrolled or centred above the panel)\n- `click.state()` †: what a save keeps: the room, the hero's place, the inventory\n- `click.take(id)` †: take an item away\n- `click.update(dt)` †: read the pointer and keys, walk the hero, run the talk and the hero's line; true while play should wait\n- `click.walkTo(x, y, then, run)` †: walk to (x, y) (the nearest point you can stand on), then(pc) when there; run: twice the speed\n- `click.where(id)` †: where a hotspot is on the screen: [x, y] (its middle; an actor's chest), or null (not in this room, or hidden)\n- `click.boxes = []` †: the panel's and the coin's buttons in the last frame ({ id: 'verb:look' | 'icon:hand' | 'item:rope' | 'coin:use' | 'inv:<', x, y, w, h }: screen pixels, for tests and bots)\n- `click.game` †: the game\n- `click.hero` †: the hero: { x, y, rig, speed, face (radians: 0 right), k (its scale), path, room }\n- `click.inv = []` †: the items held, by id\n- `click.item = null` †: the item on the cursor (or null)\n- `click.items` †: the items by id\n- `click.line = null` †: the line being said ({ lines, i, who }, or null)\n- `click.on` †: its events\n- `click.panel = true` †: false hides the verbs and the inventory, keeping their place (a cutscene)\n- `click.room = null` †: the room's id\n- `click.rooms` †: the rooms by id\n- `click.rules` †: the rules (data)\n- `click.story` †: the E.Story that talks (or null)\n- `click.ui` †: 'verbs' | 'coin' | 'two'\n- `click.verb = 'walk'` †: the verb chosen ('walk' when none)\n- `new E.Walkbox(outline, holes = []) → walkbox` †: new E.Walkbox(outline, holes): polygons of [x, y] (room pixels); holes are places inside it no one stands\n- `walkbox.inside(x, y)` †: is (x, y) in the area and in none of its holes?\n- `walkbox.nearest(x, y)` †: the point you can stand on nearest (x, y): itself when inside, else the nearest on an edge (a pixel in)\n- `walkbox.path(a, b)` †: the shortest way from a to b (b moved to the nearest point inside): [a, ..., b], round the holes\n- `walkbox.sees(a, b)` †: can one walk straight from a to b? (it crosses no edge, and its middle is inside)\n- `walkbox.edges = []` †: every side of both, as [a, b]\n- `walkbox.holes` †: its holes\n- `walkbox.nodes = []` †: the corners a shortest path turns at: the outline's inward-pointing ones and every hole's outward one, set a pixel off\n- `walkbox.outline` †: the area's edge\n\n## Module drive: cars on the ground plane, a race round a track, the drivers who race it (engine/modules/drive.js)\n\n- `new E.Car(o = {}) → car`: new E.Car({ x, y, angle, top, accel, brake, turn, grip, drift, kart, auto, surfaces, ground }): a car (the manual)\n- `E.Car.collide(cars)`: bump every pair of cars that touch apart (equal weights, a little bounce)\n- `car.boost(sec = .8, power = .4)`: a boost: seconds of it, power (.4: top speed up by that share)\n- `car.spinOut(sec = 1)`: spin out (a shot, a hazard): seconds without control, most of its speed lost\n- `car.update(dt, map, c = {})`: one step: steering, engine, grip, drift, the surface, then E.Body's walls and hop. c: an input or an intent\n- `car.airT = 0`: a hop's seconds in the air\n- `car.angle`: its heading\n- `car.boostT = 0`: a boost's seconds left\n- `car.bumped = false`: it hit a wall or a car this step\n- `car.charge = 0`: a kart's drift boost building (1 blue, 2 orange)\n- `car.drifting = 0`: -1 left, 1 right, 0 not\n- `car.o`: its options\n- `car.offroad = false`: on a surface that slows it\n- `car.onGround = false`, `car.vz`: E.Body's (a hop leaves the ground)\n- `car.slide = 0`: sideways\n- `car.speed = 0`: along it (units a second, - backing up)\n- `car.spinT = 0`: a spin-out's\n- `car.surface = null`: the floor tag under it\n- `car.vx`, `car.vy`: its speed on the ground (E.Body's): along its heading and the slide\n- `new E.Race(o = {}) → race`: new E.Race({ path, laps, checkpoints, countdown, width, items }): a race round a closed path (the manual)\n- `race.add(car, o = {})`: a racer: its car, o.name, o.input (a player's controls) or o.ai ({ skill, rubber, lane }); returns it\n- `race.box(x, y)`: an item box at (x, y): a racer that drives through it gets an item (when it holds none)\n- `race.drive(R, dt)`: a racer's way round: the intent race.update gives an AI racer's car (look ahead along the line, bends, overtakes, unsticking, the rubber band)\n- `race.grid(n)`: the start: n places behind the line, two by two, facing along it: [{ x, y, angle }]\n- `race.hud(r, R, o = {})`: the standard HUD for a racer, in its viewport: place, lap, time, item, the countdown, WRONG WAY, FINISH (o.color; o.right: the x its right-hand text ends before, clear of a corner button)\n- `race.update(dt, map)`: one step: the countdown, every car, bumps, items, progress, laps, wrong way, places, the finish\n- `race.use(R)`: fire a racer's item: boost (now), shot (ahead along its heading), hazard (behind it)\n- `race.boxes = []`, `race.hazards = []`, `race.shots = []`: the items on the track\n- `race.go = false`: racing (the countdown over)\n- `race.o`: its options\n- `race.over = false`: every racer finished\n- `race.path`: the racing line\n- `race.racers = []`: every racer, in the order added\n- `race.results = []`: the racers as they finished\n- `race.t`: seconds since GO (- before it)\n\n## Module fight: one-on-one fighting: fighters with frame data, guards, throws, specials, a meter; rounds; a CPU; training (engine/modules/fight.js)\n\n- `E.FIGHT_KEYS: up, down, left, right, lp, hp, lk, hk, start, pause, confirm, cancel` †: the fighting preset (a scene's input): the moves and menu keys of E.Input.DEFAULT, light and heavy punch (U, I; a pad's X, Y), light and heavy kick (J, K; A, B)\n- `new E.FightAI(o = {}) → fightai` †: new E.FightAI({ level: 1..8 (4), seed }): a CPU for a fighter\n- `fightai.intent(f, foe, dt)` †: what it does this step: an intent for fighter f.update(dt, intent) ({ x, y: -1..1 on screen, press, do })\n- `fightai.level` †: how well it fights (1 to 8)\n- `fightai.react` †: seconds before it sees what its foe does\n- `fightai.rng` †: its own randomness (the seed's: the same match each time)\n- `new E.FightSelect(game, o = {}) → fightselect` †: new E.FightSelect(game, { roster: [{ id, name, rig, about }], inputs: [p1, p2 or null], cpu: [false, true], seed, onDone: ids => {} }): a character select\n- `fightselect.draw(r)` †: draw the roster, the cursors and each side's fighter large\n- `fightselect.update(dt)` †: move the cursors, take the picks; onDone(ids) once both have one\n- `fightselect.at` †: each side's cursor\n- `fightselect.cpu` †: which sides the CPU picks for\n- `fightselect.game` †: the game it runs in\n- `fightselect.inputs` †: each side's E.Input\n- `fightselect.o` †: its options\n- `fightselect.picks = [null, null]` †: each side's id once chosen\n- `fightselect.rects = null` †: where each portrait was drawn ([x, y, w, h] in screen pixels: a tap there picks it)\n- `fightselect.rigs` †: each fighter's portrait rig\n- `fightselect.roster` †: the fighters to pick from\n- `new E.FightStage(x0 = 0, x1 = 520) → fightstage` †: new E.FightStage(x0, x1): a flat floor at z 0\n- `fightstage.cellsTouching()` †: no cells either\n- `fightstage.move(b, dt)` †: move a body one step: the floor and the walls stop it\n- `fightstage.touching()` †: no ladders on a stage\n- `fightstage.x0` †: its left wall\n- `fightstage.x1` †: its right wall\n- `new E.Fighter(game, o = {}) → fighter` †: new E.Fighter(game, { name, x, side: 1 (faces right), rig: Humanoid options (or a Humanoid), life: 1000, walk: 75, back: 60, jump: 390, jumpX: 90, moves, throwBtn: 'hp', throwRange, throwDmg: 120, input })\n- `E.Fighter.exchange(a, b)` †: settle one step between two fighters (E.Match calls it after both update): hits (both may land: a trade), shots against each other and against the fighters, push boxes apart\n- `fighter.draw(r)` †: draw it (and its shots) in the scene: r.actor with its rig\n- `fighter.drawBoxes(r)` †: the boxes drawn (training): hurt green, hit red, push blue\n- `fighter.free` †: true while it can act: on the ground, not stunned, down, thrown or out\n- `fighter.reset(x, side = this.facing)` †: back to the start of a round: at x, facing side (1 right), full life (the meter stays)\n- `fighter.update(dt, ctl = this.input)` †: move one step (once a step, 120 times a second): ctl is its E.Input (this.input by default) or a CPU's intent\n- `fighter.x` †: x and z: the body's feet\n- `fighter.z` †: its feet's height\n- `fighter.act = null` †: the move playing ({ m, hit: it connected, blocked }), or null\n- `fighter.air = false` †: flying from a hit\n- `fighter.blocking = false` †: guarding (holding back as an attack comes, or in block-stun)\n- `fighter.body` †: its E.Platformer (x, z: its feet; w, h: its push box)\n- `fighter.boxes` †: this step's boxes ({ hurt: [head, body, legs], hit: the striking fist or foot's, or null, push }: each { x, z, w, h }, x its left, z its bottom)\n- `fighter.combo = 0` †: the hits it has taken in a row\n- `fighter.crouch = false` †: crouching\n- `fighter.down = 0` †: seconds lying down\n- `fighter.facing` †: 1 right, -1 left\n- `fighter.foe = null` †: the other fighter (E.Match sets it)\n- `fighter.game` †: the game it fights in\n- `fighter.input` †: its E.Input, or null (a CPU passes its intent to update)\n- `fighter.inv = 0` †: seconds invulnerable\n- `fighter.juggle = 0` †: hits taken in the air\n- `fighter.kind = null` †: 'hit' | 'block' (which stun)\n- `fighter.ko = false` †: knocked out\n- `fighter.last = null` †: the frame data of its last move to connect ({ id, startup, active, recovery, adv, on: 'hit' | 'block' })\n- `fighter.life` †: what it has left (o.life at the start)\n- `fighter.meter` †: 0 to 100 a bar (bars: o.bars, 1), kept between rounds\n- `fighter.moves` †: { id: spec }, each spec with its E.Attack (atk) and its frame data (frames: [startup, active, recovery])\n- `fighter.name` †: its name on the HUD\n- `fighter.o` †: its options, with the defaults filled in\n- `fighter.rig` †: its E.Humanoid (drawn and posed from its state every step)\n- `fighter.rise = 0` †: seconds getting up\n- `fighter.shots` †: its projectiles (E.Bullets, the side plane)\n- `fighter.stun = 0` †: seconds of hit- or block-stun left\n- `fighter.thrown = null` †: a throw holding it ({ by, t, dur, dir, dmg }), or null\n- `fighter.win = false` †: in its win pose\n- `new E.Match(game, o = {}) → match` †: new E.Match(game, { fighters: [a, b], ai: [null, null] (E.FightAI each, or null for its input), inputs: [game. input, null], rounds: 2 (wins needed), time: 99, x0: 0, x1: 520, span: 250 (how far apart they may be), camera: true (the game's camera follows them), training: false | { dummy }, onEnd: winner => {} })\n- `match.draw(r)` †: draw the fighters, their shots and the HUD; in training the boxes, the frame data and player 1's inputs\n- `match.startRound()` †: the next round: both back at the middle, full life\n- `match.update(dt)` †: one step: the fighters, their exchange, the clock, the calls and the rounds\n- `match.x` †: the stage's middle\n- `match.ai` †: each one's CPU (E.FightAI), or null\n- `match.call` †: the words on the screen (ROUND 1, FIGHT!, K.O., TIME, PERFECT), or null\n- `match.clock` †: the round's seconds left\n- `match.f` †: the two fighters\n- `match.game` †: the game it runs in\n- `match.inputs` †: each one's E.Input when no CPU plays it\n- `match.o` †: its options, with the defaults filled in\n- `match.phase` †: 'intro' | 'fight' | 'ko' | 'win' | 'over'\n- `match.round = 0` †: the round's number\n- `match.stage` †: the floor and walls they stand in (E.FightStage)\n- `match.winner = null` †: 0 or 1 once known (a round's, then the match's), or null\n- `match.wins = [0, 0]` †: the rounds each has won\n\n## Module gen: generated maps: rooms and corridors, BSP, caves, mazes and scatter, from a seed (engine/modules/gen.js)\n\n- `E.gen`: the map generators (each from a seed: the same rows every time)\n- `E.gen.bsp(w, h, o = {})`: binary space partition: halves split again down to o.min (7) cells, a room in each leaf, sisters joined\n- `E.gen.cave(w, h, o = {})`: caves by cellular automata: o.fill (.45) of the cells rock, o.steps (4) rounds of the 4-5 rule, then the biggest open region alone\n- `E.gen.mark(level, ch, n, o = {})`: put character ch on n floor cells of a level (from a generator), at least o.far (4) steps from its start and o.near steps from each other (0), from o.seed; returns the cells and adds them to level.marks\n- `E.gen.maze(w, h, o = {})`: a maze on the odd cells: o.algo 'backtrack' (long halls) or 'prim' (short branches); o.braid 0..1 opens that share of dead ends into loops\n- `E.gen.open(rows)`: every floor cell ('.') of rows, as [x, y]\n- `E.gen.rooms(w, h, o = {})`: rooms placed apart and joined in order by bent corridors: { rows, rooms, start, end, marks }. o: { seed, rooms: 9, min: 4, max: 9 (a room's side, cells) }\n- `E.gen.scatter(w, h, o = {})`: Poisson disc points at least o.r (3) apart within w x h, o.k (30) tries each, where o.ok(x, y) allows (Bridson's): [[x, y], ...] in cells, not whole numbers\n\n## Module grid: square and hex grids: paths, ranges, lines, fields of view, fog, groups, movers and turns (engine/modules/grid.js)\n\n- `new E.Fog(grid) → fog`: new E.Fog(grid): every cell unseen; update() with the viewers each turn\n- `fog.draw(r, T = 16, o = {})`: draw it over the world: unseen cells black, seen ones dimmed (o.dim: .55, o.color), walls' faces covered too; call after the world and before the HUD. T: world units a cell; o.height(cx, cy): a cell's top (a TileMap's height)\n- `fog.seen(c)`: has [cx, cy] been seen (now or before)?\n- `fog.update(viewers)`: what the viewers see now ([{ cx, cy, r }]: each one's field of view) becomes visible; what was visible stays seen\n- `fog.visible(c)`: is [cx, cy] in sight now?\n- `fog.grid`: what it covers\n- `fog.state`: per cell 0 unseen, 1 seen before, 2 visible now (row by row)\n- `new E.Grid(w, h, o = {}) → grid`: new E.Grid(w, h, { hex: false | 'pointy' | 'flat', walkable(cx, cy), cost(cx, cy), height(cx, cy), opaque(cx, cy), diagonal }): w x h cells, every one walkable at cost 1 and height 0 unless the functions say otherwise\n- `E.Grid.from(map, o = {})`: a grid from a TileMap: its walkable cells (o.tops: true stands on the walls' tops at their heights, for tactics)\n- `grid.center(c, T = 16)`: a cell's centre in world units, T a cell across (square: T wide; hex: T across its flats)\n- `grid.cost(c)`: what entering [cx, cy] costs (1 by default)\n- `grid.dist(a, b)`: steps from a to b: Manhattan, Chebyshev (diagonal) or hex steps\n- `grid.face(a, b)`: the way from a looks to b, one of four [dx, dy] (the longer axis; x when they tie): a unit turning to its target\n- `grid.flood(c, same)`: the cells joined to c through neighbours for which same(cx, cy) is true (c's too): a flood fill\n- `grid.fov(c, radius = 8)`: what c sees within radius: a Set of 'cx,cy' (symmetric shadowcasting, Albert Ford's: a sees b exactly when b sees a; walls at the edge of what it sees are in it; hex grids: a line to each cell)\n- `grid.groups(key)`: the groups of neighbouring cells with the same key(cx, cy) (null or undefined: none): matches, Puyo chains, regions. [[cells]], biggest first\n- `grid.height(c)`: a cell's height (0 by default; E.Grid.from(map, { tops: true }): the wall's top)\n- `grid.inside(c)`: is [cx, cy] on the grid?\n- `grid.line(a, b)`: the cells on a line from a to b, both ends included (Bresenham; hex: one step at a time)\n- `grid.neighbours(c)`: the cells next to c on the grid: 4, 8 (diagonal) or 6 (hex), walkable or not\n- `grid.opaque(c)`: does [cx, cy] block sight? (not walkable, by default)\n- `grid.path(a, b, o = {})`: the cheapest way from a to b, [a, ..., b], or [] (A*; ties broken the same way every time). o: { blocked(cx, cy): cells taken (other units), climb, drop (the most a step rises and falls), ends: b may be unwalkable (a target), max: give up past this cost }\n- `grid.reach(a, budget, o = {})`: every cell within budget of a: { cells: [[cx, cy, cost]], has(c), cost(c) (or Infinity), path(c) } (Dijkstra: move ranges). o: { blocked, climb, drop } as path's, and pass(cx, cy): cells it may cross but not stop on (allies)\n- `grid.sees(a, b)`: can a see b: no opaque cell on the line between them (the ends themselves may be)\n- `grid.shape(c, kind = 'diamond', n = 1, dir = [1, 0])`: the cells a shape of effect covers round c (square grids; on the grid only): 'cross' (n each way), 'diamond' (n steps: dist), 'square' (n each way, corners too), 'ring' (exactly n steps), 'line' (n cells from c along dir [dx, dy], not c), 'cone' (n rows along dir, each a cell wider each side)\n- `grid.side(at, facing, from)`: where a blow from cell from lands on a unit at cell at facing [dx, dy]: 'front', 'side' or 'back' (within 45 degrees of its facing, of behind it, or between: a diagonal is a side)\n- `grid.threat(units, o = {})`: what a side can strike on its next turn: units ([{ cx, cy, move, range: [min, max] or n (1), climb, drop }]) each moving within its reach (o.blocked: cells it can't enter) and striking within range of where it stops. { cells: [[cx, cy, n]], count(c), by(c) -> [units] }: a threat map, for an AI's safe cells and a player's warning tint\n- `grid.toCell(x, y, T = 16)`: the cell under world point (x, y), T a cell across (center's inverse)\n- `grid.walk(c)`: can [cx, cy] be walked (on the grid, and walkable)?\n- `grid.diagonal`: square moves of 8 directions\n- `grid.h`, `grid.w`: its size in cells\n- `grid.hex`: false, 'pointy' (offset rows) or 'flat' (offset columns)\n- `new E.GridMover(o = {}) → gridmover`: new E.GridMover({ cx, cy, speed: 6 (cells a second) })\n- `gridmover.place(cx, cy)`: put it on a cell at once, still\n- `gridmover.update(dt, grid, want, o = {})`: a step of dt: want ([dx, dy] or null) is kept until it can be taken. o: { blocked(cx, cy): true where it can't go, push(cx, cy, dx, dy): true when what is in the way moved (a crate), keep: true goes on in the same direction (a maze) }. Returns true when it reached a cell this step\n- `gridmover.arrived = false`: true the step it reaches a cell\n- `gridmover.cx`, `gridmover.cy`: the cell it is in (or leaving)\n- `gridmover.dir = [0, 0]`: [dx, dy] it is moving (or last moved)\n- `gridmover.moving = false`: between cells\n- `gridmover.speed`: cells a second\n- `gridmover.x`, `gridmover.y`: its smooth position in cells (draw it there)\n- `new E.Turns(actors = [], o = {}) → turns`: new E.Turns(actors, { mode: 'speed' | 'ct' | 'phase' | 'atb', sides: ['hero', 'foe'] (phase), fill: 1 (atb: gauge per second at speed 100) }). An actor is any object with speed (100: normal) and, in phase mode, side\n- `turns.add(a)`: add an actor (it starts with an empty gauge)\n- `turns.done(a, cost = 100)`: after an actor acted (ct: cost 100 for a move and an action, 80 or 60 for less; atb: its gauge empties)\n- `turns.gauge(a)`: an actor's gauge 0..1 (atb and ct: how full; speed: its energy)\n- `turns.next()`: the actor whose turn it is (speed: its energy spent; ct: call done(actor, cost) after it acts; phase: each of the side once, then the next side's)\n- `turns.order(n = 5)`: who acts next, n of them, without changing anything (speed and ct: the order ahead; phase: the rest of this side)\n- `turns.ready()`: atb: the actors whose gauges are full, in the order they were added\n- `turns.remove(a)`: take an actor out (it died, it left)\n- `turns.update(dt)`: atb: fill the gauges on game time (dt seconds)\n- `turns.actors`: in the order they were added (ties go to the earlier)\n- `turns.fill`: an atb gauge's fill a second at speed 100\n- `turns.mode`: 'speed', 'ct', 'phase' or 'atb'\n- `turns.phase = null`: the side acting now (phase mode)\n- `turns.sides`: the sides in their order (phase; null: as the actors have them)\n- `E.tint(r, cells, color, alpha = .4, T = 16, height = null)`: tints on cells' tops: E.tint(r, cells ([[cx, cy], ...]), color, alpha = .4, T = 16, height(cx, cy) = 0): a move range, a path, an attack's reach, drawn on the floor (decals) where height is 0; on a raised top (a TileMap wall's) it sorts with the map: over that top, under whoever stands on it, behind what is in front\n\n## Module mode7: a perspective floor drawn a row at a time, a sky that turns with it, everything on it scaled by its distance (engine/modules/mode7.js)\n\n- `new E.Mode7(o = {}) → mode7`: new E.Mode7({ floor, size, outside, wrap, sky, fog, far }): a Mode 7 view (the manual above)\n- `mode7.add(x, y, z, fn)`: gather a billboard: fn(g, sx, sy, k) draws it with its foot at (sx, sy), k screen pixels per unit\n- `mode7.begin(rect = {}, cam = this.cam)`: set up the projection for a camera (default this.cam) and a rectangle of the screen ({ W, H, x, y, cx, cy }: its size, its top left and its centre; focal: screen pixels per unit at a distance of one, instead of the fov)\n- `mode7.chase(b, o = {})`: the camera behind a body each step: back units behind it, h high, looking along its angle (o.angle to set one)\n- `mode7.draw(r, cam = this.cam)`: draw the view as an overlay over r's frame (in a split screen, r.viewport's part): sky, floor, fog, then the billboards far to near (gathered with add, rig, sprite; they are used up)\n- `mode7.drawFloor(g, vp)`: draw the floor below the horizon in the rectangle ({ x, y, w, h }), a row at a time, and the fog over it\n- `mode7.drawSky(g, r, vp)`: the sky above the horizon: a Backdrop turned with the heading, or a gradient of two colors\n- `mode7.fullRes(game, on)`: draw at the screen's own pixels (on, called each step: sharper, slower) or the game's again (off); returns the pixel size the game had (1 when it had no other)\n- `mode7.project(x, y, z = 0)`: a world point -> [screen x, screen y, screen pixels per unit there], or null at or behind the camera\n- `mode7.refresh()`: forget the floor's baked texture (after changing the map or the function: it bakes again)\n- `mode7.rig(rig, x, y, z = 0)`: gather a rig, drawn by the engine at the size its distance gives, from the camera's side (it faces rig.facing)\n- `mode7.rowDepth(sy)`: a screen row's floor distance ahead of the camera, or 0 at or above the horizon\n- `mode7.shape(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})`: gather one of the engine's shapes (r.shape's: 'barrel', 'tree', ...; the full engine), seen from the camera's side at its distance's size: o.facing (radians), o.size (1), o.params and o.mats (as r.shape's: a door's angle, a crate's wood)\n- `mode7.sprite(spr, x, y, z = 0, o = {})`: gather a sprite (E.sprite, or E.anim's frames at o.t, the game's time by default), its foot at the point: o.scale (world units a sprite pixel, 1), o.flip\n- `mode7.toFloor(sx, sy)`: a screen point -> the floor point under it [x, y], or null above the horizon\n- `mode7.view(k)`: the engine view a rig is drawn with at k pixels per unit, seen from the camera's heading (sixteenths, reused) and pitch (whole degrees: a bobbing camera makes no new view each frame)\n- `mode7.cam`: the camera ({ x, y, h, angle, pitch, fov })\n- `mode7.horizon = 0`: the screen row of the horizon in the last drawing\n- `mode7.items = []`: the billboards gathered for the next draw\n- `mode7.o`: its options\n\n## Module physics: 2D rigid bodies: circles, boxes, polygons, segments; fast balls that never pass through (engine/modules/physics.js)\n\n- `new E.Physics(o = {}) → physics`: new E.Physics({ gravity, substeps, iterations, sleep, on, cell }): a world of bodies (see the manual)\n- `physics.add(d)`: a body from its definition (see the manual)\n- `physics.arc(x, y, r, a0, a1, o = {})`: a curved rail: a chain round (x, y) at radius r from angle a0 to a1 (radians; o.n segments)\n- `physics.box(x, y, w, h, o)`: a box w x h about its middle (dynamic unless o.type says)\n- `physics.bumper(x, y, r, o = {})`: a bumper: a static round post that kicks a ball away (kick: pixels a second)\n- `physics.chain(points, o = {})`: static segments through the points (o.closed: back to the first); returns them\n- `physics.circle(x, y, r, o)`: a circle (dynamic unless o.type says)\n- `physics.draw(g, to = (x, y) => [x, y], color = '#7cf2a0')`: outline every body into g (to(x, y) -> [sx, sy]: the plane on the screen; its identity by default)\n- `physics.flipper(x, y, o = {})`: a flipper: a kinematic tapered bar from its pivot (x, y); flip(on) swings it up or back (see the manual)\n- `physics.hash()`: a number for the world now: every body's place, angle and speed (a proof's hash)\n- `physics.kicker(x, y, o = {})`: a kicker: a sensor that catches a ball, holds it, then fires it (a saucer, a kickback); k.catch.armed\n- `physics.poly(x, y, points, o)`: a convex polygon, its points round x, y (dynamic unless o.type says)\n- `physics.query(x, y)`: the bodies over a point\n- `physics.ray(a, b, mask = 0xffff)`: the nearest body along the line from a to b (sensors aside; mask: the layers it sees): { body, x, y, t } or null\n- `physics.remove(b)`: take a body out\n- `physics.segment(a, b, o)`: a static segment from a to b (o.r: its thickness's half)\n- `physics.slingshot(a, b, o = {})`: a slingshot: a static segment that kicks a ball off it\n- `physics.step(dt)`: advance by dt seconds (from the game's fixed step), in substeps\n- `physics.wake()`: wake every body (after a change to gravity, say)\n- `physics.bodies = []`: every body in the order added\n- `physics.contacts = []`: the last substep's ({ a, b, nx, ny, pts, fresh })\n- `physics.gravity`: [gx, gy], pixels a second a second\n- `physics.iterations`: the solver's passes\n- `physics.on`: its events\n- `physics.sleep`: bodies at rest sleep\n- `physics.substeps`: steps per step(dt)\n\n## Module rhythm: charts on a song's steps, judged on the music clock: combos, grades, holds, a highway (engine/modules/rhythm.js)\n\n- `new E.Rhythm(game, o = {}) → rhythm` †: new E.Rhythm(game, { song, chart, lanes, windows, life, lead, voice, on }): see the manual\n- `rhythm.accuracy` †: how well so far, 0..1: a perfect counts whole, a great .7, a good .4\n- `rhythm.ahead(call = false)` †: the notes to show now: [{ note, dt, dtEnd }] (seconds to the note's time and to its end; a little past the line for the missed, the held while held); call: the calls instead\n- `rhythm.draw(r, o = {})` †: draw the highway on the overlay (see the manual)\n- `rhythm.grade` †: the grade of the accuracy: 'S' (95%), 'A' (85), 'B' (70), 'C' (50), 'D'\n- `rhythm.hit(lane)` †: a press in a lane: judged against the nearest note there within the good window (a hold starts held)\n- `rhythm.release(lane)` †: a lane let go: a hold let go before its end (less the good window) drops\n- `rhythm.reset()` †: back to the start: no judgments, a full combo of nothing, life as given\n- `rhythm.start()` †: play the song from its start (synced: it pauses with the game) and judge from here\n- `rhythm.total` †: the notes to play (not the calls)\n- `rhythm.update()` †: each step, after the input: the lanes' actions pressed and let go, calls sounded, notes missed, holds kept, the end\n- `rhythm.combo = 0` †: the notes hit in a row\n- `rhythm.counts` †: { perfect, great, good, miss }\n- `rhythm.done = false` †: the song is over, or failed\n- `rhythm.down` †: each lane held now\n- `rhythm.failed = false` †: the life ran out\n- `rhythm.game` †: its game\n- `rhythm.lanes` †: the actions, one a lane\n- `rhythm.last = null` †: the last judgment { j, lane, ms, t }\n- `rhythm.lead` †: seconds of notes shown ahead\n- `rhythm.life` †: the gauge 0..1\n- `rhythm.maxCombo = 0` †: the longest combo\n- `rhythm.notes` †: every note of the chart in time order ({ lane, step, end, call, j: its judgment or null, ms })\n- `rhythm.on` †: { judge, fail, done }\n- `rhythm.score = 0` †: the points\n- `rhythm.song` †: the song charted\n- `rhythm.started = false` †: start() was called\n- `rhythm.voice` †: the calls' sound\n- `rhythm.windows` †: { perfect, great, good } in ms either side of a note\n\n## Module road: the pseudo-3D road: segments with curves and hills, lanes, rumble strips, roadside scenery, tunnels (engine/modules/road.js)\n\n- `new E.Road(o = {}) → road`: new E.Road({ segment, width, lanes, rumble, draw, fov, height, colors, fog, sky }): a road (the manual above)\n- `road.add(n, o = {})`: n segments more, bending (curve) and climbing (hill: units up, - down), eased in and out; o.tunnel, o.colors\n- `road.ahead(h = this.o.height, r)`: how far past the camera the player's car stands when drawn at the screen's bottom, for a camera h over the road; square, that depends on the screen's shape: give r (draw's), and draw the camera that far behind the car (keep the car's own place along the road as it was: the game plays the same on any screen)\n- `road.curveAt(z)`: how hard the road bends at a distance\n- `road.draw(r, v = {})`: draw the road over r's frame (an overlay; r.viewport's part in a split screen): { z, x, h, turn, cars }\n- `road.hits(z, x, w)`: the scenery a body w half roads wide at (z, x) touches\n- `road.place(z, offset, what, scale = 8)`: scenery at distance z, offset across: what = an E.sprite or E.anim (scale: world units a pixel, 8) or { draw(g, sx, sy, scale), w }\n- `road.project(z, x, y = 0)`: a point (distance z, across x in half roads, height y over the road) -> [sx, sy, scale] for the camera last drawn, or null behind it\n- `road.seg(z)`: the segment at a distance (it wraps)\n- `road.yAt(z)`: the road's height at a distance\n- `road.depth`: the camera's depth (1 / tan(fov / 2): a unit that far away fills half the screen's width)\n- `road.length = 0`: the lap in world units\n- `road.o`: its options (colors over the defaults)\n- `road.segs = []`: its segments ({ i, z, y0, y1, curve, tunnel, colors, things })\n\n## Module rooms: a world of rooms joined at their edges, the map of what was explored, gates and saves (engine/modules/rooms.js)\n\n- `new E.World(game, o = {}) → world`: new E.World(game, { id, screen: [20, 12], view: 'side' | 'top', legend, types, rooms, start, transition: 'slide', make }): a world of rooms (the manual above)\n- `world.cells()`: every cell of the world grid that a room holds: [{ gx, gy, room, seen }]\n- `world.clear()`: forget the save\n- `world.draw(r)`: draw the room's map (the kit draws its actors after)\n- `world.drawMap(g, x, y, cell = 6, o = {})`: draw the explored map with its top-left cell at x, y, each cell a square of cell pixels: the seen cells (o.all: every room's), walls where rooms end with gaps at their doors, save rooms S and the boss's B, the hero's cell blinking; o: { all, color, wall, save, boss }\n- `world.drawOver(r)`: draw the transition over everything (last): the old screen sliding away, or a fade through black\n- `world.enter(id)`: go to a room by id (the camera's bounds become its map's); returns the room\n- `world.give(name)`: an ability or a key got ('dash', 'key:red'): its gates open in every room\n- `world.has(name)`: true once it has an ability or a key\n- `world.load()`: go back to the save (its room entered, what was got and seen restored): { room, x, z, got, seen, extra }, or null\n- `world.map`: the room's map\n- `world.roomAt(gx, gy)`: the room that holds world cell (gx, gy), or null\n- `world.save(b, extra)`: keep a save: the room, the hero's place, what was got and seen, and extra (anything JSON)\n- `world.shellMap()`: the explored map for E.Shell's minimap: game.shell.hud({ minimap: world.shellMap() }) (a cell a square, the save and boss rooms marked, the hero's cell)\n- `world.update(dt, b)`: each step, after the hero moves: the cell it is in marked seen; off an edge into the next room, the room changes ('enter' that step), off one with no room past it, it stops as at a wall; the transition plays on\n- `world.defs`: the rooms' definitions by id\n- `world.game`: its game\n- `world.got`: the abilities and keys it has\n- `world.grid`: the world grid's extent in cells { x0, y0, w, h }\n- `world.here = null`: its cell [gx, gy]\n- `world.id`: its name (its saves')\n- `world.moving = false`: a transition is playing\n- `world.o`: its options\n- `world.room = null`: the room it is in\n- `world.rooms`: every room by id: { id, def, map, at, w, h } (its size in screens)\n- `world.seen`: the world grid's cells the hero has been in ('gx,gy')\n\n## Module rpg: items, inventories, equipment, shops, a party's stats and levels, status, damage, encounters (engine/modules/rpg.js)\n\n- `new E.RPG(o = {}) → rpg`: new E.RPG({ items, monsters, status, curve }): the rules of a role-playing game (the manual above)\n- `rpg.card(g, x, y, m, o = {})`: a member's card: o.portrait (a Humanoid) beside its name, level, HP and MP bars, its stats and effects; → { w, h }\n- `rpg.damage(a, b, o = {})`: a hit: { hp, crit } (a's attack, or its magic with kind 'mag', less half b's defense or magic; a tenth either way; a critical hit, physical only, ignores armor; guard halves it)\n- `rpg.encounters(zones, o)`: random encounters by zone: { zone: { every: [min, max], troops: [[weight, ids]] } }\n- `rpg.icon(id)`: item id's icon: its own sprite, or one for its kind (a flask red for HP, blue for MP)\n- `rpg.inventory(o)`: a bag: { slots: 32 } or { grid: [8, 5] }\n- `rpg.item(id)`: item id's definition, or null\n- `rpg.list(g, x, y, w, rows, o = {})`: a window of rows ({ label, note, icon, off }) with a cursor; o: { cursor, title, top (the first row shown), show (rows shown: 8), bg, rowH }. → { x, y, w, h, row(px, py) }: the row index at a screen point, or -1\n- `rpg.member(o)`: a party member: { name, level, stats, growth, equip, data }\n- `rpg.party(members, o)`: a party: its members and { gold, inv }\n- `rpg.restore(s)`: the party a save held (party.state())\n- `rpg.shop(ids, o)`: a shop selling these item ids ({ sell: .5 }: what it pays back)\n- `rpg.curve`: curve(lv): the experience level lv needs in all (15 x (lv - 1) ^ 2.4 by default)\n- `rpg.items`: the items by id\n- `rpg.monsters`: the monsters by id\n- `rpg.status`: the status effects by name (the defaults and yours)\n\n## Module songs: the songbook: the kits' best songs and more, and jingles, tagged by mood (engine/modules/songs.js)\n\n- `E.Songbook`: the songbook: songs by id, their moods, get(id), find(mood) (see the manual)\n- `E.Songbook.find(mood)`: the ids of the songs of a mood, in the order listed\n- `E.Songbook.get(id)`: a song by id (playable: game.audio.music(E.Songbook.get('town'))), or undefined\n- `E.Songbook.moods`: every mood a song has, in the order of the manual\n- `E.Songbook.songs`: every song by id ({ title, mood: [...], bpm, steps, tracks, loop }; the core's own songs are E.songs')\n\n## Module story: dialogue as data, flags and values, a quest log and emote balloons (engine/modules/story.js)\n\n- `new E.Story(game, o = {}) → story`: new E.Story(game, { people, nodes, vars, on, dialog }): the talk of a game (the manual above)\n- `story.done(id)`: finish a quest (taken now if it wasn't)\n- `story.draw(r)`: draw the balloons over their targets and the box (anywhere in draw: both go to the overlay)\n- `story.emote(target, kind = '!', seconds = 1.5)`: a balloon over target (x, y, z; h: its height) for seconds: '!', '?', 'heart', 'dots', 'note'\n- `story.get(key)`: a flag or value (key may be a dotted path into vars)\n- `story.quest(id, title = id, text = '')`: take a quest (or change its title and text): it joins the log\n- `story.restore(s)`: put back what state() gave (a load)\n- `story.resume()`: after an event with wait: true, go on (to the choice's goto, or the next step)\n- `story.run(id, o = {})`: start node id (stopping any other); o.onDone(story) when it ends\n- `story.set(key, value = true)`: set a flag (value true) or a value\n- `story.state()`: its vars and quests, as JSON (for a save)\n- `story.stop()`: stop the node running (its box closes; onDone isn't called)\n- `story.test(c)`: does a condition hold? (the manual's conditions)\n- `story.update(dt)`: run the talk and the balloons; true while a node runs (play waits)\n- `story.auto = 0`: seconds a page shows before it turns by itself (0: it waits for the player)\n- `story.game`: its game\n- `story.log = []`: what was said and chosen, oldest first ({ who, name, text }, the last 200: a visual novel's backlog)\n- `story.node = null`: its id\n- `story.nodes`: the conversations by id (lists of steps)\n- `story.on`: the game's event handlers by name\n- `story.people`: who speaks, by id ({ name, portrait, at })\n- `story.quests = []`: the quest log, in the order taken ({ id, title, text, done })\n- `story.running = false`: a node runs\n- `story.skip = false`: pages type at once and turn at once (choices still wait)\n- `story.talk`: the E.Dialog it speaks through\n- `story.vars`: every flag and value (a line's {$name} reads them)\n\n## Module strategy: real-time strategy and simulation: selection, orders, armies, production, resources, placement, fog, AI; a day clock, needs, overlays (engine/modules/strategy.js)\n\n- `new E.Clock(o = {}) → clock` †: new E.Clock({ day: 240 (real seconds of game time a day), hour: 8 (to start at) })\n- `clock.hour` †: the hour (0..23)\n- `clock.label()` †: 'DAY 2 14:30'\n- `clock.light()` †: the light by the hour: { ambient (.12 at night .. 1 at noon), color (the sky's tint: blue night, warm dawn and dusk) }\n- `clock.minute` †: the minute (0..59)\n- `clock.night` †: true from 20:00 to 6:00\n- `clock.update(dt)` †: run it by dt seconds of game time\n- `clock.day = 1` †: the day (1, 2, ...)\n- `clock.length` †: seconds a day\n- `clock.t` †: hours since the first midnight\n- `new E.Strategy(game, o = {}) → strategy` †: new E.Strategy(game, { map, kinds, sides, player, fog, on }): see the manual above\n- `strategy.afford(side, cost)` †: can side pay cost ({ gold: 50 })?\n- `strategy.ai(side, plan)` †: run side by plan ({ build: [[kind, n]], waves: { first, every, min }, script }): see the manual\n- `strategy.canPlace(kind, cx, cy, by = null)` †: can a building of kind stand on cells cx, cy (its corner)? On the map, walkable, no unit there (but by, its builder), seen by the player (when it is theirs)\n- `strategy.cancel(b)` †: take back the last in building's queue (refunded)\n- `strategy.draw(r)` †: after the world: a ring under each selected unit, health bars over the selected and the hurt, the box being drawn\n- `strategy.ghost(r, kind, cx, cy)` †: the placement ghost: kind's cells at cx, cy, green where it fits, red where it doesn't\n- `strategy.input(o = {})` †: the player's pointer and keys, each step (see the manual): selection, groups, orders. o.order: the action that orders (Mouse2 by default where it is bound); o.at(sx, sy) -> the world point under a screen point (map.pick's, by default)\n- `strategy.minimap(colors = {})` †: E.Shell's minimap spec: the map, the fog, the camera's view, the units seen as dots by side, a click moving the camera\n- `strategy.of(side, kind)` †: the side's standing units of a kind (or all of them, without kind)\n- `strategy.order(units, kind, target, o = {})` †: give units an order: kind 'move' | 'attack' | 'amove' | 'gather' | 'build' | 'patrol' | 'stop', target ({ x, y }, a unit, or { kind, cx, cy } to build), o.queue: after the orders they have\n- `strategy.remove(u)` †: take u off the map (it died, a mine ran out)\n- `strategy.side(id)` †: a side by id: { id, res: { gold: 300 }, ... }\n- `strategy.smart(units, x, y, target, o = {})` †: the right click: attack a foe at the point, gather a resource, else move (o.queue as order's); a building that makes units takes the point as its rally\n- `strategy.spawn(kind, side, x, y, o = {})` †: make a unit, building or resource of kind for side at world x, y (o.built: false leaves a building to be built)\n- `strategy.state()` †: what a proof hashes: every unit's kind, side, cell and HP, each side's resources\n- `strategy.train(b, kind)` †: queue kind at building (paid now): it comes out after its time, toward building.rally. False when it can't\n- `strategy.update(dt)` †: run the orders, production, fights, the fog and the AIs by dt seconds\n- `strategy.visible(u)` †: can the player see u? (the fog: any of its cells in sight; theirs always)\n- `strategy.fog` †: an E.Fog of the player's sight (walls block it, buildings don't), or null with fog: false\n- `strategy.game` †: the game it runs in\n- `strategy.groups` †: control groups: groups[1..9], kept with Ctrl and a digit\n- `strategy.kinds` †: the kinds by id (units, buildings, resources: see the manual)\n- `strategy.map` †: the TileMap it stands on (a building blocks its cells)\n- `strategy.on` †: its events: { attack, death, made, built, gathered, ordered }\n- `strategy.player` †: the side the player commands (its fog, selection, input)\n- `strategy.selected = []` †: the player's selection\n- `strategy.time = 0` †: seconds run (rts.update's dt summed)\n- `strategy.units = []` †: everything spawned (units, buildings, resources) that still stands\n- `E.choose(agent, options)` †: the option that best meets an agent's needs ({ gives: { hunger: -.6 }, x, y, cost }): each need it lowers, weighted by that need squared (the urgent first), less the walk (a cell's worth per 400 units) and cost against agent.money; null when none helps\n- `E.needs(agent, rates, dt)` †: raise an agent's needs (agent.needs: { hunger: .2, fun: .5 }, each 0..1) by rates ({ hunger: .01 }) a second\n- `E.overlay(r, map, value, colors = ['#2050c0', '#e0e040', '#e02020'], alpha = .45)` †: tint each cell by value(cx, cy) (0..1, or null for none) along colors (cold to hot): land value, crowding, mood\n- `E.tooltip(r, x, y, lines)` †: a box of lines (text, or [text, color]) beside a screen point (the pointer), kept on the screen\n\n## Module terrain: destructible ground in the side plane: carved and added, walked on, drawn in chunks (engine/modules/terrain.js)\n\n- `new E.Terrain(o = {}) → terrain`: new E.Terrain({ w, h, x, y, materials, chunk }): see the manual\n- `terrain.add(x, y, r, id)`: fill a circle with a material\n- `terrain.addPoly(points, id)`: a material inside a polygon of world points\n- `terrain.carve(x, y, r, o = {})`: a crater: air within r of (x, y) but for hard materials; o.particles: bits of what it took fly out; returns { id: cells taken }\n- `terrain.carvePoly(points)`: air inside a polygon of world points (hard materials stay)\n- `terrain.collider()`: a field for the physics module: world.add(land.collider())\n- `terrain.draw(r)`: draw it as decals under the bodies, in the side view (its y is the engine's z); only changed chunks are redrawn\n- `terrain.fill(fn)`: set every cell from fn(x, y) (the world point at the cell's middle) -> a material id or 0\n- `terrain.get(x, y)`: the material at a world point (0: air, and outside)\n- `terrain.ground(x, y)`: the top of the first solid ground at or below (x, y) (a world y), or null when there is none\n- `terrain.hit(x, y, r)`: a circle in the ground: [nx, ny, depth] (out along the normal by depth clears it), or null\n- `terrain.normal(x, y, r = 4)`: the ground's normal near (x, y): [nx, ny], pointing away from the solid within r\n- `terrain.ray(x0, y0, x1, y1)`: the first solid cell on the line from (x0, y0) to (x1, y1): [x, y] (the last air before it), or null\n- `terrain.set(x, y, id)`: set the cell at a world point\n- `terrain.solid(x, y)`: is the point in the ground?\n- `terrain.walk(b, dt, o = {})`: a walker's step: up and down slopes of up to climb, stopped by walls, falling and landing (see the manual)\n- `terrain.cells`: the mask, row by row from the top (0: air)\n- `terrain.chunk`: the drawn squares' size\n- `terrain.drawn = 0`: how many chunks the last draw painted again\n- `terrain.h`, `terrain.w`: its size in cells (world units)\n- `terrain.materials`: their looks (a material's id is its index + 1)\n- `terrain.x`, `terrain.y`: its bottom left corner\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/docs/ASSETS.md && cat vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/platformer.js'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/docs/ASSETS.md && cat vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/platformer.js'","aggregated_output":"# Objects and materials as data\n\nThe full engine draws still objects (barrels, tables, trees, wells) from a few lines of data, in every view and facing,\nwith the house shading and outline, and gives every surface (floors, wall faces, wall tops, platform tiles, the objects'\nown faces) a material from one open registry. An object costs one to a few hundred tokens of data (the core's median is\n125); you check it headless with one command. Read this card whole before you write one. (The agent edition keeps the\nregistries and ignores the calls: `E.addAssets` returns empty lists, the others null.)\n\n## Use one\n\n```js\n// in draw(r): feet on the point, depth sorted like an actor; its front toward facing\nr.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing: Math.PI / 2 });\n// once, before the updates: bodies stop at it, a body coming down lands on it, one wholly below passes under\nmap.solid('table', 120, 80, 0, { facing: 0 });\n// a sprite of it, for an inventory or a HUD (r.sprite, px.sprite)\nconst icon = E.shapeSprite('chest', { view: 'iso' });\n```\n\n- **r.shape options:** facing (radians, where the front points: 0 east, π/2 south, as written), scale, mats ({ slot:\n  material }), flip (mirrors it x to -x, collision too), params (a generator's: { h: 30 }), bias, hides (default true),\n  light (adds its lights to game.lights), shadow (default true).\n- **map.solid** takes the same id and placement, so one object can feed both calls. Bodies span z to z + h (E.Body's h, 32).\n- **In 3D** the 3D engine draws the same entries, materials and boxes: `world.shape(id, x, y, z, o)` (its header).\n- **Steep views** (top-down) draw shapes from the characters' lower angle, as rigs are drawn. The light comes from the\n  viewer's upper left in every view.\n- **Glow and fire:** a material with glow: 1 ignores the light and stays bright in the dark; an entry's flames ([[x, y, z,\n  w, h]]) draw the engine's animated flame (r.shape only: a sprite has none). Anything else that moves stays a prop\n  (r.prop) or your own code.\n- **Dressing a generated level:** a scene is a few entries as [id, across, forward] from an anchor (a wall spot, forward\n  into the room); place it whole or not at all, each part on free floor, after the level's own random picks and from a\n  seeded stream of its own, so the level stays as its seed builds it (Emberdeep's `src/emberdeep/53-scenes.js`).\n\n## The core library\n\n**Shapes** (sized for a 26-unit hero; generators take params): barrel, crate, chest, table, chair, stool, bench, bed,\nbookcase, shelf, pot, jar, sack, cart, well, sign, lamppost, anvil; fence { len: 16 }, pine { h: 41, tiers: 4 },\ntree { h: 40, seed: 1 }, bush { r: 7 }, grass { h: 6, n: 6 }, rock { r: 7.5, seed: 2 }, crystal { h: 18, seed: 1 }.\n`r.shape('pine', x, y, 0, { params: { h: 30 } })`; `E.SHAPES.barrel.about` says what each is.\n\n**Materials:** woods oak walnut birch crate plank planks wood; stones stone cobble marble granite sandstone slate rock\nbrick flagstone ruin; metals iron steel gold copper bronze metal rusted; clay glazed porcelain; cloth linen burlap wool velvet leather;\nplants bark needles leaves shrub moss straw hedge grass; ground dirt sand snow mud; water ice crystal flame bone shingle;\nwalls timber plaster; tops speckle plain tiles slab; tiles block bonus ground pipe checker.\n\n## Materials on surfaces\n\n```js\nE.MATERIALS.mossy = { color: '#6a7a5a', pattern: 'stones', size: 6 };   // or the shorthand '#6a7a5a stones size=6'\nconst types = { 1: { h: 32, top: '#6a6280', side: '#4a4360', face: 'cobble', roof: 'slate' } };   // TileMap walls\nconst map = new E.TileMap({ rows, legend, types, floorMat: 'flagstone', floorMats: { water: 'water' } });   // floors\nconst level = new E.PlatformMap({ rows, legend, types: { 1: { kind: 'solid', style: 'block', mat: 'mossy' } } });\n```\n\nfloorMats is keyed by the floor tags of the legend ('~': { floor: 'water' }); either stands in for floorTex.\n\nA material is `{ color, pattern, ...the pattern's numbers, ramp, glow, over }`. Its five tones come from `E.tones(color)`\n(or its own `ramp` of five, dark to light); the light picks a band, the pattern moves it a step, so a material works on\nevery surface and in every palette and recolors with one hex. An unknown name warns with the list of names.\nOverlays, the last on top, each in its own color's tones: `over: [['moss', '#5f7a3a', { up: .6 }], ['cracks']]`; moss on\nwhat looks up, cracks (the material a step darker), dirt near the ground (h 6), rust; each takes amount and size.\n\n| Pattern | Numbers | What |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| none | | the light alone |\n| planks | bw 3, len 14, dir | boards with joints; staves on round parts; dir: 'v' turns them |\n| crate | frame 2, bw 3 | a framed, braced side; planks on its lid |\n| bricks | bw 6, bh 3, mortar | running bond |\n| stones | size 5 | irregular stones (Voronoi), dark joints, a lit rim |\n| tiles | size 4 | square tiles, grout |\n| speck, noise | amount .08; scale 4 | specks; soft patches |\n| foliage | size 3.5 | leaf clumps lit on top |\n| bark | | fissures; rings on a cut end |\n| metal | rivet | a shine where lit; rivets |\n| cloth | fold .9 | weave and folds |\n| glaze | | a glossy shine |\n| thatch, shingles | course; bw | straw courses; roof tiles |\n\nYour own pattern: `E.PATTERNS.waves = (s, m) => Math.sin(s.u / (m.size || 3)) > .8 ? 1 : 0` (s.u, s.v: world units\nacross and up the surface; s.lid, s.curved, s.lit, s.W, s.H; return a step, -1 to +2). A material may also paint one kind\nof surface its own way, per pixel: `face(wx, z, tones, type, cx, cy, fi, nh, h)` or `roof(wx, wy, tones, type)` returning\na color or null, `floor(x, y, tag, view)` returning [r, g, b].\n\n## Write one\n\nUnits: 16 = 1 m = one tile; x east, y south, z up. The origin is the middle of the footprint on the ground; write it\nfacing south (+y). Use whole or half units: a pixel at game size is about 0.7 units.\n\n```js\nE.SHAPES.stool = { about: 'round three-legged stool', tags: 'furniture tavern', mats: { w: 'oak', l: 'walnut' },\n  parts: [['cyl','w',[0,0,8],5,1.5], ['ring',3,3,['box','l',[0,0,0],[1.5,1.5,8],{rot:[0,-8,0]}]]] };\n```\n\nAn entry: `about`, `tags`, `mats` (slot to material; a slot name that is not in mats is a material itself), and `parts`, or\n`make(params)` returning parts with `params` its defaults. Optional: `scale` (times the placement's), `foot: [x0, y0,\nx1, y1]` (the footprint for collision, and for sorting when it stands raised; the bounds by default), `lights: [[x, y,\nz, color, radius]]`, `flames: [[x, y, z, w, h]]`.\n\n| Part | Form | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| box | `['box', m, at, [w, d, h], { rot }]` | at: the bottom middle |\n| cyl | `['cyl', m, at, r, h, { r2, seg, sq }]` | r2: top radius (0: a cone); sq squashes y; seg: 6 for a hexagon |\n| lathe | `['lathe', m, at, [[r, z], ...], { seg }]` | a profile turned about z, bottom to top; [0, z] closes an end |\n| ball | `['ball', m, at, r, { sz }]` | at: its centre; sz below 1 flattens it |\n| prism | `['prism', m, at, [[a, b], ...], h, { axis }]` | an outline pushed h: up z (outline x, y), or centred along y (outline x, z) or x (outline y, z) |\n| wedge | `['wedge', m, at, [w, d, h]]` | h tall at the back, down to the floor at the front |\n| rock | `['rock', m, at, r, { sz, seed, rough }]` | a lumpy, faceted ball; below the floor is not drawn |\n| use | `['use', id, at, { rot, scale, mats, ...params }]` | another shape inside this one |\n| mirror | `['mirror', 'x' \\| 'y' \\| 'xy', part]` | the part and its mirror copies |\n| ring, row | `['ring', n, radius, part]`, `['row', n, [dx, dy, dz], part]` | n copies round z (the part moved out to radius); n copies in a line, centred |\n| group | `['group', at, rot, [parts]]` | parts moved and turned together |\n\nEvery part takes `{ rot }`: a yaw in degrees, or [x, y, z] degrees. Write arrays densely (`[0,0,8]`, not `[0, 0, 8]`):\nspaces cost a third more tokens.\n\n### Worked entries\n\n```js\nbarrel: { about: 'oak barrel, two iron hoops', tags: 'container wood', mats: { w: 'oak', h: 'iron' },\n  parts: [['lathe','w',[0,0,0],[[0,0],[5,0],[6,3],[6.5,8],[6,13],[5,16],[0,16]]], ['row',2,[0,0,9],['cyl','h',[0,0,7.5],6.5,1.5]]] },\ncrate: { about: 'wooden crate, framed and braced', tags: 'container wood', mats: { c: 'crate' }, parts: [['box','c',[0,0,0],[15,15,14]]] },\ntable: { about: 'plank table, four legs', tags: 'furniture tavern', mats: { t: 'oak', l: 'walnut' },\n  parts: [['box','t',[0,0,10],[24,14,2]], ['mirror','xy',['box','l',[10,5,0],[2,2,10]]]] },\nchest: { about: 'oak chest, round lid, iron bands', tags: 'container treasure', mats: { w: 'oak', b: 'iron', l: 'gold' },\n  parts: [['box','w',[0,0,0],[16,10,8]], ['prism','w',[0,0,8],[[-5,0],[5,0],[4,3],[0,5],[-4,3]],16,{axis:'x'}],\n    ['mirror','x',['box','b',[5.5,0,0],[1.5,11,8.5]]], ['box','l',[0,5,5],[3,1,3]]] },\nlamppost: { about: 'iron street lamp, glowing head', tags: 'town light', mats: { m: 'iron', g: 'flame' }, lights: [[0,0,40,'#ffd070',70]],\n  parts: [['cyl','m',[0,0,0],2.5,3], ['cyl','m',[0,0,3],1,34], ['box','g',[0,0,37],[4,4,5]], ['cyl','m',[0,0,42],3.5,3,{r2:0,seg:4}]] },\nfence: { about: 'rail fence, len long (16)', tags: 'village farm', mats: { w: 'oak' }, params: { len: 16 },\n  make: p => [['mirror','x',['box','w',[p.len / 2 - 1,0,0],[2,2,12]]], ['row',2,[0,0,5],['box','w',[0,0,6],[p.len - 2,1,1.5]]]] },\n```\n\nA generator seeds its noise from its params (`E.rng(seed)`), never `Math.random`, so it draws the same pixels everywhere.\n\n## Make one: the recipe\n\n1. **Read this card whole.**\n2. **Find** the nearest entry: `node tools/asset-find.mjs \"tavern table\"` searches the core library and the store's sets\n   (`src/assets/sets/`, about a thousand objects, materials and sprites converted from CC0 libraries, each with where it\n   came from) and `--show set:id` prints one; the Asset Lab (`/asset-lab`) shows them all.\n3. **Copy** it and change its names, parts and materials, or write a new one from the parts above. Give the cue a player\n   knows it by parts of its own (a skep's coils as stacked rings, an anvil's horn, a barrow's open tub dark inside), and\n   keep small parts plain ('none', 'glaze'): a busy pattern on a part a few pixels wide reads as speckle.\n4. **Look:** `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool` (a file that sets `E.SHAPES.stool`, or a store set; a core id\n   alone works too: `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs barrel`). It draws `check-output/asset-sheet/stool.png`: every view and\n   four facings, beside the hero, on a light and a dark floor. It lints what reads badly: too small from above, a part\n   under a pixel, too few tones, low contrast with the floor, its tokens. Read the picture, fix, repeat.\n5. **Use** it: `E.SHAPES.stool = {...}` and `r.shape('stool', ...)`; or a set file\n   (`(window.ASSETS = window.ASSETS || {}).MINE = { set: 'MINE', format: 1, materials, shapes }`) and\n   `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS.MINE)`, which names its entries 'MINE:stool' so they never collide, and returns the names.\n   A store set loads the same way (its script, then `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS['kenney-furniture'])`);\n   `node tools/asset-set.mjs <sets> --ids a,b --name mine --out mine.js` cuts the few a game uses, with what they need\n   (Emberdeep's set dressing loads `src/assets/sets/emberdeep.js`, cut so).\n\n## Limits\n\n- Still objects. Lids that open, swinging signs and held weapons are code (or props) for now.\n- A shape sorts by one point, as props do; a long one (a bed, a cart) may cross a body walking right beside it.\n- Collision is the footprint's box, turned with the shape, and the bounds' height: a table is solid under its top too.\n- Bigger than about 240 pixels on screen, a shape skips the actors' effects (flash, x-ray).\n// The PLATFORMER kit's game (a side-scroller: a night approach to a castle in five zones, a sword, a dash, candles, a 2.5D view; Castlevania, Mega Man X, Klonoa), readable: the starter's shell (src/starter/00-shell.js), then\n// the slice from line 159 (src/starter/20-platformer.js: its header says what it plays and how), then the start (90-start.js), as the\n// single-file kit holds them between GAME START and GAME END. platformer.html plays it; node kits/test.mjs platformer runs it headless.\n/* ============================== GAME START ==============================\n * MY-3D2DGE STARTER: a title menu and vertical slices, one per genre (a genre kit file holds one; the file with every\n * slice adds an animation lab). Each slice is a complete small game, written to be copied and changed: its own header\n * (below this shell) says what it plays, its keys and touch controls, its deep links and how it works.\n * FULL SCREEN, ANY SHAPE: res: 'ps1', expand: true. 320 x 240 is the least the screen shows; a wider or taller window\n * shows more world (r.W x r.H grows), so every HUD anchors to r.W and r.H, never to 320 and 240, and every level fills\n * what a wide screen or an upright phone shows. Prompts name the player's own keys: prompt('attack', 'Sword').\n * To make your own game: change the slice closest to your genre into yours, with its own title, play and game-over\n * scenes, and delete this menu and the slices you do not use; this shell (the game, its scenes, prompt, best and the\n * proof) stays unless you replace it. Deep links: #<slice> starts a slice, #<slice>/<part> jumps into a part.\n * ======================================================================== */\n(() => {\n'use strict';\nconst E = My3D2dge, { px, clamp, lerp, approach, TAU } = E;\n\n/* ---- SHELL: one game, several scenes. Each slice switches view, keys and resolution on entry ----\n * expand: the picture fills the window, any shape, with no bars: at least 320 x 240 (a slice's res), more on the long side */\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'side', bg: '#07060d' });\nconst P = game.particles, L = game.lights, A = game.audio;\nconst scenes = {};\nlet gpu = null;                               // WebGPU lighting, used by the adventure slice only\nconst best = (k, v) => { const old = E.store.get('best:' + k, 0); if (v > old) E.store.set('best:' + k, v); return Math.max(old, v); };\n// a prompt in the player's own controls, after rebinding: 'J Sword' on a keyboard, 'X Sword' on a pad, 'ATTACK Sword' (the\n// button's name) on a touch screen, where the buttons sit over the bottom corner: touch() says so, to move a prompt off it\nconst prompt = (a, word) => game.input.label(a) + ' ' + word, touch = () => game.input.lastDevice === 'touch';\n// each slice: its scene's id, its title entry, a line about it, and its family (the title groups them under it)\nconst SLICES = [\n  { id: 'adventure', label: 'ADVENTURE', about: 'TOP-DOWN ACTION ADVENTURE', family: 'ACTION ADVENTURE' },\n  { id: 'pointclick', label: 'POINT-AND-CLICK', about: 'RELIGHT THE LIGHTHOUSE ON GULL ROCK', family: 'ADVENTURE' },\n  { id: 'platformer', label: 'PLATFORMER', about: 'SIDE-SCROLLING PLATFORMER', family: 'PLATFORM' },\n  { id: 'metroidvania', label: 'METROIDVANIA', about: 'A WORLD OF ROOMS, ITS MAP, ITS GATES', family: 'PLATFORM' },\n  { id: 'brawler', label: 'BRAWLER', about: 'SIDE-SCROLLING BEAT-EM-UP', family: 'FIGHTING' },\n  { id: 'fighting', label: 'FIGHTING', about: 'ONE ON ONE, BEST OF THREE', family: 'FIGHTING' },\n  { id: 'shooter', label: 'SHOOTER', about: 'VERTICAL SHOOT-EM-UP', family: 'ARCADE' },\n  { id: 'arcade', label: 'ARCADE', about: 'A CABINET OF THREE CLASSICS', family: 'ARCADE' },\n  { id: 'pinball', label: 'PINBALL', about: 'ONE TABLE: RAMP, MULTIBALL, JACKPOT', family: 'ARCADE' },\n  { id: 'kart', label: 'KART', about: 'MODE 7 KARTS, THREE LAPS', family: 'RACING' },\n  { id: 'road', label: 'ROAD', about: 'A COAST ROAD AGAINST THE CLOCK', family: 'RACING' },\n  { id: 'puzzle', label: 'PUZZLE', about: 'FALLING BLOCKS AND FALLING PAIRS', family: 'PUZZLE' },\n  { id: 'rhythm', label: 'RHYTHM', about: 'STEP UP! FOUR LANES, TWO SONGS', family: 'MUSIC' },\n  { id: 'rpg', label: 'RPG', about: 'A JOURNEY: TOWN, WORLD, CAVE, BATTLES', family: 'RPG' },\n  { id: 'roguelike', label: 'ROGUELIKE', about: 'A CRYPT OF TEN GENERATED FLOORS', family: 'RPG' },\n  { id: 'tactics', label: 'TACTICS', about: 'TAKE THE HILL FORT, TURN BY TURN', family: 'STRATEGY' },\n  { id: 'rts', label: 'RTS', about: 'GATHER GOLD, RAISE AN ARMY, BURN THEIR CAMP', family: 'STRATEGY' },\n  { id: 'artillery', label: 'ARTILLERY', about: 'TWO TEAMS OF THREE ON AN ISLAND THAT BLOWS APART', family: 'STRATEGY' },\n  { id: 'sim', label: 'SIM', about: 'BUILD A THEME PARK, KEEP THEM SMILING', family: 'SIMULATION' },\n  { id: 'stealth', label: 'STEALTH', about: 'ROOFTOP STEALTH AND PARKOUR', family: 'ACTION ADVENTURE' },\n  { id: 'sports', label: 'SPORTS', about: 'TWO AGAINST TWO IN THE CAGE', family: 'SPORTS' },\n  { id: 'animlab', label: 'ANIMATION LAB', about: 'EVERY MOVE, SKIN AND VIEW', family: 'TOOLS' }\n];\n/* the camera: each slice's scene says how the player may zoom it, camera: { zoom: [min, max] | false, start, tall }: the\n   engine zooms it with the mouse wheel, - and =, a pinch on a phone and Settings, Camera zoom (the player's own, kept, on\n   top of the slice's start: tall on a phone held upright). Keys every slice shares besides: V changes the camera view,\n   [ and ] turn it (camera.rotate), 0 resets it (the player's zoom too), G toggles GPU lighting, M mutes */\nconst camOpt = () => (game.scene && game.scene.camera) || {};\naddEventListener('keydown', e => {\n  if (e.repeat) return;\n  if (e.code === 'KeyV') { game.nextView(); game.note(game.view.label.toUpperCase() + ' VIEW'); }\n  else if (e.code === 'Digit0') { game.settings.set('zoom', 1); game.resetCamera(); game.note('CAMERA RESET'); }   // (resetCamera draws it at once)\n  else if ((e.code === 'BracketLeft' || e.code === 'BracketRight') && camOpt().rotate) { game.rotateView(e.code === 'BracketLeft' ? -45 : 45); game.note('TURN ' + game.yaw); }\n  else if (e.code === 'KeyM') A.mute();\n  else if (e.code === 'KeyG' && gpu) gpu.enabled = !gpu.enabled;\n});\n\n/* ---- TITLE: a lineup of HD heroes on a torch-lit castle ledge under a dusk sky. The ledge is wide enough for a wide\n * screen and sits on the screen's bottom edge whatever its height; the logo and menu hang from the top ---- */\nconst MID = 480, stage = new E.PlatformMap({ rows: ['S'.repeat(60), 'S'.repeat(60)], legend: { S: 1 },\n  types: { 1: { style: 'stone', side: '#6a5a78', moss: '#4a6a3a' } }, edges: 'open' });\nconst titleBg = new E.Backdrop('castle-night');\nconst HEROES = [   // six heroes, each dressed with the HD rig options\n  { face: 0, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, build: 'heroic', armor: true, hair: 'short', cape: { len: 6, width: 6, seg: 2.6 }, colors: { cloth: '#3a5ab0', cape: '#b02a3a', capeIn: '#5a1020', hair: '#e0b050', trim: '#e8c860' } }) },\n  { face: 0, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, outfit: 'tunic', hair: 'spiky', hat: { style: 'pointed', color: '#3aa050' }, colors: { cloth: '#3aa050', pants: '#e8dcc0', hair: '#e0b040', trim: '#8a5a2a' } }) },\n  { face: 0, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build: 'bulky', weapon: null, hat: { style: 'band', color: '#e03a3a' }, colors: { cloth: '#f0f0f0', pants: '#2f5fd0' } }) },\n  { face: Math.PI, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, weapon: 'gun', hat: 'cap', sleeves: 'long', colors: { cloth: '#d83a2a', pants: '#2f5fd0', glove: '#f0f0f0' } }) },\n  { face: Math.PI, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, weapon: 'staff', outfit: 'robe', hair: 'ponytail', sleeves: 'long', colors: { cloth: '#6a3ab0', hair: '#c8402a', trim: '#e8c860' } }) },\n  { face: Math.PI, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, build: 'heroic', hood: true, outfit: 'coat', sleeves: 'long', colors: { cloth: '#e8e4dc', coat: '#d8d2c8', trim: '#b02a2a', belt: '#b02a2a', pants: '#4a4a5a', boot: '#3a2e2a' } }) }   // the stealth slice's hooded assassin\n];\nHEROES.forEach((h, i) => { h.x = MID + (i - 2.5) * 35; });\n// the corner's buttons (menu, full screen, the page's ? API) in game pixels: about 170 x 60 CSS pixels at the top right\nconst corner = () => { const S = game.screen, k = S.dpr / S.S; return [170 * k, 60 * k]; };\n// where a HUD's top-right corner ends: left of the corner's menu button while it stays there (a touch screen), else the edge\nconst cornerX = () => { const ca = touch() && game.cornerArea(); return ca && ca.y0 < 40 ? Math.floor(ca.x0) - 4 : game.W; };\n/* ---- THE PROOF (tools/kits-test.mjs runs each slice's twice and compares): proof(id, n, script, state, fresh) enters the\n * slice afresh on a game clock at 0, with Math.random seeded, runs n fixed steps of 1/120 s with script(i, key) pressing\n * keys (key.hold('right'), key.up('right'), key.tap('attack')), and gives a hash of what state() returns: the numbers that\n * say how the run went. fresh(), when given, runs first (seeded): it puts back what the slice keeps between visits. A\n * slice's run(n) is one (__game.scenes.adventure.run(600)): the same number every time ---- */\nconst proof = (id, n, script, state, fresh) => {\n  const was = Math.random, I = game.input, held = new Set();\n  const key = { hold: a => { held.add(a); I.hold(a); }, up: a => { held.delete(a); I.hold(a, false); }, tap: a => I.tap(a) };\n  Math.random = E.rng(7);\n  try {\n    if (fresh) fresh();\n    game.time = 0; game.timeScale = 1; game.go(id, null, { fade: 0 }); game.advance(1 / 120);\n    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { script(i, key); game.advance(1 / 120); }\n    const s = JSON.stringify(state()); let h = 2166136261;\n    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) h = Math.imul(h ^ s.charCodeAt(i), 16777619);\n    return h >>> 0;\n  } finally { for (const a of held) I.hold(a, false); Math.random = was; }\n};\n// a proof's walk: every `every` steps it lets go of the directions and holds the next of `dirs`\nconst walk = (i, key, dirs, every) => { if (i % every) return; for (const d of dirs) key.up(d); key.hold(dirs[(i / every) % dirs.length]); };\n// START picks too (the touch START, a pad's Start), as Enter does: any player's\nconst pickOnStart = m => { const P = game.players; if (P.some(I => I.pressed('start'))) { P.forEach(I => I.consumeAll()); m.onPick(m.i, m.items[m.i]); } };\n/** the title's menu for the slices in this file: grouped by family when there are several (FamilyMenu, in 05-families.js:\n *  the reference editions carry it, a genre kit of one slice doesn't), else a plain E.Menu */\nconst menuOf = (slices, o) => new Set(slices.map(s => s.family)).size > 1 ? new FamilyMenu(slices, o) : new E.Menu(game, slices.map(s => s.label), o);\nlet titleMenu = null;   // (made at the start, once the file's slices are known)\nscenes.title = {\n  view: 'side', views: ['side', 'brawler'], input: 'DEFAULT', res: 'ps1',\n  enter() { A.music('title'); game.resetCamera(); game.follow(null); game.cam.room = null; game.cam.bounds = null; if (gpu) gpu.enabled = false; L.enabled = false; },\n  update(dt) {\n    if (!titleMenu.update(dt)) pickOnStart(titleMenu);\n    game.focus(MID, 0, 32 + (game.H / 2 - 42) / -game.view.bz);   // the ledge's top 42 px above the screen's bottom edge\n    const t = game.time;\n    HEROES.forEach((h, i) => {\n      const u = (t * .45 + i * .37) % 3, swing = u < .5 ? { spec: { a0: 1.4, a1: -1.6, z0: 12, z1: 8, reach: 7.5 }, phase: u < .15 ? 'wind' : 'active', u: u < .15 ? u / .15 : (u - .15) / .35 } : null;\n      h.rig.update(dt, { x: h.x, y: 0, z: 32, facing: h.face, vx: 0, vy: 0, attack: swing, point: i === 3 && u < .6 });\n    });\n  },\n  draw(r) {\n    const [cw, ch] = corner(), top = r.W / 2 + 132 > r.W - cw ? Math.round(ch) : 0;   // the logo drops below the corner's buttons where it would reach them (an upright phone)\n    titleMenu.y = 76 + top;\n    titleBg.draw(r);\n    stage.draw(r);\n    for (const x of [MID - 100, MID + 100]) r.prop('torch', x, 0, 32, { size: 1.3, light: false });\n    for (const x of [MID - 53, MID + 53]) r.prop('banner', x, -8, 64, { size: 1.1, color: '#8a1a2a' });\n    for (const h of HEROES) r.actor(h.x, 0, 32, (g, ox, oy) => h.rig.draw(g, ox, oy, r.view));\n    r.overlay(g => {\n      E.font.title(g, 'my-3D2dge', r.W / 2, 12 + top, { align: 'center', scale: 4, colors: ['#fffbe8', '#ffe08a', '#ffb347', '#e0662a'], depth: 3 });\n      E.font.text(g, 'My \"3D\" 2D Game Engine', r.W / 2, 48 + top, '#e8e0ff', { align: 'center', gradient: ['#ffffff', '#c8b8f0'], shadow: '#140c1c', outline: false });\n      E.font.text(g, SLICES[titleMenu.i].about, r.W / 2, r.H - 14, '#ffe7a8', { align: 'center', shadow: '#000', outline: false });\n    });\n    titleMenu.draw(r);\n    const dev = game.input.lastDevice, how = dev === 'touch' ? 'Tap a game to play it' : dev === 'gamepad' ? 'D-pad + ' + game.input.label('confirm') + '   ' + game.input.label('start') + ' menu'\n      : 'Arrows + Enter or click   V view   - = zoom   M mute';\n    r.text(how, r.W / 2, r.H - 26, '#b8b0d8', { align: 'center', shadow: '#000', outline: false });\n  }\n};\n\n/* ---- GAME OVER: shared by every slice ---- */\nconst overMenu = new E.Menu(game, ['Retry', 'Title'], { y: 118, onPick: i => game.go(i === 0 ? overMenu.from : 'title') });\nconst overBg = new E.Backdrop({ sky: ['#12040c', '#3a0c1c', '#6a1a28'], moon: { x: .5, y: .3, r: 16, color: '#e8a0a0' }, stars: 30,\n  layers: [{ kind: 'mountains', color: '#2a0c16', y: .72, height: .3, parallax: 0 }, { kind: 'fog', color: '#8a2a3a', y: .9, height: .12, parallax: 0, drift: 4 }] });\nscenes.over = {\n  view: 'side', input: 'DEFAULT', res: 'ps1',\n  enter(d) { overMenu.from = d.from; overMenu.i = 0; A.music(null); A.sfx('die'); game.follow(null); game.focus(0, 0, 0); if (gpu) gpu.enabled = false; L.enabled = false; },\n  update(dt) { if (!overMenu.update(dt)) pickOnStart(overMenu); },\n  back() { game.go('title'); return true; },   // a phone's back gesture: to the title, not off the page\n  draw(r) {   // centred on any screen\n    const y = Math.round(r.H / 2) - 60; overMenu.y = y + 58;\n    overBg.draw(r); r.overlay(g => E.font.title(g, 'GAME OVER', r.W / 2, y, { align: 'center', scale: 4, colors: ['#ffe0e0', '#ff7a6a', '#c8202a'], depthColor: '#3a0810' })); overMenu.draw(r);\n  }\n};\n\n/* =====================================================================================\n * SLICE 2  PLATFORMER  (side-scroller in the style of Castlevania: SotN, Mega Man X and Klonoa)\n * A night approach to a castle in five zones (graveyard, bailey, lava moat, rampart above the clouds, gate hall). Space jumps,\n * C dashes, X attacks (the move set is in reset()). Cut down skeletons, bats and knights, break candles for gold and hearts,\n * reach the gate. TOUCH: the stick, JUMP, ATTACK, DASH. V switches to a 2.5D view, - / = zoom. How the look is built:\n *   1. SKIES   three E.Backdrops cross-faded by zone, a painted moon, a castle on its crag, soft mist, a sea of clouds\n *   2. LAYERS  two E.PlatformMaps: BACK (small-brick walls, never solid) and ROWS (big stone blocks you stand on)\n *   3. DRESS   engine props in two depth rows (against the wall, on the path in front), baked decals, an earth cut\n *   4. BLEND   PS1-style additive and half-transparent drawing, never dithered screen-door patterns\n *   5. LIGHT   each zone has a colored darkness; every flame punches a smooth hole in it and adds a colored glow\n *   6. CAST    engine rigs with E.MOVES move sets (a caped hero, rising skeletons, a knight), a hand-drawn bat\n *   7. HITS    game.hitFx (numbers stack in rows), a short tint and a flinch; 'die' topples, skeletons fall apart into bones\n *   8. ANY SCREEN  wider shows more of the level; taller (a phone held upright) lifts it onto a band of SOIL, clear of the\n *              touch buttons, and hangs the skies from the level's foot (FOOT) instead of the screen's\n * ===================================================================================== */\nconst PLAT = (() => {\n  const T = 16, MAXHP = 80, BONE = '#e4dcc4';\n  // 2. LAYERS. PLAY LAYER: a 16x16 tile per character, top row first; letters are spawn points (LEGEND).\n  const ROWS = [\n    '                                                                                                            ',\n    '                                                                                                            ',\n    '                                                                    k                                       ',\n    '                                                      b       c c c                                         ',\n    '                               k                b          HSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSS                                ',\n    '             c                c c                          H                           k c c     k          ',\n    '           c                oooooo       c c      c        H          v                                     ',\n    '            k    k                      ====   c     c     H                                  g       F     ',\n    '           M  v       k              SS                    H k                      SSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSS',\n    '          MM             SS          SS                    H                       SDDDDGDDDDDDGDDDDDDDDGDDD',\n    ' @      sMMM    s        SS   g      SS   s                H    g             s   SSDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDD',\n    '####################SSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSS     m     SSSSSSSSSSSS^^^SSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSS',\n    '####################SSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSLLLLLLLLLLLSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSS'\n  ];\n  const LEGEND = { '#': 1, S: 2, D: 13, M: 14, G: 13, o: 5, '=': 6, H: 7, '^': 8, L: 9,\n    '@': 'hero', c: 'coin', k: 'candle', g: 'knight', s: 'skeleton', b: 'bat', v: 'roost', m: 'lift', F: 'gate' };   // v: a bat roosting upside down\n  // styles shade with tones of `side`; `top` colors tops in 2.5D\n  const TYPES = { 1: { style: 'ground', side: '#4a3a48', top: '#4a7a62', flower: '#c8b8f0' }, 2: { style: 'stone', side: '#8a8298', top: '#5e5a72', moss: '#56704e' },   // soil, castle blocks\n    13: { style: 'stone', side: '#655d78' }, 14: { style: 'stone', side: '#6c7a76', top: '#46604c', moss: '#4f7a4a' },                                    // foundation, mossy ruin\n    5: { kind: 'oneway', style: 'plank', side: '#948aa4' }, 6: { kind: 'oneway', style: 'plank', side: '#8a5a3a' }, 7: { kind: 'ladder', side: '#a07850' },   // balcony, scaffold\n    8: { kind: 'hazard', style: 'spikes', side: '#b8c0d8' }, 9: { kind: 'hazard', style: 'liquid', side: '#c8401a' } };                                  // lava: see lavaDraw()\n  // BACK LAYER, aligned with ROWS: walls behind the play plane (never solid); small bricks behind big blocks is a cheap depth\n  // cue. Letters place engine props (see PROP) or baked decals (W A t g).\n  const BACK = [\n    '                                                        v###                ################################',\n    '                                                        ####                --------------------------------',\n    '                    # # # # # # # # # # # # #           ####                #vq#########h##########h#######Q',\n    '                    -v----------------------v-          ##### # # # # # # # ################N#######N#######',\n    '                    #########N#########N######          ####                ################################',\n    '                    ########FFFFFF############          ####----------------################################',\n    '                    ####W#########W########W##          #W##w  ||  j  ||   e#########T##W####T##W##T#####T##',\n    '                    --------------------------          ####   ||     ||    ####R#########C#######U#######U#',\n    '          eV        ##########################          ####   P|     P|    ################################',\n    '                    ##T#####T#######T####T####          #T##   ||     ||    ################################',\n    'lutgpgicg  xKtigpfflI##########C##A##########I          ####   ||     ||    #Z#A############################',\n    '                                                                                                            ',\n    '                                                                                                            '\n  ];\n  // FRONT: small, darker props on the graveyard path in front of the characters\n  const FRONT = ' , k,m   ,o  ,  k,m ', FP = { ',': { name: 'grass', size: 1.3, color: '#1e3430' }, k: { name: 'skull', size: .8, color: '#8a8478', dx: -5 },\n    m: { name: 'mushroom', size: .45, color: '#4a1a3a' }, o: { name: 'rock', size: .9, color: '#2e2a3a' } };\n  const on = (spawn, tile = 1) => ({ tile, spawn }), back = new E.PlatformMap({ rows: BACK, types: { 1: { kind: 'back', style: 'brick', side: '#6e6890', course: 6 },\n    2: { kind: 'back', style: 'stone', side: '#7e789c' }, 3: { kind: 'back', style: 'stone', side: '#9a94b8' } }, legend: { '#': 1, '-': 2, '|': 3, W: on('glass'), A: on('arch'), v: on('vines'),\n    T: on('torch'), N: on('banner'), C: on('candelabra'), I: on('pillar'), Z: on('brazier'), P: on('torch', 3), R: on('ruin'), F: on('rail'), U: on('statue'), h: on('chandelier'), q: on('web'), Q: on('webR'),\n    t: 'tree', g: 'grave', f: 'fence', ',': 'grass', l: 'lamp', x: 'bones', p: 'ruin', k: 'skull', m: 'mushroom', i: 'offering', V: 'vines', w: 'web', e: 'webR', j: 'lantern',\n    c: 'cross', u: 'urn', K: 'tomb' } });\n  // shapes from the asset store (docs/ASSETS.md): a stone cross, an urn and a sarcophagus, cut from kenney-graveyard into\n  // the page's 'platformer' set (src/assets/sets/platformer.js); r.shape options per BACK tag, y as PROP's\n  const STONES = E.addAssets((window.ASSETS || {}).platformer || { set: 'platformer', shapes: {} }).shapes.length ? {\n    cross: { id: 'platformer:cross', y: -4, scale: 1.1, facing: Math.PI / 2, mats: { a: '#5a566e speck' } },\n    urn: { id: 'platformer:urn-round', y: -2, scale: 1.3, facing: Math.PI / 2 },\n    tomb: { id: 'platformer:crypt-a', y: -5, scale: .9, facing: 0, mats: { a: '#605c74 speck' } } } : {};\n  // 3. DRESS: r.prop options per BACK tag (y < 0: against the wall; top: hangs from its cell; name: a variant).\n  const PROP = { torch: { y: -7 }, banner: { y: -8, color: '#6a1830', emblem: '#d8a040' }, candelabra: { y: -4 }, pillar: { y: -6, color: '#8a84a0' }, ruin: { name: 'pillar', broken: true, y: -5, color: '#7c7892' },\n    statue: { y: -6, color: '#8e8aa4' }, brazier: { y: -4, size: 1.2 }, fence: { y: -3, color: '#34304a' }, grass: { y: -2, color: '#3e6a58' }, lamp: { y: -2, halo: false }, bones: { y: -2, color: '#b8b0a8' },\n    skull: { y: -1, color: '#d8d0c0' }, offering: { name: 'candle', y: -3, size: .5 }, mushroom: { y: -1, color: '#6a3a8a', size: .6 }, vines: { y: -1, top: 1, color: '#3e7048' }, web: { name: 'cobweb', y: -7, top: 1, color: '#a8a6c8' },\n    webR: { name: 'cobweb', y: -7, top: 1, flip: true, color: '#a8a6c8' }, chandelier: { y: -4, top: 1 }, rail: { name: 'fence', y: 3, color: '#7a7290' }, lantern: { y: -3, top: 1, color: '#e0902a' } };\n  for (const k in PROP) if (PROP[k].top) PROP[k].anchor = 'top';\n  const FLAME = { torch: 24, candelabra: 30, brazier: 20, lamp: 29, offering: 6, chandelier: -8, lantern: -8 };   // flame heights, for the light pass\n  // 1. SKIES: one Backdrop per mood, far layers pale and blue; close sky stops (ramp) hide the gradient dither. The castle,\n  // clouds and moon are drawn in sky().\n  const ramp = (...cs) => cs.slice(1).flatMap((c, i) => [0, .2, .4, .6, .8].map(k => E.mix(cs[i], c, k))).concat(cs.slice(-1)), BG = [\n    new E.Backdrop({ sky: ramp('#050820', '#0c1a3e', '#1a3460', '#2e5078'), stars: 90, layers: [{ kind: 'clouds', color: '#24345e', y: .42, height: .34, parallax: .01, drift: 2, nebula: true },\n      { kind: 'mountains', color: '#34507c', y: .6, height: .24, parallax: .03 }] }),\n    new E.Backdrop({ sky: ramp('#0c040c', '#2a0a18', '#5c1420', '#a8361c'), stars: 16, layers: [{ kind: 'mountains', color: '#4a1a24', y: .6, height: .28, parallax: .03, seed: 5 }] }),\n    new E.Backdrop({ sky: ramp('#02030f', '#0a0f34', '#1e2860', '#4a4a88'), stars: 140, layers: [{ kind: 'mountains', color: '#5a6aa8', y: .74, height: .3, parallax: .02, snow: '#d8e0ff', seed: 12 }] })];\n  // 5. LIGHT, part 1: ZONES set the sky, the darkness (color, strength) and the rim light, blended over 90 units\n  const ZONES = [{ x: 0, bg: 0, amb: '#060a28', dark: .42, rim: '#a8c4ff' }, { x: 330, bg: 0, amb: '#12061a', dark: .6, rim: '#ffc890' }, { x: 720, bg: 1, amb: '#2a0404', dark: .32, rim: '#ffa060' },\n    { x: 930, bg: 2, amb: '#060826', dark: .38, rim: '#b8c4ff' }, { x: 1250, bg: 2, amb: '#140610', dark: .62, rim: '#ffd8a0' }];\n  const zoneAt = x => ZONES.slice(1).reduce((z, Z) => { const u = clamp((x - Z.x + 45) / 90, 0, 1); return u ? { amb: E.mix(z.amb, Z.amb, u), dark: lerp(z.dark, Z.dark, u), rim: u > .5 ? Z.rim : z.rim,\n    bg: z.bg.map((w, i) => lerp(w, i === Z.bg ? 1 : 0, u)) } : z; }, Object.assign({}, ZONES[0], { bg: [1, 0, 0] }));\n  // 4. BLENDING: PS1 translucency is additive ('lighter') or half (globalAlpha), never a dither. bake(): draw once, stamp often.\n  const blend = (g, mode, a, fn) => { g.globalCompositeOperation = mode; g.globalAlpha = a; fn(); g.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over'; g.globalAlpha = 1; };\n  const cache = new Map(), bake = (key, w, h, fn) => { if (!cache.has(key)) { const c = E.mkCanvas(w, h); fn(E.ctx2d(c)); cache.set(key, c); } return cache.get(key); };\n  // soft sprites with per-pixel alpha (stacked discs would band)\n  const soft = (key, w, h, c, fn) => bake(key, w, h, g => { const im = g.createImageData(w, h), rgb = E.hex(c); for (let i = 0; i < w * h; i++) im.data.set([...rgb, 255 * fn(i % w, i / w | 0)], i * 4); g.putImageData(im, 0, 0); });\n  const halo = (R, c) => soft('h' + R + c, 2 * R + 1, 2 * R + 1, c, (x, y) => Math.max(0, 1 - Math.hypot(x - R, y - R) / R) ** 2);   // a light: quadratic falloff\n  // mist: a seamless 320 px band, billowy on top, thickest low\n  const fog = c => soft('fog' + c, 320, 32, c, (x, y) => { const u = x / 320 * E.TAU, top = 12 - 7 * Math.sin(u * 3) - 4 * Math.sin(u * 7 + 1); return y < top ? 0 : Math.sin((y - top) / (32 - top) * Math.PI) ** 1.5 * .7; });\n  // 3. DECALS. Stained glass reads by its lead lines: a red and gold rose, blue lancets with a diamond lattice.\n  const glass = () => bake('glass', 23, 46, g => { for (let y = 0; y < 46; y++) for (let x = 0; x < 23; x++) {\n    const dx = x - 11, dy = y - 11, d = Math.hypot(dx, dy), e = y < 11 ? d : Math.abs(dx), a = (Math.atan2(dy, dx) / (Math.PI / 4) + 8) % 8, h = E.hash2((x + y) / 5 | 0, (x - y + 99) / 5 | 0), K = '#140c18';\n    if (e <= 11.5) px.dot(g, x, y, e > 9.3 || y > 42 ? (dx < -1 ? '#a8a2bc' : dx > 1 ? '#4c4660' : '#7a7490') : d < 6.6 ? (d > 5.6 || a % 1 < .2 ? K : d < 1.8 ? '#fff4c8' : a & 1 ? '#e8b040' : '#c8303c')\n      : y < 19 ? '#2a3a98' : !dx || y === 19 || (x + y) % 5 === 0 || (x - y + 99) % 5 === 0 ? K : h > .86 ? '#9a3ac8' : h < .1 ? '#3aa070' : h < .2 ? '#e8b040' : y < 30 ? '#5a82e8' : '#3a58c0'); } });\n  // a deep arched recess: a dark interior, a ring of wedge stones, a pale keystone\n  const arch = (w, h) => bake('arch' + w, w, h, g => { const R = w / 2; for (let y = 0; y < h; y++) for (let x = 0; x < w; x++) {\n    const dx = x + .5 - R, dy = y + .5 - R, d = y < R ? Math.hypot(dx, dy) : Math.abs(dx), seg = y < R ? Math.atan2(dy, dx) * 5 : y / 7, j = seg - Math.floor(seg); if (d > R) continue;\n    px.dot(g, x, y, d > R - 5 ? (j < .14 || d > R - .8 ? '#1e1628' : y < 5 && Math.abs(dx) < 3 ? '#c0bad4' : Math.floor(seg) % 2 ? '#8a84a0' : '#6e6886') : y < R + 6 ? '#07040c' : y < h * .7 ? '#0e0818' : '#170e22'); } });\n  // the graveyard soil in cross-section: wavy strata, buried stones, roots\n  const earth = () => bake('earth', 160, 48, g => { const rnd = E.rng(4), S = ['#4a3848', '#3c2e3e', '#463646', '#30242f'];\n    for (let x = 0; x < 160; x++) for (let k = 0, y = 0; k < 4; k++) { const h = [6, 11, 12, 30][k] + Math.round(2 * Math.sin((x / 160 * (k + 2) + k * .3) * E.TAU)); px.rect(g, x, y, 1, h, S[k]); px.dot(g, x, y + h - 1, '#241a28'); y += h; }\n    for (let i = 0; i < 16; i++) { const x = 8 + rnd() * 144, y = 10 + rnd() * 34, w = 2.5 + rnd() * 3.5, t = E.tones(['#8a8098', '#6e6680', '#7a6a60'][i % 3]);\n      px.ell(g, x, y, w + 1, w * .7 + 1, '#1e1622'); px.ell(g, x, y, w, w * .7, t.sh); px.ell(g, x - .6, y - .6, w - .8, w * .7 - .8, t.base); px.line(g, x - w * .5, y - w * .6, x + w * .2, y - w * .7, t.lt); }\n    for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) { let x = 4 + rnd() * 152; const n = 5 + rnd() * 16; for (let y = 0; y < n; y++) { x += (rnd() - .5) * 1.5; px.dot(g, x, y, '#1a1220'); if (y % 3 === 1) px.dot(g, x + 1, y, '#5a4450'); } }\n });\n  // foreground weeds and rubble, scrolling faster than the camera (depth, as in Klonoa)\n  const fore = () => bake('fore', 480, 36, g => { const rnd = E.rng(11); for (let x = 0; x < 480; x += 2 + rnd() * 5) px.line(g, x, 36, x + (rnd() - .5) * 6, 32 - rnd() * 14, '#04050c', rnd() < .3 ? 2 : 1);\n    for (let i = 0; i < 9; i++) { const x = rnd() * 480, w = 8 + rnd() * 18; px.ell(g, x, 34, w, 5 + rnd() * 6, '#04050c'); px.ell(g, x - w * .3, 30, w * .4, 1.5, '#1a1e30'); } px.rect(g, 0, 33, 480, 3, '#04050c'); });\n  // a row of cumulus (seamless): shadowed discs, the same discs lit and nudged up-left\n  const CLOUD = ['#8088c0', '#545c96', '#30366a'], clouds = k => bake('cl' + k, 600, 72, g => { const t = E.tones(CLOUD[k]), rnd = E.rng(k + 3), puffs = [];\n    for (let x = 0; x < 600; x += 6 + rnd() * 8) puffs.push([x, 26 + Math.sin(x / 600 * E.TAU * 3 + k) * 7 + rnd() * 5, 7 + rnd() * 10]);\n    for (const [c, dx, dy, f] of [[t.deep, 0, 2, 1], [t.sh, 0, 0, 1], [t.base, -2, -3, .78], [t.lt, -4, -6, .38]]) for (const [x, y, rad] of puffs) for (const o of [-600, 0, 600]) px.disc(g, x + o + dx, y + dy, rad * f, c);\n    px.rect(g, 0, 34, 600, 38, t.deep); });\n  // dead trees: seeded recursive branches with a moonlit edge; also the far treeline\n  const branches = (g, rnd, x, y, a, len, w, c, e) => { const x1 = x + Math.cos(a) * len, y1 = y + Math.sin(a) * len; px.line(g, x, y, x1, y1, c, Math.max(1, Math.round(w)));\n    if (w > 1.4) px.line(g, x + w / 2, y, x1 + w / 3, y1, e); if (len > 4) for (const s of [-1, 1]) branches(g, rnd, x1, y1, a + s * (.3 + rnd() * .45) + (rnd() - .5) * .2, len * (.62 + rnd() * .16), w * .6, c, e); };\n  const tree = seed => bake('tree' + seed, 100, 120, g => { branches(g, E.rng(seed), 50, 120, -1.57 + (seed % 3 - 1) * .12, 36, 7, '#0a0e1a', '#3a4c72'); px.poly(g, [[38, 120], [62, 120], [52, 106], [48, 106]], '#0a0e1a'); });\n  const treeline = () => bake('tl', 320, 90, g => { const rnd = E.rng(5); for (let i = 0; i < 11; i++) branches(g, rnd, 8 + i * 30 + rnd() * 10, 90, -1.57 + (rnd() - .5) * .3, 14 + rnd() * 12, 4, '#0c1830', '#1c2e50'); px.rect(g, 0, 84, 320, 6, '#0c1830'); });\n  // the far castle on its crag: conical roofs, lit windows, a moonlit rim\n  const castle = (c, e, win) => bake('castle' + c, 190, 170, g => { const rnd = E.rng(3), hi = E.mix(e, '#ffffff', .3);\n    px.poly(g, [[0, 170], [26, 120], [50, 104], [66, 112], [82, 98], [150, 100], [168, 120], [190, 170]], c); px.line(g, 150, 100, 190, 170, e, 2); px.rect(g, 30, 84, 130, 20, c); for (let x = 30; x < 160; x += 4) px.rect(g, x, 81, 2, 3, c);\n    for (const [x, w, top, roof] of [[40, 12, 70, 14], [62, 10, 56, 12], [78, 16, 40, 22], [100, 20, 30, 30], [126, 12, 50, 16], [144, 10, 64, 12]]) {\n      px.rect(g, x, top, w, 104 - top, c); px.rect(g, x + w - 3, top, 3, 104 - top, e); px.rect(g, x + w - 1, top, 1, 104 - top, hi); px.poly(g, [[x - 2, top], [x + w + 2, top], [x + w / 2, top - roof]], c); px.line(g, x + w / 2, top - roof, x + w + 1, top, hi);\n      for (let y = top + 5; y < 96; y += 7) if (rnd() < .45) px.rect(g, x + 2 + Math.floor(rnd() * (w - 5)), y, 2, 3, win); } });\n  const grave = v => bake('grave' + v, 18, 27, g => { const t = E.tones(v ? '#6e6c86' : '#848498'); px.rect(g, 2, 8, 14, 19, t.sh); px.disc(g, 9, 8, 7, t.sh); px.rect(g, 3, 8, 11, 18, t.base); px.disc(g, 8, 8, 5.6, t.base);   // tombstone with a cross\n    px.rect(g, 12, 5, 2, 20, t.lt); px.rect(g, 7, 7, 2, 10, t.deep); px.rect(g, 5, 9, 6, 2, t.deep); px.rect(g, 0, 24, 18, 3, '#243e30'); px.rect(g, 2, 23, 5, 1, '#3e6a48'); });\n  // the dialog portrait: a hand-placed E.sprite like SotN's close-ups\n  const VALE = E.sprite([\n    '.................DDDDDDDDD',\n    '.............DDDDDDDCAADDDDD',\n    '........DDDDDDDDDDDAABCCBBDDDDD',\n    '........DDDDDDCCAAACCBCCCCBBBDDDD',\n    '.......DDDDDDDAACCCCCCBCCCCBCBBDD',\n    '.......DDDDDAADBCCCCCCBCCCCCBCCDD',\n    '......DDDDAADCDCBCCCCCCBCCCCBCCCDD',\n    '......DDDDDDDCBCCCCCCCDBCCCCCBCCCD',\n    '......DDDDDDDCBCCCCDCDcCCCCDcBCCCD',\n    '.....DDDDDDDDDBCCCCDCDcCCCCDcCCCDD',\n    '.....DDDDEDDCDBCCCCDCDccCCDEEECCDDD',\n    '....DDDDDEDDCDBCCCCDCDccCCDcccCCDDD',\n    '....DDDDDEDDCDBCCCCDDdcccDcccccDcDD',\n    '....DDDDDEDDCDCBCCCDDdcccDcccccDcD',\n    '....DDDDDEDDCDCBCCDCDccckkkkkkkccD',\n    '....DDDDEDDCDDEBCCDCDcccccwAiwcdcc',\n    '....DDDDEDDCDDEBCCDDdcccccwiiwccdc',\n    '....DDDDEDDCDDEBCCDDdcccccccccccdc',\n    '.....DDDEDDCDDEBCCDddccccccbbccccd',\n    '.....DDDEDDCDDECCCDddccccccccccccdc',\n    '.....DDDEDDCDDECCCDdddcccccccccccca',\n    '.....DDDEDDCDDEDCCDdddcccccccccccccc',\n    '.....DDDEDDCDDEDCCDdddccccccccccec',\n    '.....DDDEDDCDDEDCCDdddcccccccccccc',\n    '.....DDEDDCDDEDDCDdddddccccccccccc',\n    '.....DDEDDCDDEDDCDdddddccccccccccc',\n    '.....DDEDDCDDEDDCDddddddccccmmmmc',\n    '.....DDEDDCDDEDDDDddddddcccccdccc',\n    '.....DDEDDCDDEDDDDDddddddcccccce',\n    '.....DDEDDCDDEyDDDDddddddcccceer',\n    '.....DDEDDCDDEryyDDdddddddccerrrr',\n    '.....DDEDDCDvErrryddddeeeeeerrrrr',\n    '....DDvvvvvvvrrrrryydddddyrrrrrrr',\n    'vvvvvvvvvvvvvrrrrrrryydyyrrrrrrrrr',\n    'vvvvvvvvvvvvvrrrrrrrrryrrrrrrrrrrr',\n    'vvvvvvvvvvvvvrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrr',\n    'vvvvvvvvvvvvrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrr',\n    'vvvvvvvvvvvvrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrr'], { a: '#fff0e0', b: '#fce0c8', c: '#f0c8a8', d: '#d49a88', e: '#9a6070', A: '#ffffff', B: '#eceaf8', C: '#cfcbe6', D: '#9a94c0', E: '#5a5288',\n    v: '#2a2256', r: '#8a1a30', y: '#f0c860', k: '#2a1020', w: '#f8eef0', i: '#a01830', m: '#b05868' });\n  // 6. CAST, part 1: the bat, drawn by hand. f.beat: wing phase, f.open: spread (0..1). Upside down on the roost and dead, it\n  // rolls upright in the screen plane when it drops (never edge-on); f.tilt cocks it before a dive.\n  function bat(g, x, y, f) {\n    const s = Math.sin(f.beat) * f.open, sp = .3 + .7 * f.open, c = E.tones('#4a2a5a'), a = (f.roost || f.dead ? Math.PI : Math.PI * clamp((f.drop + .05) / .15, 0, 1)) + f.tilt; y -= 9;\n    if (a) { g.save(); g.translate(x, y); g.rotate(a); g.translate(-x, -y); }\n    for (const d of [-1, 1]) {\n      const P = (a, b) => [x + d * a * sp, y + b], w = P(7, -3 - s * 5), tips = [P(15, -s * 6), P(11, 5 - s * 3), P(5, 6)];\n      px.poly(g, [P(2, -2), w, tips[0], P(11, 1 - s * 4), tips[1], P(7, 3 - s), tips[2], P(2, 3)], c.sh);   // membrane, scalloped between the fingers\n      for (const p of tips) px.line(g, w[0], w[1], p[0], p[1], c.lt);\n      px.line(g, x + d * 2, y - 1, w[0], w[1], c.hi, 2); px.dot(g, w[0], w[1] - 1, '#e8dcf0');   // arm and thumb claw\n      px.poly(g, [[x + d * 1, y - 4], [x + d * 3, y - 10], [x + d * 3, y - 3]], c.deep);   // ear\n    }\n    px.ell(g, x, y + 1, 3, 4.5, c.deep); px.ell(g, x - .5, y + 1.5, 2, 3, c.base); px.dot(g, x - 1, y + 2, c.lt);\n    px.disc(g, x, y - 3, 2.6, c.sh); px.dot(g, x - 1, y - 4, c.lt); for (const d of [-1, 1]) { px.dot(g, x + d, y - 3, '#ff4a2a'); px.dot(g, x + d, y - 1, '#ffffff'); }\n    if (a) g.restore();\n  }\n  // 6. CAST, part 2: E.MOVES move sets. HIT per hero move: box [forward, behind, low, high], damage x, knockback, launch.\n  const HIT = { slash: [27, 6, -6, 52, 1, 90, 50], backslash: [27, 6, -6, 52, 1, 90, 70], overhead: [31, 4, -6, 60, 1.5, 170, 90],\n    rising: [26, 4, 8, 80, 1.2, 120, 240], thrust: [38, 0, 6, 40, 1.3, 180, 40], spin: [28, 28, -4, 50, 1.4, 150, 110], plunge: [12, 12, -18, 16, 1.2, 40, 0] };\n  const CHARGE = .45;                                  // seconds of holding X for the spin\n  // Enemies: their own moves, slow enough to dodge (a long wind-up, a glint on the raised blade). heavy: never staggered.\n  // LUNGE: a thrust draws the blade back (the telegraph), then lunges on the hit.\n  const LUNGE = { r0: -1.5, a0: .15, lunge: 4, active: .1 };\n  const FOES = {\n    // a knight: bulky, plate over a violet tabard; a slow two-hand cleave, a lunging thrust\n    knight: { hp: 90, w: 14, h: 34, pts: 300, walk: 20, chase: 34, dmg: 16, heavy: true, make: () => ({ moves: [new E.Attack('twohand', { range: 32, wind: .5, active: .16, recover: .5 }),\n      new E.Attack('thrust', { range: 44, wind: .42, recover: .45, ...LUNGE })], rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build: 'bulky',\n      armor: true, sleeves: 'long', outfit: 'tunic', hat: { style: 'helmet', color: '#8a92b0' }, eyeGlow: '#ff5a30', bladeLen: 14,\n      colors: { skin: '#1a1422', cloth: '#5a2a78', pants: '#767c98', boot: '#3e3e52', metal: '#9aa2bc', glove: '#767c98', belt: '#8a6a3a', trim: '#d8b050' } }) }) },\n    // a skeleton: a heap of bones (down: 1) until you come near; it grabs its blade and rises through 'kneel'\n    skeleton: { hp: 30, w: 12, h: 30, pts: 200, walk: 22, chase: 30, dmg: 10, make: () => ({ sleep: 1, rise: 0, moves: [new E.Attack('overhead', { range: 22, wind: .4, active: .15, recover: .45 }),\n      new E.Attack('thrust', { range: 32, wind: .34, recover: .4, ...LUNGE })],\n      rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.2, build: 'skeleton', weapon: null, cheat: .85, headR: 2.8, hunch: .4, bladeLen: 10, colors: { bone: BONE, cloth: '#1c1424', belt: '#6a5a44', metal: '#a89a80', hilt: '#5a4430' } }) }) },\n    bat: { hp: 12, w: 16, h: 12, pts: 150, make: () => ({ open: 1, beat: 0, cool: 1, drop: -1, tilt: 0 }) }\n  };\n  const DEBRIS = { knight: ['#c0c8e0', '#5a2a78', '#e0d4b8'], skeleton: [BONE, '#b0a68c', '#fff4dc'], bat: ['#4a2a5a', '#8a6a9a', '#1a0c22'] };\n  const S = { shadow: '#05030a', outline: false }, SC = Object.assign({ align: 'center' }, S);   // HUD text styles\n  const talk = new E.Dialog(game, { bg: ['#2a2450', '#0e0a1e'], border: '#e0d0a8' }), say = text => talk.say(text, { name: 'VALE', auto: 2.5,\n    portrait: (g, x, y) => { E.ui.box(g, x - 2, y - 2, 44, 44, { bg: ['#4a1a3a', '#0c0612'], border: '#e0d0a8' }); px.sprite(g, VALE, x, y + 2); if (game.real % 4 < .12) px.rect(g, x + 26, y + 17, 4, 2, '#f0c8a8'); } });   // + a blink\n  const fx = [], lights = [], shafts = [], addFx = (kind, x, z, o) => fx.push(Object.assign({ kind, x, z, t: 0, max: .3, size: 1, c: '#ffffff', vx: 0, vz: 0, dir: 1 }, o));\n  let level, hero, rig, combo, MV, atk, charge, idle, land, foes, coins, candles, drops, lift, lives, score, gold, hp, inv, cheer, clear, spawn, gate, lava, age, hint, darkCv, rimWas;\n  // 8. ANY SCREEN: soil = the band of earth under the level on a screen taller than it (0 on a wide one); FOOT = the level's\n  // foot on screen when the camera rests on the ground (the screen's bottom edge, or the top of the soil): the skies hang from it\n  let soil = 0, FOOT = 240;\n  const soilFor = (b, H) => { const lh = b.y1 - b.y0; return H > lh ? Math.min(160, Math.round((H - lh) * .55)) : 0; };\n\n  function reset(full) {\n    if (full) {\n      level = new E.PlatformMap({ rows: ROWS, legend: LEGEND, types: TYPES }); lives = 3; score = 0; gold = 0; clear = false; spawn = level.find('hero'); gate = level.find('gate'); drops = []; fx.length = 0;\n      coins = level.findAll('coin').map(c => ({ x: c.x, z: c.z + 3, w: 8, h: 10 }));\n      candles = level.findAll('candle').map(c => ({ kind: 'candle', x: c.x, z: c.z, w: 10, h: 20 }));\n      foes = level.spawns.filter(s => FOES[s.tag] || s.tag === 'roost').map(s => { const kind = FOES[s.tag] ? s.tag : 'bat';   // a roost is a bat asleep, wings folded\n        return Object.assign({ kind, x: s.x, z: s.z, vx: 0, vz: 0, t: Math.random() * 9, flash: 0, stun: 0, cool: 0, dir: -1 }, FOES[kind], FOES[kind].make(), s.tag === 'roost' && { roost: true, open: 0 }); });\n      const m = level.find('lift'), cols = []; lift = { x0: m.x, x: m.x, z: m.z + 10, w: 44, h: 6, vx: 0, vz: 0, t: 0 };\n      for (let cx = 0; cx < level.w; cx++) if (level.cell(cx, 0) === 9) cols.push(cx * T); lava = { x0: cols[0], x1: cols[cols.length - 1] + T, z: T };\n    }\n    hero = new E.Platformer({ x: spawn.x, z: spawn.z, w: 10, h: 30, run: 105, jump: 300, dash: 250, dashTime: .2 });\n    // The hero: 'heroic' build, cheat: .7 turns him to the camera; a readable palette on dark stages.\n    rig = rig || new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, build: 'heroic', outfit: 'coat', hair: { style: 'long', len: 4 }, sleeves: 'long', stride: 6, lift: 4.6, swing: 5.5, lean: .06, bladeLen: 13, cheat: .7, face: { eyes: 'big', bangs: .2 }, cape: { len: 5, width: 6, seg: 2.6 },\n      colors: { skin: '#f4d2b0', hair: '#e4e0f4', cloth: '#b02840', coat: '#861a30', pants: '#d8ccb8', boot: '#3a2638', belt: '#3a2020', trim: '#f0c860', glove: '#f4f0e8', metal: '#dce6f4', hilt: '#f0c050',\n        cape: '#3a2e70', capeIn: '#5a6ad8', eye: '#2a1840' } });\n    // the hero's moves, tuned for the side view: X chains a chest-high chop > a backhand around the body from behind the head\n    // (plane: 'ground', the torso turns) > a stepping, crouching overhead; up + X rising; down + X in the air a plunge (a tuck,\n    // then the blade driven below the feet); X in a dash thrust; hold X: spin\n    combo = new E.Combo([new E.Attack('slash', { a0: 1.35, a1: -.45 }), new E.Attack('backslash', { plane: 'ground', a0: -2.6, a1: .6, z0: 5, z1: -9, twist: 2 }),\n      new E.Attack('overhead', { lunge: 3.5, crouch: .7, a1: -.9 })], { window: .3 });\n    const spin = new E.Attack('spin', { a0: .3 });      // side views: a windmill loop in front that ends blade forward\n    MV = { rising: new E.Attack('rising', { a1: 1.45, recover: .42 }), plunge: new E.Attack('plunge', { hand: 'both', lean: .45, a0: 1.3, r0: 4, r1: 9.5, wind: .1, active: .28 }), thrust: new E.Attack('thrust'), spin,\n      charge: { spec: { ...spin.spec, plane: 'side', a0: 2.4 }, phase: 'wind' } };   // charging holds the blade raised behind the head; on release it chops into the spin\n    atk = null; charge = idle = land = 0; hp = MAXHP; inv = full ? 0 : 1.5; cheer = 0;   // inv: blink after a respawn\n    game.follow(hero, { z: 34, lead: 24 });            // the camera tracks the hero, looking ahead\n  }\n  // 7. HITS. A kill: an additive glow, then the engine explosion with debris in the victim's colors\n  function burst(x, z, s, colors) { addFx('glow', x, z, { max: .25, size: 22 * s, c: '#ff9a40' }); P.explosion(x, 0, z, s, { debris: colors, flash: false }); }\n  function crumble(f) {                                // the end of a foe: a burst; a skeleton falls apart into a skull and bones\n    f.corpse = 0; if (f.kind !== 'skeleton') return burst(f.x, f.z + (f.rig ? 6 : f.h / 2), f.rig ? .9 : .6, DEBRIS[f.kind]);\n    P.dust(f.x, 0, f.z + 2, 12, { color: '#8a8070' }); A.sfx('bump', { pitch: .6 });\n    for (let i = 0; i < 7; i++) addFx('blade', f.x + E.rand(-9, 9), f.z + 4, { max: 1.5, vx: E.rand(-50, 50), vz: E.rand(40, 130), a: E.rand(0, 6), spin: E.rand(-14, 14), L: i ? E.rand(4, 8) : 0, c: BONE });\n  }\n  // a fallen fighter drops the sword (weapon: null): it tumbles, bounces and lies flat (see update)\n  function disarm(rg, dir, max) {\n    const h = rg.hand(), t = rg.tip(); rg.o.weapon = null;\n    addFx('blade', (h[0] + t[0]) / 2, (h[2] + t[2]) / 2, { max, vx: dir * 50, vz: 110, a: Math.atan2(t[2] - h[2], t[0] - h[0]), spin: dir * -12, L: rg.o.bladeLen * rg.o.size, c: rg.C.metal, h: rg.C.hilt });\n  }\n  function hurt(dir, n = 10) {\n    if (inv > 0 || clear || hero.ko) return; hp -= n; inv = 1.2; hero.knock(dir); A.sfx('hurt'); game.flash('#ff2030', .12, .25); stop();   // knocked back (the hurt pose)\n    game.hitFx(hero.x, 0, hero.z + 24, { power: 1.2, angle: dir > 0 ? 0 : Math.PI, color: '#ff8080', sound: false, damage: n, textZ: 32, textScale: 2, textColor: '#ff6a6a' });\n    if (hp <= 0) die();\n  }\n  const stop = () => { if (atk) atk.cancel(); charge = 0; };   // a hit interrupts the hero's move\n  function die() {                                     // knocked out: pose 'down', then respawn or game over\n    if (hero.ko) return; hp = 0; lives--; hero.ko = 1.6; stop(); disarm(rig, -hero.facing, 1.6); A.sfx('die'); game.flash('#ffffff', .5, .7); game.shake(4);\n    addFx('glow', hero.x, hero.z + 16, { max: .5, size: 30, c: '#ff4060' }); P.sparks(hero.x, 0, hero.z + 16, 14); P.bits(hero.x, 0, hero.z + 20, 8, ['#b02840', '#e4e0f4', '#f0c860']);\n  }\n  // one hit: game.hitFx (hit-stop, shake, star, sparks, sound, a number), a slash flare, chips and a flinch (f.flash),\n  // knocked back (and up) by the move's HIT speeds\n  function damage(f, n, dir, crit, k) {\n    f.hp -= n; f.flash = .3; f.vx = dir * (k ? k[5] : 60) * (f.heavy ? .3 : 1); const z = f.z + f.h * .55;\n    if (!f.heavy) { f.stun = .4; f.vz = k ? k[6] : 60; f.swoop = null; if (f.atk) f.atk.cancel(); }\n    f.pop = game.time - f.popT < .8 ? (f.pop + 1) % 3 : 0; f.popT = game.time;   // quick hits stack their numbers in rows\n    game.hitFx(f.x - dir * 3, 0, z, { power: crit ? 2 : 1, angle: dir > 0 ? 0 : Math.PI, color: crit ? '#ffd040' : '#ffe8a0', damage: n, textZ: 26 + f.pop * 16, textScale: 2, textColor: crit ? '#ffd040' : '#ffffff' });\n    addFx('cut', f.x, z, { max: .14, dir, c: crit ? '#ffe066' : '#c8f0ff' }); P.bits(f.x, 0, z, 4, DEBRIS[f.kind]);\n    if (f.hp > 0) return;\n    f.dead = true; f.corpse = f.rig ? 1.2 : .5; score += f.pts; P.text(f.x, 0, f.z + f.h + 50, '+' + f.pts, '#ffe066');\n    if (f.rig) disarm(f.rig, dir, 1.2);\n  }\n  function snuff(c) {                                  // a candle: a puff of light, chips and loot that drops\n    c.dead = true; addFx('glow', c.x, c.z + 16, { max: .2, size: 16, c: '#ffb050' }); P.sparks(c.x, 0, c.z + 16, 6); P.bits(c.x, 0, c.z + 12, 8, ['#e8dcc0', '#ffb040', '#4a4058']); A.sfx('bump');\n    drops.push({ x: c.x, z: c.z + 8, w: 8, h: 8, vx: 0, vz: 140, heart: Math.random() < .35 });\n  }\n  // facing angle; in the 2.5D view foes turn 3/4 to the camera\n  const face = dir => (dir < 0) * Math.PI + dir * (game.view.pitchDeg > 5) * .5;\n  // is f inside the box [forward, behind, low, high] ahead of a (facing dir)?\n  const within = (a, dir, f, [fw, bk, lo, hi]) => { const dx = (f.x - a.x) * dir; return dx > -bk - f.w / 2 && dx < fw + f.w / 2 && f.z < a.z + hi && f.z + f.h > a.z + lo; };\n  function updateFoe(f, dt) {\n    const dx = hero.x - f.x, dz = hero.z - f.z, near = Math.abs(dx) < 90 && Math.abs(dz) < 30 && !(hero.ko > 0), facing = face(f.dir); f.t += dt; f.flash -= dt; f.stun -= dt; f.cool -= dt;\n    const ov = (hero.w + f.w) / 2 - Math.abs(dx), push = ov > 0 && Math.abs(dz) < f.h && f.onGround ? (Math.sign(dx) || f.dir) * ov * 10 : 0;   // never stand inside the hero: step out\n    if (f.dead) {                                      // carried by the blow, a rig dies (stagger, buckle, topple), a bat tumbles; then they crumble\n      f.corpse -= dt; f.vz -= 700 * dt; f.vx = approach(f.vx, 0, 250 * dt) - push; level.move(f, dt); if (f.corpse <= 0) crumble(f);\n      return f.rig && f.rig.update(dt, { x: f.x, y: 0, z: f.z, vx: 0, vy: 0, facing, pose: 'die' });\n    }\n    if (Math.abs(dx) > 260) return;                    // sleep until near, like 16-bit enemies\n    if (f.kind === 'bat') return fly(f, dt, dx);\n    if (f.sleep) {                                     // a heap of bones until the hero comes near; then it pulls itself up\n      if (Math.abs(dx) < 100 && Math.abs(dz) < 40) { f.sleep = 0; f.rise = .9; f.cool = 1.5; f.rig.o.weapon = 'sword'; f.dir = Math.sign(dx) || -1; P.dust(f.x, 0, f.z + 2, 10, { color: '#8a8070' }); A.sfx('bump', { pitch: .5 }); }\n      return f.rig.update(dt, { x: f.x, y: 0, z: f.z, vx: 0, vy: 0, facing, down: 1 });\n    }\n    if (f.rise > 0) { f.rise -= dt; return f.rig.update(dt, { x: f.x, y: 0, z: f.z, vx: 0, vy: 0, facing, down: clamp((f.rise - .5) * 2.5, 0, 1), pose: f.rise > .2 ? 'kneel' : null }); }   // sit up, kneel, stand\n    // walkers: patrol, close in on a near hero (weapon ready), wait at ledges, telegraph and strike\n    const busy = f.atk && f.atk.busy, free = !busy && f.stun <= 0 && f.onGround;\n    if (near && free) f.dir = Math.sign(dx) || f.dir;\n    const ahead = level.groundBelow(f.x + f.dir * 9, f.z + 1), blocked = f.hitWall === f.dir || (f.onGround && (ahead === null || ahead < f.z - 1));\n    if (blocked && !near) f.dir = -f.dir;              // turn at walls and ledges\n    f.vx = (f.stun > 0 || !f.onGround ? approach(f.vx, 0, (f.onGround ? 500 : 60) * dt) : busy || blocked || (near && Math.abs(dx) < f.moves[0].spec.range - 4) ? 0 : f.dir * (near ? f.chase : f.walk)) - push;   // stunned: slide back; in reach: hold ground\n    const m = near && free && f.cool <= 0 && Math.abs(dz) < 24 && f.moves.find(mv => Math.abs(dx) < mv.spec.range);\n    if (m) { f.atk = m; m.start(); f.cool = .8; }\n    if (f.atk) { if (f.atk.update(dt) === 'active') A.sfx('swing', { vol: .6, pitch: .7 }); f.atk.hits([hero], h => f.atk.u > .5 && within(f, f.dir, h, [f.atk.spec.range + 6, 8, -6, 52]), () => hurt(f.dir, f.dmg)); }   // once the blade is low (or the thrust out)\n    f.vz -= 900 * dt; level.move(f, dt);\n    f.rig.update(dt, { x: f.x, y: 0, z: f.z, vx: f.stun > 0 ? 0 : f.vx, vy: 0, facing: face(f.dir), attack: f.atk && f.atk.state, hurt: f.stun > 0 || f.flash > 0, stance: near ? 'ready' : null });   // every hit flinches\n  }\n  // a bat roosts until the hero passes below, drops, hovers over his head and swoops through him. The telegraph: it rears up\n  // beating hard, then folds its wings and cocks back.\n  function fly(f, dt, dx) {\n    const s = f.swoop, tuck = s && s.u > -.12 && s.u < 0, d = s ? Math.sign(s.x1 - s.x0) : 0;\n    f.open = approach(f.open, f.roost ? 0 : tuck ? .1 : s && s.u > 0 ? .5 : 1, dt * (tuck ? 12 : 4)); f.beat += dt * (f.roost ? 0 : !s ? 15 : s.u < 0 ? 28 : 7);\n    f.tilt = approach(f.tilt, tuck ? -.5 * d : s && s.u > 0 ? .3 * d : 0, dt * 8);   // lean away, then into the dive\n    if (f.roost) { if (Math.abs(dx) < 80 && hero.z < f.z) { f.roost = false; f.drop = .25; f.vz = -90; f.cool = .9; A.sfx('whoosh', { vol: .4, pitch: 1.6 }); } return; }\n    f.drop -= dt;                                    // it falls, unfolding, then rolls upright\n    if (f.stun > 0) { f.x += f.vx * dt; f.z += f.vz * dt; f.vx = approach(f.vx, 0, 300 * dt); f.vz = approach(f.vz, 0, 400 * dt); return; }   // knocked away (or bouncing off)\n    if (s) {\n      s.u += dt / .8; const u = clamp(s.u, 0, 1); f.x = lerp(s.x0, s.x1, u); f.z = s.z0 + (s.u < 0 ? 5 * Math.sin(-s.u / .3 * Math.PI) : 6 * u - Math.sin(u * Math.PI) * s.dip);\n      if (s.u >= 1) { f.swoop = null; f.cool = 1.4; f.vx = Math.sign(s.x1 - s.x0) * 60; } return;\n    }\n    f.vz = approach(f.vz, 0, 150 * dt); f.vx = approach(f.vx, Math.sign(dx) * 48, 110 * dt); f.x += f.vx * dt;\n    f.z = approach(f.z + f.vz * dt, hero.z + 46 + Math.sin(f.t * 3) * 5, 50 * dt);\n    if (f.cool <= 0 && Math.abs(dx) < 40) { f.swoop = { u: -.3, x0: f.x, z0: f.z, x1: hero.x + Math.sign(dx || 1) * 40, dip: f.z - hero.z - 14 }; A.sfx('whoosh', { vol: .5, pitch: 1.3 }); }\n  }\n  // a buffered X picks a move from the context (cutting the last one's follow-through). The plunge holds its stab until it\n  // lands or pogos off a foe. Holding X charges the spin.\n  function fight(inp, dt, diving) {\n    if (inp.buffered('attack') && !(atk && atk.busy && atk.state.phase !== 'recover') && !hero.climbing) {\n      const m = hero.dashing > 0 ? MV.thrust : inp.down('up') ? MV.rising : inp.down('down') && hero.air ? MV.plunge : combo;\n      if (atk && atk !== m) atk.cancel();\n      if (m === combo ? combo.press() : m.start(true)) {\n        inp.consume('attack'); atk = m; charge = 0;\n        if (m === MV.thrust) hero.dashing = Math.max(hero.dashing, .16);   // the thrust rides the dash on\n        if (m === MV.rising && hero.onGround) hero.vz = 170;              // and the rising cut hops\n      }\n    }\n    if (inp.down('attack') && !(atk && atk.busy) && !hero.climbing) { if ((charge += dt) >= CHARGE && charge - dt < CHARGE) A.sfx('charge'); }   // charged: the blade glints (see draw)\n    else { if (charge >= CHARGE && !inp.down('attack')) { atk = MV.spin; MV.spin.start(); } charge = 0; }   // released charged: spin\n    if (atk && !(diving && atk.active && atk.u > .9) && atk.update(dt) === 'active') A.sfx(atk === MV.spin || atk.state.spec.name === 'overhead' ? 'whoosh' : 'swing');\n    if (!atk || !atk.active) return;\n    const name = atk.state.spec.name, k = HIT[name];\n    atk.hits(foes.concat(candles), f => atk.active && atk.state.u > .3 && !f.sleep && !(f.rise > 0) && within(hero, hero.facing, f, k), f => {   // 16-24 damage x the move's, one hit in seven a critical\n      if (f.kind === 'candle') return snuff(f);\n      const crit = Math.random() < .15; damage(f, Math.round(E.randInt(16, 24) * k[4] * (crit ? 2 : 1)), Math.sign(f.x - hero.x) || hero.facing, crit, k);\n      if (name === 'plunge') { hero.vz = 250; atk.cancel(); rig.kick(3); }   // pogo off whatever the plunge stabs\n    });\n  }\n  function update(dt) {\n    for (const f of fx) { f.t += dt; f.x += f.vx * dt; f.z += f.vz * dt;\n      if (f.kind !== 'blade') continue;               // a dropped sword falls, bounces and settles flat\n      const gz = level.groundBelow(f.x, f.z + 8); f.vz -= 700 * dt; f.a += f.spin * dt;\n      if (gz !== null && f.z < gz + 1) { f.z = gz + 1; f.vz = f.vz < -90 ? -f.vz * .3 : 0; f.vx *= .6; f.spin = (Math.round(f.a / Math.PI) * Math.PI - f.a) * 10; }\n    }\n    E.prune(fx, f => f.t >= f.max);\n    const inp = game.input, ko = hero.ko > 0; inv -= dt; cheer -= dt; land -= dt; age += dt; if (!talk.open && age > 2) hint -= dt;\n    talk.update(dt);\n    if (age > 1.8 && age - dt <= 1.8 && !clear) say('The dead will not rest tonight. I must reach the gate.');\n    lift.t += dt; const nx = lift.x0 + Math.sin(lift.t * .8) * 64; lift.vx = (nx - lift.x) / dt; lift.x = nx;   // the lift glides over the moat and carries riders\n    if (ko) { hero.ko -= dt; if (!level.touching(hero, 'hazard')) hero.update(dt, level, { x: 0 }, [lift]); if (hero.ko <= 0) return lives > 0 ? reset(false) : game.go('over', { from: 'platformer' }); }\n    else {\n      const diving = atk === MV.plunge && atk.busy && atk.phase !== 'recover' && hero.air;\n      if (diving) hero.vz = Math.min(hero.vz, -300);\n      hero.update(dt, level, clear ? { x: gate.x > hero.x + 2 ? .4 : 0 } : inp, [lift]);\n      if (hero.jumped) { A.sfx('jump'); rig.kick(3); }                                 // stretch on take-off, squash and dust on landing\n      if (hero.landed) { A.sfx(diving ? 'stomp' : 'land'); rig.kick(diving ? -8 : -5); for (const s of [-1, 1]) addFx('smoke', hero.x + s * 6, hero.z + 2, { vx: s * (diving ? 70 : 30), vz: 6, max: .35, size: 2.5 }); }\n      if (hero.landed && diving) { game.shake(3); land = .3; P.dust(hero.x, 0, hero.z, 14, { speed: 70 }); P.sparks(hero.x + hero.facing * 4, 0, hero.z, 6); }   // the dive's impact\n      if (!clear) fight(inp, dt, diving);\n      for (const c of coins) if (!c.got && E.overlapBox(hero, c)) { c.got = true; gold++; score += 100; A.sfx('coin'); P.glints(c.x, 0, c.z + 5, 4, '#fff6c0', 10); }\n    }\n    for (const d of drops) {                           // candle loot falls and is picked up with a cheer (a heart heals 20)\n      d.vz -= 700 * dt; level.move(d, dt);\n      if (d.got || ko || !E.overlapBox(hero, d)) continue; d.got = true; cheer = .7; P.glints(d.x, 0, d.z + 4, 6, '#fff6c0', 12);\n      if (d.heart) { hp = Math.min(MAXHP, hp + 20); A.sfx('heal'); P.text(hero.x, 0, hero.z + 56, 20, '#7aff8a', { scale: 2, bounce: true }); } else { gold += 5; score += 500; A.sfx('powerup'); P.text(hero.x, 0, hero.z + 56, '+5', '#ffe066', { scale: 2 }); }\n    }\n    for (const f of foes) {                            // enemies: stomp from above (bounce) or take a hit\n      if (f.dead && !(f.corpse > 0)) continue; updateFoe(f, dt); if (ko || f.dead || f.sleep || f.rise > 0 || !E.overlapBox(hero, f)) continue;\n      const d = hero.x < f.x ? 1 : -1; if (hero.vz < 0 && hero.z > f.z + f.h * .5) { hero.vz = 230; A.sfx('stomp'); damage(f, 20, d, false); continue; }\n      hurt(-d, f.dmg || 8); if (f.kind === 'bat') Object.assign(f, { swoop: null, stun: .35, cool: 1.4, vx: d * 110, vz: 130 });   // a bat bounces off\n    }\n    if (Math.random() < dt * 9) {                      // ambient life: fireflies, dust, embers and smoke over the lava\n      const x = hero.x + E.rand(-170, 190), lv = x > lava.x0 && x < lava.x1, ff = x < 330;\n      addFx('mote', x, lv ? lava.z + 4 : hero.z + E.rand(0, 110), { vx: E.rand(-8, 8) - (ff ? 0 : 6), vz: lv ? E.rand(25, 45) : E.rand(-6, 8), max: E.rand(1.2, 2.6), size: E.pick([1, 1, 2]), c: lv ? '#ff7a2a' : ff ? '#b8ff70' : '#a898c8' });\n      if (lv && Math.random() < .3) addFx('smoke', x, lava.z + 4, { vz: E.rand(12, 22), vx: -4, max: 2.2, size: E.rand(6, 10) });\n    }\n    if (!clear && !ko && (level.touching(hero, 'hazard') || hero.z < -40)) die();\n    if (!clear && !ko && Math.abs(hero.x - gate.x) < 12 && hero.z >= gate.z - 2) {   // goal: step into the castle gate\n      clear = true; stop(); score += 1000; A.music('victory'); game.flash('#fff4d0', .6, .7); P.glints(gate.x, 0, gate.z + 20, 12, '#fff6c0', 30); game.after(6, () => game.go('title'));\n      say(['The gate is open. Whatever waits inside, I am ready.', 'Score ' + score + '   Best ' + best('platformer', score)]);\n    }\n    // poses: cheer (gate, pickups); down when knocked out; crouch (a tuck) over the plunge and its landing; hips after a long idle,\n    // blade tip down. The sword is held ready, except running and on ladders (weapon: null), where he turns his back to the\n    // camera and climbs hand over hand.\n    const won = clear && hero.onGround && Math.abs(gate.x - hero.x) < 3, busy = atk && atk.busy, cheering = won || (cheer > 0 && hero.onGround && !busy && !charge);\n    idle = hero.onGround && !hero.vx && !busy && !charge ? idle + dt : 0; if (!(hero.ko > 0)) rig.o.weapon = hero.climbing ? null : 'sword';\n    rig.update(dt, hero.rigState({ attack: (atk && atk.state) || (charge > .1 ? { ...MV.charge, u: clamp((charge - .1) / (CHARGE - .1), 0, 1) } : null), ...(hero.climbing && { facing: -Math.PI / 2 }),\n      stance: cheering || hero.climbing || idle > 6 || (hero.onGround && Math.abs(hero.vx) > 40) ? null : 'ready', point: idle > 6, aim: -.5, expr: cheering ? 'smile' : hero.stun > 0 ? 'wince' : null,\n      pose: hero.ko > 0 ? 'down' : cheering ? 'cheer' : land > 0 || (atk === MV.plunge && busy && hero.air) ? 'crouch' : idle > 6 ? 'hips' : null }));   // the plunge squats over its blade\n  }\n  function coin(g, x, y, ph) {                         // spinning coin: its width follows |cos| of a phase\n    const w = Math.abs(Math.cos(ph)) * 5; px.ell(g, x, y, Math.max(1, w), 6, '#a0600c'); px.ell(g, x - .5, y - .5, Math.max(.6, w - 1.2), 4.8, '#f8c030');\n    if (w > 2) { px.rect(g, x - 1, y - 3, 1, 5, '#fff2a8'); px.dot(g, x + 1, y + 3, '#c07a10'); }\n  }\n  // lights for the light pass: a world point, a radius, a color, a strength (flick() makes flames breathe)\n  const lit = (r, x, z, R, c, a, y = 0) => { const [sx, sy] = r.w(x, y, z), p = Math.max(6, Math.round(R * r.view.scale / 6) * 6); if (sx > -p && sx < r.bw + p && sy > -p && sy < r.bh + p) lights.push({ x: Math.round(sx), y: Math.round(sy), R: p, c, a }); };\n  const flick = (x, k = 1) => 1 + .12 * k * Math.sin(game.real * 13 + x) * Math.sin(game.real * 7.3 + x * 3);\n  // ground mist over the graveyard: the fog band at two depths and speeds, behind the play layer and faintly in front\n  const mist = (r, g, Z, a, k, y) => { if (Z.bg[0] < .01) return; const [, sy] = r.w(0, y, 2 * T), o = ((r.ix * k + game.time * 5 * k) % 320 + 320) % 320;\n    blend(g, 'source-over', a * Z.bg[0], () => { for (let x = -o; x < r.bw; x += 320) g.drawImage(fog('#a8b8e8'), x, Math.round(sy) - 24); }); };\n  function sky(r, g, Z) {\n    let first = true;\n    Z.bg.forEach((w, i) => { if (w > .01) { blend(g, 'source-over', first ? 1 : w, () => BG[i].draw(r)); first = false; } });   // each Backdrop draws with the alpha we set\n    const mo = Z.bg[0] + Z.bg[2], mx = Math.round(r.W * .76 - r.ix * .01), my = 44, t = E.tones('#f0ead8');\n    if (mo > .01) {   // the moon: a smooth additive halo, then craters on a lit disc\n      blend(g, 'lighter', .55 * mo, () => g.drawImage(halo(60, '#4a68c8'), mx - 60, my - 60));\n      blend(g, 'source-over', mo, () => { px.disc(g, mx, my, 16, t.sh); px.disc(g, mx - 1, my - 1, 15, t.base); px.disc(g, mx - 4, my - 5, 7, t.lt); for (const [a, b, c] of [[5, 3, 4], [-6, 6, 3], [2, -8, 2], [-2, 10, 2]]) px.disc(g, mx + a, my + b, c, t.sh); });\n    }\n    // the far castle (blue by moonlight, red over the moat), mist at its foot, a faster treeline\n    const cx = Math.round(r.W * .28 - r.ix * .03), cy = Math.round(FOOT * .72 - r.iy * .04);\n    for (const [i, c, e, w] of [[0, '#16223e', '#34507e', '#ffc868'], [1, '#3a0e18', '#8a2a20', '#ff8a3a']]) if (Z.bg[i] > .01) blend(g, 'source-over', Z.bg[i], () => g.drawImage(castle(c, e, w), cx, cy - 170));\n    blend(g, 'source-over', .8, () => { const o = ((r.ix * .05 + game.time * 3) % 320 + 320) % 320; for (let x = -o; x < r.bw; x += 320) g.drawImage(fog(Z.bg[1] > .5 ? '#b8482a' : '#5a7aa8'), x, cy - 30); });\n    if (Z.bg[0] > .01) blend(g, 'source-over', Z.bg[0], () => { const ty = Math.round(FOOT * .9 - 90 - r.iy * .12), o = ((r.ix * .22) % 320 + 320) % 320; for (let x = -o; x < r.bw; x += 320) g.drawImage(treeline(), x, ty); px.rect(g, 0, ty + 90, r.bw, r.bh, '#0c1830'); });\n    if (Z.bg[2] > .01) blend(g, 'source-over', Z.bg[2], () => { for (let k = 0; k < 3; k++) {   // the sea of clouds: three billowy rows, nearer = darker and faster\n      const y = Math.round(FOOT * (.58 + k * .12) - r.iy * .05 * (k + 1)), o = ((r.ix * (.05 + k * .07) + game.time * (2 + k * 2)) % 600 + 600) % 600;\n      for (let x = -o; x < r.bw; x += 600) g.drawImage(clouds(k), x, y); px.rect(g, 0, y + 70, r.bw, r.bh, E.tones(CLOUD[k]).deep); } });\n  }\n  function decor(r, g) {                               // baked decals behind the play layer; engine props are queued\n    for (const s of back.spawns) {\n      const [sx, sy] = r.w(s.x, -8, s.z), x = Math.round(sx), y = Math.round(sy), fl = FLAME[s.tag];\n      if (x < -90 || x > r.bw + 90) continue;\n      if (s.tag === 'glass') { g.drawImage(glass(), x - 11, y - 46); lit(r, s.x, s.z + 18, 40, '#5a7aff', .22, -8); shafts.push([x, y, (s.z - (level.groundBelow(s.x + 30, s.z) || 0)) * r.view.scale + 30]); }   // + a light shaft\n      else if (s.tag === 'arch') { g.drawImage(arch(44, 76), x - 10, y - 76); if (Math.sin(game.real * .7 + s.cx) > .8) blend(g, 'lighter', 1, () => { px.dot(g, x + 9, y - 40, '#ff4030'); px.dot(g, x + 13, y - 40, '#ff4030'); }); }   // eyes in the dark\n      else if (s.tag === 'tree') g.drawImage(tree(s.cx), x - 40, y - 100);\n      else if (s.tag === 'grave') g.drawImage(grave(s.cx % 2), x - 9, y - 26);\n      else if (STONES[s.tag] || !PROP[s.tag]) { const o = STONES[s.tag]; if (o) r.shape(o.id, s.x, o.y, s.z, o); }   // (no set on the page: no stones)\n      else { const o = PROP[s.tag], z = o.top ? s.z + T : s.z; r.prop(o.name || s.tag, s.x, o.y, z, o); if (fl) lit(r, s.x, z + fl, s.tag === 'lamp' ? 44 : s.tag === 'offering' ? 30 : 56, '#ff9a40', .45 * flick(s.x), -6); }\n    }\n    for (let i = 0; i < FRONT.length; i++) { const o = FP[FRONT[i]]; if (o) r.prop(o.name, i * T + 8 + (o.dx || 0), 7, 2 * T, o); }   // the FRONT row, in front of the path\n  }\n  // LAVA: a rolling surface, a bright crest, molten bands darkening with depth, drifting crust, streaks and bubbles that pop.\n  // It also lights the walls (lit).\n  function lavaDraw(r, g) {\n    const [x0, y0] = r.w(lava.x0, 8, lava.z), [x1, y1] = r.w(lava.x1, 8, 0), t = game.time, B = ['#fff4c0', '#ffd050', '#ffa030', '#f07020', '#d04818', '#a02c14', '#6a1810', '#3a0c0c'];\n    for (let x = Math.max(0, Math.round(x0)); x < Math.min(r.bw, Math.round(x1)); x++) {\n      const wx = x + r.ix, top = Math.round(y0 + Math.sin(wx * .09 + t * 2) * 1.5 + Math.sin(wx * .23 - t * 3.1) * .8), n = E.noise2(wx * .07 + t * .25, t * .1);\n      let y = top; for (let k = 0; k < B.length; k++) { const h = k < 7 ? [1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6][k] : y1 - y + 2; px.rect(g, x, y, 1, h, B[k]); y += h; }\n      if (n > .6) { px.rect(g, x, top + 1, 1, n > .7 ? 4 : 2, '#2a0a08'); if (n < .62) px.dot(g, x, top + 1, '#ff8a20'); }\n      if ((wx + Math.floor(t * 18)) % 29 < 7) px.dot(g, x, top + 6 + wx % 3, '#ffc040');\n    }\n    for (let k = 0; k < 4; k++) { const u = (t * .6 + k * .29) % 1, bx = lava.x0 + 8 + E.hash2(Math.floor(t * .6 + k * .29), k) * (lava.x1 - lava.x0 - 16), [sx, sy] = r.w(bx, 8, lava.z);\n      if (u < .85) { px.disc(g, sx, sy + 1 - u * 2, 1 + u * 3, '#ff9a30'); px.dot(g, sx - 1, sy - u * 4, '#fff4c0'); } else blend(g, 'lighter', 1, () => px.disc(g, sx, sy - 2, 5, '#ff6a20')); }\n    for (let x = lava.x0 + 8; x < lava.x1; x += 40) lit(r, x, lava.z + 6, 64, '#ff5a1a', .42 * flick(x, 2));\n  }\n  function drawFx(r) {                                 // FX: additive glows, slash flares and motes; half-transparent smoke\n    for (const f of fx) r.queue(f.x, 6, f.z, g => {\n      const u = f.t / f.max, [x, y] = r.w(f.x, 0, f.z), s = r.view.scale;\n      if (f.kind === 'blade') { const c = Math.cos(f.a) * f.L * s / 2, n = Math.sin(f.a) * f.L * s / 2;   // a dropped sword (blade, hilt), a bone or a skull\n        if (f.max - f.t < .3 && (f.t * 20 | 0) % 2) return;   // blinks out\n        if (!f.h) return f.L ? px.line(g, x - c, y + n, x + c, y - n, f.c, 2) : (px.disc(g, x, y - 2, 2.6 * s, f.c), px.rect(g, x - 1, y - 2, 3, 1, '#1a1020'));\n        px.line(g, x - c * .6, y + n * .6, x + c, y - n, f.c); return px.line(g, x - c, y + n, x - c * .6, y + n * .6, f.h, 2); }\n      if (f.kind === 'smoke') return blend(g, 'source-over', .5 * (1 - u), () => { px.disc(g, x, y, f.size * s * (.6 + u), '#2e2a3a'); px.disc(g, x - 1, y - 1, f.size * s * (.35 + u * .6), '#4e4860'); });\n      blend(g, 'lighter', f.kind === 'mote' ? Math.sin(u * Math.PI) : 1 - u, () => {\n        if (f.kind === 'glow') { const R = Math.round(f.size * s / 6) * 6; g.drawImage(halo(R, f.c), x - R, y - R); }\n        else if (f.kind === 'cut') { const L = 22 - u * 8, k = f.dir; px.poly(g, [[x - L * k, y - L * .55], [x + 2 * k, y - 2], [x + L * k, y + L * .55], [x - 2 * k, y + 2]], f.c); px.line(g, x - L * .7 * k, y - L * .38, x + L * .7 * k, y + L * .38, '#ffffff'); }   // SotN's slash flare across the target\n        else px.rect(g, x, y, f.size, f.size, f.c);\n      });\n    }, { bias: 2 });\n  }\n  function draw(r) {\n    const view = r.view, t = game.time, g = r.ctx, Z = zoneAt(hero.x), tilted = view.pitchDeg > 5, b = level.bounds(view);\n    lights.length = shafts.length = 0; soil = soilFor(b, r.H); FOOT = r.H - soil;\n    sky(r, g, Z);                                      // 1. skies (cross-faded), moon, castle, mist, clouds\n    back.draw(r); decor(r, g); mist(r, g, Z, .55, .6, -8);   // 2. castle walls, decals, props on them, the far mist layer\n    level.draw(r); lavaDraw(r, g);                     // 3. the play layer, the lava painted over its tiles, the earth cut\n    if (soil) {   // 8. the SOIL under the level: the earth cut's strata, darkening; above the clouds it gives way to them\n      const top = Math.round(b.y1 - 4 - r.iy), o = ((r.ix % 160) + 160) % 160;\n      blend(g, 'source-over', 1 - Z.bg[2], () => { for (let x = -o; x < r.bw; x += 160) g.drawImage(earth(), x, top); for (let k = 0; k < 4; k++) px.rect(g, 0, top + 48 + k * 10, r.bw, k < 3 ? 10 : r.bh, ['#2a2030', '#211a28', '#18131f', '#100c16'][k]); });\n    }\n    { const [ex, ey] = r.w(0, 8, 2 * T), [x1, eb] = r.w(20 * T, 8, 0), w = (x1 - ex) / 3; if (x1 > 0) for (let k = 0; k < 3; k++) g.drawImage(earth(), Math.round(ex + k * w), Math.round(ey) + 3, Math.round(w), Math.round(eb - ey) - 3); }\n    const shadow = (x, z) => { const gz = tilted && level.groundBelow(x, z); if (gz !== null && gz !== false) r.shadow(x, 0, 6, .35, '#000', gz); };   // contact shadows in the 2.5D view\n    for (const c of candles) if (!c.dead) {            // floating candles on iron cups: slash them for loot\n      r.prop('candle', c.x, -2, c.z + 5, { size: 1.1 }); lit(r, c.x, c.z + 18, 40, '#ffb060', .4 * flick(c.x));\n      r.queue(c.x, -3, c.z + 5, g2 => { const [x, y] = r.w(c.x, -2, c.z + 5); px.poly(g2, [[x - 6, y], [x + 6, y], [x + 2, y + 4], [x - 2, y + 4]], '#3a3040'); px.rect(g2, x - 6, y, 12, 1, '#9a8aa0'); px.rect(g2, x - 1, y + 4, 2, 4, '#2a2230'); px.dot(g2, x, y + 8, '#c8b060'); });\n    }\n    for (const c of coins) if (!c.got) r.queue(c.x, 0, c.z, g2 => { const [x, y] = r.w(c.x, 0, c.z + 5); coin(g2, x, y + Math.round(Math.sin(t * 3 + c.x) * 1.5), t * 4 + c.x * .1); });\n    for (const d of drops) if (!d.got) r.queue(d.x, 1, d.z, g2 => { const [x, y] = r.w(d.x, 0, d.z); if (d.heart) E.ui.hearts(g2, x - 3, y - 7, 1, 1); else coin(g2, x, y - 6, t * 5); });\n    r.queue(lift.x, 0, lift.z, g2 => {                 // the lift: a floating slab with runes\n      const [x, y] = r.w(lift.x, 0, lift.z + lift.h), w = Math.round(lift.w * view.scale), X = Math.round(x - w / 2), st = E.tones('#6a6280'), H = Math.round(lift.h * view.scale) + 3;\n      blend(g2, 'lighter', .35 + .15 * Math.sin(t * 4), () => { px.ell(g2, x, y + H + 6, w * .42, 5, '#1a5aa0'); px.ell(g2, x, y + H + 5, w * .25, 3, '#3ab8ff'); });\n      px.poly(g2, [[X + 3, y + H], [X + w - 3, y + H], [x + 6, y + H + 8], [x - 6, y + H + 8]], st.deep); px.rect(g2, X, y, w, H, st.base); px.rect(g2, X, y, w, 2, st.lt); px.rect(g2, X, y, w, 1, st.hi);\n      px.rect(g2, X, y + H - 2, w, 2, st.deep); px.rect(g2, X + 2, y + 3, w - 4, 1, st.sh); for (const k of [1, 2]) px.rect(g2, X + Math.round(w * k / 3), y + 4, 1, H - 6, st.deep);\n      blend(g2, 'lighter', .6 + .4 * Math.sin(t * 4), () => { for (const k of [-.33, 0, .33]) { px.rect(g2, x + k * w - 1, y + 5, 3, 1, '#3ab8ff'); px.dot(g2, x + k * w, y + 6, '#bfefff'); } });\n    });\n    lit(r, lift.x, lift.z - 6, 34, '#3ab8ff', .3);\n    const [gx, gy] = r.w(gate.x, -8, gate.z); g.drawImage(arch(60, 90), Math.round(gx) - 30, Math.round(gy) - 90);   // the gate: a double door in a carved arch (it swings open on clear)\n    r.prop('door', gate.x, -6, gate.z, { size: 1.55, color: '#5a2e22', open: clear });\n    if (clear) lit(r, gate.x, gate.z + 30, 80, '#ffe0a0', .6);\n    for (const f of foes) { if (f.dead && !(f.corpse > 0)) continue;\n      const o = { flash: f.flash > .24 || (f.dead && f.corpse < .4 && (game.real * 20 | 0) % 2 === 0), rimColor: Z.rim }; shadow(f.x, f.z);   // a hit: a white tint for 3-4 frames\n      if (f.kind === 'bat') { r.actor(f.x, 0, f.z, (g2, ox, oy) => bat(g2, ox, oy, f), o); lit(r, f.x, f.z + 10, 12, '#ff5030', f.swoop && f.swoop.u < 0 ? .9 : .4); }   // the eyes flare before a swoop\n      else { r.actor(f.x, 0, f.z, (g2, ox, oy) => f.rig.draw(g2, ox, oy, view), Object.assign(o, { bias: f.atk && f.atk.busy ? 1 : 0 })); f.rig.drawSmear(r, ['#fff4e0', '#f0d8b0', '#d0986a', '#8a4a3a']);   // striking: over the hero, a rusty trail\n        if (!f.dead && !f.sleep) { const h = f.rig.head(); lit(r, h[0] + f.dir * 2, h[2], 8, '#ff4020', .3); if (f.atk && f.atk.phase === 'wind') { const w = f.rig.hand(); lit(r, w[0], w[2], 20, '#ff6040', .4); } } }   // eye glow; the raised blade glints\n    }\n    {   // the hero: a red flicker when hurt, then a blink; dashing and spinning leave blue ghosts\n      const blink = inv > 0 && !hero.ko && Math.floor(game.real * 16) % 2 === 0, red = hero.stun > 0 && (game.real * 20 | 0) % 2 === 0, w = rig.tip(); shadow(hero.x, hero.z);\n      r.actor(hero.x, 0, hero.z, (g2, ox, oy) => rig.draw(g2, ox, oy, view),\n        { alpha: blink && hero.stun <= 0 ? .45 : 1, flash: red && '#ff3050', flashMix: .4, rimColor: Z.rim, ghost: (hero.dashing > 0 || (atk === MV.spin && atk.active)) && (game.real * 60 | 0) % 3 === 0 ? { color: '#5a8aff', life: .25 } : null });\n      lit(r, hero.x, hero.z + 26, 46, '#ffe8d0', .25); rig.drawSmear(r, ['#ffffff', '#e0f0ff', '#8ab8ff', '#5a6ad8']); if ((atk && atk.active) || charge >= CHARGE) lit(r, w[0], w[2], 30, '#a0d8ff', charge ? .5 * flick(w[0], 3) : .35);   // the blade lights up\n      if (charge >= CHARGE) r.queue(w[0], 9, w[2], g2 => { const [x, y] = r.w(w[0], 0, w[2]), k = 3 + 2 * Math.sin(game.real * 30);   // charged: a star glints on the tip\n        blend(g2, 'lighter', 1, () => { px.line(g2, x - k, y, x + k, y, '#bfe8ff'); px.line(g2, x, y - k, x, y + k, '#bfe8ff'); px.dot(g2, x, y, '#ffffff'); }); });\n    }\n    drawFx(r);\n    r.queue(0, -1.5, 0, g2 => shade(g2, r, Z, Z.dark));   // full darkness: after the walls and wall props, before the characters\n    r.overlay(g2 => { shade(g2, r, Z, Z.dark * .3); glow(g2); mist(r, g2, Z, .3, 1.3, 8);   // a third of it over everything, the colored glows, the near mist\n      for (const [x, y, L] of shafts) blend(g2, 'lighter', .09, () => { const k = L / 126, b = y - 30 + L; px.poly(g2, [[x - 8, y - 30], [x + 8, y - 30], [x + 70 * k, b], [x + 40 * k, b]], '#8098ff'); px.poly(g2, [[x - 3, y - 30], [x + 3, y - 30], [x + 58 * k, b], [x + 50 * k, b]], '#c0c8ff'); });   // shafts end on the floor\n      const fo = ((r.ix * 1.3) % 480 + 480) % 480, fy = soil ? Math.round(b.y1 - 4 - r.iy) : r.bh; if (Z.bg[1] < .99) blend(g2, 'source-over', 1 - Z.bg[1], () => { for (let x = -fo; x < r.bw; x += 480) g2.drawImage(fore(), x, fy - 36); });\n      hud(g2, r); });\n    talk.draw(r);\n  }\n  // 5. LIGHT, part 2: a darkness layer in the zone color with every light punched out, over the walls fully and over everything\n  // at a third, so sprites stay brighter than rooms (as in SotN).\n  function shade(g, r, Z, a) {\n    const W = r.bw, H = r.bh; if (!darkCv || darkCv.width !== W || darkCv.height !== H) darkCv = E.mkCanvas(W, H);\n    if (darkCv.frame !== game.real) {                  // built once per frame, used twice\n      const d = darkCv.getContext('2d'); darkCv.frame = game.real; d.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over'; d.globalAlpha = 1; d.fillStyle = Z.amb; d.fillRect(0, 0, W, H);\n      d.globalCompositeOperation = 'destination-out'; for (const L of lights) { d.globalAlpha = Math.min(1, L.a * 2); d.drawImage(halo(L.R, '#ffffff'), L.x - L.R, L.y - L.R); }\n    }\n    blend(g, 'source-over', a, () => g.drawImage(darkCv, 0, 0));\n  }\n  const glow = g => { for (const L of lights) { const R = Math.max(6, Math.round(L.R * .55 / 6) * 6); blend(g, 'lighter', L.a * .5, () => g.drawImage(halo(R, L.c), L.x - R, L.y - R)); } };\n  // HUD in the SotN manner: a gold HP medallion, an ornate HP bar, lives and gold\n  function hud(g, r) {\n    const gt = E.tones('#d8a848'), w = Math.round(84 * clamp(hp / MAXHP, 0, 1));\n    px.rect(g, 30, 8, 96, 11, '#0c0610'); px.rect(g, 31, 9, 94, 9, gt.sh); px.rect(g, 32, 10, 92, 7, gt.lt); px.rect(g, 33, 11, 90, 5, '#1a0610');\n    px.rect(g, 37, 11, w, 5, '#c8243a'); px.rect(g, 37, 11, w, 1, '#ff8a94'); px.rect(g, 37, 15, w, 1, '#6a0818'); px.poly(g, [[124, 7], [132, 13], [124, 20]], gt.sh); px.poly(g, [[124, 9], [130, 13], [124, 17]], gt.lt);\n    px.disc(g, 19, 19, 18, '#0c0610'); px.disc(g, 19, 19, 17, gt.sh); px.disc(g, 18, 18, 16, gt.lt); px.disc(g, 19, 19, 14, '#2a0a1c'); px.disc(g, 19, 19, 13, '#4a1430'); px.dot(g, 11, 8, gt.hi);\n    E.font.text(g, String(Math.max(0, hp)), 20, 12, '#ffffff', { align: 'center', scale: 2, gradient: ['#ffffff', '#ffb0b8'], shadow: '#10040a', outline: false });\n    E.font.text(g, '♥', 40, 23, '#ff5a6a', S); E.font.text(g, '×' + lives, 48, 23, '#f0e8ff', S); coin(g, 74, 27, 0); E.font.text(g, String(gold), 81, 23, '#ffe066', S);\n    const ribbon = (y, h, a) => blend(g, 'source-over', a, () => { px.rect(g, 0, y, r.W, h, '#05030a'); px.rect(g, 0, y, r.W, 1, '#d8a848'); px.rect(g, 0, y + h - 1, r.W, 1, '#d8a848'); });\n    if (age < 1.8) { const a = clamp((1.8 - age) * 2, 0, 1); ribbon(58, 26, .5 * a); if (a > .3) E.font.title(g, 'Castle Approach', r.W / 2, 64, { scale: 2, align: 'center', colors: ['#ffffff', '#d8c8f0', '#9a7ad0'], depth: 1, depthColor: '#1c1430' }); }\n    else if (hint > 0 && !talk.open) {   // controls after the dialog, in the player's own keys; over the soil when there is some\n      const k = a => game.input.label(a), y = soil ? r.H - soil + 6 : r.H - 17, s = hint > 4 ? [prompt('jump', 'jump'), prompt('attack', 'slash'), prompt('dash', 'dash'), touch() ? '' : 'V 2.5D'].filter(Boolean).join('  •  ')\n        : ['↑' + k('attack') + ' rise', '↓' + k('attack') + ' dive', k('dash') + '+' + k('attack') + ' thrust', 'hold ' + k('attack') + ' spin'].join('  •  '), fit = E.font.width(s) < r.W - 8;\n      ribbon(y, 13, .55 * clamp(hint, 0, 1)); E.font.text(g, s, r.W / 2, y + (fit ? 3 : 4), '#e8e0ff', fit ? SC : Object.assign({ font: 'tiny' }, SC));\n    }\n    if (clear) { ribbon(40, 30, .6); E.font.title(g, 'STAGE CLEAR', r.W / 2, 46, { scale: 2, align: 'center', colors: ['#fffbe8', '#ffd36a', '#e0662a'] }); }   // a translucent ribbon keeps the lit gate visible\n  }\n  return { view: 'side', views: ['side', 'brawler'], input: 'PLATFORMER', res: 'ps1', pausable: true, touch: ['jump', 'attack', 'dash'], propSize: 1.5, camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], tall: 1.5 },   // props at the hero's scale; the player zooms .75x to 2x (no turning), a phone held upright starts at 1.5x: the level fills more of it\n    enter() { reset(true); age = 0; hint = 8; talk.close(); game.cam.bounds = v => { const b = level.bounds(v); return Object.assign({}, b, { y1: b.y1 + soilFor(b, game.H) }); }; A.music('adventure'); L.enabled = false; if (game.r) { rimWas = game.r.rimAlpha; game.r.rimAlpha = .5; }   // a stronger, zone-colored rim light (headless, no renderer)\n      P.ground = (x, y, z) => level.groundBelow(x, z + 1); },              // sparks and debris land and bounce on the platform under them\n    exit() { if (game.r) game.r.rimAlpha = rimWas; P.ground = null; }, update, draw,\n    /** the proof: a run to the right through the graveyard, jumping, cutting and dashing; a hash of the run (with a new rig:\n     *  the hero keeps his between visits, and his pose decides where the blade lands) */\n    run: (n = 600) => proof('platformer', n, (i, k) => { if (!i) k.hold('right'); if (i % 70 === 10) k.tap('jump'); if (i % 45 === 30) k.tap('attack'); if (i % 240 === 200) k.tap('dash'); if (i % 150 === 100) k.tap('confirm'); },\n      () => [hero.x, hero.z, hp, lives, score, gold, clear, foes.map(f => [f.x, f.z, f.hp])], () => { rig = null; }) };\n})();\nscenes.platformer = PLAT;\n\n/* ---- START: the title menu, or a slice named in the address (#platformer) ---- */\n// a genre kit file holds one slice: the menu lists only the slices in this file\nfor (let i = SLICES.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) if (!scenes[SLICES[i].id]) SLICES.splice(i, 1);\ntitleMenu = menuOf(SLICES, { y: 76, onPick: i => game.go(SLICES[i].id) });\n// #<slice>/<part> jumps in (#arcade/maze, #shooter/horizontal): the slice's enter(data) gets { part }; a part that is a\n// scene of its own (the slice's parts: { battle: 'rpg-battle' }) starts there\nconst [first, part] = (location.hash || '').slice(1).split('/'), sc = scenes[first], to = sc && part && sc.parts && sc.parts[part];\ngame.start({ scenes, scene: to || (sc ? first : 'title'), data: sc && part ? { part } : undefined });\nwindow.__game = { game, scenes, titleMenu };   // for tests\n})();\n/* =============================== GAME END =============================== */\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md'","aggregated_output":"# my-3D2dge guide for AI models\n\nThis guide is for an AI (or a person) asked to build, port, remaster or remix a game with my-3D2dge. `API.md` is the compact reference, read whole: every single file carries it (a genre kit, `dist/my-3d2dge.html`, the compact one), and so does the handoff package. This guide explains the ideas, the workflow for remaking a classic, recipes for each genre, and the checks to run before handing a game back: search it for what you need. Its paths are the repository's (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge); the handoff package's `README.md` says which of them it holds. Everything here matches `engine/my-3d2dge.js` v0.89.1.\n\nA coding agent that reads files gets the handoff package (`dist/handoff/`: the full engine readable, the cards, the tools, every 2D kit's game and a template game) or the agent edition alone (`dist/my-3d2dge-agent.js`: the essential engine in one readable file, its header, about 8k tokens, a complete manual with three example games). Games written for the agent edition run unchanged on the full engine, which adds the lighting, props, backdrops, touch controls and camera tools this guide also covers.\n\n## North star\n\nmy-3D2dge is a general-purpose retro-modern 2D engine built for AI models. It exists so that any model can:\n\n- port, remaster, reimagine and remix games from the 8-bit to 64-bit eras;\n- make spiritual successors that feel like modern remasters, with better visuals, physics and controls;\n- mash up mechanics across titles and genres.\n\nIt aims for **consistent quality**: the engine carries the hard parts (feel, physics, rendering, sound, UI), so each game only has to describe its rules and content.\n\n## The mental model\n\n1. **The world is 3D, the camera is 2D.** Game logic works in world units: x east, y south, z up, one tile = 16 units. A `View` projects world points to screen pixels. Switch the view and the same game renders as isometric, three-quarter, top-down, brawler, side-on or any custom angle.\n2. **Characters are puppets, not sprite sheets.** A `Humanoid` is a 3D skeleton posed by math every step (IK legs and arms, gait from velocity, jumps, attacks, cape cloth). A `Blob` is a squashy sphere. Both project through the current view. Small objects can be string sprites (`E.sprite`).\n3. **Genre kits.** Each kit handles one hard problem and knows nothing about genres, so they combine freely:\n   - `TileMap` + `Body`: ground-plane levels and physics.\n   - `PlatformMap` + `Platformer`: side-scrolling levels and a platformer controller.\n   - `Bullets` + `E.pattern`: projectiles.\n   - `Dialog` + `Menu`: text boxes and menus.\n   - scenes: title, play, game over.\n   - `game.audio`: chip sound.\n\n## Remake workflow (port, remaster, reimagine, remix)\n\n1. **Name the core loop.** Mega Man: run, jump, shoot, reach the boss. Zelda: explore rooms, fight, find keys, open doors. 1942: dodge, shoot, survive waves, beat the boss. Write it as one sentence at the top of the game.\n2. **Pick the view and the kit.** Use the genre playbook in `API.md`. A side-scroller is `PlatformMap` + `Platformer` in `'side'`; a top-down game is `TileMap` + `Body`. Pick a console resolution (`res: 'ps1'`, 320 x 240, or `'snes'`) so pixels are whole and the HUD's are known, and `expand: true` so the game fills any screen: the resolution is then the least it shows, and the HUD anchors to `r.W` and `r.H`.\n3. **Start from the closest kit.** Each genre has one, a complete small game with gameplay, HUD, win and lose, sound and touch controls: the playbook in `API.md` names it (in a single file its slice sits between `GAME START` and `GAME END`; in the handoff package it is `kits/<id>.js`). Change it into your game, with its own title and game-over scenes, and ship only that. Do not start from an empty file.\n4. **Rebuild the content.** Levels are ASCII rows with a legend, enemies are small factories, items are sprites. Keep the original's layout ideas but make your own maps; for a remix, change the rules on purpose.\n5. **Modernize.** The engine gives you most of it for free:\n   - forgiving controls: coyote time, jump buffering, variable jump height, input buffering;\n   - hit feedback: hit-stop, shake, sparks, damage numbers;\n   - smooth animation, lighting and glow;\n   - a 2.5D view (`views: ['side', 'brawler']`);\n   - pause, save data, touch controls.\n6. **Tune the feel** with the feel numbers in `API.md`, then check the game with the skeleton overlay (`rig.debug(r)`) and in slow motion (`game.timeScale = .25`).\n7. **Verify** with the checker and look at the screenshots (see the checklist at the end).\n\n## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\nPlayers judge a remake in the first screenshot. The engine's defaults reach SNES quality by themselves; games reach the PS1/N64 bar (Symphony of the Night, Metal Slug, Legend of Mana, Klonoa) when they use every layer of the look kit.\n\n**Why games look cheap, and the fix for each:**\n- *Tiny stick characters.* Use the HD Humanoid (the default) at `size: 1.2-1.4` on a 320x240 `res`, pick a `build` (`'heroic'` for tall heroes, `'bulky'` for brawlers and bosses, `'chibi'` for Mana-style RPGs), and dress every character: outfit, hair, hat, armor, sleeves, cape, and a palette of 4-6 colors per character (skin, hair, cloth, pants, boot, trim). Give enemies different silhouettes (hunch, bulky, blobs with `ears`, `horns`, `wings`, `feet`, `tail`, `mouth: 'fangs'`). Use poses (`pose: 'cheer'` on victory, `'cast'` for spells, `'guard'`, `'kneel'`, `'down'` for the defeated) instead of leaving everyone in the idle stance.\n- *Empty backgrounds.* Side views get an `E.Backdrop` (a preset or custom layers of mountains, castle, forest, city, clouds, fog) with parallax. Ground views get textured floors from `E.tex.*` with floor tags (water, lava shimmer), and walls with a material: `face: 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge'` and `roof: 'tiles' | 'slab' | 'leaves' | 'grass'`. Never leave every wall the same brick.\n- *Flat colors.* Shade everything with `E.tones(hex)`: `deep` and `sh` on the shadow side, `base`, `lt` on lit edges, a `hi` sparkle. Shadows lean cool, highlights warm; never shade by only darkening.\n- *Bare rooms.* Put 3-8 props on every screen with `r.prop`: candles, chandeliers and stained-glass windows in castles; crates, barrels, signs and lamps in towns; trees, bushes, flowers, rocks and grass outdoors; crystals, bones, skulls, cobwebs and vines in caves; awnings, lanterns, streetlamps, hydrants, trash cans and fire drums on streets; sandbags, cactus and palms in deserts; mushrooms in forests. Tint props with `color` so they match the palette, and set `E.style.propSize = 1.4` when heroes are size 1.2+ so a door is taller than the hero.\n- *Weak hits.* Every hit gets hit-stop (`game.freeze(.04-.08)`), shake, sparks and a sound. Every death and explosion gets `particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)` and, for big ones, `game.flash`. Pickups get `particles.glints`.\n- *NES typography.* Use the default 5x7 font with a drop shadow for HUD text, `E.ui.box` gradient panels and bars, and `E.font.title` with a 3-4 color gradient and extrusion for logos. A modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild, Arkham) wants an HTML HUD instead: `E.Shell` (below), as the STEALTH slice and the template game do; its dialogue stays `E.Dialog` with portraits.\n- *Faceless dialog.* Every talking character gets a portrait: `talk.say(lines, { name, portrait: rig })`, and `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 40)` on HUDs and RPG party and status screens.\n- *Muddy lighting.* Use lights for mood, not darkness: a few warm lights (`r.prop(..., { light: true })`) over an ambient of .4-.6; GPU lighting in top-down dungeons.\n\n**Art recipes by classic** (combine with the genre recipes below):\n- *Symphony of the Night:* `res: 'ps1'`, the `'castle-night'` backdrop, PlatformMap `stone` tiles with `moss` and `brick` walls, `'back'` tiles for the rear wall, candles and candelabras every few tiles, chandeliers, stained-glass `window` props, banners and statues. The hero is `build: 'heroic'`, `outfit: 'coat'`, `hair: 'long'`, `cape`, pale skin and silver hair, and the sword chops in the screen plane by itself in the side view; enemies are skeletons (`build: 'skeleton'`, add `outfit: 'robe'` and a staff for a lich), bats (`Blob` with `wings: true, mouth: 'fangs'`), cobwebs and vines on the walls.\n- *Metal Slug:* the `'desert'` backdrop, `ground` and `metal` tiles, crates, barrels, sandbags, palms and cactus, `broken` pillars, `particles.explosion` everywhere (with `particles.ground` set so debris bounces on platforms), `bulky` soldiers with `weapon: 'gun'`, `point: true` while firing, and screen shake on every blast.\n- *Legend of Mana / Secret of Mana:* the `threequarter` view, `E.tex.grass` / `dirt` / `water` floors by floor tag (a river with `block: true` and a plank bridge), houses of `timber` or `plaster` walls under `tiles` roofs, `hedge` walls with `leaves` tops and `rock` cliffs with `grass` tops, trees, bushes, flowers, fences and mushrooms as props, `chibi` characters with `face: { eyes: 'big' }` and bright palettes, rabbites (`Blob` with `ears: 'rabbit', feet: true`), and dialog boxes with portraits.\n- *Klonoa and 2.5D platformers:* PlatformMap in the `brawler` view (tiles show their top faces), a bright `'day'` or `'forest'` backdrop, props in front of and behind the play plane (`y` of -12 and +12), and a soft camera `lead`.\n- *Final Fight / Streets of Rage:* the `brawler` view, a long street TileMap with brick and `plaster` building walls, awnings, streetlamps, hydrants, trash cans and burning fire drums along the sidewalk, `bulky` heroes and thugs with varied palettes, and big impacts.\n\n## Animation: fluid, varied, readable\nBroken animation ruins a good-looking game faster than flat art. The Humanoid rig animates procedurally, so use its vocabulary instead of moving joints by hand:\n- **Moves, not raw specs.** `new E.Attack('overhead')`, `new E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook', 'uppercut'])`. Every move in `E.MOVES` winds up, strikes with a lunge, step, lean and twist, follows through, holds, and eases back to the stance. It never sweeps backwards through its own arc.\n- **A move set per character.** A knight gets `overhead`, `thrust` and `spin`; a mage `cast`; a rogue `jab`, `thrust` and `backslash`; a monk `jab`, `cross`, `roundhouse` and `flyingkick`; a brute `haymaker`, `bash` and `sweep`; a beast `claw`. Two heroes must never share one swing.\n- **Stances and poses between attacks.** Brawlers walk with `stance: 'guard'`, knights with `stance: 'ready'`. Win with `pose: 'cheer'`, cast with `'cast'`, block with `'block'` (weapons) or `'guard'` (fists), duck with `'crouch'`, taunt with `'hips'` or `'wave'`, fall with `down`, die with `'die'` (stagger, knees, topple), and climb ladders with `climb: true`. Faces act too: `expr: 'smile' | 'shout' | 'angry' | 'wince'`, and a Dialog portrait's mouth moves while its line types.\n- **Enemies telegraph.** Give enemy attacks a longer `wind` (.2-.35 s) than the hero's, so players can read and dodge them. Flash and knock back on hit, lie `down` on death.\n- **Check the motion.** `node tools/filmstrip.mjs game.html#scene --steps \"... rec:16:2 press:KeyJ\" --crop x,y,w,h` records the swing frame by frame. Look for the wind-up, the strike and a clean settle. Add `--seed 1` for a repeatable strip, and `--compare before.html` (a copy of the page from before your change) to see exactly which pixels your change moved.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n1. **Game logic in world units, `dt` everywhere.** Never store screen pixels. Convert only when drawing (`r.w(x, y, z)`) or reading the mouse (`game.mouseGround()`). `update(dt)` runs in exact fixed steps: every 1/120 s of real time is one step of `dt = 1/120 * game.timeScale`, so the same inputs play the same at 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz.\n2. **Draw only with engine primitives** (`E.px.*`, `E.font`, `E.ui`, sprites, props, `r.*` helpers). `ctx.arc`, `ctx.stroke`, `ctx.fillText`, canvas gradients and scaled `drawImage` anti-alias and break the pixel look. Translucency goes through the engine helpers (`px.blend`, `px.polyDither`, `px.ddisc`, `r.glowDisc`, `r.shadow`): stepped alpha with blend modes by default (PS1 / N64 / SNES color math), or ordered dithering with `E.style.trans = 'dither'` for NES, Game Boy and Genesis looks.\n3. **Anything that overlaps goes through the queue**: `r.actor`, `r.sprite` or `r.queue`. Only backgrounds are drawn directly (`r.sky`, `map.drawFloor`, `level.draw`, parallax shapes).\n4. **Animate with continuous values** (phases, velocities, eased timers), never frame lists.\n5. **Read input in `update`**: `pressed` for one-shot actions, `down` for held, `buffered` + `consume` for combat.\n6. **A game is one script on the engine:** in a single file, the code between `GAME START` and `GAME END`; with files, `game.js` beside the `index.html` that loads the engine (inline both into the page to hand a person one file). **The engine is yours to change too.** Your copy (a clone of the repository, the handoff package, a single file) is yours to fix, extend or rewrite in any way you see fit. The readable engine is `engine/my-3d2dge.js` and `engine/modules/` in the repository (`node tools/build.mjs` rebuilds every page from them), `my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package (every page and test there loads it), and the script marked \"inlined from engine/my-3d2dge.js\" in `dist/my-3d2dge.html`; a genre kit's is minified. If you hand the game back, a line on what you changed and why helps: it shows what the engine lacks.\n7. **Don't rebuild the standard features** (below): the pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen and pausing on focus loss come with every game. Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: {...} })`.\n\n## Standard features (built in: adjust them, don't rebuild them)\nEvery `new E.Game(...)` comes with what players expect of a modern release, so your code goes to the game itself. They're tested on both editions, they work with keys, mouse, gamepad and touch, and the player's choices are saved per game.\n- **Pause menu** (full engine): a pausable scene pauses on Escape, P, Start, the touch START button or the corner's menu button, and shows Resume, your items, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help (when your `E.Shell` has one) and Quit to title (it asks first; Keep playing has the focus). Arrows, d-pad and stick move, Enter or A chooses, Escape or B goes back one screen, P or Start closes the menu. The game sees none of those presses.\n- **Settings** (`game.settings`): Display (full screen; pixel scaling for a fixed `res`: Auto, Sharp, Fill; a screen filter: Clean, Scanlines or CRT, drawn as the picture reaches the canvas, never on the game's buffer, and off under a test harness, so screenshots and filmstrips stay clean), Sound (sound, volume, music, effects, the audio offset with its calibration), Comfort (reduce motion, screen shake, screen flashes), and yours. Saved per game with a version (`E.store` key `settings:` + the page's path, or `id`); a bad saved value falls back to its default, and where storage is blocked the game plays on (`game.settings.saved` is false).\n- **Controls**: every action of the current preset, rebound by pressing the new key, mouse button or gamepad button (a conflict is said and nothing changes; Escape, Enter, the F keys, Start, the arrow keys and the d-pad stay with the menus, so a player can't lock themselves out), the controller (dead zone, invert aim, button symbols: Xbox, PlayStation, Nintendo or positions, vibration) and the touch buttons (always, automatic or hidden; right or left side; size; opacity). Restore defaults resets the controls and never touches a save.\n- **Full screen** (`game.fullscreen`): a phone's first tap goes full screen, the top-right button and the menu toggle it, and once the player leaves it the game never forces it back. Where a browser can't (an iPhone), the settings say to use Share, then Add to Home Screen; the page carries the home-screen app tags.\n- **Pausing by itself**: a pausable scene pauses when the page is hidden, the window loses focus or the controller in use is unplugged (`game.pauseReason` says which), and only the player resumes.\n- **The corner**: a menu button and a full-screen button at the top right, inside the safe area. They show on titles, game overs and pauses, for a moment when the mouse nears the top, and the menu stays on a touch screen with no touch buttons; in play they're gone. Keep a title's art clear of the top-right 110 x 60 CSS pixels, or pass `corner: false`.\n- **Phones**: a viewport that reaches the safe areas (pinch zoom left alone), no page scrolling, zooming, selection or callouts on the canvas, touch buttons inside the safe areas, a touch stick with a dead zone that also presses the directions past halfway (menus, dialog choices, ladders), a bottom dialog that keeps clear of the touch buttons, menus picked by a tap or click on an entry and dialogs turned by a tap on the box (a title needs no touch buttons), and a fixed `res` filling a high-density screen (`expand: true` fills every screen).\n- **Boot** (a loading screen and a crash report): heavy setup (a procedural level, a big table, baked caches, data from a promise) goes in boot steps, run one a frame behind a boot screen that shows the system's checks (engine, browser, screen, canvas, GPU, sound, storage, input), each step, a progress bar and lines of the game's own, like the Sims' \"Reticulating splines\"; then the game draws a warm-up frame under it, so play starts without a hitch. `game.start({ scenes, scene: 'title', boot: { title: 'VESPER', version: '1.0', steps: [['Raising the harbour', () => { map = buildHarbour(); }], ['Waking the guards', () => loadGuards()]], messages: ['Counting the bells', 'Oiling the hinges'], min: 0 } })`. A step may return a promise; headless (and with `loop: false`) the steps run at once and `await game.booted` waits for any promise. If a step throws, or anything fails before play, the screen becomes a report a player can screenshot: what failed, the error, a short code, the engine and game versions, browser, screen, canvas, GPU, sound, storage and input, with Try again, Copy report and Continue. `E.diagnose()` gives the same report any time (`.text`, `.short`); after play, errors go to the red box with its short line. `E.bootSplash(title)` is the screen's markup for the very start of `<body>`, so it shows while the page's scripts load (the starter files and the handoff template have it). Keep a boot short: no step should wait on purpose (`min` is for a splash you want seen).\n- **Juice that respects the player**: `game.shake` and `game.flash` follow the comfort settings, `game.hitFx` rumbles a gamepad (the vibration setting; `rumble: false` skips it), and `game.input.rumble(strength, seconds)` does it for your own moments.\n\nAdjust them:\n```js\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', view: 'side', input: 'PLATFORMER',\n  menu: {\n    items: [['Restart level', () => game.go('play', { level })]],                 // pause-menu buttons after Resume\n    settings: [{ key: 'hints', label: 'Hints', value: true },                      // yours, beside the standard ones\n      { key: 'difficulty', options: ['Easy', 'Normal', 'Hard'], value: 'Normal', help: 'How hard enemies hit' }],\n    controls: { attack: 'Sword', skill: 'Magic', debug: false },                  // names on the Controls screen; false hides one\n    quit: 'title'                                                                  // the scene Quit goes to (false: no Quit)\n  } });\nconst hurt = { Easy: .5, Normal: 1, Hard: 1.5 }[game.settings.difficulty];       // read them anywhere, any time\nr.text('PRESS ' + game.input.label('jump') + ' TO JUMP', 8, 8, '#fff');           // 'SPACE', 'A' or '✕', after the player's rebinding\n```\n- Leave parts out: `menu: false` (you draw your own pause: the canvas PAUSED overlay comes back), `fullscreen: 'button'` (no tap) or `false`, `corner: false`, `autoPause: false`, `id: 'mygame'` (the name the settings are saved under).\n- A canvas title menu can offer OPTIONS: `game.menu('settings')` opens the settings over the title, which waits; `game.menu('controls')` the controls.\n- From code: `game.settings.set('volume', .5)` (checked, saved, applied), `game.settings.reset('Sound')`, `game.fullscreen.toggle()` (browsers allow it only from a key, click or tap), `game.pause(reason)`, `game.resume()`.\n- Your own `E.Shell` takes over from the engine's and keeps the standard menu (`standard: false` leaves it out).\n- The agent edition has `game.settings`, `game.fullscreen`, the corner, the pausing and `input.label`, but not the menu screens: its corner's menu button pauses and resumes.\n\nWhat stays your game's job, because only the game knows it:\n- Every gameplay scene `pausable: true`, so the menu and the pausing reach it; titles and game overs not.\n- Prompts from `game.input.label(action)` (or `ui.hud({ prompt: { act: 'interact' } })`), never hard-coded keys; touch buttons (`touch: [...]`) for every action a level needs (the stick moves).\n- The assists your mechanics call for, as settings: difficulty, aim assist, auto-fire, hold or toggle for a sustained action, hints after repeated failure.\n- Saves: `game.saves` (three slots and an autosave with their place, level and time played; versioned; `menu: { saves: true }` for the pause menu's Save and Load and the standard screen, which asks before a save is lost), written at checkpoints and save points (not on page close).\n- Information never in color alone; flashes short (`game.flash` already follows the settings).\n\n## Frame order\n\n`game.start(...)` runs this every animation frame:\n\n1. The clock adds the frame's real time (at most 50 ms) to what is left from the last frame, and runs one step for each whole 1/120 s of it: 2 steps a frame at 60 Hz, 1 at 120 Hz, 1 or 0 at 144 Hz. Each step ticks input, then (except during hit-stop and pause) runs the timers, the current scene's `update(dt)`, particles and the camera. The rest waits for the next frame: `game.alpha` (0..1) is how much of a step it holds, for interpolating drawing.\n   - Slow motion (`game.timeScale = .3`) makes each step shorter (`dt = 1/120 * .3`), never fewer, so it stays smooth. Above 1 a step is split so none is longer than 1/120 s.\n   - Paused, or at `timeScale` 0, the steps still run with `dt` 0, so input keeps ticking.\n2. The renderer clears the buffer and positions the camera.\n3. The scene's `draw(r)`. Draw backgrounds first, then register decals, queue items, actors, sprites, overlays and lights.\n4. The renderer finishes the frame, in this order:\n   1. particles and afterimages are queued;\n   2. decals draw (ground marks, shadows, telegraphs);\n   3. the queue sorts and draws by depth;\n   4. lighting darkens the frame;\n   5. overlays draw on top (HUD, dialog, menus, damage numbers);\n   6. the frame is presented with a sub-pixel camera offset.\n5. With GPU lighting active, every queue item is also drawn into a hidden info image, and WebGPU does the lighting and presentation.\n\nAn error in `update` or `draw` does not stop the loop. It is shown in a red box on screen and logged once to the console (`game.errors` keeps the list).\n\n## Systems in more depth\n\n### Views and resolution\n- Presets: `iso` (yaw 45, pitch 30), `threequarter` (pitch 55), `topdown` (pitch 80), `brawler` (pitch 25), `side` (pitch 0).\n- `overhead` (pitch 90, scale 1) makes world x/y equal screen pixels, for shooters, puzzle and arcade games. It is not in the default view cycle.\n- Make your own with `new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost)`.\n- `res` fixes the internal resolution with whole-number scaling. Without it, the size adapts to the window between `minH` and `maxW` × `maxH`; `portrait: { maxH }` lets the view grow on a screen held upright (a phone), so it fills the screen.\n- `res` with `expand: true` (what the starter kits use) fills any window with no bars: `res` is the least the screen shows, and `r.W` × `r.H` grows on the long side (a 16:9 monitor shows about 427×240 of `'ps1'`, an ultrawide 640×270, a phone held upright 320×690). The game's job is what fills the extra space, edge to edge, with the HUD over the world's corners (never a band of nothing). The kits show the ways. A wider screen shows more level (the adventure's neighbouring rooms, dimmed a little: they wait) or widens the play area itself (the shooter's waves spread across the whole screen). A taller one shows more world (the forest round the adventure's rooms, the RPG's sky darkening to stars), zooms a side view in (the platformer, the brawler and the RPG's battle at 1.5x, a scene's `camera.tall`, the battle's two sides closing up to fit) and lifts the level onto a band below it (the platformer's soil, the brawler's foreground wall). A painted backdrop is painted at the screen's size and zoom, its ground meeting the floor's edge. Keep prompts clear of `game.input.touchArea()` and HUDs clear of `game.cornerArea()` (the menu button stays at the top right on a phone). On a phone, make the controls smart: a button there only when it has something to do (`input.touchSet`: TALK beside someone), taps on what you see (the RPG's commands and foes), a thumb that steers from anywhere on the screen (`input.steer = 'drag'`, `input.drag()`: the shooter's ship goes 1.5x as far, so one swipe crosses it), what a phone player shouldn't have to hold done for them (the shooter fires by itself), buttons a thumb hits (44 CSS px and more: the RPG's battle pads, with AUTO), and a camera that never shows ground a fighter can't walk to (the brawler stops at a fight's lock). And keep the frame a phone's: a 120 Hz screen gives 8.3 ms, and frames that miss it (their cost, or the GPU behind them) are drawn every other refresh (`game.drawEvery`), so draw background characters at half rate (`r.actor(..., { every: 2, key })`: the adventure's townsfolk) and leave GPU lighting to a computer (its main pointer fine). Settings, Display, Show frame rate reads it on the device, and Frame rate lets the player fix it (60: every other refresh of a 120 Hz screen; Full: every refresh). `npm run test:phone` checks the kits on an emulated phone.\n- `game.views` limits view cycling (`game.nextView()`) and tells the checker which views to test.\n\n### Scenes\nA scene is `{ enter(data), exit(), update(dt), draw(r), pausable, view, views, input, res, expand, touch, camera }`.\n- `game.go('play', data)` switches before the next step; timers, tweens, scripts and particles are cleared (weather too) and the camera snaps. `{ fade: 'iris', at: hero }` (or `'wipe'`, `'mosaic'`, `'swirl'`, `'dissolve'`, `'white'`; `seconds` each way) picks the transition: an iris closing on the hero for a door, a swirl into a battle, a mosaic into a dream. A world point `at` is placed by each scene's camera; `[x, y]` is a point on the screen. Reduce motion turns each into a plain fade.\n- `pausable: true` lets `pause` (Escape, P, Start, the touch START) toggle `game.paused`: the pause menu opens (the canvas PAUSED overlay with `menu: false`) and the music is ducked. It also lets the page pause the scene by itself when it is hidden or loses focus.\n\n### Camera\n- `game.follow(hero, { z, lead })` once, or `game.focus(x, y, z)` every update.\n- `cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v)` (or `level.bounds`) keeps it inside the level.\n- `cam.room = [w, h]` snaps the target to the center of the current room; the camera then slides, so Zelda- and Metroid-style room transitions come for free. Freeze gameplay while `cam.moving` is true.\n- The player's zoom: a scene's `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5 }` sets how close the camera starts (`start`; `tall` on a phone held upright) and how far the player may take it (`zoom`): the mouse wheel, `-` and `=`, a two-finger pinch (both fingers moving, so a thumb on the stick isn't one) and Settings, Camera zoom. Their zoom is kept, a factor on top of the scene's, so it carries across scenes and screen shapes; each change shows `ZOOM 1.65x` at the top, the number to make `start` when tuning by feel. `zoom` may be a function of the game, a range per screen shape (the adventure's: out to .85x on a wide screen, none upright, where its forest is sized for 1x); `zoom: false` where zooming would break the game (the shooter's playfield is the screen), `wheel: false` where the wheel is the game's.\n- **The window and framing:** `cam.window = [48, 24]` is a dead zone (Super Mario World's): the camera stays still while the focus moves inside it and follows from its edge, so small steps don't jiggle the screen. `cam.snapY = true` follows a followed body's height only when it lands (its `onGround`) or nears the screen's edge, so a jump doesn't drag the camera up and down. `cam.frame([p1, p2], { margin: 24, zoom: [.5, 1] })` keeps several things in view, centred between them, zooming out (to at least `zoom[0]`) as they part and back in as they meet: two players, a boss and the hero, a race's leaders. `zoom: false` only centres.\n- Screen shake respects the player's reduced-motion setting.\n\n### Players (two to four on one machine)\n- `game.players` is `[game.input, ...the ones who joined]`. `game.input` stays player 1, so a game of one is written as before. Each other player is an input of its own with the same API (`move()`, `pressed()`, `label()`), plus `player` (2 to 4), `name` (`'P2'`), `color` (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`: blue, red, green, yellow) and `device`.\n- `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin: (input, n) => dropIn(input) })` in a scene's `enter` lets them in: Start on a gamepad no one holds, or 2 on the keyboard. Two on one keyboard each get half: player 1 WASD with F G H R T Y (`Input.halfKeys(preset, 'left')`), player 2 the arrows with `.` `/` `,` L `;` `'` and the numpad (`'right'`), each half taking the preset's actions in turn. `game.addPlayer({ pad: 1 })` or `({ keys: 'right' })` adds one directly (tests, a versus menu); `game.removePlayer(p)`.\n- The standard features follow: each player's prompts name its own keys (`input.label`), a pause says who paused (P2 PAUSED), a lost controller names its player, the Controls screen has a page each. Recorded sessions replay every pad and both halves of the keyboard.\n- One shared screen: `cam.frame(heroes, { margin: 90 })` keeps everyone in view. A split screen: `game.splitScreen(2, { follow: [hero, hero2] })`, `layout: 'auto'` (side by side on a wide screen, stacked on a tall one, a grid for 3 or 4), `'columns'`, `'rows'` or `'grid'`. The scene's `draw(r)` runs once per viewport, its camera its own (`game.viewports[i].follow(t)`, `.focus(x, y)`), with `r.viewport` saying which (`{ i, x, y, w, h }`: draw that player's HUD there); `scene.hud(r)` then runs once over the whole screen. GPU lighting is off while split; a scene's start ends the split.\n\n### Motion and timing (tweens, scripts, paths)\nThe code every game writes by hand, in the engine and on game time: each pauses with the game, stands still in hit-stop, slows with `timeScale` and ends with the scene (unless `keep: true`), and runs the same at every refresh rate.\n- **Tweens** (Phaser's tweens, Unity's DOTween): `game.tween(target, { x: 10, y: 4 }, seconds, { ease, delay, repeat, yoyo, onDone, keep })` eases a target's numbers to new values, from where they are when its delay ends; a door sliding, a title dropping in, a coin flying to the HUD, a camera move. `ease` is a name in `E.ease` (Penner's: `linear`, `inSine`, `outBack`, `outElastic`, `outBounce`, ...; `outQuad` by default) or a function of 0..1. It replaces a timer, a start value and a lerp in every update.\n- **Scripts** (Unity's coroutines): `game.script(function* (game) { ... })` runs a generator in the update step and waits at each `yield`: a number of seconds, a condition (`E.until(fn)` or a function), a tween, another script or a generator (run and waited for), `E.all([...])`, `E.any([...])`, or nothing (one step). A wave timeline is a loop (`for (const w of WAVES) { spawn(w); yield E.any([w.time, () => !enemies.length]); }`); a cutscene is its lines in order (`talk.say(...)`, `yield E.until(() => !talk.open)`, `yield game.tween(cam, ...)`), instead of a state machine with a timer per state. It runs at once to its first `yield`; `.cancel()` stops it and runs its `finally`.\n- **Paths:** `new E.Path(points, { closed, smooth: 'catmull' })` measures a line by distance along it: `at(d)` is a point with its heading (a moving platform's place, an enemy's dive, a rail, a camera track: `d = speed * time`), `nearest(x, y)` the distance along of the nearest point (a racer's progress round the lap, for places), `draw(r)` shows it while you build.\n- **Weather:** `game.weather = { kind, amount, wind, lightning }` (rain, snow, ash, leaves, sand): drops fall through the world's height in ground views and in three layers of parallax in side and overhead views, splash or settle on the floor (`particles.ground`, the platforms' height in a side view), and lightning flashes (the player's Screen flashes setting) and thunders. It is cosmetic: its own seeded random stream, never `Math.random`, so replays and gameplay are untouched. `E.WEATHER` holds the kinds; add your own.\n- **Sprites** (frames, turns, swaps): `E.anim([...frames], fps, { loop, pingpong })` (or one sheet cut into `frames`) is played by `r.sprite(x, y, z, anim, { t })`; `rot` turns a sprite in whole pixels, cached in 16 or 32 steps as the era's games pre-rotated theirs (a ship facing its heading, a spinning blade); `scale` grows it nearest-neighbour; `E.recolor(spr, map)` is a palette swap (the second player, a stronger enemy).\n- **Wrap-around:** `E.wrap(v, lo, hi)` keeps a position on a world whose edges meet (Asteroids, Pac-Man's tunnel, a looping stage), `E.wrapDiff(a, b, size)` the short way across the seam (aim and chase across it), and `wrap: [w, h]` on `r.sprite` and `r.actor` draws a copy across each edge a thing straddles.\n\n### TileMap (ground-plane levels)\n- ASCII `rows` and a `legend`: a number is a wall type, a string is a spawn tag, `{ tile, floor, spawn }` combines them.\n- Wall `types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut, cutH }`. `cut` lowers a wall when it stands between the camera and the floor.\n- `floorTex(x, y, floorTag)` colors the floor per pixel; it is baked once per view. Ready-made: `E.tex.flagstone`, `grass`, `dirt`, `water`, `planks`, `checker`, `plain`.\n- `map.set(cx, cy, 0)` opens doors or breaks walls; the floor and wall caches rebuild themselves.\n- Collision is height-aware: `collide(body)` ignores walls lower than `body.z + step`, and `groundAt(x, y, r, z)` returns the surface to stand on.\n- Things standing on a block sort after it, and `r.shadow(..., surfaceZ)` draws on top of blocks.\n- `map.block(cx, cy, on)` and `map.set(...)` bump `map.version`; a `FlowField` rebuilds at its next `update` when it changes.\n- Maps built in code: `new E.TileMap({ w, h, types })` starts as open floor; `map.fill(cx, cy, w, h, id)` puts a block of wall type `id` in (0 clears it), and `map.setFloor(cx, cy, tag, block)` tags a floor cell (`'grass'`, `'water'`) and blocks it or not.\n- Facades come from wall-type options: `windows: { w, h, step, from, lit }` (or `true`), `door: { chance, color }`, `shutters: color`, `balcony: { z, color }`, `arch: { chance }`. For anything else, `types[id].decorate(face)` paints each wall face as it is baked (so it costs nothing per frame): `face.side`, `face.u0..u1` along the wall in world units (patterns line up across cells), `face.z0..z1`, `face.at(u, z)`, `face.quad(u0, z0, u1, z1, color)`, `face.line`, `face.dot`, `face.tones`, `face.view`.\n- A big thing over several blocks (a canopy over a tower, a cart across two cells) sorts by its whole base: `r.queue(x, y, z, fn, { footprint: [x0, y0, x1, y1] })` lifts it over every block under it. `r.prop` and `r.sprite` take `footprint` too.\n\n### Heights: roofs, ledges, sight and reach\nOne rule for collision, standing, sight, reach, navigation and drawing, so a game with roofs never writes its own:\n- World units, one tile = 16, z up. A body's `z` is its feet. A cell's top is `map.height(cx, cy)`: 0 on the floor, and a wall is solid from 0 up to its `h` (one column per cell: no passages under things yet).\n- A body stands on the highest top within `z + step` under its footprint (`step` 2, `Body.step`), so a kerb of 2 is walked over and a block of 14 is jumped onto.\n- Eyes are at `z + E.HEIGHTS.eye` (30) and a target is seen at `z + E.HEIGHTS.chest` (22), the heights of a size-1.3 Humanoid. A body's own `eye` and `chest` override them: a crouching hero sets `hero.chest = 8`.\n- \"The same level\" is `|dz| <= E.HEIGHTS.level` (8) unless a call says otherwise. `E.dist` is 3D; `E.inArc` is flat unless you pass `dz`, and `E.overlap` is always flat (pair it with `E.sameLevel`).\n\nThe queries on a `TileMap` (`dir` is an angle in radians or a ground vector `[dx, dy]`; `from` is a body or any `{ x, y, z, r }`):\n- `map.ray(a, b, { eye, target })` walks every cell between two points and returns `{ clear, t, x, y, z }`, where it stops. `map.sight(a, b)` is eyes to chest; `los()` only looks across the ground (a 12-unit planter blocks it, not `sight`).\n- `map.standable(x, y, z, r)`: room to stand, held up, not in deep water.\n- `map.ledge(from, dir, { reach: 18, min: 4, rise: 82, spread: .28 })` → `{ x, y, z, rise, facing, edge: [x, y] }`: the nearest top to pull up onto, and where the hands grip.\n- `map.landing(from, dir, { min: 35, max: 83, rise: 11, drop: 18, apex: 20 })` → `{ x, y, z, d }`: a spot across a gap that a jump arc clears.\n- `map.drop(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, fall }`: what lies past the edge ahead (hop-downs, fall warnings, guards that won't walk off roofs).\n- `map.arcClear(a, b, apex, r)`: a parabola from feet to feet, peaking `apex` above the higher end, clears every top (jumps, throws, aerial takedowns).\n\nReach and perception:\n- `E.targets(from, list, { range, arc, facing, dz, sight: map, filter })` → `[{ target, d, dz, angle }]`, nearest first. `dz` defaults to the same level; `arc` is a half angle around `facing`. Melee auto-aim, lock-on, takedowns and interaction prompts use it.\n- `E.inArc(a, facing, b, range, halfAngle, dz)` and `E.sameLevel(a, b, tol)`.\n\nNavigation: `new E.FlowField(map, { step: 2, climb: 0, drop: 0, jump: 0 })` walks the tops a body can stand on. Next-door cells connect when their tops differ by at most `step`, or by up to `climb` going up and `drop` going down. A guard on a roof gets a field across the roofs; with `climb` and `drop` set, it chases between levels. With `jump` (the widest gap, world units) it also links roofs across a gap a jump clears, and a climb costs more than a leap, so a chase leaps rather than drop to the street and climb back. `flow.next(x, y)` → `{ x, y, z, kind: 'walk' | 'climb' | 'drop' | 'jump', edge }` says where to head: an AI on the ground presses jump for a `climb` or a `jump` once `edge` (the distance to the side of its cell it leaves by) is under its radius plus a little, and `E.Mover` does the climb or the leap.\n\n```js\nconst seen = map.sight(guard, hero) && E.inArc(guard, guard.facing, hero, 120, .7);   // eyes over planters, not through roofs\nif (input.pressed('jump')) {\n  const up = map.ledge(hero, hero.facing);   // a wall to climb in front?\n  const across = !up && map.landing(hero, hero.facing);   // or a gap to clear?\n  if (up) climbTo(up); else if (across) leapTo(across); else hero.jump(210);\n}\nconst prey = E.targets(hero, guards, { range: 22, arc: 1.2, facing: hero.facing, sight: map })[0];   // the same level by default\nif (prey && input.pressed('attack')) takedown(prey.target);\n```\n\n### PlatformMap (side-scrolling levels)\n- Tiles live on the x/z plane; the ASCII top row is the highest.\n- Kinds:\n  - `solid`;\n  - `slope`: `dir: 1` rises to the right, `-1` to the left, `from` / `to` (0..1) for gentle slopes; bodies walk up and down them and step up onto low ledges;\n  - `oneway`: you stand on it and jump up through it, and down + jump drops through;\n  - `ladder`: climbable, and you can stand on its top;\n  - `hazard`: not solid; test it with `touching(body, 'hazard')`;\n  - `deco`: drawn in the play plane;\n  - `back`: a darker background wall.\n- Styles give each tile its look: `stone` (with `moss`), `brick`, `wood`, `metal`, `ground` (grass top when open to the sky, with `flower`), `block` (bevelled), `bonus` (`?` block, `glyph` option), `pipe`, `plank`, `spikes`, `ladder`, `liquid`, `plain`; `mat` gives a solid tile's face any material (`E.MATERIALS`).\n- In `side` the level is flat like an NES or SNES game. In a tilted view with yaw 0 (`brawler` or custom), tiles show top faces and the level becomes a 2.5D diorama.\n- `move(body, dt, solids)` moves any box `{ x, z, vx, vz, w, h }` with sub-steps. It reports:\n  - `onGround` and `onOneWay`;\n  - `hitWall`: -1 left, 1 right, 0 none;\n  - `hitCeiling`;\n  - `bumped`: the tile hit from below;\n  - `ground`: the moving solid it stands on.\n- `solids` are extra boxes (moving platforms, elevators, crates); a body standing on one is carried by its `vx` / `vz`.\n\n### Platformer (side-scroller controller)\n- Defaults feel like a modern NES remaster: run 95, jump 285 (about 3 tiles), gravity 800 rising and 1150 falling, fall cap 330.\n- Forgiving controls: coyote time 0.09 s, jump buffer 0.13 s, jump cut 0.45 when jump is released early.\n- Optional moves: `airJumps` (double jump), `wallJump` (with wall slide), `dash`, `climb` speed.\n- `update(dt, level, input | intent, solids)`. The same controller runs enemies: pass `{ x, jump, jumpPressed }` from your AI.\n- `rigState(extra)` returns the object `Humanoid.update` wants, including the airborne pose.\n\n### Body (ground-plane physics)\n- Velocity with optional `friction` (per second) and `bounce` (off walls and the floor).\n- `push(ix, iy, iz)` adds knockback; `jump(v)` jumps with `gravity`.\n- It lands on the highest walkable surface: the floor, or the top of a block low enough to step onto.\n- Use it for heroes, enemies, bombs, crates, pickups and balls.\n\n### Mover and actions (third-person ground controller)\n`E.Mover` is the ground views' answer to `Platformer`: a `Body` with a third-person game's movement.\n- `const mover = new E.Mover({ x, y, z })` (the index files its members as `mover.`); each step `mover.update(dt, map, game.input)` with `input: 'THIRDPERSON'` (move, Shift sprint, Space jump and parkour, C crouch, J attack, K block, L dodge; touch buttons), or an AI's intent `{ move: [dx, dy], sprint, crouch, jumpPressed }`. Screen directions turn into ground ones through the view.\n- Walk 50, run 85, sprint 125 and sneak 32 with acceleration; a jump with coyote time and a buffer, once a press.\n- A jump press is parkour first: a ledge up to `climbMax` (82) is climbed (`climbUp`; crouch while hanging lets go), a wall up to `vault` (14) is vaulted at a run, a gap ahead is leapt (it aims for the far side's landing), a drop up to `hopMax` (48) is hopped down; otherwise it jumps.\n- Climbing any wall (`climbAny: true`, Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild): a jump press at a wall too tall for a ledge latches on, on the ground or in the air (`mover.wall` is the face, `map.wall(from, dir)`'s: its point, normal, top and facing). The stick climbs as the screen shows it (up the screen is up the wall; an AI's intent: `move: [along, -up]`), it turns outer and inner corners, a jump press leaps up (`climbJump`, which ends on the wall) or, pulled away, pushes off; near the top it pulls up (`climbUp`), at the floor it stands, crouch lets go. Lower `climbMax` (30) so roofs are climbed, not jumped onto. The STEALTH slice and the template do this.\n- Gliding (`glide: true`, Breath of the Wild, Arkham): a jump press in the air, `glideMin` (24) over the ground, opens a sail: it sinks at `glideSink`, flies at `glideSpeed`, takes no fall damage, rises over an updraft (`lift`: a speed, or `m => inUpdraft(m) ? 40 : 0`), and closes on a press, on landing, or at a wall (a climber takes hold). `mover.gliding` says so; the rig hangs under it with its hands up, so draw the sail over them (the template does).\n- Stamina (off until you set `staminaMax`; the template's is 100): climbing, climb jumps, gliding and sprinting spend `mover.stamina` (`tire`), standing refills it (`staminaRegen`); at 0 it lets go of the wall, the glide closes and it can't sprint (`mover.exhausted`) until a third is back. Show it with `ui.hud({ bars: [{ label: 'Stamina', value: mover.stamina, max: mover.staminaMax }] })`.\n- Falls: past `rollAt` (26) at speed it rolls, past `hurtAt` (72) it sets `hurt`. `lastSafe` and `respawn()` bring it back from water and pits (`inHazard`).\n- `mover.rigState(extra)` feeds `rig.update`: its crouch (a sneaking gait), sprint lean, air, landing, or the playing action's pose.\n- Actions (`E.ACTIONS`): `climbUp` (played backwards to climb down), `vault`, `hopDown`, `roll` (invulnerable in its middle), `getUp`, and `takedown`, a paired action that moves two bodies: `mover.play('takedown', { other: guard, aerial, map })`. While `mover.action` plays it moves the body instead of physics; `act.beat`, `act.u`, `act.canCancel`, `act.invulnerable`, `act.otherState()` for the victim's rig. Your own: `E.ACTIONS.slide = { beats: [['go', .4]], setup(A) {}, at(A, beat, u) { return { x, y, z, state } } }`.\n\n### Perception (sight, hearing, suspicion)\n- `E.sees(guard, hero, { map, range: 120, fov: 1.6, eye })` → `{ seen, d, angle, exposure }`: inside the cone, and three points on the hero (feet, chest, head) checked against every top with `map.ray`. Crouching shows less and lower; `hero.exposure` (0..1) is cover you set: tall grass, shadow.\n- `E.hears(guard, { x, y, z, loud: 80 }, map)`: 1 at the noise, 0 at its radius, halved through a wall.\n- `const mind = new E.Suspicion({ rise, decay, notice, search, alert, forget })`; each step `mind.update(dt, E.sees(...), hero)` → `'calm' | 'notice' | 'search' | 'alert'` (it rises faster the nearer and more exposed the hero is), `mind.last` (where it last saw or heard), `mind.changed`. An alert guard that loses the hero searches for `forget` seconds; `mind.hear(strength, at)` notices a noise.\n- Drawing: `r.sightCone(guard, { map, range, fov, color, alpha })` (cut short by walls) and `r.marker(x, y, z, { label, color })`, an objective marker that becomes an arrow at the screen's edge.\n\n### Attack (melee timing)\n- `new E.Attack(spec)` owns the wind-up, active and recover timers of one move; the spec is also the Humanoid attack spec.\n- `start()` begins a swing (pass `true` to chain from recover into a combo); `update(dt)` returns the phase that just began, which is where swing sounds go.\n- `hits(targets, test, fn)` calls `fn` once per target per swing while active. `state` feeds `rig.update({ attack })`.\n- `E.Combo([spec1, spec2, spec3], { window })` chains attacks: `press()` flows into the next hit during recover (or shortly after), otherwise restarts. `E.knockback(from, target, speed, up)` pushes the target away.\n- **Special moves** (input sequences): `game.input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing: hero.facing })` is true on the step a fireball's motion ends: down, down-forward, forward, then the button (numpad notation, as fighting games write moves: `'623'` a dragon punch, `'41236'` a half circle, `'66'` a dash), mirrored when the fighter faces left; the button may land with the last direction, and the whole move must come within `window` seconds (.45). `input.sequence(['up', 'up', 'down', 'down', 'left', 'right', 'left', 'right', 'kick', 'attack'], { window: 3 })` reads any run of inputs (a cheat code, a rhythm pattern); a step joins actions with `+`. Both read `input.history`, the last second of directions (numpad digits) and presses, which an input display can draw.\n\n### Bullets and patterns\n- A pool on the `'ground'` plane (top-down) or the `'side'` plane.\n- Bullets die on walls with a small spark; `hit(targets, fn, team)` tests circles (side targets use their box center).\n- `burst(base, E.pattern.spread(...))` fires a pattern: `dir`, `aim`, `aimSide`, `spread`, `ring`.\n- Bullets glow under GPU lighting. Set `sprite` for custom art and `pierce` to pass through targets.\n\n### Humanoid\n- Proportions: `legUpper legLower hipZ torso shoulderHalf headR armUpper armLower limbW torsoW`.\n- Style: `lean hunch stride swing speedRef`.\n- Gear: `weapon` (`'sword'`, `'gun'`, `'staff'`), `hat` (`'cap'`, `'pointed'`, `'helmet'`, `'band'`, `'crown'`), `hood`, `cape`, `eyeGlow`, `colors`.\n- `update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, dash, hurt, air, point, attack })`.\n- Attacks:\n  - You own the timing: move `phase` from `'wind'` to `'active'` to `'recover'` with your own timers, and pass `u` (0 to 1) within the phase.\n  - `spec.a0` / `a1` are hand angles relative to facing; `z0` / `z1` are heights.\n  - `spin: true` swings a full circle, `kick: true` swings the right foot, `blade: 0` hides the smear.\n- `hand()` and `tip()` give world points for spawning bullets or sparks; `worldOffset(p)` gives where any point in the rig's own frame (a joint in `J`) sits from its feet. `debug(r)` draws the skeleton.\n\n### Audio\n- Browsers start sound only after the first key press or click; the engine waits for it.\n- `sfx(name)` ignores the same sound fired twice in 35 ms, and varies the pitch slightly so repeats don't sound robotic.\n- `music(song)` loops tracks of its own step notation. Every track loops on its own length, so short drum or bass loops can run under long melodies.\n- Built-in songs: `title`, `adventure`, `dungeon`, `boss`, `victory` (plays once). The songs module's songbook has more by mood (`E.Songbook.find('town')`) and jingles.\n- The music clock (the full engine): a song's place is game time (`audio.pos`), not the sound's, so beats land on the same update step headless and on any device, and the sound is held to that clock (it moves its notes to meet it). Time gameplay to `audio.pos`, `audio.at(step, fn)` and `audio.onBeat(fn)`, never to the sound. A song with `sync: true` pauses with the game.\n- Dynamic music: one song in layers rather than cuts. Name the tracks, start the extra ones with `on: false`, and bring them in with `audio.layer('drums', 1, .5)` (the stealth slice's chase); `audio.tempo(bpm)` for a hurry-up; `audio.sting(song, { at: 'bar' })` for a jingle on the next bar; `music(song, { fade: 1 })` to crossfade.\n\n### Text and UI\n- `E.font` is a 5x7 proportional pixel font with lower case (the old 3x5 font is `font: 'tiny'`), with `scale`, `align`, `wrap`, outline, drop `shadow` and `gradient`; `E.font.title` draws extruded gradient logos.\n- `E.ui.box/bar/hearts` draw classic windows and meters inside `r.overlay`.\n- `Dialog` does typewriter text with blips, pages, speaker names and choices; `update` returns true while it is open, so the world waits. Its markup gives a word its color (`{c=#ffd36a}gold{/c}`), a beat before the next line (`{w=.5}`), a slow dread (`{s=12}...{/s}`), a scream that shakes (`{shake}`) or a song that bobs (`{wave}`), and the hero's name (`{$name}` from `vars`).\n- `Menu` handles up and down with key repeat, confirm, cancel and disabled items.\n\n### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\nA modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild) wants readable HTML screens over the pixel world, not pixel windows. `E.Shell` builds them from data, so you write none of the HTML, CSS or focus handling. It shows the game's state and holds none of it: you pass values in each step and callbacks for the buttons. The STEALTH slice (`src/starter/70-stealth.js`: the objective, health, status, the takedown prompt, toasts, the results) and the template game use it; copy from them.\n- `const ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme: { ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font, titleFont }, settings: { items: [{ key: 'cones', label: 'Sight cones', value: true }], onChange(s, key) {} }, screens: { pause: {...}, help: {...} } })`. It takes over from the engine's own and keeps the standard menu (see Standard features). Your items join `game.settings` (`ui.settings` is the same object), saved and checked with the rest; `onChange` hears every change.\n- Screens: `ui.show(name, spec)`, `ui.back()`, `ui.hide()`, `ui.screen` (the open one's name, or null). Every spec takes `kicker`, `title`, `text` (strings or functions), `actions: [[label, fn], { label, act, primary, disabled }]` (falsy entries are skipped, so `cond && [...]` works; `act` taps an input action) and `back`. Then: `'title'` (`name`, `subtitle`, `meta`, `footer`), `'pause'` (Resume first, then your actions, then Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help and Quit to title; it opens while `game.paused` and Resume unpauses; `screens: { pause: false }` leaves it out), `'settings'`, `'controls'`, `'help'` (`keys: [[key, what]]`, `sections: [[heading, text]]`), `'results'` (`emblem`, `stats: [[value, label]]`, `medals: [[label, earned]]`) and `'map'` (`map`, `at`, `markers`, `labels`, `onPick(x, y)` for a waypoint). A screen opened from another stacks; cancel goes back.\n- The HUD, each step: `ui.hud({ objective: { step, title, text, at, from }, status, bars: [{ label, value, max, kind: 'pips' | 'bar', color }], counts: [[label, value]], prompt: { key, label, hint, progress }, tip, place, keys, buttons: [[label, fn]], minimap: { map, at, markers, onClick } })`. A piece left out is hidden, only what changed is redrawn, and the HUD hides while a screen is open. `objective` shows the distance in meters (`meters: .125` a world unit) and whether the target is above or below. `ui.toast(title, text, seconds)` and `ui.tutorial(title, text, seconds)` come and go on their own.\n- Input: the game's own input moves the focus (keys, d-pad, stick: up / down / left / right, a slider or a choice turns left and right), confirm presses, cancel goes back, pause closes the menu; touch taps. While a screen is open the game's actions are held back, so a menu press doesn't also attack. Touch players reach the menu with the touch START or the corner's button; `buttons: [['Map', openMap]]` adds your own.\n- It honors reduced motion and the safe-area insets, is headless-safe (it keeps its screens' names and draws nothing), and `game.dispose()` takes it away.\n\n### Lighting\n- Light every scene (the Look target's step 9): a dark game with no lights reads flat, and a judge says so. Canvas lighting leads because it draws the same in every browser and every headless check: set `lights.enabled` and `ambient` (.4-.6 for dusk and interiors) in the scene's `enter`, then call `lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity, { color })` every frame where light would come from (lamps, torches, windows, the objective's glow). Light is measured on the ground in ground views, and on screen in side views and for PlatformMaps. The template game and the STEALTH slice show it. Its pass costs a few milliseconds a frame at 320x240 (more on a phone): a heavy scene can keep it for a computer, `matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches`, as the STEALTH slice does.\n- GPU lighting (`game.enableGPU({ map })`) needs WebGPU and a TileMap. It adds:\n  - colored lights, and soft shadows cast by walls and `lights.caster(...)`;\n  - light that wraps around characters;\n  - glow from `px.glow`, emissive decals and actors, and `[r, g, b, glow]` floor texels;\n  - bloom and heat shimmer (`lights.heat`).\n- Where GPU lighting can't draw (no WebGPU, still loading, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with `lights.ambient` and the same lights: set both ambients (`gpu.ambient = [r, g, b]`, `lights.ambient`) so the two look alike (the ADVENTURE slice does); `gpu.fallback = false` leaves such frames unlit. Tune the settings, not the shaders.\n\n### Errors and warnings\n- Runtime errors appear in a red box (click to hide) and in the console, labeled with `update` or `draw` and the scene.\n- Setup errors (before `game.start`) are shown too.\n- One-time `my-3D2dge:` warnings flag:\n  - unknown views, actions, sounds, songs, resolutions and input presets;\n  - legend characters, wall or tile types missing from `types`;\n  - uneven level rows and non-hex colors;\n  - lights with bad numbers, and PlatformMaps drawn in the wrong view.\n- The checker prints them all.\n\n### Testing without a browser\nA game runs in Node with no screen, so its rules can be tested in a second, exactly:\n- `new E.Game()` is headless where there is no DOM (or with `{ headless: true }`): no screen, renderer, sound or DOM input; scenes update and `draw` is skipped. Sprites made while the game loads get a stand-in canvas.\n- `game.advance(seconds)` runs exact fixed steps at once: `advance(1)` is 120 updates. In a page, `new E.Game({ loop: false })` waits for `advance` too, and `advance(s, { draw: true })` draws a frame after.\n- Input: `game.input.tap('jump')` presses an action for one step (`pressed` and `down` in the next step, `released` in the one after); `hold('right')` holds it until `hold('right', false)`; `press(code)` and `release(code)` press keys.\n- `game.debug = { snapshot() { return { scene, hero, score } }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }`: your game's handle. Tests read it; `tools/check.mjs` prints it after play; `tools/replay.mjs` logs it at the end of a replay.\n- `game.stop()` stops the loop, `game.start()` resumes it, `game.dispose()` ends the game and removes every listener the engine added to the page.\n\n```js\n// game/test.mjs in the handoff package: load the engine and the game into one context, then drive it\nconst ctx = { console }; vm.createContext(ctx);\nfor (const f of ['../my-3d2dge.js', './game.js']) vm.runInContext(readFileSync(new URL(f, import.meta.url), 'utf8'), ctx);\nconst game = ctx.My3D2dge.current;\nconst I = game.input; I.tap('start'); game.advance(.5);\ngame.debug.teleport(100, 88, 0, 0); I.tap('jump'); game.advance(.1);   // facing the roof's wall: jump takes hold of it\nI.hold('up'); game.advance(.6); I.hold('up', false); game.advance(1);   // up the screen is up the wall: it pulls up at the top\nif (game.debug.snapshot().hero[2] !== 32) throw new Error('the hero should have climbed onto the roof');\n```\n\n### The handoff package\n`dist/handoff/` (on the site: `/handoff/`; `node tools/handoff.mjs --zip <file>` zips it for a chat) holds what a coding agent needs to build on the full engine without the repository: both editions, each module on its own (`modules/`), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, this guide, the tools that check, film and replay a game, the asset card with its tools and the store, a `package.json` pinning Playwright, `game/` (a template game with a headless test) and `kits/` (each 2D genre kit's game, readable, with the page that plays it and a headless test). Its `README.md` says where to start; `tools/build.mjs` writes it from `src/handoff/`. It leaves out the 3D engine and its kits (on the site and in the repository).\n\n### Modules (general systems in files of their own)\nA module (`engine/modules/<name>.js`) holds one general system that several genres share, outside the core, so that only the games that use it carry it. It loads after the engine, in a `<script>` of its own, and attaches its names to `My3D2dge`; its header is its manual (what it holds, its API, a complete example, its rules), and the index has a section for it. Its manual's third line says whether it also loads after the agent edition, and which modules it needs first.\n- **In a single file:** the reference editions (`dist/my-3d2dge.html`, the compact one) carry every module; a kit carries the modules its slice names in its header (`@modules grid gen`). To use one a kit hasn't got, paste the module's file in a `<script>` after the engine's (if the console then names a section the kit's engine left out, start from the reference edition).\n- **With files:** `dist/my-3d2dge.js` (`my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package) is the core and every module in one file; the handoff package also has each module on its own (`modules/<name>.js`), to load after the agent edition (the twelve that can).\n- **The modules:** `grid` (square and hex grids: A* paths, move ranges with climb and drop past allies, lines, symmetric fields of view, `E.Fog`, flood fills and groups, tactics' shapes of effect, facing and threat maps, `E.GridMover` for Pac-Man's and Sokoban's moves, `E.Turns` by speed, charge time, phase or ATB), `gen` (floors from a seed: rooms and corridors, BSP, caves, mazes, Poisson scatter, as ASCII rows a `TileMap` reads, with `E.gen.mark` for spawns), `rooms` (a world of rooms on a grid of screens: doors at open edges, a slide or a fade between them, the explored map, gates and saves: `E.World`), `arcade` (the coin-op's frame: a score that floats up where it was won, extra lives at thresholds, two players taking turns, credits and a continue, high-score tables with initials on a letter wheel, stage cards, the attract mode), `mode7` (Super Mario Kart's and F-Zero's view: a camera over a flat floor drawn a row at a time, the sky turning with it, billboards scaled by distance, `E.Mode7`) and `road` (OutRun's road: segments with curves, hills and tunnels, scenery and cars scaled by distance, `E.Road`) and `drive` (cars with grip, drift and the ground's tag, races with laps, checkpoints, drivers and items: `E.Car`, `E.Race`), `story` (dialogue as data: lines, choices with conditions, flags, quests, events, emote balloons: `E.Story`), `rpg` (items, bags, members with levels and gear, status, a party, shops, damage, encounters, windows: `E.RPG`), `physics` (2D rigid bodies: circles, boxes, polygons, chains; flippers, bumpers, kickers: `E.Physics`), `terrain` (destructible ground in the side plane: carve, walk, ray: `E.Terrain`) and `songs` (the songbook: `E.Songbook`): these twelve load after the agent edition too (a race's `E.Path` is the full engine's). Four load after the full engine only: `fight` (one-on-one fighting: `E.Fighter`, `E.Match`, `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`, training), `click` (point-and-click: `E.Click`, `E.Walkbox`), `rhythm` (`E.Rhythm`: charts, judgments, holds, calls) and `strategy` (`E.Strategy`, `E.Clock`, `E.needs`; it needs `grid`).\n- **Writing one:** `engine/modules/README.md` (the header's form, the rules, its budget of 1,500 tokens; `tools/modules-test.mjs` proves the path with a fixture). `docs/KITS.md` says which systems become modules, and what every kit has.\n\n### Kits and the diet (which file for which game)\nA genre kit (`dist/kits/my-3d2dge-<genre>.html`) is the engine, the API card and one slice, sized for a chat model's context: a 2D kit is at most 200,000 tokens, six of them up to about 241,000 (`doctrine-check` counts them). The handoff package's `kits/` holds each 2D kit's game readable, on the full engine. Its engine is on the **kit diet**: the core less the sections its game never calls (the tile map in the platformer's kit, the third-person kit and GPU lighting in most), as the kit's header lists. Calling a left-out name warns once in the console, naming its section (a constructor throws). So:\n- **A game in the kit's genre:** start from the kit; anything its slice uses is there.\n- **A game of several genres** (a platformer with a top-down overworld, an RPG with a shoot-'em-up stage), or one that needs a section a kit left out: start from `dist/my-3d2dge.html` (every slice and section, readable) or `dist/my-3d2dge-compact.html` (the same, minified), or `dist/my-3d2dge.js` with your own page.\n- **The presentation sections** (Penner's easings beyond the core's, sprite frames and turns, tweens and scripts, paths, transitions, weather, input sequences, the camera's window and framing) are each a part of the diet: a kit carries the ones its slice uses, and a kit whose slice uses none carries about 1,600 tokens of them in its engine (the screen filter, a standard setting, and the small ways in) and 1,000 in its card.\n- **Its proof:** each slice has `run(n)` (`__game.scenes.<id>.run(600)`): n fixed steps of scripted play from a seed, returning a hash that is the same every time; `node kits/test.mjs` in the handoff package runs each kit's twice, headless (the repository's `tools/kits-test.mjs`, in a browser). A game copied from a slice can keep one: the shell's `proof()` is how.\n\n### Recorded sessions (a bug from a phone, replayed exactly)\n- `E.session.record(true)` turns recording on for every later page load (`?record=1` does it for one load). It starts before the game's code and keeps everything a game's next frame depends on:\n  - a seed for `Math.random`;\n  - each display frame's time (`performance.now` and `Date.now` read it during that frame);\n  - every input and the frame it arrived before: keys, pointer and touch, wheel, clicks and form edits, focus, visibility, resize, full screen;\n  - gamepad readings, the media queries the game asked, the safe-area insets, the screen, and the save (`localStorage`) at the start;\n  - a checksum every 60 frames.\n- `E.session.share()` saves the session: the share sheet on a phone, a download elsewhere. In Emberdeep it is Settings → Tools → Record a session; on any recorded page the standard pause menu has Save session (a phone has no console). `node tools/replay.mjs <file> --pacing` reads the device's refreshes from it without replaying: the refresh, the ones missed second by second, how unevenly (a report of choppy play).\n- `node tools/replay.mjs session.json` replays it in headless Chromium at the recorded screen size, frame by frame, and reports the first checksum that differs. Useful options:\n  - `--to 1800` stops at a frame; `--shots 600,1200` saves screenshots;\n  - `--log \"My3D2dge.current.sceneName\"` prints any state at the end (a game's `game.debug.snapshot()` prints by itself);\n  - `--page` replays on another page. Fix the bug, rebuild, and replay the same session to see it gone.\n- `game.stateHash = () => [hero.x, hero.y, hero.hp, foes.length]` adds a game's own state to the checksum, so a replay stops at the first frame where that state differs.\n- Why it works: the clock turns the same frame times into the same fixed steps, so the same frame times, inputs and seed give the same game.\n- Any browser: while recording and replaying, `Math.sin`, `cos`, `tan`, `atan`, `atan2`, `asin`, `acos`, `exp`, `log`, `log2`, `log10`, `pow`, `hypot` and `cbrt` are the engine's portable versions (`E.session.math`).\n  - Browsers may round these differently in the last bit: an iPhone's Safari and Chromium use different libraries.\n  - The portable versions are built only from `+ - * /` and `sqrt`, which are exact everywhere.\n  - So a session recorded in one browser replays in another bit for bit.\n  - `sin`, `cos`, `atan`, `atan2`, `exp` and `log` are fdlibm's algorithms, the same bits as Chromium's own.\n  - The others are within a few units in the last place.\n  - `node tools/cross-engine-math.cjs` checks this in Safari's own engine. It runs the same code in V8 and in JavaScriptCore, through Bun (`BUN=/path/to/bun`).\n  - Native math gives different bits in the two engines, and so does a Humanoid animated with it.\n  - The portable math, and the same Humanoid with it, give the same bits in both.\n- Limits:\n  - the GPU lighting is off while recording, because it starts up on real time;\n  - a session is one page load;\n  - replay on the build it was recorded on: its version and build are in the file.\n\n## Genre recipes\nEach recipe names the kit to start from (`kits/<id>.js` in the handoff package). The 3D ones build on the 3D engine, whose kits are on the site and in the repository, not in the handoff package.\n\n**Side-scroller (Mario, Mega Man, Castlevania).**\n```js\nconst level = new E.PlatformMap({ rows: LEVEL, legend: { '#': 1, '=': 2, '?': 3, '@': 'hero', 'g': 'walker' },\n  types: { 1: { style: 'ground', side: '#8a5a32' }, 2: { kind: 'oneway' }, 3: { style: 'bonus', side: '#e8a830' } } });\nconst s = level.find('hero'), hero = new E.Platformer({ x: s.x, z: s.z }), rig = new E.Humanoid({ weapon: 'gun', hat: 'cap' });\n// scene: { view: 'side', views: ['side', 'brawler'], input: 'PLATFORMER', res: 'snes', pausable: true, ... }\n// update: hero.update(dt, level, game.input, lifts); if (hero.jumped) game.audio.sfx('jump');\n//         if (hero.bumped) { ...change level.set(cx, cz, id)... }\n//         rig.update(dt, hero.rigState({ point: game.input.down('attack') })); game.focus(hero.x, 0, hero.z + 24);\n// draw:   r.sky([...]); level.draw(r); r.actor(hero.x, 0, hero.z, (g, ox, oy) => rig.draw(g, ox, oy, r.view));\n```\n- Enemies are boxes moved by `level.move(e, dt)` with your own gravity: turn at `hitWall`, and at ledges using `groundBelow`.\n- Stomp when `hero.vz < 0` and the hero's feet are above the enemy's middle.\n- Pits: `hero.z < -40`. Spikes: `level.touching(hero, 'hazard')`.\n- Further moves, on request: `new E.Platformer({ crouch: true, ledge: true, wallCling: true, momentum: true })`; tiles of `kind: 'water' | 'rope' | 'bars'`, and grip on any tile type: `friction` (ice: .15), `carry` (a conveyor's speed), `bounce` (a spring), `crumble` with `respawn` (call `level.update(dt)` each step); `hero.swingFrom(x, z)` hangs it from a grapple point. Draw `level.drawWater(r)` after the actors; `rigState()` poses every move (hang, climb, crawl, swim, swing).\n\n**Metroidvania (Metroid, Super Metroid, Symphony of the Night).** Start from the METROIDVANIA slice (the rooms module).\n- The world is an `E.World` of PlatformMaps: each room its rows and `at: [gx, gy]` on a grid of screens; rooms meet where they touch, and an open edge is a door (leave walls elsewhere). After the hero moves, `world.update(dt, hero)` returns `'enter'` on the step it crosses into the next room: spawn that room's creatures then (from `world.map.spawns`); hold the game while `world.moving` (the slide).\n- Abilities open the way: `world.give('boots')` then `hero.airJumps = world.has('boots') ? 1 : 0` (and `dash`); a gate is a tile type with `gate: 'dash'`, solid until given. The map: `world.drawMap(g, x, y, cell)` on a map screen and, clipped round `world.here`, in a corner. Saves: `world.save(hero)` at a station, `world.load()` on the next visit.\n- An arm cannon aimed eight ways (the run-and-gun recipe): from `game.input.move()`, up aims up, up and forward the diagonal, down in the air (and forward) down or its diagonal, else forward; `rig.update(dt, hero.rigState({ point: true, aim }))` with `aim` the angle above level (`Math.PI / 2` straight up); shots leave `rig.tip()` on `new E.Bullets(game, { plane: 'side' })`. Holding fire charges a bigger shot; a held button stands still to aim.\n- Bosses are state machines: a telegraph (a glow, a warning line) before each attack, an opening when they can be hurt (armor turns shots otherwise); seal the room's doors while one lives and keep both in view with `game.cam.frame([hero, boss])`.\n\n**Zelda-like adventure.**\n- Build rooms with `TileMap` rows and set `game.cam.room = [roomW, roomH]`.\n- The hero is a `Body` moved by `game.view.screenDirToGround(...game.input.move())`. A sword swing is an `E.Attack` with `hits(foes, f => E.inArc(...), ...)`.\n- Talk with `Dialog`, and open a locked door with `map.set(cx, cy, 0)` after checking a key count. Only run enemies in the hero's room, and freeze everything while `cam.moving`.\n\n**Beat-'em-up (Final Fight, Streets of Rage).** Start from the BRAWLER slice.\n- Use the `brawler` view with a long `TileMap` floor strip (walls along the top edge only) and `cam.bounds` that scroll right.\n- Heroes and thugs are `Body` + `Humanoid` with `weapon: null`. Punches are attack specs with `blade: 0`; kicks use `kick: true, z0: 6, z1: 9`.\n- Hits need `E.inArc` plus a small y difference (same lane). Knock down with `body.push(dx, dy, 120)` and let gravity drop them.\n- A second player drops in (`game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin })`): a hero of its own driven by its input, foes going for the nearer hero, `cam.frame(heroes)` instead of `follow`, game over when both are out.\n\n**Fighting game (Street Fighter II, Darkstalkers).** Start from the FIGHTING slice (the fight module).\n- Each fighter is an `E.Fighter`: a roster entry's rig (a Humanoid sized about 2.2 to 2.7 at 320x240) and its moves, each an `E.MOVES` name with frame data in frames at 60 (`f: [startup, active, recovery]`), damage, hit- and block-stun, a guard (`'low'` sweeps, `'overhead'` jump-ins and axe kicks), `cancel: true` on normals a special may cut short, and specials by motion (`motion: '236'`, `'623'`, `'41236'`, `'236236'` for a super with `meter: 100`) or charge (`charge: '46'`). `shot` makes a projectile, `throw` a command grab, `teleport` a teleport, `vx` and `vz` a move that travels, `inv` invulnerable frames, `aa: true` an anti-air the CPU reaches for.\n- `E.Match` runs the rounds, the clock, KO and time over, the calls and the HUD; `E.FightAI({ level, seed })` is a CPU whose matches repeat with its seed; `E.FightSelect` is the character select; `training: { dummy }` draws the boxes, the frame data and the inputs. Update the match once a step (motions are read at 120 Hz) and draw the stage before `match.draw(r)`.\n- Two players: `game.allowJoin({ max: 2 })` and `inputs: [game.input, game.players[1]]` (a pad each, or half a keyboard each); the select and the menus read every player.\n\n**Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius).** Start from the SHOOTER slice: its first stage is vertical, its second horizontal.\n- Use `view: 'overhead'` so world x/y equal screen pixels; `res: [240, 320]` for a vertical stage, `[320, 240]` for a horizontal one, with `expand`.\n- Ships are sprites or vector parts drawn by code; a timeline array spawns waves by time or by how far the camera has come; enemies move on formulas (sine lines, dives aimed with `E.pattern.aim`) or along `E.Path`s.\n- Flyers keep up with the camera: each step add the scroll to their position (and to the bullets'), while the ground and the rock stay put.\n- Horizontal stages (Gradius, R-Type): the rock above and below is two functions of x (`highY(x)`, `lowY(x)`) drawn in columns and tested for crashes; a power bar is a cursor that capsules move along, a button taking the lit slot; options follow the ship's past path (a ring of its positions, an option every 12 behind); a mid-boss loops a closed `E.Path` while the scroll waits.\n- Bosses fire `E.pattern.ring` and `spread`, each attack telegraphed. A bomb clears `shots.clear('foe')`. The end: `E.Arcade`'s continue, then initials and the table (the arcade module).\n\n**Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids).** Start from the ARCADE slice: a cabinet of three.\n- One fixed screen (`res: [232, 270]` or `[320, 240]` with `expand`): centre the playfield and fill the rest with cabinet art drawn by code, or let the field be the whole screen (the rocks wrap at its edges).\n- `new E.Arcade(game, { id, lives: 3, extra: [10000] })` keeps the score, lives, two players' turns (each one's stage in `p.save`), the continue, the tables with initials and the attract mode; `cab.update(dt)` each step and `cab.draw(r, box)` after the game.\n- A maze is an `E.Grid` with `E.GridMover`s: the player's turn asked early is kept (`{ keep: true }`); a chaser picks, at each cell, the open way nearest its own target (never straight back). Moths fly in on `E.Path`s and turn with `r.sprite`'s `rot`; rocks drift with `E.wrap` and draw at every edge they cross.\n- Each game's demo is its own bot, playing the real rules; a remaster (light, rigs, glow) with the classic look one key and one setting away.\n\n**Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero).** Start from the KART slice (the mode7 and drive modules).\n- The track is a TileMap: its walls bounce the karts, its floor tags (`grass`, `sand`, `boost`, ...) slow or boost them (`E.Car`'s `surfaces`), and `E.Mode7({ floor: (x, y) => [r, g, b] })` draws it from the same cells. The racing line is a closed `E.Path` through the track's middle: `E.Race({ path, laps: 3 })` counts laps through its checkpoints in order (a shortcut counts nothing), gives places, and drives every car not a player's (`race.add(car, { input })` for a player, `{ ai: { skill } }` for the rest).\n- Each step `race.update(dt, map)` (it moves every car), then the camera behind yours, turning after it more slowly than the kart turns (a drift shows): `m7.cam = cam; m7.chase({ x, y, angle: camAngle }, { back: 42, h: 24 })`. Draw the karts with `m7.add(x, y, z, (g, sx, sy, k) => ...)`: a few boxes through `m7.view(k)`'s projection and the rig seated (`down: .4`) in the middle, sorted far to near from the camera's side.\n- Two players: `game.allowJoin({ max: 2 })`, `game.splitScreen(2, { layout: 'rows' })`, and in `draw` each viewport its own camera: `m7.draw(r, cams[r.viewport.i])`, `race.hud(r, players[r.viewport.i])`. On a phone the gas goes on by itself (`car.o.auto`), the stick steers, DRIFT and ITEM are buttons; held upright, draw from further back with a wider field of view.\n\n**Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Hang-On).** Start from the ROAD slice (the road module).\n- `E.Road` is the course: `road.add(n, { curve, hill, tunnel, colors })` stretch by stretch; scenery with `road.place(z, offset, sprite, scale)` (`E.prop('palm').frames[0]` works), traffic as `cars: [{ z, x, w, draw }]` passed to `road.draw`. The player is a position (`z` along, `x` across in half roads) and a speed, not a body.\n- Each step: `x -= road.curveAt(z) * speed² * k` throws the car outward on a bend (make the hardest faster than the player can steer: they slow down), off the road (`|x| > 1`) it slows, `road.hits(z, x, w)` crashes it into the scenery (put it back on the road), and a car close in `z` and `x` bumps it. A clock that counts down and checkpoints that add time make the race.\n\n**Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA).** `E.Car` on a TileMap in the `topdown` view: the same cars, laps and drivers as the kart (`E.Race`), drawn from above (a box of `px.poly` on `r.w()` corner points, turned by `car.angle`); GTA's car is a `E.Car` the hero gets into (`kart: false`: drift is a handbrake).\n\n**JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy).** Start from the RPG slice (the story and rpg modules).\n- The field is a scene of `TileMap`s: a town, a world map (past 128 x 128 cells its floor bakes in chunks: paint woods and peaks into `floorTex`, block them), dungeons; a map's floor tag leads to another (an exit, a cave's mouth). Walk a `Body` with the stick; the other members follow the leader's trail.\n- People talk through an `E.Story`: their lines as data, with choices, flags, quests, emote balloons and events for the game's scripts (a shop's window, the inn's night, a boss's fight; `wait: true` holds the talk until `story.resume()`).\n- The party is an `E.RPG` party: members with stats, levels and gear, a bag, gold; shops, items and `reward(troop)` after a fight. Encounters by zone (`rpg.encounters`) as the party walks: a swirl (`game.go(battle, data, { fade: 'swirl', at })`) into the battle scene, which reads the members' `stat()` and writes their hp back.\n- Battles are a scene in the `brawler` view with rigs lined up, a `Menu` of commands and a queue of actions (the RPG slice's ATB battle). Each action steps forward, swings, applies damage and steps back.\n- Saves: `game.saves` (the party's `state()`, the story's `state()`, the map and the place); a crystal to save at, the inn's autosave, Continue and Load on the title. The menu's windows are `rpg.list` and `rpg.card`, rows a thumb's height on a phone.\n\n**Puzzle (Tetris, Dr. Mario, Puyo Puyo).** Start from the PUZZLE slice.\n- The field is an array of cells drawn in screen pixels (`r.overlay`) over a painted stage; every rule is a small function: spawn, fits, kicks (SRS), lock (half a second, reset by a move up to 15 times), clear, gravity (the guideline's curve by level). A bag of seven from `E.rng`, a next queue, hold, a ghost.\n- `input.repeat('left', .17, .05)` gives the delayed auto-shift. Touch: `input.steer = 'drag'` and `input.drag()` move a cell a step of the thumb, `input.clicked()` (a tap) turns, a quick flick drops or holds.\n- Versus: one field's state swapped in and out (save and load its variables) to step and draw two side by side from one seed, each read through its own player's input; two lines or more cleared send rows to the other, rising at its next piece.\n- Matching games (Puyo, Columns, match-3): `new E.Grid(w, h).groups((x, y) => field[y * w + x] || null)` gives the groups of one colour; pop those of four or more, let what is above fall, and count each round that pops as a chain.\n\n**Roguelike (Rogue, Mystery Dungeon, Shiren).** Start from the ROGUELIKE slice.\n- A floor is `E.gen.rooms(46, 30, { seed })` (or `bsp`, `cave`), its spawns put in with `E.gen.mark(level, 'g', 5)` and read by a `TileMap` legend; `E.Grid.from(map, { diagonal: true })` gives `path`, `reach` and `fov`, and `E.Fog` covers what isn't in sight (`fog.draw(r, 16, { height })` over the walls too).\n- `E.Turns([hero, ...foes], { mode: 'speed' })`: the hero acts on a key, then `next()` runs every monster's turn until the hero's comes round again (speed 200 acts twice); a monster walks its next step of `grid.path` toward the hero, or strikes when it is next to it.\n- The game is turn-based but its pictures are not: each step is `game.tween(actor, { x, y }, .13)` and the rigs walk through it. The stairs are `game.go(scene, { depth }, { fade: 'iris', at: hero })`. A bot (`bot()` in the slice) that plays to the end is the best test: pathing, fighting and items all exercised.\n\n**Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Tactics Ogre, Fire Emblem).** Start from the TACTICS slice (the grid and story modules).\n- The map is a `TileMap` whose cells are all blocks at their heights (a base of 16 so the earth shows at its edges; a level is `E.HEIGHTS.level`, 8), in the `iso` view turned a quarter at a time (`game.rotateView(90)`) and zoomed (`camera: { zoom }`). `E.Grid.from(map, { tops: true, cost })` stands units on the tops (shallows `cost` 2).\n- The cursor: keys step it on the grid (turn the screen's direction into the grid's with `view.screenDirToGround`), and `map.pick(sx, sy)` finds the cell under the pointer or a tap in any view, turn and zoom (the top or wall face the line of sight meets first). The camera looks at the cursor's top.\n- A move range is `grid.reach(cell, move, { climb: jump * 8, drop, blocked: foes, pass: allies })` and `reach.path(cell)` the walk (hop a level with a little arc); tints go on the tops with `E.tint(r, cells, color, alpha, 16, (x, y) => map.height(x, y))`, under the units.\n- Blows: `grid.side(target, facing, from)` gives front, side or back (x1, x1.25, x1.5), the height between them a bonus; areas are `grid.shape(cell, 'diamond' | 'cross' | 'cone' | 'line', n, dir)`; a bow's arc is `map.arcClear(from, to, apex)`, reaching further from higher up.\n- Turns: `E.Turns(units, { mode: 'ct' })` (next(), then done(unit, 100 for a move and an act, 80 one, 60 neither); `order(n)` is the queue to show), or phases (each side in turn, the player's units in any order).\n- The AI scores every cell it can reach and every blow from there (damage, kills, heals, friendly fire against it) less the danger of the place (`grid.threat(foes)`), and takes the best; the same state gives the same turn, so a bot that plays the battle with it is the test.\n\n**Point-and-click adventure (Monkey Island, Day of the Tentacle, Broken Sword).** Start from the POINT-AND-CLICK slice (the click and story modules). The click module (the full engine) keeps the rooms, the walking, the verbs, the inventory and the rules; the game paints the rooms and writes the puzzles.\n- Each room is a picture painted once in room pixels (`paint(g)` with `px` helpers: dithered skies, planks, stones; `pad: [120, 160]` paints margins a wide or tall screen shows), a walk area (`walk: [outline, ...holes]`, an `E.Walkbox`: the hero takes the shortest way round a table or a lamp), `depth: [yFar, kFar, yNear, kNear]` (smaller far away), and `things` drawn by depth (a bar counter in front of the barkeep).\n- Hotspots name what can be clicked: a rectangle, a polygon or an actor (a rig), where the hero stands and faces, its verbs (a line, a function, or actions: `{ give, take, set, say, talk, go }`), `if: '!rope'` to hide it once taken, `exit: 'tavern'` for a door. Puzzles are rules as data: `{ item: 'rope', on: 'hook', take: ['rope', 'hook'], give: 'grapple' }` (an item on an item works either way round); the first that matches runs, else the hotspot's own verb, else a stock line.\n- Talk goes through `E.Story` (`talk: { talk: 'morwen' }`): nodes, choices, flags; a conversation to win is a node whose right answers goto the next and wrong ones end it, and an event gives the prize. The interface is a setting: `ui: 'verbs'` (SCUMM's nine), `'icons'` (Sierra's: a right click turns the cursor; bind `look: ['Mouse2']`), `'coin'`, `'two'`. A phone taps: a tap walks, a tap on a hotspot opens its verbs, a long press looks. `pc.panel = false` hides the verbs for a cutscene. A bot clicks through the whole chain with `input.feed` on `pc.where(id)` and `pc.boxes`: the slice's test.\n\n**Visual novel (Phoenix Wright, Snatcher, Clannad).** A painted backdrop per scene (`r.overlay`), the speaker's rig large (`rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 120, { zoom: 2 })`, its expression by `rig.update(dt, { expr: 'smile' })`), and the story module's nodes and choices for the script, its flags for the routes. `story.log` is the backlog (`{ who, name, text }`: draw the last lines when the player asks), `story.auto = 2` turns pages by themselves after two seconds, `story.skip = true` types and turns them at once (choices still wait). Save with `game.saves` (`story.state()` in its data).\n\n**Real-time strategy (Dune II, Warcraft II, Command & Conquer, StarCraft).** Start from the RTS slice (the grid and strategy modules). The strategy module (the full engine, after the grid module) keeps the rules; the game draws.\n- `const rts = new E.Strategy(game, { map, kinds, sides, player })`: kinds are data (a unit's hp, speed, attack, range, sight, cost and time; a building's size, what it makes, a depot; a resource's amount). `rts.spawn` places them (a building blocks its cells, so flow fields go round it); `rts.update(dt)` and `rts.input()` each step.\n- The camera is free (`game.cam.free = { bounds }`): keys, the screen's edge, a drag on open ground, a zoom toward the pointer. Draw each unit with `{ pick: u }` (`r.actor`, `r.sprite`) so a click finds what is drawn under it (`r.pick`), and a crowd with `every: 3, key: u` (each body redrawn every third frame: 200 hold a phone's frame), then `rts.draw(r)` for the rings, the bars and the box; `ui.hud({ minimap: rts.minimap() })`.\n- On a phone: a tap selects or orders, a drag pans, a long press and a drag draws a box, a pinch zooms. The enemy is data too: `rts.ai('red', { build: [['worker', 5], ['barracks'], ['soldier', 6]], waves: { first, every, min } })`, the same game for the same orders (a match is its own proof).\n- Buildings are `E.SHAPES` entries drawn with `r.shape(id, x, y, 0, { pick: u, mats: { team: '#3a6ad0 cloth' } })` (one entry, each side's colors); a site going up is a shape of its own. The enemy's plan keeps counts (`['footman', 8]`): what it loses it makes again. A bot that gives your side an AI of its own plays the match to its end, the same each time: the slice's test.\n\n**Theme park or city sim (Theme Park, RollerCoaster Tycoon, SimCity).** Start from the SIM slice (the grid and strategy modules).\n- Paths are the TileMap's floor tags (`map.setFloor(cx, cy, 'path')`); guests walk an `E.Grid` whose walkable cells are the path (`grid.path` to a ride's door). A ride fits on open grass with a path beside it, its door the path cell nearest its front.\n- Guests carry money and `needs` (fun, hunger, thirst, tired) that `E.needs` raises each step; `E.choose(guest, options)` takes the ride or shop that serves them best for the walk and the price (an option's `gives: { fun: -.5 }`, its door's x, y, its cost). They queue, ride, pay, think aloud (\"THIS LINE IS TOO LONG\"), and leave when tired, broke or at closing; the rating is how happy they leave.\n- Time runs at `game.timeScale` 0, 1, 2 or 4 (more fixed steps a frame, not longer ones); `E.Clock` gives the day, the hour and its light (night: `game.lights` on the rides); the empty night can run faster. `E.overlay` shows a value per cell (crowds), `E.tooltip` a box of lines under the mouse.\n- Moving rides are shapes too: a generator's `params` (an angle in 16 or 24 steps) or `facing` (32 steps) each make a still picture, kept, so a turning carousel costs what a still one does.\n\n**3D-era remake (Mario 64, Ocarina, Crash).**\n- Use the `iso` or `threequarter` view with a `TileMap` whose wall heights are platforms (h 8, 16, 24...). The hero is a `Body` with `jump(260)`; walls lower than the hero's feet are walkable, so the level becomes a height map you platform across.\n- Add GPU lighting for mood and `cam.bounds` for framing.\n\n**Third-person stealth and parkour (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear, Tenchu).** Start from the STEALTH slice.\n```js\nconst map = new E.TileMap({ rows: CITY, legend: { '#': 1, A: 2, ',': { floor: 'grass' }, '@': 'hero' },\n  types: { 1: { h: 64, cut: true }, 2: { h: 40, face: 'plaster', roof: 'tiles', windows: true, door: { chance: .5 } } } });\nconst hero = new E.Mover({ x, y }), guard = new E.Mover({ x: gx, y: gy, walk: 30, run: 70 });\nguard.mind = new E.Suspicion(); guard.flow = new E.FlowField(map, { climb: 56, drop: 56, jump: 60 });\n// scene: { view: 'iso', views: ['iso', 'threequarter'], input: 'THIRDPERSON', touch: ['jump', 'attack', 'block', 'crouch'], ... }\n// update: hero.exposure = hero.crouch && map.floorAt(hero.x, hero.y) === 'grass' ? 0 : 1; hero.update(dt, map, game.input);\n//         const st = guard.mind.update(dt, E.sees(guard, hero, { map, range: 110 }), hero);\n//         goal: 'alert' the hero, 'search' guard.mind.last, else the next patrol point; guard.flow.update(goal.x, goal.y);\n//         const nx = guard.flow.next(guard.x, guard.y);   // walk toward nx; jump for a 'climb' or 'jump' once nx.edge < guard.r + 4\n//         guard.update(dt, map, { move, jumpPressed });   rig.update(dt, hero.rigState({ attack: slash.state }));\n// draw:   map.drawFloor(r); map.queueWalls(r); r.sightCone(guard, { map, range: 110 }); actors; r.marker(goal.x, goal.y, goal.z, { label: 'LEDGER' });\n```\n- Roofs a jump apart (three or four cells, heights within 11 up and 18 down of each other), several heights, and `cut: true` on the walls nearest the camera so the street stays visible.\n- Takedowns: from behind (same level, close, outside the guard's view, not alert) or from a roof above (`aerial: true`); mark the guard down, and feed its rig `act.otherState()` while the action plays.\n- Telegraph guards' swings (`new E.Attack('overhead', { wind: .38 })`) and let a block in the wind-up's last moments parry and stun them.\n- A checkpoint where the objective is taken: respawn there, and reset the guards.\n\n**Physics toys (Breakout, Asteroids with real collisions, Lemmings).** The physics and terrain modules; each step `world.step(dt)` from the game's fixed step (never the frame's time), so a run is the same at any refresh rate.\n- Breakout: a closed `world.chain` for the walls (its floor a sensor that loses the ball), the bricks static boxes taken out on `on.hit` (`world.remove`), the paddle a kinematic box moved by `moveTo` (it gives the ball its speed), the ball a `bullet` circle with `bounce: 1, friction: 0` and a `maxSpeed`; nudge its angle away from flat on each paddle hit.\n- Asteroids with real collisions: rocks as dynamic convex `world.poly`s with a little `spin`, the ship a poly with `gravity: 0` and `damping`, wrap-around by moving bodies across the edges yourself (`E.wrap`); a rock that splits is removed and two smaller polys added at its place with its speed.\n- Lemmings: an `E.Terrain` level (a picture's cells as materials), each lemming a walker (`land.walk`: a step up of 5, a fall that kills past a landing speed), diggers and bashers carving a small circle or a polygon each step, builders adding a short bar (`addPoly`); hundreds of walkers are cheap (no physics bodies).\n- What touches the terrain fast (a shot) uses `land.ray` for its line; what bounces on it (a grenade) is a physics circle with `world.add(land.collider())`.\n\n**Pinball (Pinball Fantasies, Space Cadet).** The PINBALL kit. A view of its own looking steeply down the table (`new E.View('pinball', 'Table', 0, 62, 1, 1)`), the physics world in table units with `gravity: [0, 450]` down its slope.\n- The walls `world.chain`s with `r: 1.5`; the flippers `world.flipper` (flip on the keys' down and up: `flip(input.down('flipL'))`), bumpers and slingshots with a `kick`, a saucer and a kickback as `world.kicker`s, drop targets static boxes set `off` when hit, lanes and the spinner sensors (`on.enter`); the ball a `bullet` with a `maxSpeed`.\n- A ramp: rails on a layer of their own; a sensor at its mouth switches a climbing ball to that layer (`ball.layer = ball.mask = 2`), one at its end switches it back; draw the ball at the ramp's height there.\n- The rules are counters over the events: targets light the ramp, ramps light the jackpot, the saucer pays it and starts a multiball (more balls in the world); a ball saver's seconds after a launch; a nudge pushes every ball and fills a tilt meter that, full, kills the flippers for the ball.\n- A dot-matrix display: draw the words small on a 128 x 32 canvas, then each of its pixels as a dot (lit or dim). Paint the playfield once to a canvas and draw it each frame.\n- On a phone: `input.zones = [{ x0: 0, y0: 0, x1: .5, y1: 1, act: 'flipL' }, { x0: .5, y0: 0, x1: 1, y1: 1, act: 'flipR' }]` (each half holds its flipper, two thumbs at once); while the ball waits in the lane, the whole screen one zone for the plunger, a swipe up (`input.pan()`) its pull.\n\n**Artillery (Worms, Scorched Earth).** The ARTILLERY kit. A side view at a whole scale, so the terrain's cells stay square: `new E.View('artillery', 'Island', 0, 0, 1, 1)`; the island an `E.Terrain` (y up: draw it with `land.draw(r)`, its points at `r.w(x, 0, y)`).\n- Soldiers are walkers (`land.walk`): its landing speed past a limit is fall damage; under the water line they drown. A blast `land.carve`s, hurts each one by its distance and throws it (`vx`, `vy`, `ground = false`).\n- Turns: `new E.Turns(teams, { mode: 'speed' })` with the two teams as its actors (equal speeds alternate them); each team sends its next one still standing.\n- Shots: a rocket steps its own flight (gravity, the wind, `land.ray` for the line); a grenade is a physics circle in a world of its own with `land.collider()` (bounce, a fuse in steps); a cluster bursts into a fan of small ones.\n- The CPU tries its shots in simulation: for each weapon, angle and power it runs the same step the real shot will (a grenade in a fresh world), scores the blasts it would make (the foes' hurt, its own side's against it), takes the best and fires it with a little error (`Math.random`, seeded in tests). Spread the tries over steps (a few each) so the frame stays smooth.\n- On a phone: `input.steer = 'drag'`; while `input.stickOn`, add up `input.drag()`: the shot goes the other way, its power the pull's length; let go fires. A tap (`clicked()`) walks there. A minimap at the top shows the whole island.\n\n**Rhythm (Dance Dance Revolution, PaRappa, Beatmania).** The RHYTHM kit. The songs module's songs and the rhythm module's `E.Rhythm`.\n- A chart is a string a lane over the song's steps (`'x...x...'`, holds `'h==='`), in parts and an order (`{ parts: { A: [...], B: [...] }, order: 'I A A B' }`), or a track's own notes (`{ track: 1, lanes: 4 }`). Give the song a `len` and let its tracks loop under it.\n- `rh.start()`, then `rh.update()` each step: it judges each lane's press by its distance in ms from its note on the music clock (windows of about 45, 90 and 135 ms suit a phone), and counts combo, score, life and grade. A miss and its sound are yours (`on.judge`).\n- PaRappa's call and response: the teacher's notes are calls (`'c'`), sounded on their steps (`sfx(voice, { step })`) and drawn on a highway of their own (`rh.draw(r, { call: true })`); the player answers in the next bar.\n- On a phone the lanes are columns: `input.zones` of a quarter of the screen each, under a highway of four lanes a quarter of the screen apart, so a thumb plays the note above it.\n- Let the player set the audio offset (Settings, Sound, Calibrate; or `audio.calibrate()` on a screen of your own) before blaming them for being late. Move the dancer, the lights and the crowd with `audio.onBeat`.\n\n**Survival horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark).** The 3D HORROR kit. Each room its own fixed camera (`world.camera.zones`, mode `'zones'`: fixed, turning or on a rail; the cut waits `camera.margin` into the next room, and the stick keeps its way across it), tank or modern controls (`camera.steer` by the player's `E.World3D.CONTROLS`), a pistol held to aim (`camera.lock(hero, foes)`, high and low), doors between rooms (`world.through(door, hero)`, keys with `world.unlock(door, inv)` and the rpg module's inventory), an item box and a typewriter (`trunk`, `typewriter` things; `game.saves`), a flashlight (`world.flashlight`, a world made with `{ spots: 1 }`) and fog.\n\n**3D kart racing (Mario Kart 64, Crash Team Racing).** The 3D RACING kit (the drive module). The circuit is a `world.track` along a closed `E.Path` with heights (banked bends, a hill, a hump to jump, boost `pads`, `gaps` in its walls where a shortcut, a track of its own with `surface: 'dirt'`, leaves and rejoins); karts are `world.vehicle({ kind: 'kart', rig })` raced by `E.Race` (`race.update(dt, null)`: laps, places, its drivers, items); the camera `'behind'` each; set a kart that has left the course back on the road after a moment (Lakitu); two players through `game.splitScreen(2)` and `world.addCamera`.\n\n**Rail shooter (Star Fox 64, Panzer Dragoon).** The 3D FLIGHT kit. The ship is a `world.flyer({ rail: { path, speed, w, h } })`: the rail's point rides on and the ship moves in a window across it; the camera `'behind'` follows the rail's point (`f.frame`), not the ship. Waves of foes live in the rail's frame (a distance along it and an offset across), so they keep up; a roll (`f.rolling`) turns shots aside; a held fire charges a shot that locks on (`f.seek`); at the end, `f.o.rail = null` and `bounds` give free flight round a boss. A wingman talks on `Dialog`'s radio (`modal: false`).\n\n**Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA Jam, Tecmo Bowl).** Start from the SPORTS slice: two against two in a walled cage, up to four players.\n- The field is a `TileMap` cage in `threequarter` (boards that cut low before the camera, nets); the ball and the players are `E.Body`s. The ball bounces off the ground and the boards (`bounce`), and curls: turn its velocity a little across its path each step while the shooter pushes sideways (aftertouch).\n- A pass leads the teammate (aim where it is going, not where it is); a lob over a defender is `E.arc(from, to, gravity, { apex })`, the throw that lands at its feet; a shot's power fills a meter while its button is held and goes on letting go.\n- The players a person doesn't run play by formation, by where the ball is: with it, the other one runs ahead on the far side; without it, the nearer presses and the other covers the goal. `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })` lets players 2 to 4 in, each taking a side's next player.\n\n**First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder).** The 3D CRAWLER kit (the grid module). The 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera at the party's eyes over a level of 2 x 2 tiles a cell; each step and quarter turn is a tween, so the view glides. Gates are `world.thing('door')`s that rise (`world.unlock` for one with a key); the monsters walk an `E.Grid` cell to cell; `E.Fog` keeps what the party has seen for the automap; `E.Turns` in `'atb'` is real time (Dungeon Master), in `'speed'` the dungeon waits for the party (Wizardry). The party's portraits sit under the view (`rig.drawPortrait`).\n\n**Twin-stick shooter (Robotron 2084, Smash TV).** Start from the ADVENTURE kit (a `Body` hero in rooms) with the SHOOTER's ships and bullets.\n- One fixed room in `topdown` (`res: [320, 240]`, `expand`): the hero a `Body` moved by `input.move()`, the left stick or WASD; it fires the way the right stick points (`input.padAim`), else toward the mouse (`input.pointer()` to a ground point), else the way it moves, a shot every 0.08 s from an `E.Bullets` pool (`plane: 'ground'`).\n- Waves spawn from the room's edges by a timeline; grunts walk straight at the hero, others wander, hulks can't be killed, humans to rescue for points. `cam.shake` and `game.freeze(.03)` on every kill keep it loud; `E.Arcade` keeps the score, the lives and the table.\n\n**Bomberman.** Start from the PUZZLE slice's grid or the ARCADE's maze (the grid module).\n- The arena is an `E.Grid` of hard blocks on even cells and soft blocks to break; players and foes are `E.GridMover`s (a turn asked early is kept).\n- A bomb is a cell with a fuse (seconds in your state); its blast runs out cell by cell in four lines (`grid.line`) up to its range, stopping at a hard block, breaking the first soft one and lighting other bombs in its way (a chain). Power-ups drop from broken blocks: range, bombs, speed, a kick (`mover.update(..., { push })`).\n- Two to four players on one machine: `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`, a mover and an input each (`game.players[i]`).\n\n**Gauntlet.** Start from the ADVENTURE slice (a `TileMap` dungeon in `threequarter` or `topdown`).\n- Up to four heroes (`game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`, `cam.frame(heroes)`), health draining by the second, food and potions, keys for doors.\n- Generators spawn monsters until destroyed; hundreds of them walk one shared `E.FlowField` toward the nearest hero (one field for all, recomputed when a hero crosses a cell), drawn as rigs with `{ every: 3 }` (kept images) to stay fast.\n\n**Frogger.** An `E.Grid` of lanes in `overhead`: the frog an `E.GridMover` that hops a cell per press; each lane a row of cars or logs moving at its own speed (`x += v * dt`, wrapping at the screen's edge). On a road a car's box kills; on the river the frog rides a log (add its speed) and drowns off one. Five homes at the top; a clock bar per life.\n\n**Sokoban.** An `E.Grid` from text rows (`#` walls, `$` crates, `.` goals, `@` you); you an `E.GridMover` with `push(cx, cy, dx, dy)` that moves a crate when the cell beyond is free; won when every goal has a crate. Keep each move's state for undo (Backspace) and a reset; count moves and pushes.\n\n**Sonic without the loops (Sonic the Hedgehog's speed on flat ground and slopes).** Start from the PLATFORMER slice: `new E.Platformer({ momentum: true })` (speed builds up and carries, slowing on the way up a slope and gathering on the way down: tiles of `kind: 'slope'`), a roll on down at speed, rings that scatter when hit (`E.Bullets` fired with `grav`) and are lost after a while, springs and long camera leads (`game.follow(hero, { lead: 60 })`). Loops and walls run round need a ground of any angle the Platformer doesn't have: leave them out.\n\n**Cinematic platformer (Prince of Persia, Flashback, Another World).** Start from the PLATFORMER slice with `new E.Platformer({ ledge: true, crouch: true })`: a step is a fixed distance, a jump a fixed arc (no control in the air), a ledge grabbed and climbed in steps, a run that needs room to stop. Screens one at a time (`game.cam.room`), each a room of the rooms module's `E.World`. Animation is the point: the Humanoid's moves at their full key poses, or a mocap clip (`docs/MOCAP.md`) for the climb and the fall; a sword fight is an `E.Fighter` pair from the fight module, in the side view.\n\n**Light guns (Time Crisis, The House of the Dead, Virtua Cop).** Start from the 3D FLIGHT slice's rails (or a 2D screen of `overhead` sprites): the camera rides an `E.Path` (`world.camera` following the rail's point), foes pop out from behind cover on a script by distance, and the player aims with the pointer: a click (a tap on a phone) at `input.pointer()` hits the nearest foe whose `world.project(x, y, z)` point lies within its size on the screen of it and that the camera sees (`world.clear` from the camera's pose); the reticle is drawn at the pointer. Reload by shooting off the screen (or a button); a pedal to take cover (a button that lowers the camera behind cover). Each enemy's shot is telegraphed by a ring that closes.\n\n**Indoor corridor shooter (Doom, GoldenEye 007) on the 3D engine.** Start from the 3D SHOOTER slice's first person: its gun, hitscan (`world.ray`), recoil and hit markers, with a level of text instead of its terrain (`level: { rows }`: corridors of walls, doors that slide or swing, `world.thing('door', ...)` with `locked: 'red'` and keys, lifts as platforms). Monsters are mobs that walk the level's flow field toward you and fire when `world.clear` says they see you; the camera is `'fixed'` with `capture: true`, turned by `camera.takeMouse()`. GoldenEye adds aiming down the sights (the overlay's fov) and guards that react to where they are shot.\n\n**Mash-ups.** The kits don't depend on each other. Some examples:\n- a platformer with Zelda rooms: `cam.room` in the side view;\n- a shooter with RPG shops: scenes plus `Menu`;\n- a brawler with bullet-hell bosses: `Bullets` with the `'ground'` plane;\n- a puzzle game with dialog cutscenes: `Dialog`.\n\n## Building a big game: lessons from Emberdeep\n`examples/emberdeep.html` is the engine's signature game: an endless Diablo-style hack-and-slash of about 2 MB of code, built from some fifty source files. Its structure (`src/emberdeep/DESIGN.md`) scales to any large game.\n\n- **A spine, then content.** A small core defines:\n  - the shared state;\n  - one damage pipeline for every unit;\n  - a handful of effect verbs (projectile, nova, ground area, telegraph, strike, meteor, chain, beam, wave, pull);\n  - the hero's action model;\n  - the monster runtime;\n  - the level builder;\n  - the UI kit.\n  Content never edits the core.\n- **Registries make a language.** Every part is registered: `def('skills' | 'archetypes' | 'affixes' | 'bosses' | 'patterns' | 'itemBases' | 'powers' | 'mechanics' | 'themes' | 'layouts' | 'npcs', id, spec)`. Parts refer to each other by id and combine freely. A monster is an archetype × an element × affixes × depth scaling × a palette. A level is a layout × a theme × mechanics × a monster pool. A boss is a body × an element × patterns.\n- **An event bus instead of hooks everywhere.** Legendary powers, passive keystones, mechanics and tips listen to `hit`, `kill`, `skill`, `dodge`, `step` and `draw` events and check whether they apply. Adding a power never touches the combat code.\n- **Procedural forever.** A recipe per depth draws from the registries. Past the hand-planned depths it composes new combinations in a fixed order (every pair of mechanics, then every triple), recolors themes by depth, varies packs (elements, shared affixes, giants, swarms) and assembles bosses from a pattern library. Both sides of the power curve grow exponentially, the monsters' a little faster.\n- **New animation on the rigs.** Post-process the Humanoid's joints after `rig.update`:\n  - a dodge roll turns every joint around the hips;\n  - drinking re-solves one arm with `E.ik3`;\n  - held tools are drawn from the hand and elbow joints.\n  New creature rigs (IK-legged spiders, a path-following serpent) are posed in 3D and projected with `view.p`.\n- **Test like a player.**\n  - An autopilot drives the hero through a virtual input for balance runs and an attract-mode demo.\n  - Scripted headless playtests (`tools/ed-play.mjs`) and a smoke test over every scene and depth (`tools/ed-smoke.mjs`) keep a big game honest.\n  - A Gallery scene plays every skill and monster for visual review.\n\n## Where to find more: animation and examples\nThe engine files carry the engine; the repo (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge) also holds a store of motion and a whole game of worked examples. Take from it rather than inventing a walk cycle or a spider from nothing, but take only what a game needs: the store's files are large (a clip set is 75-820 KB, each motion-capture subject about 2.5 MB), so search them and never read one whole. All of it runs at https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/labs.\n\n**Motion clips** (`docs/MOCAP.md`): 325 curated clips and all 2,548 takes of the CMU motion capture database, in a readable text format of key poses (whole numbers, a legend in every file's header) that plays on any Humanoid at its own build, on either engine (the full engine also turns the face and hair with the body, for rolls and cartwheels). Every clip is free to use: Quaternius and Mesh2Motion are CC0, CMU is free for all uses.\n- **Find** a clip by what it does. The curated sets have catalogs, each clip's name with its tags and a description: `src/mocap/catalogs/quaternius.json` (88 clips: idles, walks, runs, jumps, sword, gun, shield, magic and fist attacks, dodges, hits, deaths, sitting, talking, work), `mesh2motion.json` (177: idles, attacks, emotes and gestures, dodges, climbing, sneaking, zombies, chores) and `cmu.json` (60 moments cut from motion capture: gaits, fights and kicks, dodges, falls and get-ups, chores, monsters and zombies acted by people). The motion-capture takes are in a ledger, one a line with its category, length and description: `node tools/cmu.mjs ledger kick` searches it, or grep `src/mocap/catalogs/cmu-takes.tsv`.\n- **Take** the clips you want into a small set of your own: `node tools/anim-set.mjs src/mocap/sets/quaternius.js examples/cmu-lib/CMU_10.js --clips Idle_Loop,10_01 --name MINE --out mine.js` (the curated sets are `src/mocap/sets/<set>.js`; take `NN_xx` is in `examples/cmu-lib/CMU_<NN>.js`). A clip copied by hand also needs its set's preamble (its `sources`, the bodies it was measured on), so use the tool when you can run commands.\n- **Play** it: load `src/mocap/readable.js`, `src/mocap/mocap.js` and the set after the engine, then `const lib = Mocap.load(MOCAP.MINE); Mocap.drive(rig, lib);` once, and each step `rig.mocap = lib.sample(lib.clip('Idle_Loop'), t)` before `rig.update`. `rig.mocapW` (0 to 1) fades a clip in and out over the rig's own animation, `rig.mocapMask = 'upper'` plays only the chest, head and arms over the rig's own walk, and `lib.moveAt(clip, t)` says how far a travelling clip has carried the body.\n- **Edit** it: a clip is text. Change a key pose's numbers to fix or vary it (the Mocap Lab's AI panel shows a clip as text and plays an edit).\n\n**Objects and materials** (`docs/ASSETS.md`, full engine): still objects written as a few lines of named parts (a barrel is a turned profile of oak and two iron hoops), drawn from any view and facing with `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` and made solid with `map.solid(...)`; materials (`E.MATERIALS`) dress floors, walls and platform tiles. The card has the format, the core library and the recipe: copy the nearest entry, change it, look at it with `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool`, use it.\n\n**Worked examples** (`src/emberdeep/`, `src/emberdeep/DESIGN.md`): Emberdeep registers every part on one line, so one search lists them all: `grep -nE \"def\\(['\\\"](archetypes|characters|skills)\" src/emberdeep/*.js` gives the 29 monster, boss and dummy bodies (with tags: beast, spider, flying, ranged...), the 3 heroes and the skills (Codex's six come from one helper in `29-codex-skills.js`). Bodies that are not people are classes: `grep -n \"^class \" src/emberdeep/*.js` (an eight-legged crawler with IK legs, a burrowing serpent, a floating eye with a beam, Codex the living book, Dan the scythed beast on reverse-kneed legs). Perception and paired actions in play: `32-wardens.js` (a sentry's mind from `E.Suspicion` and `E.sees`, its cone from `r.sightCone`, an alarm heard through `E.hears`) and `23-executions.js` (`E.ACTIONS.takedown` on the hero and a monster at once, the target from `E.targets`). They lean on the game's helpers and shared state, so adapt them rather than paste them; `docs/CHARACTERS.md` is the recipe for a new body and what makes one read at game scale.\n\n**Starters**: `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-<genre>.html`, one vertical slice per genre (21 of them: the README's kits list, the API card's playbook), each a complete game to copy from (in the handoff package, `kits/`).\n\n**Real 3D**: `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` puts a three.js and Rapier world under a game (make the game with `bg: null`); its header is its manual, with a complete game. Its kits, `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-<action|platformer|shooter|horror|racing|flight|crawler>.html`, are served, not opened from a disk (they load `vendor/`); the handoff package leaves them out. `docs/ENGINE-3D.md` says how it fits the engine.\n\n## Performance\n- **Rigs:** projection costs microseconds. The outline composite is the expensive part, so crowds should use `outline: false`.\n- **Walls, floors and PlatformMap chunks** are baked once per view.\n- **Canvas lighting** scans pixels inside each light, so keep radii sensible or use GPU lighting.\n- **Particles** are capped at 900 (`game.particles.max`) and bullets at 800 per pool.\n- **Crowd separation:** use `E.SpatialHash`, not all-pairs checks.\n- The stress test (my-3d2dge.vercel.app/stress-test) benchmarks a device and prints a copyable report.\n\n## Checklist before handing a game back\n- One IIFE starting with `const E = My3D2dge`, holding only your game (no leftover starter menu or slices): between `GAME START` and `GAME END` in a single file, `game/game.js` in the handoff package. No libraries or network requests.\n- A title scene, a gameplay scene with `pausable: true`, and a game-over or win path back to the title.\n- No pause menu, settings screen, key rebinding or full-screen code of your own: they're standard. Your settings, names and menu items go in `new E.Game({ menu: {...} })`; prompts come from `game.input.label(action)`.\n- The camera follows the player and the level is visible. The HUD uses screen pixels (`r.text`, `r.overlay`), anchored to `r.W` and `r.H`, or for a modern game `E.Shell`'s HTML pieces (`ui.hud` every step). With `expand: true`, look at it on a wide monitor and on a phone both ways (`tools/check.mjs` takes `--size 390x844@3`).\n- Sounds for jumping, hitting, pickups and dying; music per scene.\n- Positions and speeds are in world units, `dt` is used everywhere, and nothing draws with anti-aliased canvas calls.\n- Every view in `views` looks right (press `V`), and the skeleton overlay and slow motion look right.\n- `node tools/check.mjs <your page>` (`game/index.html` in the handoff package) passes; look at its screenshots, not only its exit code.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
