{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"agent_message","text":"The first complete simulation is written: movement, chops and redirects, tool breakage, collisions, supplies, ladders, doors, rescues, and chamber progression. The native assets and coded audio are also implemented. I’m now testing progression and recovery before connecting the presentation.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"head -85 vendor/engine/my-3d2dge.js && sed -n '1,75p' src/view/assets.js && rg -n 'P\\\\.ASSETS|PD\\\\.ASSETS|updateMinerRig|shapeIds|ASSETS =' src/view/assets.js && sed -n '1,90p' src/audio/audio.js && cat docs/hub-review.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"head -85 vendor/engine/my-3d2dge.js && sed -n '1,75p' src/view/assets.js && rg -n 'P\\\\.ASSETS|PD\\\\.ASSETS|updateMinerRig|shapeIds|ASSETS =' src/view/assets.js && sed -n '1,90p' src/audio/audio.js && cat docs/hub-review.md\"","aggregated_output":"/*!\n * my-3D2dge v0.89.1  (My \"3D\" 2D Game Engine)\n * Procedural pixel-puppet engine for every 2D perspective.\n * https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge  (MIT License)\n *\n * READ THIS FIRST (humans and AI models extending a my-3D2dge game)\n * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * 0. EVERY GAME STARTS WITH  const E = My3D2dge;  and then uses E.Game, E.px, ...\n * 1. WORLD SPACE IS 3D. x = east, y = south, z = up. One floor tile = 16 units.\n *    Game logic never thinks about screen pixels.\n * 2. A VIEW turns world points into screen pixels. Swap the view and the whole game\n *    re-renders from another angle: E.VIEWS.iso, .threequarter, .topdown,\n *    .brawler, .side  (or build your own: new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost)).\n * 3. CHARACTERS ARE RIGS, NOT SPRITES. E.Humanoid and E.Blob recompute their\n *    joints from math every step, in 3D, then project them. That is why they move\n *    fluidly and can face any angle in any view without extra art. Small things\n *    (coins, bullets, icons) can be pixel sprites written as strings: E.sprite().\n * 4. PIXEL RULES. Draw with E.px (rect, line, disc, ell, poly, sprite...). Every shape\n *    snaps to whole pixels. Never use ctx.arc / ctx.stroke / ctx.fillText on the game\n *    buffer: they anti-alias and break the look. Translucency and glow go through\n *    px.blend / polyDither / ddisc / r.glowDisc: stepped alpha and blend modes like PS1\n *    hardware (E.style.trans = 'dither' switches to NES / Genesis style dithering).\n * 5. FRAME ORDER (handled by the Renderer):\n *      background (r.sky, map.drawFloor or level.draw) -> decals (ground marks, shadows)\n *      -> sorted queue (walls, props, actors, particles) -> lighting -> overlays -> present\n *    Anything that can be in front of or behind something else goes through\n *    r.queue(x, y, z, fn), r.actor(x, y, z, drawFn, opts) or r.sprite(...).\n * 6. TIME. game.start({ update(dt), draw(r) }) or game.start({ scenes, scene }).\n *    update runs in exact fixed steps: each 1/120 s of real time is one update with\n *    dt = 1/120 * timeScale, so motion is identical at 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz (game.alpha =\n *    the part of a step left over). game.freeze(s) = hit-stop, game.shake(n) = screen shake,\n *    game.timeScale = slow motion, game.paused = pause, game.after(s, fn) = timer.\n * 7. LEVELS. Top-down / isometric / brawler floors: E.TileMap (walls on the ground\n *    plane). Side-scrollers: E.PlatformMap + E.Platformer (tiles on the x/z plane,\n *    solid, one-way, ladders, hazards, moving platforms; crouching, ledges, clinging, water, ropes, bars, swinging and\n *    momentum on request: 16c). Both read ASCII rows. Third person (Assassin's Creed):\n *    E.Mover on a TileMap of roofs climbs, vaults and leaps; E.ACTIONS, and E.sees / E.Suspicion for guards (17b-17d).\n * 8. SOUND, TEXT, MENUS. game.audio.sfx('jump'), game.audio.music(song);\n *    E.font.text(...) (5x7 proportional font), E.font.title(...), E.ui.box/bar/hearts,\n *    new E.Dialog(game) (markup: {c=#hex}, {w=.5}, {s=12}, {shake}, {wave}, {$name}), new E.Menu(game, items). An HTML\n *    interface (title, pause, settings, help, results, map screens and a HUD with a minimap) from data: new E.Shell(game,\n *    {...}) (21d). Save slots: game.saves (10k).\n * 8a. STANDARD FEATURES come with every game (10b): a pause menu with settings and controls the player rebinds, saved\n *    per game (game.settings), full screen (game.fullscreen; a phone's first tap), pausing when the page is hidden, and\n *    input.label(action) for prompts, and a boot (10c): heavy setup in game.start({ boot: { steps } }) runs behind a\n *    boot screen, and an error before play shows a report to screenshot. Don't build your own: adjust them with\n *    new E.Game({ menu: { items, settings, controls, quit, saves } }), or leave them out (menu: false, fullscreen: false,\n *    corner: false, autoPause: false).\n * 8b. LOOK. Aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES: HD rigs (size, outfit, hair, armor),\n *    new E.Backdrop('castle-night') behind levels, textured tiles (stone, brick, wood,\n *    metal), r.prop('torch', ...) set dressing, particles.explosion / smoke / fire,\n *    game.flash, and E.tones(hex) / E.ramp(hex) for hue-shifted shading.\n * 9. GPU LIGHTING is optional: game.enableGPU({ map }). Mark glowing things with\n *    px.glow(g, 1) inside queue callbacks, pass { color, shadow } to lights.add, and\n *    register shadow casters with game.lights.caster(). Falls back to Canvas lighting.\n * 10. SESSIONS. E.session.record(true) records every later page load (or ?record=1 for one): the frame times, the\n *    inputs, the random seed, the screen and the save. E.session.share() saves it as a file; node tools/replay.mjs\n *    plays it back headless, exactly, and stops where it differs (game.stateHash = () => [...] sharpens that).\n * 11. TESTS. new E.Game({ headless: true }) (the default in Node) runs with no screen: game.advance(1) runs 120 fixed\n *    steps at once, game.input.tap('jump') and hold('right') script input, game.debug = { snapshot() { ... } } is\n *    your game's handle for tests and tools; game.stop(), start() and dispose() stop, resume and end it.\n * Errors in your game are shown on screen and in the console (game.errors).\n * THE HANDOFF PACKAGE holds this file and what it needs (dist/handoff/ in the repo, my-3d2dge.vercel.app/handoff/):\n *    API.md, the card (read it whole; the single-file editions embed it), API-INDEX.md (every public name, one line\n *    each: search it), AI_GUIDE.md (the recipes), tools/check.mjs, filmstrip.mjs and replay.mjs, and game/, a\n *    template game with a headless test.\n * MORE, outside this file (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge; search it and take only what a game needs, its files\n *    are large): 325 curated motion clips and 2,548 motion-capture takes as readable key poses that play on any\n *    Humanoid (src/mocap/: the catalogs list every clip's tags and description, tools/anim-set.mjs cuts the ones you\n *    want into a small set, src/mocap/mocap.js plays them), and Emberdeep (src/emberdeep/), a whole game of worked\n *    examples: monsters, bosses, heroes with bodies that are not people, skills. AI_GUIDE.md, \"Where to find more\",\n *    says how to search them and take what you need. Still objects and materials as data (r.shape, E.MATERIALS):\n *    docs/ASSETS.md, its recipe checked with tools/asset-sheet.mjs.\n */\n(function (root) {\n'use strict';\n\n/** TAU: 2π, a full turn in radians; DEG: π/180, one degree in radians (angle * DEG) */\nconst TAU = Math.PI * 2, DEG = Math.PI / 180;\n/** version: the release (package.json, written everywhere by node tools/version.mjs); build: the commit this copy was built\n *  from, a placeholder in the repo that tools/stamp.mjs replaces when the site deploys (e.g. 'a1b2c3d 2026-10-07');\n *  name: the engine's name, 'my-3D2dge'; current: the latest game made (My3D2dge.current, for tools and tests) */\nconst E = { version: '0.89.1', build: 'dev-build', name: 'my-3D2dge', TAU, DEG, current: null };\n/** 'v0.7.0 · a1b2c3d 2026-10-07': the label to show on screens and in reports */\nE.versionLabel = () => 'v' + E.version + ' · ' + E.build;\n/* Pick Axe Deplete: original native shapes, equipment and held poses. */\n(() => {\n  'use strict';\n  const PD = (globalThis.PD ||= {});\n  const E = globalThis.My3D2dge;\n  const { px, lerp, clamp } = E;\n  const A = (PD.ASSETS = Object.freeze({\n    doorAmber: 'PD:doorAmber', doorCyan: 'PD:doorCyan', doorRose: 'PD:doorRose',\n    ore: 'PD:ore', hopper: 'PD:hopper', heavy: 'PD:heavy', key: 'PD:key',\n    pick: 'PD:pick', extractor: 'PD:extractor', lamp: 'PD:lamp', timber: 'PD:timber',\n    brokenPick: 'PD:brokenPick', helmet: 'PD:helmet', rescue: 'PD:rescue',\n    ladderTop: 'PD:ladderTop', oreCart: 'PD:oreCart', wheel: 'PD:wheel',\n  }));\n  let installed = false;\n  const mat = (color, pattern = 'none', extra = {}) => ({ color, pattern, ...extra });\n  const box = (m, at, size, opts) => ['box', m, at, size, ...(opts ? [opts] : [])];\n  const cyl = (m, at, r, h, opts) => ['cyl', m, at, r, h, ...(opts ? [opts] : [])];\n  const prism = (m, at, pts, h, axis = 'y') => ['prism', m, at, pts, h, { axis }];\n  const blendJoint = (J, k, p, w) => { const a = J[k]; if (a) J[k] = a.map((n, i) => lerp(n, p[i], w)); };\n  const arm = (J, side, p, w) => {\n    const sh = J[`sh${side}`];\n    blendJoint(J, `hand${side}`, p, w);\n    blendJoint(J, `elbow${side}`, [(sh[0] + p[0]) * .5 - .7, (sh[1] + p[1]) * .5, (sh[2] + p[2]) * .5 - .8], w);\n  };\n  const setBlade = (J, p, w) => {\n    const v = J.bladeDir.map((n, i) => lerp(n, p[i], w)); const n = Math.hypot(...v) || 1;\n    J.bladeDir = v.map(x => x / n);\n  };\n\n  function doorParts(color, emblem) {\n    const p = [\n      box('dark',[0,0,0],[26,7,32]),\n      box(color,[0,1.5,1],[22,7,29]),\n      box('void',[0,5.5,2],[15,1.5,24]),\n      box('iron',[-11,1,0],[3,9,28]), box('iron',[11,1,0],[3,9,28]),\n      prism('iron',[0,0,26],[[-13,0],[-9,7],[9,7],[13,0]],9),\n      box(color,[0,5.8,27],[19,2,3]),\n      box('gold',[0,7,5],[3,1,5]),\n      box('steel',[-8,6.5,8],[1.5,1,10]), box('steel',[8,6.5,8],[1.5,1,10]),\n      box('gold',[-11,6,3],[2,1,2]), box('gold',[11,6,3],[2,1,2]),\n      box('gold',[-11,6,23],[2,1,2]), box('gold',[11,6,23],[2,1,2]),\n    ];\n    if (emblem === 'sun') {\n      p.push(cyl('gold',[0,7.8,20],3,1.2,{rot:[90,0,0],seg:8}),\n        box('glowGold',[0,8.4,17],[1.5,1,6]), box('glowGold',[0,8.4,19],[6,1,1.5]));\n    } else if (emblem === 'diamond') {\n      p.push(prism('glowCyan',[0,7.8,16],[[-4,5],[0,10],[4,5],[0,0]],1.2));\n    } else {\n      p.push(box('glowRose',[0,8,16],[1.5,1,8]),box('glowRose',[0,8,19],[8,1,1.5]),\n        box('glowRose',[0,8,17],[5,1,5],{rot:[0,45,0]}));\n    }\n    return p;\n  }\n\n  PD.installAssets = function installAssets() {\n    if (installed) return A;\n    installed = true;\n    Object.assign(E.MATERIALS, {\n      'PD:dark': mat('#15212c','metal'), 'PD:iron': mat('#35475a','metal',{rivet:7}),\n      'PD:steel': mat('#a0b6bd','metal'), 'PD:gold': mat('#e8b44d','metal'),\n      'PD:amber': mat('#c27e38','metal'), 'PD:cyan': mat('#4b9499','metal'),\n      'PD:rose': mat('#a7657f','metal'), 'PD:void': mat('#0c1520'),\n      'PD:oak': mat('#6c4835','planks',{bw:4,len:19}),\n      'PD:wood': mat('#b17b48','planks',{bw:3,len:12}),\n      'PD:oreStone': mat('#7c6c5d','stones',{size:6}),\n      'PD:hopperStone': mat('#537d91','stones',{size:5}),\n      'PD:heavyStone': mat('#645e79','stones',{size:7}),\n      'PD:glowGold': mat('#ffe18a','none',{glow:1}),\n      'PD:glowCyan': mat('#99e6e6','none',{glow:1}),\n      'PD:glowRose': mat('#fcb8c8','none',{glow:1}),\n    });\n    const mats = Object.fromEntries(['dark','iron','steel','gold','amber','cyan','rose','void','oak','wood','oreStone','hopperStone','heavyStone','glowGold','glowCyan','glowRose'].map(k => [k,`PD:${k}`]));\n    function shape(name, about, parts, more = {}) { E.SHAPES[A[name]] = { about, tags:'mine original prospecting', mats, parts, ...more }; }\n    shape('doorAmber','Amber sun gate; broad riveted timbers and sun emblem',doorParts('amber','sun'));\n    shape('doorCyan','Cyan diamond gate; contrasting diamond seal',doorParts('cyan','diamond'));\n7:  const A = (PD.ASSETS = Object.freeze({\n245:  PD.updateMinerRig = function updateMinerRig(rig,miner,dt) {\n/* Pick Axe Deplete: native chip score and original procedural mine instruments.\n * Presentation-only. No random draw here can affect the mine simulation. */\n(() => {\n\tglobalThis.PD ||= {};\n\tconst PD = globalThis.PD;\n\tconst clamp = (n, lo = 0, hi = 1) =>\n\t\tMath.max(lo, Math.min(hi, Number.isFinite(n) ? n : lo));\n\tconst defaults = Object.freeze({\n\t\tmusic: 0.1,\n\t\tsfx: 0.7,\n\t\tvoice: 0.4,\n\t\tmute: false,\n\t});\n\tconst NOTES = { C: 0, D: 2, E: 4, F: 5, G: 7, A: 9, B: 11 };\n\tconst frequency = (token) => {\n\t\tconst m = /^([A-G])([#b]?)(\\d)$/.exec(token);\n\t\treturn m\n\t\t\t? 440 *\n\t\t\t\t\t2 **\n\t\t\t\t\t\t((NOTES[m[1]] +\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(m[2] === \"#\" ? 1 : m[2] === \"b\" ? -1 : 0) +\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(+m[3] + 1) * 12 -\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t69) /\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t12)\n\t\t\t: 0;\n\t};\n\n\t// Both the native sequencer and the offline proof play these exact functions.\n\tfunction tone(ctx, bus, t, f, dur, vol, kind, track) {\n\t\tconst end = t + Math.max(0.025, dur);\n\t\tconst envelope = ctx.createGain();\n\t\tconst filter = ctx.createBiquadFilter();\n\t\tfilter.type = kind === \"bass\" ? \"lowpass\" : \"bandpass\";\n\t\tfilter.frequency.setValueAtTime(\n\t\t\tkind === \"bell\" ? f * 2.2 : kind === \"bass\" ? 800 : 1800,\n\t\t\tt,\n\t\t);\n\t\tfilter.Q.value = kind === \"bell\" ? 1.7 : 0.55;\n\t\tenvelope.gain.setValueAtTime(0, t);\n\t\tenvelope.gain.linearRampToValueAtTime(vol, t + 0.004);\n\t\tenvelope.gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(0.00001, end);\n\t\tfilter.connect(envelope);\n\t\tenvelope.connect(bus);\n\t\tconst waves =\n\t\t\tkind === \"bell\"\n\t\t\t\t? [\n\t\t\t\t\t\t[\"sine\", 1, 0],\n\t\t\t\t\t\t[\"sine\", 2.01, 0.2],\n\t\t\t\t\t]\n\t\t\t\t: kind === \"bass\"\n\t\t\t\t\t? [[\"triangle\", 1, 0]]\n\t\t\t\t\t: [\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t[\"triangle\", 1, 0],\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t[\"sawtooth\", 1.003, 0.08],\n\t\t\t\t\t\t];\n\t\tfor (const [wave, ratio, share] of waves) {\n\t\t\tconst osc = ctx.createOscillator();\n\t\t\tconst part = ctx.createGain();\n\t\t\tpart.gain.value = share || 1;\n\t\t\tosc.type = wave;\n\t\t\tosc.frequency.setValueAtTime(f * ratio, t);\n\t\t\tif (kind === \"pluck\")\n\t\t\t\tosc.frequency.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(f * ratio * 0.995, t + 0.06);\n\t\t\tosc.connect(part);\n\t\t\tpart.connect(filter);\n\t\t\tif (track) track(osc, end + 0.02, [part, filter, envelope]);\n\t\t\tosc.start(t);\n\t\t\tosc.stop(end + 0.02);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tfunction strike(ctx, bus, t, f, dur, vol, track) {\n\t\tconst oscillator = ctx.createOscillator(),\n\t\t\tamp = ctx.createGain();\n\t\toscillator.type = \"triangle\";\n\t\toscillator.frequency.setValueAtTime(f, t);\n\t\toscillator.frequency.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(\n\t\t\tMath.max(35, f * 0.35),\n\t\t\tt + dur,\n\t\t);\n\t\tamp.gain.setValueAtTime(0, t);\n\t\tamp.gain.linearRampToValueAtTime(vol, t + 0.002);\n\t\tamp.gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(0.00001, t + dur);\n\t\toscillator.connect(amp);\n\t\tamp.connect(bus);\n\t\tif (track) track(oscillator, t + dur + 0.02, [amp]);\n\t\toscillator.start(t);\n\t\toscillator.stop(t + dur + 0.02);\n\t}\n\n# Hub integration and harness review\n\nInspected 2026-11-06. Vendored repository `vendor/hub-repo`, commit `35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869`. Protocol 1; released client 1.4.0. Reviewed README, session.js, shared-world arena example, harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, scripts/hub-check.mjs, scripts/lib/hub-client.mjs and scripts/lib/serve.mjs.\n\n## Bootstrap and ownership\n\n- Paste **exact** `lib/hub/session.js` as the first script in head, immediately after charset; exclude it from minification, escaping or other transforms. Its released banner-to-last-line LF-normalized SHA-256 is `529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a`. Use `findClients()` from the vendored checker support module to assert exactly one pristine client.\n- Declare session 1, seats 1-2, mode shared-world, minutes 15, plus goal and controls metadata. `AIHub.active` is available before any game script. `AIHub.connect({mode:'shared-world', seats:[1,2]})` resolves to info or null. Null means ordinary standalone flow.\n- After connect returns, info includes `me`, `players:[{id,name,color}]`, `host`, `isHost`, `server`, `seed`, `round`, `limitMs`, `mode`. Players use zero-based IDs. Never assume `me` is a player: with `server:true` the hidden host has no seat in players.\n- Install lifecycle handlers and initialize simulation/runtime, then `AIHub.ready()` exactly once without gesture or sound permission. Default Hub card requires no game-specific Ready UI.\n- `start({in})` means countdown began, **not gameplay began**. `AIHub.time()` is milliseconds, -Infinity before start, negative during three-second countdown. Tick only at time >=0. Clock is wall time independent of menus and game speed.\n- Only `AIHub.isHost` advances canonical world. A dedicated server must initialize and ready, tick, send snapshots, handle leave/time-up, and finish; it requires no renderer/input/audio.\n- `AIHub.on(type,fn)` returns unsubscribe. Events queued before registration are delivered asynchronously in order after first handler attaches.\n\n## Messaging and completion\n\n- `AIHub.send(data,{to,reliable})`; default reliable=true, omitted to broadcasts to every other copy. It returns false when dropped. `AIHub.on('message',(data,from)=>...)`: authenticate the actual `from`, not a player ID inside data. Send client intent to `AIHub.host`; accept snapshots only from that host.\n- Hard JSON limit 16,384 bytes; 20 outgoing messages/second per copy, burst 40. Aim at <=1,200 bytes for unreliable messages. Count combined snapshot/keyframe/event sends, not each type independently. A 10Hz snapshot + 2Hz keyframe schedule and 12Hz client intent are within budget.\n- Reliable messages preserve order; unreliable messages may be lost or reordered. Carry epoch, monotonically increasing input/snapshot sequences, repeated edge counters + host acknowledgements, and bounded critical event IDs. Reject old chamber epochs, old sequences and nonplayer senders. Silence neutralizes held intent. Use one bounded send scheduler.\n- `AIHub.rng(label)` returns a new generator **starting from the beginning** each call. Retain one gameplay generator and one presentation generator. Shared-world does not seed Math.random. Serializable owned PRNG initialized from session seed is suitable for canonical simulation restore; keep cosmetic randomness independent.\n- `leave({id,reason})` has already removed that ID from AIHub.players. Remove/recover its entity/key, preserve remaining roster. No migration: host leave voids round and end follows.\n- Host finishes exactly once: `AIHub.finish({standings:[remainingPlayerIds],scores:{[id]:score},won:boolean,stats?,awards?})`. For co-op, every remaining ID is in the same inner array. `stats` is `{[id]:{name:value}}`; awards are `[{id,title,detail}]`. Finish loss on wipe or time-up, victory on extraction.\n- `time-up({graceMs})` requires finish within supplied grace (current default 5,000ms). Do not wait for another simulation tick, input, menu or sound.\n- `end({reason,standings})`: stop gameplay/input/network schedulers and all sound immediately. Do not offer local restart/title for active Hub. No external socket/server/broker fetch, chat/voice/emote/custom names, dialogs, popups or navigation. Ignore Options when Create held.\n- Active online pause/settings overlay sends neutral local intent, while authority and deadline continue. Disable engine automatic hidden-page/disconnect pause in online mode. Render Hub player names via text, never HTML.\n\n## Official test harness\n\nRequired browser support files are only `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js`, all side-by-side. Harness imports the latter two as ES modules and loads game URLs only at its own origin. It does not need session.js separately when game embeds it. Serve our own folder on `127.0.0.1` with OS-selected port; assert fetched game title/metadata before trusting results.\n\nThe harness URL is `<base>/<harness-dir>/harness.html?game=<same-origin-game-path>&players=2&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&scenario=standard&play=8000`. Supported scenario values: standard, host-leave, server. `limit` is seconds and `seed` fixes the round seed. Server adds a hidden authority **copy**, not an extra player seat.\n\nWait by state for BOTH `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`. On failure retain failed check details and harness log. Harness default timing may take tens of seconds; software rendering can take longer. It automatically checks hello, metadata/seats, ready with no input, countdown, directional input, two-way traffic, time-up/result, end, protocol misuse and errors. Host-leave expects void. Server expects the nonplayer authority to send snapshots and finish a played round.\n\nCover standard with players=1 and players=2; two-player 150ms latency / 50ms jitter / 5% loss; two-player host-leave; and two-player + server. Add game assertions for actual canonical progression, carrier departure, stale/duplicated inputs and post-end silence because official conformance alone does not prove gameplay.\n\n### Running the unchanged CLI checker\n\n`node vendor/hub-repo/scripts/hub-check.mjs /path-to-game.html --base http://127.0.0.1:<OS-port> --players 1,2 --json` requires a Node Playwright 1.63.0 dependency resolved from the vendored checker location or ancestor. No browser installation needed. The official script accesses harness at **/lib/hub/harness.html** on base, so expose that path or copy required files to development lib/hub. A local Node staticHandler server binds 127.0.0.1 port 0 itself when --base omitted, but defaults document root to vendor/hub-repo, requiring artifact under that origin.\n\nDo **not** use `--record` (catalogue writes). Checker otherwise writes only its own `.cache/hub-check.json`. With `--base`, disk-only pristine client check silently skips when game is outside vendored checker root; separately run `findClients()` on final root HTML. An absolute HTTP game URL also skips that static check.\n\nThe installed Python Playwright environment can wrap unchanged harness directly without installing Node packages. This gives identical `window.__hubCheck` checks while keeping own quiet aggregate runner. Run official harness, assert both completion conditions, and independently assert client pristine + standalone file:// launch. Keep at most one browser with a few contexts; close all contexts, browser and owned HTTP server.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
