{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_64","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '763,800p' vendor/engine/my-3d2dge.js && rg -n 'Input\\\\.DEFAULT|Input\\\\.PLATFORMER|COMMON_INPUT|PadLeft|PadUp|glyphs' vendor/engine/my-3d2dge.js | head -25 && sed -n '947,985p' vendor/engine/my-3d2dge.js && cat docs/pilot-evidence.md\"","aggregated_output":"    this._fresh = new Set(); this._now = new Set(); this._freshUp = new Set(); this._nowUp = new Set(); this._rk = {}; this._freshCodes = new Set();\n    this._suppressed = new Set();\n    /** mouse: { cx, cy } its page position, active once it has moved over the canvas, t the input clock when it last\n     *  moved */\n    this.mouse = { cx: 0, cy: 0, active: false, t: -99 };\n    /** clickAt: where a click or tap on the canvas landed, in screen pixels [x, y], for the one step after it (clicked()) */\n    this._clickAt = null; this._click = null; this._down = null;\n    /** stick: the touch stick's direction [x, y], length <= 1; stickOn: a finger is on it; padMove: the gamepad's\n     *  left stick after the dead zone; padAim: its right stick, or null when centered; padPrev: which gamepad buttons\n     *  were down at the last step; aimSource: what aims right now: 'move' | 'mouse' | 'pad' */\n    this.stick = [0, 0]; this.stickOn = false; this.padMove = [0, 0]; this.padAim = null; this.padPrev = []; this.aimSource = 'move';\n    /** padValues: each gamepad button's travel, 0 to 1 (the triggers; input.value reads them); invertMoveX, invertMoveY:\n     *  the move stick turned round (the Controller settings) */\n    this.padValues = []; this.invertMoveX = this.invertMoveY = false;\n    /** deadzone: how far a gamepad stick moves before it counts (0..0.8); padIndex, padId: the gamepad in use (null\n     *  and '' without one); lastDevice: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch', the last one used (show the\n     *  right button prompts) */\n    this.deadzone = .18; this.padIndex = null; this.padId = ''; this.lastDevice = 'keyboard';\n    /** pad: the gamepad this input reads: 'any' (the first connected one another player hasn't: player 1's), an index (a\n     *  joined player's own), or null (none); keysTo: the other players' inputs the keyboard's codes go to as well (players\n     *  sharing it, each with half its keys: game.players) */\n    this.pad = 'any'; this.keysTo = null; this._claimed = null;\n    /** touchEnabled: false turns the touch stick off; stickSide: 'left' or 'right', the half of the screen the stick\n     *  lives on; stickRadius: how far (CSS pixels) a finger drags for full tilt; stickDeadzone: the part of that radius a\n     *  thumb moves before the stick counts (0..0.5); steer: 'stick' (a stick drawn under the thumb) or 'drag' (a finger\n     *  anywhere on the screen but the buttons steers by drag(), with no stick drawn: a shooter's ship that follows the\n     *  thumb); padAxes: the gamepad's raw axes */\n    this.touchEnabled = true; this.stickSide = 'left'; this.steer = 'stick'; this.stickRadius = 44; this.stickDeadzone = .12; this.padAxes = []; this._taps = new Set(); this._tapsDue = new Set(); this._holds = new Set();\n    /** zones: areas of the screen a finger holds an action down in, [{ x0, y0, x1, y1, act }] (fractions of the canvas,\n     *  0..1; the first that holds the finger wins), or null: a pinball's two halves, a rhythm game's lanes. A finger\n     *  landing in one presses act until it lifts (several fingers, several zones), and starts no stick */\n    this.zones = null; this._zoneOf = new Map();\n    /** pinching: two fingers on the canvas are pinching the camera's zoom (they moved apart or together: not the stick,\n     *  not a tap). The game lets the player zoom only where its scene does (camera.zoom): then the wheel, - and = (keys no\n     *  action uses) and a pinch ask for it, read by zoom() */\n    this._pinching = false; this._zoomAcc = 1; this._zoomNow = 1; this._zoomOn = false; this._wheelOn = false; this._fingers = new Map();\n    // the pointer's gestures (box, longPress, doubleTap, pan): the press followed (_g), what this step reads, what the next will\n    this._g = null; this._gBox = null; this._gLong = null; this._gTap2 = null; this._gPan = null; this._lastTap = null; this._zoomPt = null; this._fedAt = null;\n802:    /** glyphs: the gamepad's button symbols for label(): 'auto' (from the controller's name) | 'xbox' | 'playstation' |\n805:    this.glyphs = 'auto'; this.rumbleScale = .6; this.padLost = false;\n863:  /** switch key layout at runtime: input.use(E.Input.PLATFORMER) or input.use('SHMUP'). The player's own bindings\n864:   *  (bindings, set by rebind() and the Controls screen) are laid over it. Every preset (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER,\n868:    if (typeof map === 'string') map = Input[map.toUpperCase()] || Input.DEFAULT;\n882:    /** preset: the layout last given to use() (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, SHMUP or your own) */\n916:   *  button by its symbol on the controller in use ('A', '✕', 'B'...; glyphs picks the family) */\n925:  /** the controller family whose symbols label() shows: glyphs, or with glyphs 'auto' the one its name says */\n927:    if (this.glyphs && this.glyphs !== 'auto') return this.glyphs;\n1041:  _check(a) { if (!this.map[a]) warn('action:' + a, 'unknown input action \"' + a + '\". Known actions: ' + Object.keys(this.map).join(', ') + '. Add your own with new E.Game({ input: { ...E.Input.DEFAU [... omitted end of long line]\n1309:Input.DEFAULT = {\n1315:Input.PLATFORMER = {\n1338:// Both fonts are tables of glyphs: each entry a character, then one digit for each row of its pixels, top to bottom, the\n1345:// under its accent); any other character draws as ?. It is the agent edition's table. To see glyphs as rows of # and .:\n1359:  normal: { glyphs: parseFont(G57), h: 7, desc: 2, gap: 1, space: 3, upper: false },\n1360:  tiny: { glyphs: parseFont(GLYPHS, 3), h: 5, desc: 0, gap: 2, space: 3, upper: true, mono: 3 }\n1374:  _glyph(f, ch) { return f.glyphs[f.upper ? ch.toUpperCase() : ch] || f.glyphs[ch.toUpperCase()] || f.glyphs['?']; },\n1431:   * Big logo text for title screens: scaled glyphs with a vertical color gradient, an outline and an extruded 3D edge.\n2344:    if (typeof o.input === 'string') o.input = Input[o.input.toUpperCase()] || (warn('input:' + o.input, 'unknown input preset \"' + o.input + '\" (use DEFAULT, PLATFORMER or SHMUP)'), Input.DEFAULT);\n2347:    this.o = Object.assign({ view: 'iso', minH: 190, minW: 300, maxW: 560, maxH: 330, bg: '#07060d', step: 1 / 120, input: Input.DEFAULT, loop: !this.headless }, o);\n3024: *      one with a standard key and no label changes that setting, its other fields kept: { key: 'glyphs', value:\n3026: *      player picks Xbox (game.settings.set('glyphs', 'xbox') switches); one with a label of its own is the game's, in the\n3066:  glyphs: { group: 'Controller', label: 'Button symbols', type: 'select', value: 'auto', options: [['auto', 'Auto'], ['xbox', 'Xbox'], ['playstation', 'PlayStation'], ['nintendo', 'Nintendo'], ['gener [... omitted end of long line]\n3741:  const P = typeof preset === 'string' ? Input[preset.toUpperCase()] || Input.DEFAULT : preset || Input.DEFAULT, H = KEY_HALVES[side] || KEY_HALVES.left, own = (P.halves && P.halves[side]) || {}, out  [... omitted end of long line]\n3766:  const q = new Input(_pStub(this), Input.DEFAULT), n = P.length + 1;\n    this._rumbling = a; return true;\n  }\n  /** stop a rumble now (pause, focus lost, unplugged) */\n  stopRumble() { const a = this._rumbling; this._rumbling = null; try { if (a && a.reset) { const r = a.reset(); if (r && r.catch) r.catch(() => {}); } } catch (e) { /* gone */ } }\n  /**\n   * On-screen buttons for phones and tablets (shown on touch screens; the player's settings can show them always or hide\n   * them, put them on the left, and size them): input.touchButtons(['jump', 'attack'], { labels: { jump: 'A', attack:\n   * 'B' }, force }). The other half of the screen is a move stick (past halfway it presses up, down, left and right too,\n   * as a d-pad does: menus and ladders). START is the corner's menu button, top right: it opens the menu, or pauses a\n   * pausable scene into it (without the corner a START button there starts a title and pauses, as a gamepad's Start)\n   */\n  touchButtons(actions, o = {}) {\n    if (!this.screen.canvas) return null;\n    this._touchArgs = { actions, o }; this._touchLabels = o.labels || null; this._touchSet = {};\n    return this._touchSync();\n  }\n  /** one touch button as the moment changes: its label (TALK beside someone, NEXT in a dialog; null: its own) and whether\n   *  it shows (false: nothing for it to do here). Cheap to call every step: input.touchSet('confirm', near ? 'TALK' : null,\n   *  near || talking) */\n  touchSet(a, label = null, show = true) {\n    const t = this._touchSet || (this._touchSet = {}); t[a] = { label, show: !!show };\n    const b = this._touchBtns && this._touchBtns[a]; if (b && b.isConnected) this._touchLook(b, a);\n  }\n  _touchLook(b, a) {\n    const t = this._touchSet && this._touchSet[a], o = this._touchArgs ? this._touchArgs.o : {}, text = (t && t.label) || (o.labels && o.labels[a]) || a.toUpperCase(), d = t && !t.show ? 'none' : '';\n    if (b.textContent !== text) b.textContent = text;\n    if (b.style.display !== d) { b.style.display = d; if (d) { b._inputPointers && b._inputPointers.clear(); b.classList.remove('on'); this.release('Act:' + a); } }   // (hidden under a finger: let go)\n  }\n  /** where the touch buttons cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (the same space as r.W and r.H), or\n   *  null while none show: keep a prompt or a box clear of it (a bottom E.Dialog does by itself) */\n  touchArea() {\n    const el = this._touchEl, S = this.screen; if (!el || !S.canvas || !el.isConnected) return null;\n    let x0 = 1e9, y0 = 1e9, x1 = -1e9, y1 = -1e9;\n    for (const b of el.querySelectorAll('button')) { const q = b.getBoundingClientRect(); if (!q.width) continue; x0 = Math.min(x0, q.left); y0 = Math.min(y0, q.top); x1 = Math.max(x1, q.right); y1 = Math.max(y1, q.bottom); }\n    if (x1 < x0) return null;\n    const a = this.screenPoint(x0, y0), b = this.screenPoint(x1, y1);\n    return { x0: a[0], y0: a[1], x1: b[0], y1: b[1] };\n  }\n  /** build the touch buttons again from the player's settings (touch, hand, touchSize, touchOpacity) */\n# Canonical pilot expedition evidence\n\nValidated 2026-11-06 against `src/sim/mine.js`, native `E.Platformer`/`E.Attack`, and authored rooms, with default tuning (`spawnInterval=6.8`, rock life 17s, collection grace 2s), seed 73082 and fixed 1/120s steps.\n\n`P.Pilot` is a deterministic intent producer. It reads visible world and authored geometry, outputs `P.neutral()` canonical controls, and does not write miner positions, health, inventory, RNG, score, collision outcomes, completion flags or any other simulation state. Normal evidence uses `new P.Mine()` with `god=false`, `attract=false`, `developer=false`; no test-only completion route exists.\n\n## Default solo results\n\n| Route chosen in each chamber | Finish (simulation seconds) | Score | Total hits taken | Tool breaks | Replacement catches | Redirects high / low | Deliberate redirected collisions | Key expiries |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Forge | 185.967 | 484 | 2 | 2 | 6 | 4 / 4 | 2 | 0 |\n| Spring | 250.933 | 632 | 2 | 2 | 8 | 4 / 5 | 3 | 1 |\n| Crusher | 207.275 | 585 | 3 | 4 | 6 | 5 / 5 | 2 | 0 |\n\nEvery route:\n\n- Completes all three chambers through explicit Interact presses, with exactly three caught keys and three doorway entries.\n- Exercises move, jump, duck, climb, keyless jam, ore chops, high/low redirects, genuine rock collisions, tool break, unarmed traversal and replacement collection.\n- Has ordered events `break → pick → key → door`, followed by all three extraction wave clears and victory.\n- Takes genuine damage where applicable, with ordinary three-mark health restored only at chamber transitions.\n- Finishes with over ten minutes of margin against Hub's 900-second wall-clock limit at normal simulation speed.\n\nThe Spring run deliberately declines an unsafe high key, survives its expiry, and catches the guaranteed replacement from a bottom-lane mine event. The pilot also pursues reachable picks while waiting; it does not wait unarmed indefinitely just because a high key remains active.\n\nAdditional robustness: all 15 combinations of routes 0/1/2 and seeds 73082, 73083, 73084, 1 and 12345 completed without god or attract immunity.\n\nTwo independently controlled simultaneous miners also completed all default-seed routes through the same canonical path: Forge 194.692s, Spring 202.625s, Crusher 207.317s. These successful runs required no rescue; separate simulation cases must exercise rescue and player departure.\n\n## Fixes from investigation\n\n1. Navigation formerly rounded the current feet position to a tier while airborne. A gap jump could therefore be mistaken for arrival on the higher tier; the pilot repeatedly reversed toward the wrong ladder, exhausted tools and eventually died. Tier navigation now uses the floor containing the feet. Actual engine traversal rules did not need changing for this defect.\n2. The former pilot chased every available rock through the center of supply collision lanes. The revised pilot defends the clear intake lane, pursues supplies when needed, then navigates the chosen door.\n3. Fixed-distance unarmed jumping began too early for slow incoming ore. The pilot now estimates contact time and forecasts bounces, jumping shortly before low contact and ducking high hopper passes. It retains variable-height jump holds.\n4. Goal priority formerly ignored picks after work quota, or while a high key existed. Supply recovery remains a priority when a safe key is not immediately catchable.\n5. Facing changes on ladders and native crouch/descent overlap were simulation issues reported to the root agent and fixed there. This pilot uses those public canonical controls and has no engine modifications.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nFrom development folder, a pure native route run is:\n\n```js\nimport { load } from './tools/load.mjs';\nconst P = load();\nfor (let route = 0; route < 3; route++) {\n  const mine = new P.Mine();\n  const pilot = new P.Pilot(route);\n  while (mine.time < 900 && ['playing', 'transition'].includes(mine.status)) {\n    mine.step({ 0: pilot.intent(mine) });\n    mine.takeEvents();\n  }\n  if (mine.status !== 'victory' || mine.god || mine.attract) throw Error(`Route ${route}`);\n  for (const type of ['jump', 'duck', 'climb', 'jam', 'redirect', 'collision', 'waveClear', 'victory']) {\n    if (!mine.features[type]) throw Error(`Route ${route} missed ${type}`);\n  }\n  const types = mine.history.map(event => event.type);\n  let at = -1;\n  for (const type of ['break', 'pick', 'key', 'door']) {\n    at = types.indexOf(type, at + 1);\n    if (at < 0) throw Error(`Route ${route} missed ordered ${type}`);\n  }\n  if (mine.stats.keys !== 3 || mine.stats.doors !== 3 || mine.features.waveClear !== 3 ||\n      !mine.stats.high || !mine.stats.low) throw Error(`Route ${route} incomplete features`);\n}\n```\n\nRoot's aggregate verification runner should contain equivalent route checks so final packaging reruns this evidence. This investigation used native headless state; it does not establish physical controller feel, touch usability or audiovisual presentation.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
